// Chapter headings and background written for Joined: Joshua through Ezekiel (Genesis to Deuteronomy are in chapters10 and 11).
Object.assign(CHAPTERS,{
"josh.1":{title:"Be strong and of a good courage",year:"Date uncertain (about 1400 or 1200 BC)",
 sum:"After Moses’ death the Lord commissions Joshua to lead Israel over Jordan—“Every place that the sole of your foot shall tread upon, that have I given unto you”—“Be strong and of a good courage”—Joshua is to meditate on the book of the law day and night—The people promise to follow him.",
 bg:["The name Joshua (Hebrew Yehoshua) means “the LORD saves.” Moses had called him Oshea (Hoshea, “salvation”) before changing his name (Numbers 13:16), and Jesus is the Greek form of the same name.","Joshua’s task is to lead Israel into the promised land, and the Lord’s repeated promise is “I will not fail thee, nor forsake thee” (1:5). Later chapters show that success depends on keeping the law and trusting God, not only on strength in battle."]},
"josh.2":{title:"The LORD your God, he is God in heaven above",year:"Date uncertain (about 1400 or 1200 BC)",
 sum:"Joshua sends two spies from Shittim to Jericho—Rahab hides them and tells how fear of Israel has fallen on her people—She asks for mercy for her family—The spies promise protection if she hangs a scarlet cord from her window—They return and report that the people “faint because of us.”",
 bg:["Rahab is described as a harlot (Hebrew zonah). Some later readers have tried to soften the word, but the text states it plainly, and Rahab is honored in the New Testament as a woman of faith: she appears in Matthew 1:5 in the ancestry of Jesus, in Hebrews 11:31, and in James 2:25.","The scarlet cord (2:18) is a sign for her household’s rescue, much like the blood on the doorposts at the first Passover. The Hebrew word for “cord” is tiqvah, which also means “hope.”"]},
"josh.3":{title:"Sanctify yourselves: for to morrow the LORD will do wonders among you",year:"Date uncertain (about 1400 or 1200 BC)",
 sum:"Israel camps by the Jordan and is told to follow the ark of the covenant at a distance—Joshua tells the people to sanctify themselves, for the Lord will do wonders—When the priests’ feet touch the water, the river stops and rises in a heap far upstream—All Israel crosses over on dry ground.",
 bg:["Jordan is usually thought to come from a Hebrew root meaning “to go down,” since the river descends a long way below sea level. The crossing happens at flood time, “all the time of harvest” (3:15), which makes the miracle greater.","The Jordan crossing echoes the parting of the Red Sea. Israel passed through water to leave Egypt under Moses and passes through water again to enter the promised land under Joshua. The ark leads the way, and the priests must step into the river before the water stops."]},
"josh.4":{title:"What mean ye by these stones?",year:"Date uncertain (about 1400 or 1200 BC)",
 sum:"Twelve men, one from each tribe, take twelve stones from the Jordan riverbed—The stones are set up as a memorial so children will ask “What mean ye by these stones?”—Joshua also sets up twelve stones in the river—Israel camps at Gilgal and remembers that the Lord dried up the Jordan.",
 bg:["The memorial stones were meant to start conversations between parents and children: when a child asks, the parent tells the story of what the Lord did (4:6–7, 21–24). Remembering is a central theme in Joshua and in the Book of Mormon.","Gilgal sounds like the Hebrew word for “to roll” and is explained in chapter 5. It became Israel’s first camp in Canaan and a base for the early campaigns. Its exact location is not certain."]},
"josh.5":{title:"As captain of the host of the LORD am I now come",year:"Date uncertain (about 1400 or 1200 BC)",
 sum:"Canaanite kings lose heart at the news of the Jordan—The men born in the wilderness are circumcised at Gilgal—Israel keeps the Passover and the manna ceases—Joshua meets a man with a drawn sword, the captain of the Lord’s host, and is told, “Loose thy shoe from off thy foot; for the place whereon thou standest is holy.”",
 bg:["The name Gilgal is explained in 5:9: “This day have I rolled away the reproach of Egypt from off you.” The Hebrew verb galal means “to roll.” Circumcision renewed the covenant sign with the generation born in the wilderness (5:5).","The command to remove the shoe (5:15) closely echoes Moses at the burning bush (Exodus 3:5). The text does not say who the heavenly captain is, but Joshua worships him and he speaks with divine authority."]},
"josh.6":{title:"The wall of the city shall fall down flat",year:"Date uncertain (about 1400 or 1200 BC)",
 sum:"The Lord tells Joshua to march around Jericho once a day for six days, and seven times on the seventh—Priests blow rams’ horns, the people shout, and “the wall fell down flat”—Rahab and her family are spared—The city is devoted to destruction, and Joshua pronounces a curse on anyone who rebuilds it.",
 bg:["This chapter is hard for many readers. The Hebrew word herem means “devoted” or set apart for God, and here it means that the city and its people were given over to destruction. Scholars debate how literally the language of total destruction should be read and how it fits ancient war rhetoric, but the story is troubling, and it is balanced by the mercy shown to Rahab and her household.","Jericho (Tell es-Sultan) is among the oldest cities known, but archaeologists disagree about when its walls fell and whether the evidence matches Joshua’s account. Some excavators date the destruction layer to about 1400 BC, while others see no occupied city then. The question is still open."]},
"josh.7":{title:"Israel hath sinned",year:"Date uncertain (about 1400 or 1200 BC)",
 sum:"Achan secretly takes devoted things from Jericho—Israel is defeated at Ai and Joshua falls before the ark—The Lord says “Israel hath sinned” and Achan is found out by lot—Achan confesses and is stoned in the valley that is named Achor.",
 bg:["Achor means “trouble” (7:26), and Joshua’s words, “Why hast thou troubled us? the LORD shall trouble thee this day” (7:25), play on the name. The chapter shows that one person’s hidden sin was treated as affecting the whole covenant community.","The prophet Hosea later turned this sad place into a promise, saying the Lord would make “the valley of Achor for a door of hope” (Hosea 2:15)."]},
"josh.8":{title:"He wrote there upon the stones a copy of the law of Moses",year:"Date uncertain (about 1400 or 1200 BC)",
 sum:"The Lord tells Joshua to go up against Ai and to set an ambush behind the city—The ambush takes Ai while the main force feigns retreat—Israel may keep the spoil this time—Joshua builds an altar on Mount Ebal, writes the law on stones and reads the blessings and curses.",
 bg:["Ai means “the ruin.” Its location is debated: the usual site, et-Tell, shows no occupation in the period most proposed for Joshua, so scholars either suggest a different site or question the dating.","Joshua’s ceremony at Mount Ebal and Mount Gerizim fulfills Moses’ command in Deuteronomy 27:1–8 and 11:29. An altar-like structure found on Mount Ebal has been proposed as this altar, though archaeologists disagree about it."]},
"josh.9":{title:"They did work wilily",year:"Date uncertain (about 1400 or 1200 BC)",
 sum:"Kings of Canaan gather to fight Israel—The Gibeonites pretend to come from a far country, with old sacks, patched shoes and moldy bread—Israel makes a league with them without asking counsel of the Lord—When the trick is found out, the Gibeonites are made “hewers of wood and drawers of water.”",
 bg:["The Gibeonites were living only about ten miles from Israel’s camp, yet they claimed to come from far away. Israel’s leaders “asked not counsel at the mouth of the LORD” (9:14), but they kept their oath once it was made (9:19–20).","Centuries later King Saul broke this oath and the land suffered a famine until it was set right (2 Samuel 21:1–6). It shows how seriously an oath in the Lord’s name was taken."]},
"josh.10":{title:"Sun, stand thou still upon Gibeon",year:"Date uncertain (about 1400 or 1200 BC)",
 sum:"Five Amorite kings attack Gibeon, and Joshua marches all night to rescue it—The Lord throws down great hailstones on the fleeing enemy—Joshua says, “Sun, stand thou still upon Gibeon,” and the day is lengthened—The five kings are defeated, and Israel takes cities of the south.",
 bg:["Joshua’s words are written in poetry and are quoted from the book of Jasher (10:13), a lost collection of songs. Readers differ on what exactly happened: some see a literal lengthening of the day, others see poetic language about the Lord fighting for Israel. The text itself says “the LORD fought for Israel” (10:14).","The Book of Mormon refers to this event: Helaman 12:13–15 says the earth stood still at Joshua’s command, to show God’s power over the elements. The chapter again involves devoting cities to destruction, which many readers find difficult, and scholars debate how widely that language applied."]},
"josh.11":{title:"So Joshua took the whole land",year:"Date uncertain (about 1400 or 1200 BC)",
 sum:"Jabin king of Hazor gathers a great northern coalition—The Lord promises victory, and Israel defeats them at the waters of Merom—Hazor is burned—Joshua does as Moses had commanded, and the land rests from war.",
 bg:["Hazor was a large city in the far north, and excavations there show a major destruction in the Late Bronze Age. Scholars still argue about who caused it and when, and whether it matches this chapter.","The chapter says Joshua took “the whole land” (11:23), yet later chapters (13:1) say much land remained. Many scholars read this as summary language about breaking the main power of the Canaanite kings, not a total removal of the peoples. The war accounts are troubling to many readers, and the book itself presents the Lord’s promises as being kept (21:43–45)."]},
"josh.12":{title:"These are the kings of the land which the children of Israel smote",year:"Date uncertain (about 1400 or 1200 BC)",
 sum:"The kings defeated east of Jordan under Moses, Sihon and Og, are listed—The kings defeated west of Jordan under Joshua are named city by city—The total is thirty-one kings.",
 bg:["This chapter is a summary list, like a victory record. It begins with Sihon of Heshbon and Og of Bashan, who were defeated before Israel crossed the Jordan (Numbers 21:21–35).","Many names are otherwise unknown, but the list includes Jericho, Ai, Jerusalem, Hebron, Lachish and Hazor, each tied to earlier chapters. It reminds readers that these were real places and that the book treats them as part of a remembered history."]},
"josh.13":{title:"There remaineth yet very much land to be possessed",year:"Date uncertain (about 1400 or 1200 BC)",
 sum:"The Lord tells aged Joshua that much land remains—The areas still held by the Philistines, Geshurites and Phoenicians are listed—Joshua is to divide the land among the nine and a half tribes—The inheritance of Reuben, Gad and half of Manasseh east of Jordan is described, and the Levites receive no land, for “the LORD God of Israel was their inheritance.”",
 bg:["The Philistines named here (13:2–3) had five main cities, each with a “lord.” They later became Israel’s great enemies in Judges and Samuel, so the chapter shows that the conquest was unfinished.","The tribe of Levi was given no territory because their inheritance was the Lord and His service (13:14, 33; Numbers 18:20–24). Verse 22 also notes that Balaam was killed here, linking back to Numbers 22–24."]},
"josh.14":{title:"He wholly followed the LORD God of Israel",year:"Date uncertain (about 1400 or 1200 BC)",
 sum:"Eleazar and Joshua divide the land by lot—Caleb, now eighty-five, reminds Joshua of Moses’ promise—Caleb asks, “Now therefore give me this mountain”—Joshua blesses Caleb and gives him Hebron, because he “wholly followed the LORD God of Israel.”",
 bg:["Casting lots was a way of letting the Lord decide, and Proverbs 16:33 says that the decision belongs to Him. Caleb and Joshua were the only two adults from the generation of the Exodus who entered the land (Numbers 14:30).","Hebron was formerly called Kirjath-arba (14:15) and was a place of Abraham’s family, where Sarah was buried (Genesis 23). Caleb’s faith at age eighty-five shows that his courage had lasted forty-five years."]},
"josh.15":{title:"Give me a blessing",year:"Date uncertain (about 1400 or 1200 BC)",
 sum:"The borders of Judah’s inheritance are described—Caleb drives the sons of Anak from Hebron—Caleb offers his daughter Achsah to the man who takes Kirjath-sepher, and Othniel does so—Achsah asks, “Give me a blessing,” and receives springs of water—Cities of Judah are listed, but the Jebusites remain in Jerusalem.",
 bg:["Achsah is a bold and resourceful woman: she asks her father for land with water, because a dry southern land would be of little use without springs (15:18–19). The same story is told in Judges 1:12–15.","The Jebusites could not be driven out of Jerusalem (15:63), and the city stayed in their hands until David took it (2 Samuel 5:6–9)."]},
"josh.16":{title:"The lot of the children of Joseph",year:"Date uncertain (about 1400 or 1200 BC)",
 sum:"The land given to Ephraim and Manasseh, the sons of Joseph, is described—Ephraim’s border runs from the Jordan near Jericho to the sea—Ephraim fails to drive out the Canaanites at Gezer, who serve “under tribute.”",
 bg:["Joseph’s descendants were counted as two tribes, Ephraim and Manasseh, as Jacob had blessed them (Genesis 48:5). This is why there are still twelve tribes in the land even though Levi received no territory.","Ephraim’s land lay in the central hill country and included Shechem, Shiloh and Bethel, important religious places in early Israel. Latter-day Saint readers will recall that Lehi’s family descended from Joseph (1 Nephi 5:14), and Alma 10:3 names Manasseh as Lehi’s ancestor."]},
"josh.17":{title:"He gave them an inheritance among the brethren of their father",year:"Date uncertain (about 1400 or 1200 BC)",
 sum:"Manasseh receives land on both sides of Jordan—The daughters of Zelophehad ask for an inheritance and are given one—Ephraim and Manasseh complain that their lot is too small—Joshua tells them to clear the forested hill country, and the Canaanites with iron chariots can be driven out.",
 bg:["The five daughters of Zelophehad had asked for their father’s inheritance when Moses was alive (Numbers 27:1–11), and here they receive their share because the Lord had commanded it. The text treats them as full heirs.","The Canaanites’ iron chariots (17:16) made the valleys hard to conquer. Joshua tells Joseph’s tribes to use their strength to cut down the forest and take the hills."]},
"josh.18":{title:"How long are ye slack to go to possess the land?",year:"Date uncertain (about 1400 or 1200 BC)",
 sum:"The tabernacle is set up at Shiloh—Joshua asks the seven tribes without land, “How long are ye slack to go to possess the land?”—Three men from each tribe survey and describe the land in seven parts—Joshua casts lots at Shiloh, and Benjamin’s inheritance is defined.",
 bg:["Shiloh became the center of Israel’s worship during the period of the judges. It is where the tabernacle stood until the time of Eli and young Samuel (1 Samuel 1–4).","Joshua’s rebuke shows that the work was not finished for lack of will, not lack of God’s promise. The survey was done in writing (18:4, 9), so the lots could be drawn fairly."]},
"josh.19":{title:"The children of Israel gave an inheritance to Joshua the son of Nun among them",year:"Date uncertain (about 1400 or 1200 BC)",
 sum:"Lots are cast for Simeon, Zebulun, Issachar, Asher, Naphtali and Dan—Simeon’s land lies within Judah’s—The Danites are crowded and go north to take Leshem—Joshua receives Timnath-serah, the city he asked for, in Ephraim.",
 bg:["Joshua asked for only one city and received it last, after every tribe had its land (19:49–50). He puts others before himself in the way he leads.","Dan’s northern move (19:47) is told in more detail in Judges 18. Verse 47 calls the city Leshem, which was renamed Dan, and the phrase “from Dan even to Beer-sheba” came to mean the whole land."]},
"josh.20":{title:"Appoint out for you cities of refuge",year:"Date uncertain (about 1400 or 1200 BC)",
 sum:"The Lord tells Joshua to set apart cities of refuge—A person who kills “unawares and unwittingly” may flee there from the avenger of blood—He is to stand at the gate, state his case and be received, until he stands before the congregation—Six cities are named, three on each side of Jordan.",
 bg:["In ancient Israel a relative, the “avenger of blood” (Hebrew go’el hadam), had a duty to seek justice for a death. The cities of refuge protected a person whose act was accidental until a fair hearing was held (Numbers 35:9–34; Deuteronomy 19:1–13).","The six cities were Kedesh, Shechem and Hebron west of Jordan, and Bezer, Ramoth and Golan east of it (20:7–8). They were spread so that one was within reach from anywhere. Hebrews 6:18 speaks of those “who have fled for refuge,” and many Christians see in this a picture of Christ."]},
"josh.21":{title:"There failed not ought of any good thing which the LORD had spoken",year:"Date uncertain (about 1400 or 1200 BC)",
 sum:"The Levites ask for the cities promised through Moses—Forty-eight cities with pasture lands are given by lot from all the tribes—“The LORD gave unto Israel all the land which he sware to give unto their fathers”—“There failed not ought of any good thing which the LORD had spoken.”",
 bg:["The Levites had no tribal land and were scattered through all of Israel so they could teach the people the law (Numbers 35:1–8; Deuteronomy 33:10). Six of their cities were also cities of refuge.","Verses 43–45 give a summary: the Lord kept every promise to give Israel rest and land. The same book also admits that Israel had not driven out all the peoples (13:1; 15:63; 16:10; 17:12), and scholars discuss how these statements fit together."]},
"josh.22":{title:"A witness between us that the LORD is God",year:"Date uncertain (about 1400 or 1200 BC)",
 sum:"Joshua blesses the eastern tribes and sends them home—They build a great altar by the Jordan, and the western tribes prepare for war—Phinehas and the princes ask why—The eastern tribes explain the altar is a witness and not for sacrifice, and all are satisfied.",
 bg:["The eastern tribes name the altar Ed, which means “witness” (22:34, where the KJV supplies the word). They wanted their children never to be told, “Ye have no part in the LORD” (22:25–27).","The story is a reminder to ask before judging others. Israel almost went to war with its own brothers over a misunderstanding, and Phinehas’s careful questions resolved it peacefully."]},
"josh.23":{title:"Cleave unto the LORD your God",year:"Date uncertain (about 1400 or 1200 BC)",
 sum:"Old Joshua gathers Israel’s leaders—He reminds them that the Lord has fought for them—He urges them to keep the law and to “cleave unto the LORD your God”—He warns that marriage with the nations and their gods will bring them to ruin—“Not one thing hath failed.”",
 bg:["Joshua calls the remaining peoples “snares and traps” (23:13), and his warning is mostly about religious faithfulness, not about ethnicity. The danger he names is that Israel would worship the gods of the land.","Verse 14, “I am going the way of all the earth,” is a farewell, like the speeches of Moses and, later, Samuel and David. Joshua names both the promised blessings (23:14) and the curses that will follow if Israel breaks the covenant (23:15–16)."]},
"josh.24":{title:"Choose you this day whom ye will serve",year:"Date uncertain (about 1400 or 1200 BC)",
 sum:"At Shechem Joshua recounts the Lord’s dealings from Abraham to the conquest—“Choose you this day whom ye will serve”—“but as for me and my house, we will serve the LORD”—The people covenant to serve the Lord, and Joshua sets up a stone as a witness—Joshua dies at 110, and Joseph’s bones are buried at Shechem.",
 bg:["Shechem was where Abraham first built an altar in Canaan (Genesis 12:6–7) and where Jacob buried his household’s foreign gods (Genesis 35:4). Joshua renews the covenant at the same place. The agreement has a form like ancient treaties: history, commands, witnesses and blessings and curses.","Joshua’s call recalls the choice described in 2 Nephi 2:27: “Wherefore, men are free … to choose liberty and eternal life.” The book ends with Israel faithful “all the days of Joshua” (24:31), and Joseph’s bones, carried out of Egypt (Exodus 13:19; Genesis 50:25), are finally buried (24:32)."]},
"ruth.1":{title:"Whither thou goest, I will go",year:"Date uncertain (about 1100 BC)",
 sum:"In the days of the judges a famine drives Elimelech’s family from Bethlehem to Moab—Elimelech and his two sons die, leaving Naomi with her Moabite daughters in law—Naomi returns home and Orpah turns back—Ruth clings to Naomi, “Whither thou goest, I will go”—Naomi says, “Call me Mara,” and they arrive in Bethlehem at barley harvest.",
 bg:["Names carry meaning here. Elimelech means “my God is king,” Naomi means “pleasant,” and Bethlehem means “house of bread,” which is ironic given the famine. Traditionally Mahlon and Chilion are understood as “sickly” and “wasting,” but their meaning is uncertain. Ruth’s name is also of uncertain meaning, and Naomi says to call her Mara, which means “bitter” (1:20).","Ruth’s promise in 1:16–17 is a covenant oath, and she chooses Naomi’s people and Naomi’s God. Moabites were barred from the assembly (Deuteronomy 23:3), so her faithfulness is all the more striking. The book of Ruth is set in the days when “the judges ruled,” a time Judges describes as one of disorder."]},
"ruth.2":{title:"Under whose wings thou art come to trust",year:"Date uncertain (about 1100 BC)",
 sum:"Ruth gleans in the field of Boaz, a kinsman of Elimelech—Boaz notices her, praises her loyalty to Naomi and protects her—“The LORD recompense thy work”—Ruth brings home an ephah of barley and food she has saved—Naomi sees that Boaz is “one of our next kinsmen.”",
 bg:["The law of Moses told landowners to leave the edges of their fields and fallen grain for the poor, the widow and the stranger (Leviticus 19:9–10; 23:22; Deuteronomy 24:19). Ruth, a foreign widow, was in just that position. An ephah is about half a bushel, so what she brought home was a large amount for a day.","Boaz’s name is uncertain in meaning. He is called “a mighty man of wealth” (2:1), and he blesses Ruth by saying she has come to trust under the Lord’s “wings” (2:12), a picture of a bird sheltering its young. In chapter 3 Ruth will use the same image when she asks Boaz to spread his wing, or skirt, over her."]},
"ruth.3":{title:"Thou art a virtuous woman",year:"Date uncertain (about 1100 BC)",
 sum:"Naomi sends Ruth to the threshing floor to seek rest and security—Ruth lies at the feet of Boaz and asks him to spread his skirt over her—Boaz blesses her kindness and calls her “a virtuous woman”—He promises to act, though a nearer kinsman has the first right—Ruth returns with six measures of barley.",
 bg:["The scene at the threshing floor is not fully explained, and scholars discuss what “uncover his feet” would have meant to the first readers. The story stresses that Boaz acts with honor: he tells Ruth to stay safe, sends her away before dawn so that no one gossips, and puts the matter before the proper authority.","“Virtuous woman” translates the Hebrew eshet hayil, which means a woman of strength and worth, the same phrase used in Proverbs 31:10. Boaz also praises her hesed, her loyal kindness, as even greater than at the beginning (3:10)."]},
"ruth.4":{title:"There is a son born to Naomi",year:"Date uncertain (about 1100 BC)",
 sum:"Boaz settles the matter at the city gate before ten elders—The nearer kinsman declines to redeem the land, drawing off his shoe—Boaz redeems Elimelech’s land and takes Ruth as his wife—The women bless Naomi, “which is better to thee than seven sons”—Obed is born, the grandfather of David.",
 bg:["A kinsman-redeemer (Hebrew go’el) protected family land and a family’s name when a relative was in need (Leviticus 25:25). The marriage to Ruth also recalls the duty to raise up the name of the dead (Deuteronomy 25:5–10), though the custom in Ruth is not exactly the same, and scholars debate how the two laws fit together. Obed means “servant.”","The book ends with a genealogy from Perez to David (4:18–22), and Matthew 1:5–6 includes Boaz, Ruth and David in the ancestry of Jesus. Ruth, a Moabite and a widow, thus becomes a great-grandmother of King David, as the Lord brings fullness out of emptiness in Bethlehem, “house of bread.”"]},
"judg.1":{title:"The LORD was with Judah",year:"Date uncertain (about 1200–1050 BC)",
 sum:"After Joshua’s death Israel asks who shall go up first, and the Lord answers, “Judah shall go up”—Judah and Simeon defeat Adoni-bezek, and Caleb’s daughter Achsah is given to Othniel—“The LORD was with Judah,” but the tribes fail to drive out many Canaanites, and “the Amorites would dwell in mount Heres.”",
 bg:["Judges picks up the story after Joshua. The Hebrew word for “judge” (shophet) means a leader or deliverer who rules and rescues, not only someone who decides court cases. Judah’s name means “praise” (Genesis 29:35), and here his tribe leads the way.","Achsah asks her father Caleb for “springs of water” along with her dry southern land (1:15), a small scene of wisdom in a chapter that ends with Israel falling short. Verse 19 says the tribe could not drive out the people of the valley “because they had chariots of iron,” a sign of how strong the Canaanite cities were."]},
"judg.2":{title:"Another generation after them, which knew not the LORD",year:"Date uncertain (about 1200–1050 BC)",
 sum:"An angel of the Lord comes to Bochim and rebukes Israel for making covenants with the land’s people—The people weep and sacrifice—Joshua dies, and “another generation” arises “which knew not the LORD”—Israel serves Baalim and Ashtaroth—The Lord raises up judges and delivers them, but they return to corruption when each judge dies.",
 bg:["Bochim means “weepers” or “weeping ones” (2:5). Verses 11–19 give the pattern the book follows: Israel does evil, is oppressed, cries out, and the Lord raises up a deliverer, then peace lasts only “all the days of the judge.” Baalim (plural of Baal, “lord”) and Ashtaroth were the fertility gods of Canaan.","The Book of Mormon shows the same pattern. Helaman 12:2 says that when the Lord prospers his people, that is when they are likely to “harden their hearts, and do forget the Lord their God.”"]},
"judg.3":{title:"The LORD raised up a deliverer to the children of Israel",year:"Date uncertain (about 1200–1050 BC)",
 sum:"Nations are left in the land to prove Israel—Israel marries among them and serves their gods—Othniel, with the Spirit of the Lord upon him, defeats Chushan-rishathaim, and the land rests forty years—Left-handed Ehud slays the Moabite king Eglon with a dagger—Shamgar slays six hundred Philistines with an ox goad.",
 bg:["Ehud was a Benjamite, and Benjamin means “son of the right hand” (Genesis 35:18, margin), yet Ehud is described as left-handed. Some scholars note that Eglon’s name resembles the Hebrew word for “calf,” and the writer may be hinting at how well-fed he was. This is possible but not stated.","Othniel was Caleb’s nephew and the husband of Achsah (see 1:12–13), so the first judge comes from the faithful generation of Joshua and Caleb. A goad was a long pointed stick used to drive oxen; Shamgar’s story takes only one verse (3:31)."]},
"judg.4":{title:"She judged Israel at that time",year:"Date uncertain (about 1200–1050 BC)",
 sum:"Jabin of Hazor and his captain Sisera, with nine hundred chariots of iron, oppress Israel twenty years—Deborah the prophetess calls Barak to gather ten thousand men—Barak: “If thou wilt go with me, then I will go”—Deborah foretells that Sisera will fall into the hand of a woman—Sisera flees, and Jael drives a tent pin through his temple.",
 bg:["Deborah means “bee,” and Barak means “lightning.” Deborah is the only judge described as a prophetess, and she gave counsel under a palm tree in the hill country of Ephraim (4:5). Jael’s name is often explained as “mountain goat” or “ibex,” though that is less certain.","The Kenites, Jael’s husband Heber’s people, were descended from Moses’ father-in-law (4:11), so they were friendly to Israel. Sisera’s iron chariots were useless once the Kishon river flooded the plain; chapter 5 tells that part in poetry."]},
"judg.5":{title:"Awake, awake, Deborah: awake, awake, utter a song",year:"Date uncertain (about 1200–1050 BC)",
 sum:"Deborah and Barak sing of the Lord’s coming from Seir, when “the mountains melted”—Zebulun and Naphtali are praised, while Reuben, Gilead, Dan and Asher stay behind—“They fought from heaven; the stars in their courses fought against Sisera”—Jael is blessed above women—Sisera’s mother waits at her window—“So let all thine enemies perish, O LORD.”",
 bg:["This is the Song of Deborah, which many scholars consider among the oldest Hebrew poetry in the Bible, perhaps older than the prose story in chapter 4. Its language is compact and sometimes hard to translate, so some lines are uncertain.","Deborah calls herself “a mother in Israel” (5:7). Notice that the song names tribes that came and tribes that stayed home; Israel at this time was a loose group of tribes, not a united nation. The poem ends with a quiet picture of Sisera’s mother, a reminder that the enemy’s loss was also a family’s sorrow."]},
"judg.6":{title:"The LORD is with thee, thou mighty man of valour",year:"Date uncertain (about 1200–1050 BC)",
 sum:"Midian oppresses Israel seven years, and the people hide in dens—An angel finds Gideon threshing wheat by the winepress and calls him “thou mighty man of valour”—Gideon offers a gift, and fire consumes it—He throws down his father’s altar of Baal by night, and is called Jerubbaal—The Spirit comes upon him, and he asks for a sign of the fleece.",
 bg:["Gideon’s name is usually explained as “hewer” or “cutter,” which fits the man who cuts down the Asherah beside Baal’s altar. His new name Jerubbaal means “let Baal contend” or “let Baal plead,” because his father said that if Baal is a god he can defend himself (6:31–32). Gideon’s altar is named Jehovah-shalom, “the LORD is peace” (6:24).","Gideon calls himself the least in the weakest family of Manasseh (6:15), and he asks for signs more than once. The text does not say whether the fleece shows doubt or careful faith; it simply shows God patiently answering. Midian’s raiders came with camels “without number” (6:5), a threat to Israel’s harvests."]},
"judg.7":{title:"The sword of the LORD, and of Gideon",year:"Date uncertain (about 1200–1050 BC)",
 sum:"The Lord says Gideon’s army is too large—Twenty-two thousand fearful men go home—Ten thousand are tested at the water, and three hundred who lap are chosen—Gideon hears a Midianite’s dream of a barley cake—With trumpets, empty pitchers and lamps they cry, “The sword of the LORD, and of Gideon,” and Midian flees.",
 bg:["The Lord reduced the army so that Israel would not boast, “Mine own hand hath saved me” (7:2). Letting fearful men return home matches the instruction in Deuteronomy 20:8. Scholars do not agree on why lapping like a dog marked the three hundred; the text only says that the Lord chose them.","Isaiah later speaks of this victory as “the day of Midian” (Isaiah 9:4), a picture of God breaking the oppressor’s yoke without human power. The Book of Mormon includes that verse at 2 Nephi 19:4."]},
"judg.8":{title:"The LORD shall rule over you",year:"Date uncertain (about 1200–1050 BC)",
 sum:"Gideon calms the Ephraimites—He pursues the Midianite kings, punishes Succoth and Penuel for refusing help, and slays Zebah and Zalmunna—Israel offers him a crown, but he answers, “The LORD shall rule over you”—He makes an ephod that becomes a snare, and after his death Israel returns to Baal.",
 bg:["Gideon’s refusal of kingship is striking, since later Israel does ask for a king (1 Samuel 8). Yet he asks for the people’s gold earrings and makes an ephod, and verse 27 says “all Israel went thither a whoring after it: which thing became a snare unto Gideon.” The writer does not explain exactly what the ephod was, but it led to idolatry.","Verse 31 introduces Abimelech, a son by a concubine in Shechem; his name means “my father is king.” That name sits uneasily beside his father’s words “neither shall my son rule over you.” Chapter 9 shows what came of it."]},
"judg.9":{title:"The trees went forth on a time to anoint a king over them",year:"Date uncertain (about 1200–1050 BC)",
 sum:"Abimelech persuades Shechem to make him king, and slays seventy of his brothers—Jotham alone escapes and tells the parable of the trees that chose the bramble—God sends an evil spirit between Abimelech and Shechem—Abimelech destroys Shechem and is killed at Thebez when a woman casts a millstone on his head.",
 bg:["Jotham’s parable is one of the few true fables in the Bible, in which the olive, fig and vine refuse to rule and only the useless bramble accepts. It warns that people who seek power for its own sake are dangerous. Compare 2 Kings 14:9, where another king answers with a thistle and a cedar.","Abimelech means “my father is king,” and he is the first in Israel to rule as a king, though never called a judge. The chapter closes by saying that God repaid his wickedness and Jotham’s curse came true (9:56–57). Shechem, an old center of covenant (Joshua 24), is left in ruins."]},
"judg.10":{title:"His soul was grieved for the misery of Israel",year:"Date uncertain (about 1200–1050 BC)",
 sum:"Tola and Jair judge Israel in turn—Israel again serves Baalim and Ashtaroth and the gods of other nations—The Lord sells them to the Philistines and the Ammonites—Israel cries out and the Lord answers, “Go and cry unto the gods which ye have chosen”—They put away their strange gods, and “his soul was grieved for the misery of Israel.”",
 bg:["Tola and Jair, with Ibzan, Elon and Abdon (chapter 12), are sometimes called the minor judges because the book tells very little about them. Jair’s thirty sons rode thirty young donkeys, a sign of wealth and rank, and had thirty towns called Havoth-jair, “villages of Jair” (10:3–4).","The Lord’s first answer to Israel’s cry is a hard one, but when they remove their idols, the text says he could no longer bear their suffering (10:16). Read in order, this shows that God’s mercy responds to real change of heart, not only to words."]},
"judg.11":{title:"I have opened my mouth unto the LORD, and I cannot go back",year:"Date uncertain (about 1200–1050 BC)",
 sum:"Jephthah the Gileadite, son of a harlot, is driven out by his brothers—The elders of Gilead call him back to lead them against Ammon—The Spirit of the Lord comes on him, and he vows to offer whatever comes out of his house—His only child, a daughter, meets him—She asks two months to bewail her virginity.",
 bg:["Scholars and readers have long disagreed about what happened. Some read verses 31 and 39 to mean she was offered as a burnt offering; others point to her mourning “her virginity” and the line “she knew no man,” and understand that she was devoted to lifelong service and never married. The text does not say plainly, and the law of Moses forbids human sacrifice (Deuteronomy 12:31; Leviticus 18:21).","Jephthah’s name comes from the Hebrew verb “to open,” and he says twice that he “opened my mouth” to the Lord (11:35–36). Hebrews 11:32 lists him among those who showed faith, but the vow itself is never praised in the text."]},
"judg.12":{title:"Say now Shibboleth",year:"Date uncertain (about 1200–1050 BC)",
 sum:"The Ephraimites quarrel with Jephthah for not calling them, and he fights them—The men of Gilead hold the fords of Jordan—Anyone who cannot say “Shibboleth” is slain, and forty-two thousand fall—Jephthah dies, and Ibzan, Elon and Abdon judge Israel.",
 bg:["Shibboleth probably means an “ear of grain” or a “stream.” Ephraim’s speech apparently turned the “sh” sound into “s,” which gave them away. The word now means a test that shows who belongs, and it shows how much local dialects differed across Israel’s tribes.","The chapter is another picture of Israel at war with itself. Ibzan, Elon and Abdon, the last of the minor judges, are listed with their children and families; the text says nothing more about how they delivered Israel (12:8–15)."]},
"judg.13":{title:"The child shall be a Nazarite to God from the womb",year:"Date uncertain (about 1200–1050 BC)",
 sum:"The Philistines oppress Israel forty years—The angel of the Lord appears to Manoah’s barren wife and promises a son who will be “a Nazarite to God from the womb”—Manoah prays, and the angel returns and ascends in the flame of the offering—Samson is born, “and the Spirit of the LORD began to move him at times.”",
 bg:["A Nazarite (Hebrew nazir, “consecrated” or “set apart”) vowed to avoid wine and grape products, never cut his hair, and avoid dead bodies (Numbers 6). Most Nazarite vows were short, but Samson, Samuel (1 Samuel 1:11) and John the Baptist (Luke 1:15) were set apart for life. Samson’s name is usually linked to the Hebrew word for “sun,” so “sunny” or “little sun,” though this is not certain.","Samson’s birth is announced by an angel, as with Isaac, Samuel and John. Manoah’s name is often explained as “rest.” Samson was born in Zorah, near the Philistine border, and the Philistines were a people of the coastal plain who were Israel’s chief enemy for the next several generations."]},
"judg.14":{title:"Out of the eater came forth meat, and out of the strong came forth sweetness",year:"Date uncertain (about 1200–1050 BC)",
 sum:"Samson asks his parents to get him a Philistine woman of Timnath—On the way he slays a lion, and later finds honey in the carcass—At the wedding feast he puts a riddle to thirty companions—They threaten his wife, who gets the answer from him—He slays thirty Philistines, and his wife is given to his companion.",
 bg:["The text says the matter “was of the LORD, that he sought an occasion against the Philistines” (14:4). The Nazarite law forbids touching a dead body (Numbers 6:6); the story does not comment on Samson eating honey from the lion’s carcass, but he keeps it from his parents (14:9).","The riddle in verse 14 is a small poem, and the answer, honey from a lion, can only be known by someone who was there. The feast lasted seven days, and the thirty “sheets” and “change of garments” were fine linen and clothing, a heavy price for a bet."]},
"judg.15":{title:"With the jaw of an ass have I slain a thousand men",year:"Date uncertain (about 1200–1050 BC)",
 sum:"Samson’s father-in-law gives his wife to another—Samson burns the Philistine grain with torches tied to foxes’ tails—The Philistines burn his wife and her father—Three thousand men of Judah bind him and hand him over—With a fresh jawbone he slays a thousand men, and God brings water from a hollow place.",
 bg:["It is a sad detail that three thousand men of Judah are willing to surrender their own deliverer to keep the peace with the Philistines (15:11–13). It shows that Israel had grown used to foreign rule.","Ramath-lehi probably means “height of the jawbone” (or “throwing of the jawbone”), and En-hakkore means “spring of the caller” (15:17–19), a place named for Samson’s prayer when he was thirsty. After this the text says he judged Israel twenty years in the days of the Philistines (15:20)."]},
"judg.16":{title:"Let me die with the Philistines",year:"Date uncertain (about 1200–1050 BC)",
 sum:"Samson carries off the gates of Gaza—Delilah, whom he loves, is offered silver by the Philistine lords to learn the secret of his strength—After three deceptions he tells her that his hair has never been cut—His hair is shorn, he “wist not that the LORD was departed from him”—Blinded, he grinds in prison, then prays and pulls down the pillars of Dagon’s temple.",
 bg:["Delilah’s name is of uncertain meaning; some connect it with Hebrew words for “night” or for “weak,” but this is only guessed at. The story stresses that Samson’s strength came from the Lord, tied to his Nazarite vow, with the uncut hair as its outward sign; when Samson breaks faith, “he wist not that the LORD was departed from him” (16:20).","The Philistines credited their god Dagon with their victory (16:23). In the end Samson prays for strength, and “the dead which he slew at his death were more than they which he slew in his life” (16:30). Hebrews 11:32 names Samson among those who acted by faith."]},
"judg.17":{title:"Every man did that which was right in his own eyes",year:"Date uncertain (about 1200–1050 BC)",
 sum:"Micah of Ephraim returns eleven hundred shekels of silver to his mother, who has them made into an image—Micah sets up a household shrine and an ephod and ordains his son as priest—“In those days there was no king in Israel”—A Levite from Bethlehem comes to live with him, and Micah says, “Now know I that the LORD will do me good.”",
 bg:["The name Micah means “Who is like the LORD?” which is ironic in a chapter where the family makes an image despite the second commandment (Exodus 20:4). They mix the worship of the Lord with idols, and the writer wants readers to see how far Israel had drifted.","Chapters 17–21 are a kind of appendix. They tell of events probably from earlier in the period, not after Samson, and they repeat that “there was no king in Israel” (17:6; 18:1; 19:1; 21:25). Many readers see that phrase as preparing the way for the kings of Samuel."]},
"judg.18":{title:"A people that were at quiet and secure",year:"Date uncertain (about 1200–1050 BC)",
 sum:"The tribe of Dan, not yet settled, sends five spies who find Micah’s Levite priest—They find the peaceful city of Laish—Six hundred Danites take Micah’s ephod, images and priest—Micah protests, “Ye have taken away my gods which I made… and what have I more?”—They burn Laish, build Dan, and set up the graven image.",
 bg:["Dan’s allotment on the coast had been hard to hold (Joshua 19:47), so the tribe moved north. Laish was far from allies, and its people were “at quiet and secure.” The renamed city of Dan became the northern edge of Israel, in the phrase “from Dan even to Beersheba,” and later King Jeroboam set up a golden calf there (1 Kings 12:29).","Verse 30 names the priest as Jonathan, the son of Gershom, son of “Manasseh.” In the Hebrew text the letter n is written slightly raised, and many scholars think the name was originally Moses, changed to spare Moses’ name. If so, the line shows how far the priesthood had slipped."]},
"judg.19":{title:"There was no such deed done nor seen",year:"Date uncertain (about 1200–1050 BC)",
 sum:"A Levite goes to Bethlehem to bring back his concubine and is hosted by her father—Traveling home, they stay in Gibeah of Benjamin, where an old man shelters them—Wicked men of the city surround the house, and the concubine is abused and dies—The Levite divides her body into twelve parts and sends them through Israel.",
 bg:["This is one of the darkest stories in the Bible. It deliberately echoes the men of Sodom in Genesis 19:4–8, to show that Israel had become like the Canaanites. A concubine (Hebrew pilegesh) was a legal wife of lower rank. The old man’s offering of women to the crowd is told without approval, and the narrator never praises any of the men.","Gibeah was in Benjamin’s territory and later the hometown of King Saul. The prophet Hosea refers to “the days of Gibeah” as a time of deep corruption (Hosea 9:9; 10:9). Verse 30 says all who saw the body said that nothing like it had been seen since Israel left Egypt."]},
"judg.20":{title:"Shall I yet again go out to battle against the children of Benjamin",year:"Date uncertain (about 1200–1050 BC)",
 sum:"Israel assembles at Mizpeh “from Dan even to Beer-sheba,” and the Levite tells his story—Benjamin will not surrender the guilty men of Gibeah—Israel is twice beaten and weeps before the Lord, with Phinehas ministering—On the third day an ambush defeats Benjamin, and only six hundred escape to the rock Rimmon.",
 bg:["The phrase “from Dan even to Beer-sheba” (20:1) is the earliest use of the expression for all Israel, north to south. Benjamin means “son of the right hand,” yet its army included seven hundred chosen left-handed slingers who “could sling stones at an hair breadth, and not miss” (20:16), recalling Ehud in chapter 3.","The chapter does not say why the righteous side lost the first two battles, though it shows Israel inquiring of God each time and fasting after the second defeat. Phinehas, grandson of Aaron (20:28), is still serving at the ark, which places this story early in the period of the judges."]},
"judg.21":{title:"In those days there was no king in Israel",year:"Date uncertain (about 1200–1050 BC)",
 sum:"Israel has sworn not to give daughters in marriage to Benjamin, and mourns that a tribe is lost—They find that Jabesh-gilead sent no one to the assembly, and take four hundred young virgins—Two hundred more wives are found by letting Benjamin catch daughters of Shiloh—“Every man did that which was right in his own eyes.”",
 bg:["The book ends with Israel trying to fix one wrong with more wrongs: a hasty oath, an attack on Jabesh-gilead, and the seizing of the women of Shiloh. The text does not approve; the repeated refrain in 21:25 explains why. Later, King Saul rescues Jabesh-gilead (1 Samuel 11), and its men honor his body after his death (1 Samuel 31:11–13).","Compare Judges 2:11–19: the book begins with God raising up judges and ends with no leader at all, only people doing what seems right to each one. Readers see this as the setting for the books of Samuel and for Israel’s request for a king."]},
"esth.1":{title:"The queen Vashti refused to come at the king’s commandment",year:"About 480–470 BC",
 sum:"Ahasuerus reigns over one hundred and twenty-seven provinces from India to Ethiopia—He holds a feast of one hundred and eighty days for his princes and servants, then a seven-day feast at Shushan—Queen Vashti refuses to appear before the guests—The king’s counselors say her act will spread to all women—Vashti is deposed, and a decree goes out.",
 bg:["Ahasuerus is usually identified with the Persian king Xerxes I, who ruled from 486 to 465 BC. His winter capital was Shushan, called Susa in Greek, in what is now southwest Iran. Persia’s empire stretched from India to Ethiopia (1:1), the largest the world had yet seen. Some scholars suggest the long feast in the third year was planning for his campaign against Greece.","The text does not say why Vashti refused; commentators have suggested several reasons, but none is certain. The book of Esther never names God, a fact that stands out through the whole story. The book does not say that he was absent, and readers differ on what to make of that silence."]},
"esth.2":{title:"He brought up Hadassah, that is, Esther",year:"About 480–470 BC",
 sum:"Fair young virgins are gathered to Shushan for the king—Mordecai, a Benjamite carried captive from Jerusalem, has raised Hadassah, “that is, Esther,” his uncle’s daughter—Esther finds favor with Hegai and the king, and is crowned queen, still hiding her people—Mordecai learns of a plot against the king and tells Esther, and it is written in the chronicles.",
 bg:["Hadassah is Hebrew for “myrtle.” Esther is probably a Persian or Babylonian name; it may be linked to the Persian word for “star,” a meaning given by tradition, or to the goddess Ishtar. Mordecai’s name may echo the Babylonian god Marduk. Scholars note that Jews in exile often had a Hebrew name and a local one.","Mordecai’s family had been exiled with Jeconiah, about 597 BC (2:5–6). Esther was made queen in the seventh year of Ahasuerus (2:16), about 479 BC. The text says Esther hid her family because Mordecai told her to (2:10), and it does not explain why."]},
"esth.3":{title:"Mordecai bowed not, nor did him reverence",year:"About 480–470 BC",
 sum:"The king promotes Haman the Agagite above all the princes—All bow to Haman except Mordecai, who will not—Haman, learning that Mordecai is a Jew, plans to destroy all the Jews—They cast Pur, “that is, the lot,” for a day, and Haman offers ten thousand talents of silver—The decree is sealed with the king’s ring and sent by post to every province.",
 bg:["Haman is called an Agagite, which many readers connect with Agag, king of the Amalekites, whom Saul spared (1 Samuel 15). Mordecai is from Saul’s tribe of Benjamin, and the book may be hinting at an old rivalry. The text does not say why Mordecai refused to bow, whether out of religious conviction or for another reason.","Pur is an Akkadian word for “lot,” as verse 7 explains. The lot fell on the twelfth month, Adar, nearly a year away, which gave time for the story to turn. Haman describes the Jews as a people with laws “diverse from all people” (3:8), a charge made without naming them."]},
"esth.4":{title:"Who knoweth whether thou art come to the kingdom for such a time as this",year:"About 480–470 BC",
 sum:"Mordecai puts on sackcloth and ashes, and the Jews mourn—He asks Esther through Hatach to plead for her people—Esther replies that no one may go to the king uncalled—Mordecai answers, “Who knoweth whether thou art come to the kingdom for such a time as this?”—Esther asks the Jews to fast three days: “If I perish, I perish.”",
 bg:["God is never named in the book of Esther, and the book has no direct mention of prayer. Esther’s call for a fast (4:16) is the nearest it comes. Many readers see providence in Mordecai’s words that “enlargement and deliverance arise to the Jews from another place” (4:14), but the text leaves it for readers to judge.","Esther’s fear was real. Anyone who came before the king unsummoned could be put to death, unless he held out the golden sceptre (4:11). She had also not been called for thirty days. Her choice to go anyway made her a model of courage."]},
"esth.5":{title:"Esther drew near, and touched the top of the sceptre",year:"About 480–470 BC",
 sum:"On the third day Esther stands in the inner court, and the king holds out the golden sceptre—She invites the king and Haman to a banquet, and again the next day—Haman goes out joyful but is filled with wrath at Mordecai—At his wife’s and friends’ counsel he has a gallows fifty cubits high made.",
 bg:["The text says nothing of Esther’s reason for waiting until a second banquet. Commentators suggest she was building trust or timing her request, but it is not stated. The fast of chapter 4 hangs over the whole scene.","A cubit is about eighteen inches, so the gallows were around seventy-five feet high. The Hebrew word for gallows means “tree” or “wood,” and was likely a stake on which the body was impaled, and its great height meant it would be seen from afar. Haman’s pride is shown when he lists his riches and honors but says they mean nothing while Mordecai sits at the gate (5:11–13)."]},
"esth.6":{title:"On that night could not the king sleep",year:"About 480–470 BC",
 sum:"The king cannot sleep and hears the book of records read—He finds that Mordecai’s service in exposing the plot was never rewarded—Haman, who has come to ask for Mordecai’s hanging, is asked, “What shall be done unto the man whom the king delighteth to honour?”—He thinks it is himself, and must honor Mordecai through the street—His wife says, “Thou shalt not prevail against him.”",
 bg:["The Persian kings kept careful records of events and service, and Greek writers noted this as well. The sleepless night is a small turning point where everything changes; the text does not say that God caused it, but many readers see in it a hidden hand, especially because the book never names God.","The honors Haman describes for himself, a royal robe, a horse the king had ridden, and a crown, show what he wanted most (6:8). His wife and advisers, who earlier told him to build the gallows, now say he will surely fall before Mordecai (6:13)."]},
"esth.7":{title:"Let my life be given me at my petition",year:"About 480–470 BC",
 sum:"At the second banquet Esther asks that her life and her people be spared, for “we are sold, I and my people, to be destroyed”—The king asks, “Who is he, and where is he?”—Esther answers, “The adversary and enemy is this wicked Haman”—Haman pleads with Esther, and is hanged on the gallows he built for Mordecai.",
 bg:["Esther uses the same words as Haman’s decree, “to be destroyed, to be slain, and to perish” (7:4; compare 3:13), which shows that the king had unknowingly signed away his own queen. Haman’s fall as he lay on the couch or bed before the queen (7:8) fits the Persian custom of reclining to eat.","The hanging of Haman on his own gallows is often seen as the story’s great reversal, similar to Proverbs 26:27, “Whoso diggeth a pit shall fall therein.” Haman’s house is then given to Esther (8:1)."]},
"esth.8":{title:"The Jews had light, and gladness, and joy, and honour",year:"About 480–470 BC",
 sum:"Esther receives Haman’s house, and Mordecai receives the king’s ring—She weeps and asks that Haman’s letters be reversed—A new decree allows the Jews to gather and defend themselves—Mordecai goes out in royal apparel—“The Jews had light, and gladness, and joy, and honour,” and many of the people of the land become Jews.",
 bg:["Under Persian law a decree sealed with the king’s ring could not be revoked (8:8; see Daniel 6:8). So the king could not cancel Haman’s order, and instead allowed a second decree that let the Jews defend themselves. It was sent on the twenty-third day of the third month, about two months after the first.","The king’s decree was carried by couriers on swift horses, mules and camels (8:10, 14). The Persian empire was known for its royal post, which the Greek writer Herodotus described. Verse 17 says the fear of the Jews fell on the people, and many “became Jews.”"]},
"esth.9":{title:"They called these days Purim after the name of Pur",year:"About 480–470 BC",
 sum:"On the thirteenth of Adar the Jews gather and no one can withstand them—In Shushan five hundred men and Haman’s ten sons are slain, and a second day is granted—In the provinces seventy-five thousand are slain, and the Jews “laid not their hands on the prey”—Mordecai records the days, and Purim is kept yearly.",
 bg:["Purim comes from pur, an Akkadian word for “lot,” and the ending -im makes it plural: “lots.” It is still celebrated in February or March, on the fourteenth and fifteenth of Adar, with the reading of the scroll of Esther, gifts to the poor, and “sending portions one to another” (9:19, 22). The festival turns “from sorrow to joy.”","This chapter describes large-scale killing, and readers still wrestle with it. The second decree allowed the Jews to defend themselves (8:11), and the text stresses that they took no plunder. How literally to take the numbers, and how to read the story, is discussed by scholars, and the book does not explain."]},
"esth.10":{title:"Seeking the wealth of his people, and speaking peace to all his seed",year:"About 480–470 BC",
 sum:"King Ahasuerus lays a tribute upon the land and the isles of the sea—His acts and the greatness of Mordecai are written in the book of the chronicles of the kings of Media and Persia—Mordecai the Jew is next unto the king, great among the Jews, “seeking the wealth of his people, and speaking peace to all his seed.”",
 bg:["This is a very short chapter, only three verses. Like Joseph in Egypt and Daniel in Babylon, Mordecai rises to high office in a foreign court and uses that position to care for his people. The Persian chronicles mentioned in 10:2 have not survived.","Esther is remembered in the Hebrew Bible as one of the “Writings,” and the whole scroll is read aloud at Purim. A Greek version of Esther adds prayers and the name of God; these are found in the Apocrypha, which the Lord said can be read with the Spirit’s help (see Doctrine and Covenants 91)."]},
"1sam.1":{title:"For this child I prayed",year:"About 1100 BC",
 sum:"Elkanah’s wife Hannah, barren and grieved, goes up to Shiloh and vows that if the Lord gives her a son she will give him to the Lord “all the days of his life”—Eli at first thinks her drunk, then blesses her—Hannah conceives and names her son Samuel, “Because I have asked him of the LORD”—She keeps her vow and “lent” the child to the Lord at Shiloh.",
 bg:["Hannah’s name is usually understood to mean “grace” or “favor.” The text plays on the Hebrew word sha’al, “to ask”: she “asked” (1:20, 27) and so “lent” (1:28) the boy to the Lord. Scholars discuss how the name Samuel fits this wordplay, since it sounds more like “name of God” or “heard of God.” The text gives the “asked” explanation, and the word “lent” in 1:28 uses the same Hebrew root that gives the name Saul, “asked for.”","Shiloh, in the hill country of Ephraim, was where the tabernacle and the ark were kept at this time. Hannah’s vow that “no rasor” would touch her son’s head is like the Nazarite vow described in Numbers 6, though 1 Samuel does not use that word."]},
"1sam.2":{title:"The LORD killeth, and maketh alive",year:"About 1100 BC",
 sum:"Hannah’s song of praise: the Lord brings down the proud and “raiseth up the poor out of the dust”—The sons of Eli are “sons of Belial” who despise the Lord’s offering—Young Samuel ministers before the Lord “girded with a linen ephod,” and Hannah brings him a little coat each year—A man of God warns Eli: “them that honour me I will honour”—The Lord promises “a faithful priest” and a sure house.",
 bg:["Hannah’s song (2:1–10) is echoed in Mary’s song in Luke 1:46–55, which also praises the God who lifts up the lowly and fills the hungry. Both women rejoice over a child given by the Lord, and Hannah’s song ends by speaking of the Lord’s “anointed” (2:10), the first use of that word for a king in the Bible’s story.","“Belial” means “worthlessness” in Hebrew; calling Eli’s sons “sons of Belial” means they were worthless, wicked men. The text says they took the best of the offerings by force (2:12–17). Against this, Samuel is shown growing “in favour both with the LORD, and also with men” (2:26), a description Luke uses of the boy Jesus in Luke 2:52."]},
"1sam.3":{title:"Speak; for thy servant heareth",year:"About 1100 BC",
 sum:"“The word of the LORD was precious in those days; there was no open vision”—The Lord calls Samuel three times in the night, and Eli at last perceives who is calling—Samuel answers, “Speak; for thy servant heareth,” and is told that Eli’s house will be judged because his sons “made themselves vile, and he restrained them not”—Samuel tells Eli all—All Israel “from Dan even to Beer-sheba” knows he is a prophet.",
 bg:["The text says revelation was rare (“precious”) in these years, yet it does not say the Lord was silent for good. When Samuel is young, “the LORD was with him, and did let none of his words fall to the ground” (3:19). Dan in the far north and Beer-sheba in the far south are a standard way of saying “the whole land.”","The text says Samuel “did not yet know the LORD, neither was the word of the LORD yet revealed unto him” (3:7), so his not recognizing the voice is explained. It also says he was afraid to tell Eli the vision (3:15), which makes his honest report all the more notable. The call comes at Shiloh, where Samuel slept near the ark."]},
"1sam.4":{title:"The glory is departed from Israel",year:"About 1075–1050 BC",
 sum:"Israel is defeated by the Philistines at Aphek and brings the ark of the covenant from Shiloh into the camp—The Philistines fear but fight, defeat Israel, kill Hophni and Phinehas, and capture the ark—Eli, ninety-eight years old, falls backward and dies on hearing the news—Phinehas’s wife dies in childbirth, naming her son I-chabod, “The glory is departed from Israel.”",
 bg:["Ichabod is generally understood to mean “no glory” (some read it as “where is the glory?”); the verse gives its meaning as “The glory is departed from Israel.” Israel treated the ark almost like a lucky charm, and the story shows that having the ark did not replace obedience; the Lord had already said judgment would come on Eli’s house (2:34; 3:11–14).","Eben-ezer, mentioned in 4:1, is named only as a place here; the name is explained later, in 7:12. The Philistines were a sea-peoples group who settled the southern coast, and their five chief cities appear in the next chapters. Their defeat of Israel here is usually dated about the mid-eleventh century BC."]},
"1sam.5":{title:"The hand of the LORD was heavy upon them of Ashdod",year:"About 1075–1050 BC",
 sum:"The Philistines put the ark in the house of Dagon at Ashdod—Dagon is found fallen on his face before the ark, and the next morning with his head and palms cut off—The people of Ashdod are struck with emerods—The ark is sent on to Gath and Ekron, where fear of death falls on the people and their cry goes up to heaven.",
 bg:["Dagon was a Philistine god, probably connected with grain or farming; the name is related to the Semitic word for grain. The story shows that the Lord needs no army: even as a captured object, the ark shows the idol powerless. The “emerods” (tumors or swellings) are not identified with certainty; some scholars suggest bubonic plague, which would fit the later mention of mice in chapter 6, though the text does not say.","The ark is moved to three of the five Philistine cities in turn: Ashdod, Gath and Ekron. The Philistine leaders are a council of “lords of the Philistines” (5:8, 11), who decide together what to do with it."]},
"1sam.6":{title:"Who is able to stand before this holy LORD God?",year:"About 1075–1050 BC",
 sum:"The Philistine priests and diviners advise returning the ark with a trespass offering of golden emerods and golden mice—Two milch kine, never yoked, pull the cart straight to Beth-shemesh—The men of Beth-shemesh rejoice, then the Lord smites those who looked into the ark—“Who is able to stand before this holy LORD God?” the people ask, and the ark is sent to Kirjath-jearim.",
 bg:["The Philistines test whether their trouble is from the Lord or simply chance: if cows that have never pulled a cart and have calves at home go straight to Israel’s border, “then he hath done us this great evil” (6:9). The cows head for Beth-shemesh (“house of the sun”) and keep going, “lowing as they went” (6:12). The ark is later placed in Kirjath-jearim, where it stays until David brings it to Jerusalem in 2 Samuel 6.","The number struck at Beth-shemesh in 6:19 is “fifty thousand and threescore and ten men” in the King James Version, but some ancient manuscripts have a much smaller number, and many scholars think the town could not have held so many. The text explains the judgment by saying they “looked into the ark,” which only the Levites were to handle (Numbers 4:5, 15, 20)."]},
"1sam.7":{title:"Hitherto hath the LORD helped us",year:"About 1050 BC",
 sum:"After twenty years Samuel calls Israel to put away “strange gods” and serve the Lord only—Israel gathers at Mizpeh, fasts, and confesses—The Lord thunders against the Philistines, who are routed—Samuel sets up a stone called Eben-ezer: “Hitherto hath the LORD helped us”—Samuel judges Israel in a yearly circuit from Ramah.",
 bg:["Eben-ezer means “stone of help.” The hymn “Come, Thou Fount of Every Blessing” borrows the line “Here I raise my Ebenezer” from this verse. Setting up a stone was a way of making sure a delivering moment was not forgotten, as with the stones at the Jordan in Joshua 4.","Samuel’s circuit went through Beth-el, Gilgal and Mizpeh, and he returned each year to Ramah, his home (7:16–17). These places were spread through the hill country, so he functioned as a traveling judge and teacher, while the ark remained at Kirjath-jearim (7:1–2)."]},
"1sam.8":{title:"They have not rejected thee, but they have rejected me",year:"About 1050 BC",
 sum:"Samuel is old and his sons “walked not in his ways,” taking bribes—The elders ask for “a king to judge us like all the nations”—The Lord tells Samuel, “they have not rejected thee, but they have rejected me”—Samuel warns what “the manner of the king” will be: soldiers, taxes, and servants—The people answer, “Nay; but we will have a king over us.”",
 bg:["The people’s request is not wrong in itself, since the law of Moses had foreseen a king (Deuteronomy 17:14–20), but the reason they give is “like all the nations,” and the Lord says that for him it is a rejection. Samuel’s warning is plain: a king will take sons for war, daughters as servants, and a tenth of the crops and flocks (8:11–17).","The Hebrew word for “ask” (sha’al) runs through this chapter and the next, and the name Saul means “asked for.” The people asked for a king, and the king they receive carries that very name."]},
"1sam.9":{title:"There is in this city a man of God",year:"About 1050 BC",
 sum:"Saul, son of Kish of Benjamin, “a choice young man, and a goodly,” goes to look for his father’s lost asses—On his servant’s advice they seek “a man of God” in the city—The Lord had told Samuel the day before, “I will send thee a man out of the land of Benjamin”—Samuel seats Saul at the head of the feast and gives him the special portion set aside for him.",
 bg:["The text explains that a prophet used to be called a “Seer” (ro’eh, “one who sees”), and the later word is “prophet” (navi) (9:9). Saul’s name means “asked for” (from the same Hebrew root sha’al). His lack of a gift for Samuel (9:7) and the lost asses show a man with little pride at first, though the text also stresses his size, “higher than any of the people” (9:2).","Benjamin was the smallest tribe, a point Saul himself raises in 9:21. The Lord says he has “looked upon my people, because their cry is come unto me” (9:16), so the king is given to save them from the Philistines."]},
"1sam.10":{title:"God gave him another heart",year:"About 1050 BC",
 sum:"Samuel anoints Saul with oil and gives him three signs—Saul meets prophets, and “God gave him another heart”; he prophesies, and people ask, “Is Saul also among the prophets?”—At Mizpeh Saul is chosen by lot, found hiding “among the stuff,” and the people shout, “God save the king”—Samuel writes down “the manner of the kingdom” in a book.",
 bg:["Anointing with oil set a person apart for a task; the Hebrew word mashiach, “anointed,” is the source of the title Messiah. Samuel pours the oil from a “vial” (10:1) and tells Saul he is anointed to be “captain over his inheritance,” not yet called king.","The saying “Is Saul also among the prophets?” appears again in 19:24, where it seems to mean something different. Here the chapter says Saul was changed by the Spirit; there it follows Saul’s fall. Saul’s hiding “among the stuff” (baggage) at his own coronation is the text’s picture of his reluctance."]},
"1sam.11":{title:"To day the LORD hath wrought salvation in Israel",year:"About 1050 BC",
 sum:"Nahash the Ammonite besieges Jabesh-gilead and demands to “thrust out all your right eyes”—When Saul hears, “the Spirit of God came upon Saul,” and he sends pieces of a yoke of oxen through Israel to call the tribes—Saul wins the victory and spares those who had doubted him: “To day the LORD hath wrought salvation in Israel”—Samuel renews the kingdom at Gilgal.",
 bg:["Jabesh-gilead was east of the Jordan. Saul’s act of cutting up the oxen and sending the pieces as a summons shows how seriously a call to arms was taken: whoever did not come would see the same done to his own oxen. The numbers given in the King James Version (300,000 from Israel, 30,000 from Judah, 11:8) are large, and some ancient manuscripts have different figures.","The people of Jabesh-gilead remember this rescue: in chapter 31, the valiant men of Jabesh go all night to bury Saul and his sons."]},
"1sam.12":{title:"Only fear the LORD, and serve him in truth",year:"About 1050 BC",
 sum:"Samuel’s farewell address: “Behold, here I am: witness against me”—He reminds Israel of the Lord’s deliverances from Egypt to the judges—Thunder and rain in wheat harvest show that asking for a king was wickedness—“Fear not”: do not turn aside from the Lord—Samuel promises “God forbid that I should sin against the LORD in ceasing to pray for you.”",
 bg:["Samuel invites the people to accuse him of any wrong, and they say there is none (12:4–5); a leader’s honesty is part of his strength. Rain in the dry wheat harvest, around May or June, was very unusual in Israel, which is why it served as a sign (12:17–18).","The chapter holds two truths together: the request for a king was wrong, yet “the LORD will not forsake his people for his great name’s sake” (12:22). The people and the king are both to follow the Lord (12:14–15, 24–25)."]},
"1sam.13":{title:"Thou hast done foolishly",year:"About 1050–1030 BC",
 sum:"Jonathan strikes a Philistine garrison, and the Philistines gather in great numbers—Saul waits seven days at Gilgal, and when Samuel does not come, he offers the burnt offering himself—Samuel: “Thou hast done foolishly,” and the Lord “hath sought him a man after his own heart”—Israel has no smith, and only Saul and Jonathan have a sword or spear.",
 bg:["Verse 1 reads “Saul reigned one year” in the King James Version, but the Hebrew text appears to have lost numbers in copying, so the length of his reign is not clear from this verse. Paul later says Saul reigned forty years (Acts 13:21). The same sermon in Acts 13:22 quotes the Lord’s word about David as “a man after mine own heart,” echoing 13:14.","Samuel had told Saul to wait seven days (10:8). Saul did the priestly work of offering when the people were scattering, and the Lord’s verdict was that he had not kept the commandment. The Philistines are said to have controlled iron-working so Israel could not make weapons (13:19–22); archaeologists note that iron tools did spread through Canaan about this time."]},
"1sam.14":{title:"There is no restraint to the LORD to save by many or by few",year:"About 1050–1030 BC",
 sum:"Jonathan and his armourbearer climb up to the Philistine garrison at Michmash, trusting that “it may be that the LORD will work for us”—The Philistines panic, and Israel pursues them—Saul’s rash oath forbidding food leaves the army weary, and Jonathan unknowingly eats honey—The lot falls on Jonathan, but the people rescue him—Saul’s wars and family are listed.",
 bg:["Jonathan’s name in Hebrew (Yehonatan, Jonathan for short) means “the LORD has given.” His faith in this chapter fits the name: he does not wait for numbers but acts on the hope that the Lord can save “by many or by few.” The valley at Michmash has steep rocky sides, which fits the account of the climb in 14:4–13.","Saul’s oath is called a curse (14:24, 28), and it hurts his own army, which is faint with hunger. The people say Jonathan “hath wrought with God this day” and refuse to let him die (14:45); the text does not say that Saul was commanded to make the oath."]},
"1sam.15":{title:"To obey is better than sacrifice",year:"About 1030 BC",
 sum:"Samuel tells Saul to destroy Amalek—Saul spares King Agag and the best of the flocks—The Lord says, “It repenteth me that I have set up Saul to be king,” and Samuel tells Saul, “to obey is better than sacrifice, and to hearken than the fat of rams”—“Rebellion is as the sin of witchcraft”—Samuel says the Lord has rejected Saul from being king, and does not see him again.",
 bg:["The Amalekites had attacked Israel in the wilderness (Exodus 17:8–16; Deuteronomy 25:17–19). The command here is the ancient “ban” on a people, an extreme kind of warfare that the Bible places in the particular context of that time. The chapter focuses not on the battle but on Saul’s partial obedience and his excuse that the best animals were saved for sacrifice (15:15, 21).","Samuel’s answer in 15:22 is poetry, and it gives the heart of the matter: the Lord wants a listening heart more than offerings. Other prophets say similar things, for example Hosea 6:6. Verse 29 says the Lord does not “repent” the way people do, while verse 11 and 35 say he was grieved; the writer holds both ideas together."]},
"1sam.16":{title:"The LORD looketh on the heart",year:"About 1030–1025 BC",
 sum:"The Lord sends Samuel to Bethlehem to anoint one of Jesse’s sons—He passes over Eliab, since “man looketh on the outward appearance, but the LORD looketh on the heart”—The youngest, David, is brought from the sheep and anointed, “and the Spirit of the LORD came upon David from that day forward”—The Spirit departs from Saul and a troubling spirit comes; David plays the harp to refresh him.",
 bg:["David’s name is generally understood to mean “beloved” or “loved one.” Jesse’s family was from Bethlehem; Jesse was the son of Obed and grandson of Ruth and Boaz (Ruth 4:17, 22), so Ruth’s story leads to David’s. The New Testament traces the line of Jesus through Jesse (Matthew 1:5–6).","The King James Version says an evil spirit “from the LORD” troubled Saul (16:14, 15, 16, 23). The Joseph Smith Translation of 1 Samuel 16:14–16, 23 changes this so that the evil spirit was not from the Lord, and JST 1 Samuel 18:10 does the same. Other translations take the Hebrew to mean a distressing spirit sent under the Lord’s permission, so readers differ."]},
"1sam.17":{title:"The battle is the LORD’s",year:"About 1025 BC",
 sum:"The Philistines gather at the valley of Elah, and Goliath of Gath challenges Israel for forty days—David, bringing food to his brothers, asks why no one answers the defier of the armies of the living God—Saul tries to arm David, who goes out with a staff, a sling, and five smooth stones—“I come to thee in the name of the LORD of hosts”—David slays Goliath with a stone, and the Philistines flee.",
 bg:["Goliath’s height is “six cubits and a span” in the King James Version, about nine feet; some ancient manuscripts, including the Greek Septuagint and a Dead Sea Scroll, say “four cubits and a span,” about six and a half feet. Gath was a Philistine city, and the valley of Elah runs west from the hills of Judah toward Philistine land.","David says that “the LORD saveth not with sword and spear” (17:47), and he credits the Lord and not his skill with the sling. At the end, Saul asks whose son David is (17:55–58), though David has already played for him in chapter 16; scholars explain this in different ways, and the text does not."]},
"1sam.18":{title:"The soul of Jonathan was knit with the soul of David",year:"About 1020–1015 BC",
 sum:"Jonathan loves David “as his own soul” and makes a covenant, giving David his robe, sword, and bow—David prospers, but the women sing, “Saul hath slain his thousands, and David his ten thousands”—Saul’s jealousy grows, and he twice throws a javelin at David—Saul gives David his daughter Michal on the price of Philistine foreskins, hoping he will be killed—“Saul was afraid of David, because the LORD was with him.”",
 bg:["Jonathan (“the LORD has given”) gives David his own robe and weapons, the signs of a prince’s rank (18:4); the text does not say he intended to step aside, though he later says David will be king (23:17). The Joseph Smith Translation of 1 Samuel 18:10 changes the evil spirit “from God” to one that was not from God.","The women’s song in 18:7 is a poem of praise using “thousands” and “ten thousands,” a common way of speaking in Hebrew poetry. Saul hears it as a threat to his throne, and the text then adds, “Saul eyed David from that day and forward” (18:9). The price of one hundred foreskins (18:25) was Saul’s plan to have David killed by the Philistines, and David brought two hundred."]},
"1sam.19":{title:"Michal let David down through a window",year:"About 1020–1015 BC",
 sum:"Saul tells Jonathan and his servants to kill David, but Jonathan pleads for David and Saul swears that David will not be slain—When David again plays before Saul, Saul throws a javelin at him—Michal lets David down through a window and puts an image in his bed—David flees to Samuel at Naioth in Ramah—Saul’s messengers and Saul himself prophesy, and “Is Saul also among the prophets?”",
 bg:["Psalm 59 carries the title “when Saul sent, and they watched the house to kill him,” which links it to the night described in verses 11–17. Michal puts a “teraphim” (a household image) in the bed, with goats’ hair; the word shows that such figures were present in David’s own household, though the text does not comment.","Saul’s falling down “naked” and prophesying (19:24) means he took off his outer clothes. The Spirit of God moves on Saul’s men and on Saul, so that they cannot seize David; the saying “Is Saul also among the prophets?” turns up again, now at a moment of weakness (compare 10:11–12)."]},
"1sam.20":{title:"Go in peace, forasmuch as we have sworn both of us in the name of the LORD",year:"About 1020–1015 BC",
 sum:"David asks Jonathan, “what have I done?”—Jonathan promises to learn Saul’s mind and to make a covenant of kindness with David’s house—At the new moon feast, David’s seat is empty, and Saul hurls a javelin at Jonathan—Jonathan shoots arrows as a sign, and the friends weep and part: “The LORD be between me and thee, and between my seed and thy seed for ever.”",
 bg:["The new moon was a monthly time of feasting and sacrifice, so David’s place at Saul’s table would be noticed (20:5, 18). The arrow signal lets Jonathan tell David the truth without being seen: if the arrows fall beyond the lad, the answer is danger.","The word for kindness in the covenant is chesed, a Hebrew word for loyal, faithful love (20:8, 14). David later keeps the promise by caring for Jonathan’s son Mephibosheth (2 Samuel 9)."]},
"1sam.21":{title:"There is none like that; give it me",year:"About 1020–1015 BC",
 sum:"David flees to Nob and tells the priest Ahimelech he is on the king’s business—He receives the hallowed bread, and Goliath’s sword—Doeg the Edomite sees him—David goes to Achish king of Gath, is recognized, and feigns madness to escape.",
 bg:["Jesus pointed to David’s eating the shewbread in this chapter when his disciples were accused of breaking the Sabbath (Matthew 12:3–4; Mark 2:25–26; Luke 6:3–4). He used the story to teach that human need matters. Mark names the priest as Abiathar, while this chapter says Ahimelech, and readers explain it in different ways.","Psalm 34 has the title “when he changed his behaviour before Abimelech,” which many link to this event; Abimelech is probably a title for the king of Gath, who is called Achish here. Psalm 56 also has a title connected to Gath."]},
"1sam.22":{title:"Every one that was in distress, and every one that was in debt",year:"About 1020–1015 BC",
 sum:"David escapes to the cave of Adullam, where his family and about four hundred men “in distress,” “in debt,” and “discontented” gather around him—He places his parents with the king of Moab—The prophet Gad tells David to return to Judah—Doeg reports David’s visit to Saul, and the priests of Nob are killed—Abiathar escapes to David.",
 bg:["David’s great-grandmother Ruth was a Moabite, which may be why he turns to Moab for his parents’ safety (Ruth 4:17). The titles of Psalms 57 and 142 place them “in the cave,” and Psalm 52 is connected to Doeg’s report in this chapter.","The killing of eighty-five priests at Nob (22:18) is one of the darkest scenes in the book; Saul’s own guards refuse to strike the priests, and Doeg, an Edomite, does it. Abiathar’s escape later makes him David’s priest (23:6)."]},
"1sam.23":{title:"Strengthened his hand in God",year:"About 1020–1015 BC",
 sum:"David inquires of the Lord and saves the city of Keilah from the Philistines—The Lord tells him that the men of Keilah would give him to Saul, so he leaves—Jonathan finds David in the wood and “strengthened his hand in God,” saying, “thou shalt be king over Israel, and I shall be next unto thee”—The Ziphites betray David’s place, and Saul almost traps him at Maon before being called away.",
 bg:["David asks the Lord through the ephod brought by Abiathar (23:6, 9–12), and he follows the answers he receives even when they cost him a city he has just saved. The name given to the rock where Saul turned back, Sela-hammahlekoth, probably means “rock of division” or “rock of escape” (23:28); scholars are not certain which.","The Ziphites’ betrayal is remembered in Psalm 54, whose title says “when the Ziphims came and said to Saul, Doth not David hide himself with us?” Jonathan’s visit, with its final covenant (23:18), is the last time the text shows the two friends together."]},
"1sam.24":{title:"The LORD judge between me and thee",year:"About 1020–1015 BC",
 sum:"Saul pursues David with three thousand men to En-gedi and enters a cave where David and his men are hiding—David cuts off the skirt of Saul’s robe but will not harm “the LORD’s anointed”—David calls to Saul, showing the skirt and saying, “mine hand shall not be upon thee”—Saul weeps: “Thou art more righteous than I”—Saul admits David will be king and asks him to spare his family.",
 bg:["En-gedi (“spring of the young goat”) is an oasis with caves and waterfalls above the Dead Sea, a place well suited for hiding. David’s men said the Lord had delivered Saul into his hand (24:4), but David’s conscience pricked him even for cutting a corner of the robe (24:5).","David’s self-description, “a dead dog” and “a flea” (24:14), is a way of saying he is no threat. The text records that Saul wept aloud, yet David and his men “gat them up” to the stronghold (24:22), not trusting the reconciliation would last, and the next chapter shows he was right to be cautious."]},
"1sam.25":{title:"Blessed be thy advice, and blessed be thou",year:"About 1020–1015 BC",
 sum:"Samuel dies and is buried at Ramah—David’s men ask provisions of Nabal, a rich man of Carmel, who answers with insults—David sets out to kill every male of his house, but Abigail meets him with food and a wise plea—David blesses her: “blessed be thy advice, and blessed be thou, which hast kept me this day from coming to shed blood”—About ten days later “the LORD smote Nabal,” and David takes Abigail as his wife.",
 bg:["Nabal means “fool” or “senseless,” and his wife says, “as his name is, so is he; Nabal is his name, and folly is with him” (25:25). The Hebrew word does not mean lacking intelligence so much as lacking moral sense. Abigail’s name is usually explained as “my father is joy” or “father of joy.” Carmel here is a town in the hills of Judah, not the mountain on the coast.","Abigail’s speech turns David from avenging himself with his own hand, which he himself says would have been a wrong (25:31–34). The chapter ends with David taking Abigail and Ahinoam of Jezreel as wives, and Saul having given Michal to another man (25:42–44)."]},
"1sam.26":{title:"The LORD render to every man his righteousness and his faithfulness",year:"About 1020–1015 BC",
 sum:"The Ziphites again report David to Saul, who goes with three thousand men to the hill of Hachilah—David and Abishai enter the camp by night and find all asleep, “a deep sleep from the LORD”—David refuses to let Abishai kill Saul, “for who can stretch forth his hand against the LORD’s anointed, and be guiltless?”—He takes the spear and the cruse of water and calls out to Abner and Saul—Saul blesses him.",
 bg:["This episode is much like chapter 24, and some scholars think the two are different tellings of one event, while others see two separate occasions; the text itself presents them as two. In both, David has the chance to kill Saul and chooses not to, trusting that the Lord will deal with Saul in his own time (26:10).","David leaves Saul’s spear and cruse of water as a test of Saul’s honor. Saul answers, “I have sinned: return, my son David” (26:21), and “thou shalt both do great things, and also shalt still prevail” (26:25), but the two do not meet again."]},
"1sam.27":{title:"I shall now perish one day by the hand of Saul",year:"About 1015–1010 BC",
 sum:"David, fearing Saul, goes with his six hundred men to Achish king of Gath—Achish gives him the city of Ziklag—David raids the Geshurites, the Gezrites, and the Amalekites, leaving no survivors to report, and tells Achish he has been raiding Judah—Achish trusts him; David lives with the Philistines a year and four months.",
 bg:["This is a hard chapter: David lives among Israel’s enemies and deceives Achish about whom he is raiding. The text reports what he did without comment; the Lord’s guidance, which David sought in earlier chapters, is not mentioned here. Ziklag, in the southern desert, belonged to Judah from this time on (27:6).","The peoples David raids are the old enemies of Israel in the south, including the Amalekites of chapter 15. His claim to Achish that he is raiding “the south of Judah” was a lie that made Achish think David had made himself hated in Israel."]},
"1sam.28":{title:"Why hast thou disquieted me, to bring me up?",year:"About 1010 BC",
 sum:"The Philistines gather at Shunem and Saul camps at Gilboa, and his heart trembles—The Lord does not answer him by dreams, Urim, or prophets—Saul disguises himself and goes to a woman at Endor with a familiar spirit and asks her to bring up Samuel—Samuel says, “Why hast thou disquieted me?” and tells Saul that “to morrow shalt thou and thy sons be with me”—Saul eats and goes out into the night.",
 bg:["The text says Saul had put the mediums and wizards out of the land (28:3), as the law demanded (Deuteronomy 18:10–11; Leviticus 19:31), and then goes to one himself. The text says what happened as the woman describes it: “I saw gods ascending out of the earth,” and Samuel speaks to Saul. Readers differ on what happened. Some hold that Samuel truly appeared by God’s permission, some think it was a deception or counterfeit, and some that the woman used a trick. The text and 1 Chronicles 10:13–14 both treat Saul’s asking a medium as a sin, and Isaiah 8:19 warns against it.","Notice the woman’s kindness at the end: she and Saul’s servants urge him to eat, and she kills a calf and bakes bread for him (28:21–25)."]},
"1sam.29":{title:"Thou art good in my sight, as an angel of God",year:"About 1010 BC",
 sum:"The Philistines gather at Aphek, and David and his men march with Achish in the rear—The Philistine lords object: “Is not this David, of whom they sang one to another in dances?”—Achish sends David home, saying he is “good in my sight, as an angel of God”—David returns to Ziklag early the next morning.",
 bg:["David is spared from having to fight against Israel by the Philistine lords’ distrust, not by anything he does; the text does not say what he would have done. The lords quote the women’s song of 18:7 and fear that David would win back Saul’s favor by turning on them in battle (29:4–5).","Achish speaks of David as having been blameless while with him (29:6, 9). The text does not say how David would have answered the call to march against Israel."]},
"1sam.30":{title:"David encouraged himself in the LORD his God",year:"About 1010 BC",
 sum:"Amalekites burn Ziklag and take the women and children captive—David’s men talk of stoning him, but “David encouraged himself in the LORD his God”—He inquires of the Lord through Abiathar and pursues—An Egyptian servant left behind leads them to the raiders—David recovers all and divides the spoil, saying the share of the one who tarries by the stuff is equal to the one who goes to battle.",
 bg:["Four hundred men follow David across the brook Besor, and two hundred stay behind with the baggage because they are too weak (30:9–10). When some want to keep the spoil from them, David rules that they “shall part alike” (30:24), and the text says this became “a statute and an ordinance for Israel” from that day.","The Amalekites’ raid is a cost of David’s long absence in Philistine territory, and it also shows the Lord’s mercy: David loses nothing (30:19). He sends gifts of the spoil to the elders of Judah in many towns (30:26–31), which helps prepare the way for the kingship of Judah in 2 Samuel 2."]},
"1sam.31":{title:"All the valiant men arose, and went all night",year:"About 1010 BC",
 sum:"The Philistines defeat Israel on Mount Gilboa and kill Jonathan, Abinadab, and Malchi-shua—Saul is wounded by archers, and when his armourbearer will not kill him, Saul falls on his own sword—The Philistines fasten Saul’s body to the wall of Beth-shan—The valiant men of Jabesh-gilead take down the bodies, burn them, bury the bones under a tree, and fast seven days.",
 bg:["The account of Saul’s death here differs from the one an Amalekite gives in 2 Samuel 1:6–10, and the text lets the reader judge. 1 Chronicles 10 retells this chapter and says that Saul died for his unfaithfulness, including the matter of the medium (1 Chronicles 10:13–14). Saul’s story ends where it began, with the people of Jabesh-gilead whom he rescued in chapter 11.","The Philistines put Saul’s armor in the house of the Ashtaroth and his body on the wall at Beth-shan, an ancient city commanding the passage from the Jordan Valley to the Jezreel plain. Jonathan, whose name means “the LORD has given,” dies with his father, and David’s grief is the opening of 2 Samuel."]},
"2sam.1":{title:"How are the mighty fallen",year:"About 1010 BC",
 sum:"An Amalekite brings Saul’s crown and bracelet to David and claims he killed Saul—David tears his clothes, mourns, and has the messenger put to death—David’s lament: “The beauty of Israel is slain upon thy high places”—“How are the mighty fallen”—“Thy love to me was wonderful, passing the love of women.”",
 bg:["The Amalekite’s story (1:6–10) differs from 1 Samuel 31:4–5, where Saul falls on his own sword. The text does not explain the difference; many readers think the man invented his part, hoping for a reward from David. It is a bitter irony that an Amalekite claims the deed, since Saul’s failure to destroy the Amalekites cost him his kingdom (1 Samuel 15).","David’s poem is called “the use of the bow” and is said to come from the book of Jasher (1:18), a lost collection of songs that is also named in Joshua 10:13. Its refrain, “How are the mighty fallen,” honors both Saul and Jonathan, and its words about Gilboa are a cry that even the hills should share Israel’s grief (1:21)."]},
"2sam.2":{title:"Shall the sword devour for ever?",year:"About 1010 BC",
 sum:"David goes up to Hebron and the men of Judah anoint him king—He blesses the men of Jabesh-gilead for burying Saul—Abner makes Saul’s son Ish-bosheth king over Israel—Twelve men from each side die at the pool of Gibeon, and a fierce battle follows—Asahel pursues Abner and is slain—Abner asks, “Shall the sword devour for ever?” and Joab calls off the pursuit.",
 bg:["Hebron, in the hill country of Judah, was already sacred to Israel because of Abraham (Genesis 13:18; 23:2). Joab, Abishai and Asahel are named as the sons of Zeruiah (2:18), who was David’s sister (1 Chronicles 2:16), so they were his nephews. “Joab” most likely means “the LORD is father.”","Saul’s son is called Ish-bosheth here, “man of shame.” 1 Chronicles 8:33 gives his name as Esh-baal, “man of Baal,” and scholars widely think later writers replaced the word baal with bosheth, “shame,” because Baal had become a name for a false god. In Saul’s day baal could also simply mean “lord.”"]},
"2sam.3":{title:"A prince and a great man fallen",year:"About 1010–1008 BC",
 sum:"The war between the house of Saul and the house of David is long, “but David waxed stronger and stronger”—Six sons are born to David at Hebron—Ish-bosheth rebukes Abner, who offers to bring all Israel to David—David asks for Michal, and Abner promises to gather Israel—Joab murders Abner at Hebron to avenge Asahel—David mourns and fasts, and says, “Know ye not that there is a prince and a great man fallen this day in Israel?”",
 bg:["Abner’s name means “my father is Ner” (also written Abiner). He was Saul’s cousin and general, and he held the northern kingdom together until a quarrel over Rizpah, Saul’s concubine, turned him toward David (3:7–10). Joab’s motive is given in 3:27 and 3:30: revenge for his brother Asahel, whom Abner had killed in battle (2:23).","Hebron was one of the cities of refuge, set apart so that someone who killed by accident could find protection (Joshua 20:7). Joab took Abner aside “in the gate” of that city to kill him, and David’s curse on Joab’s house (3:29) and his public mourning show that David did not approve of the act, though he could not bring Joab to justice at once (3:39)."]},
"2sam.4":{title:"Who hath redeemed my soul out of all adversity",year:"About 1008 BC",
 sum:"Saul’s son Ish-bosheth loses heart when Abner dies—The lame young prince Mephibosheth is mentioned—Rechab and Baanah murder Ish-bosheth in his bed and bring his head to David—David swears, “As the LORD liveth, who hath redeemed my soul out of all adversity,” and has them put to death—Ish-bosheth’s head is buried in Abner’s tomb at Hebron.",
 bg:["The writer pauses (4:4) to tell how Jonathan’s son, five years old when the news of Gilboa came, was dropped by his nurse in her flight and became lame in both feet. This is Mephibosheth, whose story is picked up in chapter 9. 1 Chronicles 8:34 gives his name as Merib-baal, and as with Ish-bosheth the ending bosheth, “shame,” seems to have replaced baal. The exact meaning of his name is uncertain.","Rechab and Baanah expected a reward, as the Amalekite had in chapter 1, but David answers them in the same way: no one may strike down “a righteous person in his own house upon his bed” (4:11). He refuses to gain the throne by murder, and so he is able to receive the whole kingdom with a clear conscience."]},
"2sam.5":{title:"The sound of a going in the tops of the mulberry trees",year:"About 1003 BC",
 sum:"The tribes come to Hebron and anoint David king over all Israel—David takes the stronghold of Zion from the Jebusites and calls it the city of David—Hiram of Tyre sends cedar and craftsmen to build David a house—David knows that the LORD has established him—Twice the Philistines come up and are defeated; the LORD goes before David “as the breach of waters.”",
 bg:["David reigned forty years, seven and a half at Hebron and thirty-three at Jerusalem (5:4–5). The Jebusites’ city, a walled hilltop fortress, lay on a ridge south of the later temple mount, and it stood between Judah and the northern tribes, so it belonged to no single tribe. The Hebrew word translated “gutter” in 5:8 is uncertain; some think it means a water shaft or tunnel, but the text does not say how the city was entered.","David names the first battlefield Baal-perazim, “lord of breakings” (5:20), because “the LORD hath broken forth upon mine enemies before me as the breach of waters.” The name turns a title often used for Canaanite gods into a witness that the LORD alone breaks through. In the second battle David is told to wait until he hears marching in the tops of the mulberry trees, a sign that God had gone out ahead of him."]},
"2sam.6":{title:"David danced before the LORD with all his might",year:"About 1003 BC",
 sum:"David and thirty thousand men go to bring up the ark of God from Abinadab’s house—Uzzah puts out his hand to steady the ark and is struck down, and David is afraid—The ark stays three months with Obed-edom, whose house is blessed—David brings it up with gladness and dances before the LORD—Michal despises him, and David answers that he will be humbled for the LORD.",
 bg:["The place of Uzzah’s death was named Perez-uzzah, “breach of Uzzah” (6:8), which the text itself explains. The law said the ark was to be carried on poles by the Levites and that the holy things must not be touched (Exodus 25:14; Numbers 4:15), but David’s men placed it on a new cart as the Philistines had once done (1 Samuel 6:7). David later admitted, “we sought him not after the due order” (1 Chronicles 15:13).","Obed-edom is called “the Gittite,” which means a man from Gath; he may have been a Levite living there (1 Chronicles 15:18, 21 lists an Obed-edom as a Levite) or a foreigner, and the text does not say. His name means “servant of Edom.” The chapter closes by noting that Michal, Saul’s daughter, “had no child unto the day of her death” (6:23)."]},
"2sam.7":{title:"Thine house and thy kingdom shall be established for ever",year:"About 1000 BC",
 sum:"David wants to build a house for the ark, and Nathan at first says to do all that is in his heart—That night the LORD tells Nathan that David’s son shall build the house—The LORD promises David a great name, a place for Israel, and a house: “I will stablish the throne of his kingdom for ever”—“I will be his father, and he shall be my son”—David prays, “Who am I, O Lord GOD?”",
 bg:["The chapter plays on the word “house.” David wants to build God a house, a temple, and God replies that he will build David a house, a dynasty (7:5, 11, 13, 16). This promise to David is called the Davidic covenant, and later Israel returned to it again and again, as in Psalm 89:3–4, 28–37. The promise includes correction as well as comfort: if David’s son does wrong, “I will chasten him with the rod of men” (7:14), but God’s mercy will not leave him.","The angel Gabriel told Mary that her son would receive “the throne of his father David” and that “of his kingdom there shall be no end” (Luke 1:32–33), echoing this chapter. Hebrews 1:5 also quotes 2 Samuel 7:14 (“I will be to him a Father, and he shall be to me a Son”) and applies it to Jesus Christ."]},
"2sam.8":{title:"The LORD preserved David whithersoever he went",year:"About 1000 BC",
 sum:"David defeats the Philistines, Moab, Hadadezer of Zobah, the Syrians of Damascus and Edom—King Toi of Hamath sends his son with gifts of silver, gold and brass, which David dedicates to the LORD—“The LORD preserved David whithersoever he went”—David reigns over all Israel and executes judgment and justice—His chief officers are named.",
 bg:["This short summary of David’s wars shows Israel growing from a loose group of tribes into a small kingdom with its own officers: Joab over the army, Jehoshaphat the recorder, Zadok and Ahimelech the priests, and Benaiah over the Cherethites and Pelethites (8:16–18). The Cherethites and Pelethites seem to have been the king’s bodyguard, and many scholars think they came from Philistine or Cretan peoples, though the text does not say.","The chapter reports without comment that David measured the Moabites with a line and put two-thirds to death (8:2), even though his own great-grandmother Ruth was a Moabite (Ruth 4:17–22) and he once left his parents in Moab for safety (1 Samuel 22:3–4). The text does not explain why relations changed. Such harsh wars were common in the ancient world, and the writer’s main point is that the LORD gave David victory."]},
"2sam.9":{title:"That I may shew the kindness of God unto him",year:"About 1000 BC",
 sum:"David asks whether anyone is left of Saul’s house “that I may shew the kindness of God unto him” for Jonathan’s sake—Ziba tells him of Mephibosheth, lame in both feet—David restores all Saul’s land to Mephibosheth, and Ziba and his household are to serve him—Mephibosheth eats continually at the king’s table “as one of the king’s sons.”",
 bg:["“Kindness” translates the Hebrew hesed, a word for loyal, covenant love that keeps a promise even when it costs something. David is keeping the oath he swore to Jonathan that he would not cut off his kindness from Jonathan’s house (1 Samuel 20:14–17). In the ancient world a new king might easily have put the family of the old king to death, so this is a surprising act of mercy.","Mephibosheth calls himself “a dead dog” (9:8), a way of saying he is of no account, and he lives at Lodebar east of the Jordan until David sends for him. His name’s exact meaning is uncertain, and 1 Chronicles 8:34 calls him Merib-baal. He is lame in both feet (9:13), and he now sits at David’s table in Jerusalem, a picture of grace given to one who could not earn it."]},
"2sam.10":{title:"Let us play the men for our people",year:"About 1000 BC",
 sum:"David sends servants to comfort Hanun king of Ammon after his father’s death—Hanun’s princes call them spies, shave half of their beards and cut their garments short—The Ammonites hire Syrian armies—Joab and his brother Abishai divide the army, and Joab says, “Be of good courage, and let us play the men for our people, and for the cities of our God”—The Syrians flee and are defeated again.",
 bg:["In the ancient Near East a man’s beard was a sign of dignity, and half-shaved faces and cut garments were meant to humiliate David’s messengers. David allowed them to stay at Jericho until their beards grew back (10:5). The Ammonites knew they had provoked David and quickly hired Syrian troops from the north.","Joab’s speech to his brother Abishai is one of the better moments in his story: he plans carefully, divides his forces, and then puts the outcome in God’s hands: “the LORD do that which seemeth him good” (10:12). The opening line, “at the time when kings go forth to battle” (11:1 in the next chapter), sets up the contrast between the army in the field and David at home."]},
"2sam.11":{title:"The thing that David had done displeased the LORD",year:"About 990 BC",
 sum:"In the spring David stays in Jerusalem while Joab besieges Rabbah—David sees Bathsheba, wife of Uriah the Hittite, takes her, and she conceives—He tries to cover his sin by calling Uriah home, but Uriah will not go to his house while the ark and the army are in tents—David sends Uriah back with a letter to Joab, and Uriah dies in battle—David takes Bathsheba as his wife, “But the thing that David had done displeased the LORD.”",
 bg:["Bathsheba is the daughter of Eliam (11:3), and a man named Eliam, son of Ahithophel, is listed among David’s mighty men in 23:34, as is Uriah in 23:39. Many readers suggest she was Ahithophel’s granddaughter, which might help explain his later turn against David, but the text does not say so. The meaning of her name is uncertain: some suggest “daughter of the oath,” others “daughter of seven.” Uriah’s name means “the LORD is my light,” and although he is called a Hittite, he is loyal to Israel’s God and to the ark.","The writer tells the story plainly and does not hide David’s guilt. The abuse of royal power, the attempts at a cover-up, and the murder of a loyal soldier are told without softening, and the final sentence gives God’s verdict. Matthew 1:6 includes Bathsheba in the genealogy of Jesus, naming her only as “her that had been the wife of Urias.”"]},
"2sam.12":{title:"Thou art the man",year:"About 990 BC",
 sum:"Nathan tells David of a rich man who took a poor man’s one ewe lamb—David’s anger is kindled, and Nathan says, “Thou art the man”—David confesses, “I have sinned against the LORD,” and Nathan says the LORD has put away his sin, but the sword will not leave his house—The child dies, and David rises, worships, and says, “I shall go to him, but he shall not return to me”—Solomon is born and Nathan calls him Jedidiah—Joab takes Rabbah, and David wears its crown.",
 bg:["Nathan’s parable led David to pass judgment on himself before he knew it was about him. Psalm 51 is headed, “A Psalm of David, when Nathan the prophet came unto him, after he had gone in to Bathsheba,” and it gives voice to repentance: “Create in me a clean heart, O God.” The consequences Nathan names (12:10–12) are seen in the troubles of David’s family in chapters 13–18.","Solomon’s name is from the Hebrew shalom, “peace,” and Nathan gives him a second name, Jedidiah, “beloved of the LORD” (12:24–25). Doctrine and Covenants 132:39 says that David did not sin “save in the case of Uriah and his wife” and that for this he “hath fallen from his exaltation.” Repentance did not undo the consequences, but God’s mercy remained."]},
"2sam.13":{title:"Tamar remained desolate in her brother Absalom’s house",year:"About 985 BC",
 sum:"Amnon, David’s eldest son, pretends to be ill and assaults his half sister Tamar, then casts her out—Tamar tears her robe and cries aloud, and Absalom takes her into his house and tells her to hold her peace—David is “very wroth” but does not act—After two years Absalom has Amnon killed at a sheepshearing feast—Absalom flees to Geshur, and David mourns for Amnon and longs for Absalom.",
 bg:["This is a painful story and the text treats Tamar as the wronged person. She pleads, “no such thing ought to be done in Israel” (13:12), and she refuses Amnon’s later cruelty by saying that sending her away is “greater than the other” wrong (13:16). The law forbade such relations (Leviticus 18:9, 11), and the narrator lets the damage speak: she “remained desolate” (13:20).","Tamar’s name means “palm tree,” and Absalom’s means “father of peace,” a bitter irony for a man who brings violence into his father’s house. The events fulfill Nathan’s warning that the sword would not leave David’s house (12:10). David’s failure to deal with what Amnon did, and his anger without action, left Absalom to take matters into his own hands."]},
"2sam.14":{title:"Doth he devise means, that his banished be not expelled from him",year:"About 983 BC",
 sum:"Joab sends a wise woman of Tekoa to David with a made-up story of a son who killed his brother—She says, “we must needs die, and are as water spilt on the ground,” yet God “doth he devise means, that his banished be not expelled from him”—David sees that Joab is behind it and lets Absalom return to Jerusalem but not see his face—Absalom burns Joab’s field to get his attention—After two more years David receives Absalom and kisses him.",
 bg:["Tekoa lay south of Bethlehem, in the wilderness of Judah, and was the home of the prophet Amos (Amos 1:1). The woman’s story is a parable like Nathan’s: she gets the king to rule on a case, then shows him that he has not applied the same mercy to his own son. Her words that God does not simply banish someone, but “doth he devise means” to bring him back, are one of the most hopeful lines in the Old Testament.","Absalom’s long hair, cut once a year, weighed “two hundred shekels after the king’s weight” (14:26), perhaps about five pounds; scholars debate whether this is a precise weight or a way of saying that it was remarkable. His beauty and charm, and the fact that he was allowed back but not forgiven, prepare for his rebellion. He also named a daughter Tamar after his sister (14:27)."]},
"2sam.15":{title:"Absalom stole the hearts of the men of Israel",year:"About 980 BC",
 sum:"Absalom wins the people by sitting at the gate and promising justice—He goes to Hebron and has himself proclaimed king—David and his household flee Jerusalem; Ittai the Gittite swears to follow him “whether in death or life”—Zadok and the priests bring the ark, but David sends it back—David weeps barefoot up the mount of Olives, hears that Ahithophel has joined Absalom, and sends Hushai back to defeat his counsel.",
 bg:["The Hebrew text says Absalom went to Hebron “after forty years” (15:7), but most scholars read “four years,” as an early Greek and other ancient translations have it, since David’s whole reign was only forty years. Hebron was where David was first made king and where Absalom was born (3:2–3), so it was a fitting place for him to try to take the throne.","Psalm 3 is headed “A Psalm of David, when he fled from Absalom his son.” David’s sad climb up the mount of Olives (15:30), weeping with his head covered, is a scene many Christian readers recall when Jesus crossed the Kidron to the same mountain on the night before his death (John 18:1). Ittai, a foreigner from Gath who gives his loyalty in an hour of exile, is a surprising example of faithfulness."]},
"2sam.16":{title:"Let him alone, and let him curse",year:"About 980 BC",
 sum:"Ziba brings provisions and says Mephibosheth hopes to gain the kingdom, and David gives him Mephibosheth’s land—Shimei curses David and throws stones, and David stops Abishai from striking back: “Let him alone, and let him curse; for the LORD hath bidden him”—Hushai pledges loyalty to Absalom—Ahithophel advises Absalom to go in to his father’s concubines publicly.",
 bg:["Shimei, a Benjaminite of Saul’s family, calls David a “bloody man” (16:7–8), blaming him for the fall of Saul’s house. David does not argue; he says “it may be that the LORD will look on mine affliction” (16:12). Ziba’s claim about Mephibosheth may be false, and the chapter does not tell us; Mephibosheth gives his own account in 19:24–30.","Ahithophel’s advice to Absalom fulfills Nathan’s words to David: “I will take thy wives before thine eyes” and the act would be done “in the sight of all Israel” (12:11–12; 16:22). Ahithophel’s counsel was so valued that it was said to be “as if a man had inquired at the oracle of God” (16:23), which makes Hushai’s mission in the next chapter more important."]},
"2sam.17":{title:"The LORD had appointed to defeat the good counsel of Ahithophel",year:"About 980 BC",
 sum:"Ahithophel advises striking David that very night—Hushai gives other counsel, to gather all Israel, and Absalom prefers it—“The LORD had appointed to defeat the good counsel of Ahithophel”—Jonathan and Ahimaaz carry word to David and are hidden in a well—David crosses the Jordan to Mahanaim, where friends bring supplies—Ahithophel puts his house in order and takes his own life.",
 bg:["Hushai’s counsel gave David time, which the narrator sees as God’s answer to David’s prayer that Ahithophel’s counsel would be turned to foolishness (15:31; 17:14). Messages were passed through the priests’ sons and a servant girl, and two men escaped from Absalom’s soldiers by hiding in a well in Bahurim (17:17–21). Mahanaim, east of the Jordan, had been Ish-bosheth’s capital (2:8).","Ahithophel’s death is told briefly. When he saw that his advice was not followed, he went home, set his house in order, and hanged himself (17:23). Among the helpers at Mahanaim was Shobi, son of Nahash of Rabbah (17:27), apparently a brother of Hanun, whose people David had fought; the text does not say why he now helped David."]},
"2sam.18":{title:"O my son Absalom, my son, my son Absalom",year:"About 980 BC",
 sum:"David divides his army among Joab, Abishai and Ittai and asks them to “deal gently for my sake with the young man, even with Absalom”—In the wood of Ephraim Israel is defeated, and Absalom’s head catches in an oak—Joab thrusts three darts through him, and he is buried in a pit—Messengers bring the news to David at the gate—David weeps, “O my son Absalom, my son, my son Absalom! would God I had died for thee!”",
 bg:["Absalom’s name means “father of peace,” and his end is a sad contrast: he dies fleeing a battle in a forest, and is buried under a heap of stones in a pit (18:17), not in a royal tomb. He had set up a pillar for himself in the King’s Dale, saying that he had no son to keep his name (18:18). The monument in the Kidron Valley called “Absalom’s Tomb” is a much later structure, from about the first century, and has no real connection to him.","Joab ignored David’s order and killed Absalom himself (18:14). The messenger Cushi (“the Cushite,” probably a man from Cush, in what is now Sudan) brings the news, and David’s first question is not about the victory but about his son’s safety (18:29, 32). His cry, “would God I had died for thee,” is the cry of every parent who loses a child."]},
"2sam.19":{title:"Thou hast shamed this day the faces of all thy servants",year:"About 980 BC",
 sum:"Joab tells David that his grief has shamed those who saved his life, and David sits in the gate—Judah and Israel argue over bringing the king back—David pardons Shimei and hears Mephibosheth, who says, “let him take all”—Barzillai, aged eighty, declines to go to Jerusalem, and Chimham goes in his place—Israel and Judah quarrel over who has the greater claim on the king.",
 bg:["Joab’s rebuke (19:5–7) is harsh but wise; David’s kingdom could have been lost if the army felt its victory was treated as a defeat. David’s return was a political task as well as a personal one. He promises Amasa, Absalom’s general, the command in Joab’s place (19:13), hoping to win over Judah, but this sets up the crisis of chapter 20.","The dispute between Judah and “the men of Israel” at the end of the chapter, with the northern tribes saying “We have ten parts in the king” (19:43), shows the tension that later splits the kingdom (1 Kings 12). Mephibosheth’s answer to David, that he does not mind losing his land now that the king is home in peace, is a model of loyalty and of the kindness David had shown him in chapter 9."]},
"2sam.20":{title:"We have no part in David",year:"About 980 BC",
 sum:"Sheba, a Benjaminite, blows a trumpet and says, “We have no part in David”—Israel follows Sheba while Judah follows David—Amasa is slow to gather Judah, and Joab kills him with a sword while pretending to greet him—Joab pursues Sheba to Abel of Beth-maacah, where a wise woman persuades the city to give up Sheba’s head—David’s chief officers are named.",
 bg:["Sheba’s cry, “We have no part in David,” is almost the same as the northern tribes’ cry when the kingdom splits under Rehoboam (1 Kings 12:16). Joab, who had been replaced by Amasa, takes Amasa’s life with the sword while pretending to greet him (20:9–10). The text reports this plainly, as it did Joab’s killing of Abner.","Abel of Beth-maacah, near the northern border, was known for wisdom: the woman who speaks from the wall calls it “a mother in Israel” (20:19) and persuades the city to hand over Sheba rather than be destroyed. At the end of the chapter, one of David’s officers is “over the tribute” (20:24), a hint of the forced labor that later became a grievance in Solomon’s reign (1 Kings 12:4)."]},
"2sam.21":{title:"Thou quench not the light of Israel",year:"Late in David’s reign, about 975 BC",
 sum:"A three-year famine comes because Saul had wronged the Gibeonites—David asks what he can do, and seven of Saul’s descendants are given up and hanged—Rizpah keeps watch over the bodies from harvest until the rain—David gathers the bones of Saul and Jonathan and buries them in Benjamin—In wars with the Philistines, Abishai saves David, and the men say, “Thou shalt go no more out with us to battle, that thou quench not the light of Israel.”",
 bg:["Chapters 21–24 are an appendix to the book, with accounts, poems and lists that are not in strict time order. The Gibeonites were the people who had tricked Israel into a covenant in Joshua’s day, and Israel swore by the LORD to let them live (Joshua 9:15–20). The text here says that Saul had tried to destroy them but does not describe it elsewhere. David spared Mephibosheth because of his oath to Jonathan (21:7).","Rizpah was Saul’s concubine (3:7), and her long watch over the bodies of her sons, driving away birds and beasts, moved David to give Saul and Jonathan an honorable burial. The chapter also lists contests with Philistine giants. Verse 19 says Elhanan killed Goliath, and in the KJV the words “the brother of” are in italics to show they were added. 1 Chronicles 20:5 says Elhanan killed Lahmi, Goliath’s brother, and scholars debate how the two texts relate."]},
"2sam.22":{title:"The LORD is my rock, and my fortress, and my deliverer",year:"Late in David’s reign, about 975 BC",
 sum:"David sings to the LORD for delivering him from Saul and his enemies—“The LORD is my rock, and my fortress, and my deliverer”—In his distress he called, and the earth shook as God came down in smoke, fire and darkness—The LORD drew him out of many waters—“With the merciful thou wilt shew thyself merciful”—God is “a buckler to all them that trust in him”—David will give thanks among the heathen.",
 bg:["This song is almost the same as Psalm 18, with small differences in wording. The fact that David’s song survives in two forms shows how carefully the people of Israel kept their songs. The heading of Psalm 18 says it was sung “in the day that the LORD delivered him from the hand of all his enemies, and from the hand of Saul.”","The images of rock, fortress, shield and “horn of my salvation” (22:2–3) come from a world of strongholds and warfare. Paul quotes 22:50 in Romans 15:9 to show that praise to God was to go out to the nations. Zacharias uses the same “horn of salvation” in Luke 1:69, and Helaman 5:12 also speaks of building on the “rock of our Redeemer.”"]},
"2sam.23":{title:"He that ruleth over men must be just",year:"Late in David’s reign, about 975 BC",
 sum:"David’s last words: “The Spirit of the LORD spake by me, and his word was in my tongue”—“He that ruleth over men must be just, ruling in the fear of God”—The LORD has made with David “an everlasting covenant”—David’s mighty men are named—Three break through the Philistine camp to bring him water from Bethlehem, and he pours it out to the LORD—The list closes with “Uriah the Hittite.”",
 bg:["David is called “the sweet psalmist of Israel” (23:1), which may also mean “the one pleasing in the songs of Israel.” His last words describe a righteous ruler as “the light of the morning when the sun riseth, even a morning without clouds” (23:3–4) and speak again of the covenant of chapter 7, “ordered in all things, and sure” (23:5).","The list of David’s mighty men includes Eliam, son of Ahithophel (23:34), and ends with Uriah the Hittite (23:39), which reminds the reader of what David did in chapter 11. The story of the three who risked their lives for water from the well of Bethlehem shows both the loyalty David inspired and his reverence: he would not drink what had been won at such a cost, but poured it out to the LORD (23:16–17)."]},
"2sam.24":{title:"Of that which doth cost me nothing",year:"Late in David’s reign, about 975 BC",
 sum:"The LORD’s anger is kindled against Israel, and David is moved to number the people—Joab objects, but the census is taken—David’s heart smites him, and he confesses, “I have done very foolishly”—Through Gad, the LORD offers three punishments, and David chooses to fall “into the hand of the LORD; for his mercies are great”—Pestilence kills seventy thousand, and the LORD stays his hand at Araunah’s threshingfloor—David buys it and builds an altar, refusing to offer “that which doth cost me nothing.”",
 bg:["The text does not say exactly why the census was wrong. Some think it showed pride in military strength or paved the way for forced service or taxes, but scholars disagree. The first verse says that the LORD’s anger moved David, while 1 Chronicles 21:1 says that Satan stood up against Israel and provoked David, so the two accounts together show that the writers saw more than one cause at work.","Araunah the Jebusite (called Ornan in Chronicles) owned a threshing floor, a flat rock where grain was beaten out, and David insisted on paying for it instead of taking it as a gift. 2 Chronicles 3:1 says that Solomon later built the temple on this spot, on “mount Moriah,” the same region where Abraham was told to offer Isaac (Genesis 22:2). David’s words, “that which doth cost me nothing,” are often quoted as a principle of true sacrifice."]},
"lam.1":{title:"How doth the city sit solitary, that was full of people",year:"About 586 BC",
 sum:"Jerusalem sits solitary like a widow, and she “weepeth sore in the night”—Judah is gone into captivity, and her enemies have become her masters—“Is it nothing to you, all ye that pass by?”—The LORD has trodden the virgin daughter of Judah as in a winepress—Zion spreads out her hands and none comforts her—“The LORD is righteous; for I have rebelled against his commandment.”",
 bg:["The book is five poems of grief written after Babylon destroyed Jerusalem and its temple in 586 BC (2 Kings 25). Chapters 1–4 are acrostics: each verse begins with the next letter of the Hebrew alphabet, which has twenty-two letters, so each of these chapters has twenty-two verses (chapter 3 has sixty-six, three for each letter). The form gives the grief an order and a completeness, “from A to Z.” The Hebrew title is Eikhah, “How!”, the first word of chapters 1, 2 and 4, and the book does not name its author. Jewish and Christian tradition has attributed it to Jeremiah.","Jerusalem is pictured as a widow and “daughter of Zion,” a woman abandoned by her friends, while the poet repeatedly says that her suffering is the result of sin (1:5, 8, 14, 18). Lehi and his family left Jerusalem about ten years before it fell, and Lehi later said he had seen in vision that “Jerusalem is destroyed” (2 Nephi 1:4)."]},
"lam.2":{title:"The Lord was as an enemy",year:"About 586 BC",
 sum:"The Lord covers the daughter of Zion with a cloud in his anger and casts down the beauty of Israel—“The Lord was as an enemy”—He has destroyed his tabernacle, and the feast and sabbath are forgotten—Elders sit silent on the ground and the young women hang their heads—The poet’s eyes fail with tears for the children fainting in the streets—Prophets saw vain visions, and passers-by hiss—“Arise, cry out in the night … pour out thine heart like water before the face of the Lord”",
 bg:["The poem names the temple as God’s “footstool” (2:1), a title for the ark and the sanctuary (1 Chronicles 28:2). The writer sees the fall of Jerusalem as the covenant warnings of Leviticus 26 and Deuteronomy 28 coming true, and blames false prophets who “have not discovered thine iniquity” and so did not lead the people to repent (2:14). In chapters 2, 3 and 4 the letters ayin and pe are in the opposite order to chapter 1, and scholars do not agree on why.","Those who pass by clap their hands, hiss, and “wag their head” at the ruined city (2:15). The same gesture appears when people mocked Jesus on the cross: “they that passed by reviled him, wagging their heads” (Matthew 27:39). In verse 11 the poet weeps openly, and in verse 19 the city is told to pray through the night for the lives of her children."]},
"lam.3":{title:"Great is thy faithfulness",year:"About 586 BC",
 sum:"“I am the man that hath seen affliction by the rod of his wrath”—He has set me in dark places and made my chain heavy—“This I recall to my mind, therefore have I hope”: the LORD’s mercies are new every morning, “great is thy faithfulness”—“It is good that a man should both hope and quietly wait for the salvation of the LORD”—“Let us search and try our ways, and turn again to the LORD”—Out of the low dungeon I called, and thou saidst, “Fear not”—The poet asks God to judge his enemies.",
 bg:["This is the center of the book and the longest poem: a triple acrostic of sixty-six verses, in which each of three verses begins with the same Hebrew letter. The speaker is “the man that hath seen affliction,” and scholars debate whether he is Jeremiah, a representative of the people, or a poetic voice for all who suffer. The center of the poem (3:22–33) turns from complaint to hope: “It is of the LORD’s mercies that we are not consumed, because his compassions fail not” (3:22). The Hebrew for “compassions” is related to the word for the womb.","In 3:30 the sufferer “giveth his cheek to him that smiteth him,” which is close to Jesus’s teaching in Matthew 5:39, repeated in 3 Nephi 12:39. The hymn “Great Is Thy Faithfulness,” written by Thomas Chisholm in 1923, takes its title and its theme from verses 22–23."]},
"lam.4":{title:"How is the gold become dim",year:"About 586 BC",
 sum:"The gold has grown dim and the stones of the sanctuary lie in the street corners—The precious sons of Zion are counted as earthen pitchers—Sucklings and children go without food—The punishment of the daughter of my people is greater than the sin of Sodom—Her Nazarites were purer than snow and are now blacker than coal—Prophets and priests shed the blood of the just—The anointed of the LORD is taken in the pits—Edom will drink the cup, and “the punishment of thine iniquity is accomplished, O daughter of Zion.”",
 bg:["The poet says that Jerusalem’s fall was worse than Sodom’s, because Sodom was overthrown “in a moment” while Jerusalem suffered a long siege (4:6; compare Genesis 19). Hunger in the siege is described plainly: even tender women boiled their own children (4:10), a horror also foretold in Deuteronomy 28:53–57. “The breath of our nostrils, the anointed of the LORD” (4:20) most likely refers to King Zedekiah, who was captured near Jericho, blinded, and taken to Babylon (2 Kings 25:4–7).","Edom, the neighbor descended from Esau, is told to “rejoice” for the moment, because the cup of God’s anger will pass to her as well (4:21). Obadiah 10–14 and Psalm 137:7 also accuse Edom of taking pleasure in Jerusalem’s fall. The poem ends with a promise: the punishment is complete, and the LORD will no more carry Zion away into captivity (4:22)."]},
"lam.5":{title:"Turn thou us unto thee, O LORD, and we shall be turned",year:"About 586 BC",
 sum:"“Remember, O LORD, what is come upon us: consider, and behold our reproach”—Our inheritance is turned to strangers, and we are orphans and fatherless—We pay for our water and our wood, and servants rule over us—“Our fathers have sinned, and are not; and we have borne their iniquities”—Elders and young men are shamed, joy has ceased, “Woe unto us, that we have sinned!”—Mount Zion is desolate—“Thou, O LORD, remainest for ever”—“Turn thou us unto thee, O LORD, and we shall be turned; renew our days as of old.”",
 bg:["This last poem has twenty-two verses, the number of letters in the Hebrew alphabet, but it is not an acrostic, as if the writer has run out of ordered words and can only pray. It is a prayer from the whole community, asking God to remember. Because verse 22 ends in a doubt, “thou art very wroth against us,” Jewish custom in the synagogue is to repeat verse 21 after it, so the book is not read aloud on a note of despair.","The people say, “Our fathers have sinned, and are not; and we have borne their iniquities” (5:7), which is the sorrow of children suffering for the deeds of earlier generations. Ezekiel 18:20 stresses that each person is responsible for his own sin, and Lamentations holds both together: it confesses guilt (5:16) and also cries for help. Jeremiah 29:10 had said that the exile would last seventy years, and the return began under Cyrus in 538 BC."]},
"1kgs.1":{title:"Solomon thy son shall reign after me",year:"About 970–931 BC",
 sum:"David is old and cannot get warm—Adonijah exalts himself, saying, “I will be king,” and gathers Joab and Abiathar to his side—Nathan and Bathsheba remind David of his oath—Solomon is anointed at Gihon, and the people cry, “God save king Solomon”—Adonijah flees to the altar and is spared on condition of good behavior.",
 bg:["The book of 1 Kings begins with a succession crisis. Nothing in the law said which son of a king must reign, so the oldest surviving son, Adonijah (“my lord is the LORD”), and the son David had promised the throne to, Solomon (“peace”), each had supporters. The story does not hide that palace politics were involved, and that David acted on his promise to Bathsheba.","Gihon was Jerusalem’s main spring, in the Kidron Valley just below the city of David, so the trumpet blast there was heard quickly by Adonijah’s feast at En-rogel (verses 41–45). To take “hold on the horns of the altar” (verse 50) was an act of asking for sanctuary; the horns were the projecting corners of the altar. Solomon’s answer, “If he will shew himself a worthy man,” shows mercy with a condition."]},
"1kgs.2":{title:"Shew thyself a man",year:"About 970–931 BC",
 sum:"David charges Solomon to be strong and to “keep the charge of the LORD thy God”—David dies after reigning forty years—Adonijah asks for Abishag and is put to death—Abiathar is removed from the priesthood, Joab is slain at the altar, and Shimei is killed for breaking his oath—“And the kingdom was established in the hand of Solomon.”",
 bg:["David’s last words begin with faith (“walk in his ways, to keep his statutes”) and then turn to unfinished business with Joab and Shimei. Readers have long found this uncomfortable. The text presents Solomon as dealing with those who had threatened the throne, and it ties Joab’s death to the earlier murders of Abner and Amasa. Removing Abiathar the priest, verse 27, is said to fulfill the word of the LORD spoken against the house of Eli at Shiloh (1 Samuel 2:31–35).","Asking for Abishag, David’s attendant, was no small request. In the ancient Near East, taking a former king’s wife or attendant could be a claim to his throne (compare 2 Samuel 16:21–22), which is how Solomon understood it. The “forty years” of David’s reign (verse 11) is given as seven years in Hebron and thirty-three in Jerusalem."]},
"1kgs.3":{title:"Give therefore thy servant an understanding heart",year:"About 970–931 BC",
 sum:"Solomon marries Pharaoh’s daughter and sacrifices at the high place in Gibeon—In a dream the LORD says, “Ask what I shall give thee”—Solomon asks for “an understanding heart” to judge the people and is also promised riches and honor—Two harlots come before him, and he discerns the true mother by proposing to divide the living child.",
 bg:["Solomon calls himself “a little child” who does not know “how to go out or come in” (verse 7). The Hebrew for “understanding heart” literally means a “hearing heart,” a heart that listens. God is pleased because Solomon did not ask for long life, riches, or the death of enemies, and he gives him those other things as well, with the condition, “if thou wilt walk in my ways” (verse 14).","The verses admit that “the people sacrificed in high places” because the temple was not yet built (verse 2), and Gibeon’s great high place held the tabernacle at this time (2 Chronicles 1:3). The story of the two women shows wisdom in action, not in theory: Solomon’s test works because a real mother would give up her claim to save her child. The chapter also notes a political marriage with Egypt, which sits uneasily beside later warnings about foreign alliances."]},
"1kgs.4":{title:"Every man under his vine and under his fig tree",year:"About 970–931 BC",
 sum:"Solomon’s princes and twelve district officers are listed—Judah and Israel are “many, as the sand which is by the sea in multitude”—Daily provisions for the royal court are recorded—“God gave Solomon wisdom and understanding exceeding much”—He speaks three thousand proverbs and one thousand and five songs, and kings send to hear his wisdom.",
 bg:["The twelve districts of verses 7–19 did not follow the old tribal borders exactly, and each supplied the court for one month of the year. This efficient system also carried the seed of later resentment, as Solomon’s tax and labor demands rose (see 1 Kings 12). Verse 25 describes ideal peace: each family safe “under his vine and under his fig tree,” an image the prophets later reuse (Micah 4:4).","Verse 32 credits Solomon with three thousand proverbs. The Bible book of Proverbs says in its opening line that it contains proverbs of Solomon (Proverbs 1:1), though it also includes other sayings and was gathered over time. Verses 29–34 say his understanding was larger than all the wise men of the East and Egypt, which fits the fame that draws the queen of Sheba in chapter 10."]},
"1kgs.5":{title:"I purpose to build an house unto the name of the LORD my God",year:"About 970–931 BC",
 sum:"Hiram king of Tyre sends greetings to Solomon—Solomon says David could not build because of wars, but now “the LORD my God hath given me rest on every side”—Hiram agrees to send cedar and fir in exchange for wheat and oil—Solomon raises a levy of thirty thousand workers and Israelites and hews great stones for the foundation.",
 bg:["Hiram (or Huram) ruled Tyre, a Phoenician port city on the Mediterranean in what is now Lebanon. The Phoenicians were famous builders and sailors, and Lebanon’s cedar was the most prized timber in the region. Hiram had been a friend of David, and the treaty with Solomon was a trade deal that helped both sides: timber and skilled workers for grain and olive oil. The stonecutters in verse 18 include the men of Gebal, that is, Byblos.","The “name” theme is central: the temple is a house “unto the name of the LORD” (verses 3, 5). In Hebrew thinking, the name stood for the presence and character of the person. David’s wars kept him from building (compare 1 Chronicles 22:8), and Solomon, whose name means “peace,” is the one chosen to build."]},
"1kgs.6":{title:"Neither hammer nor axe nor any tool of iron heard in the house",year:"About 970–931 BC",
 sum:"The temple is begun in the fourth year of Solomon’s reign, the 480th year after the Exodus—Its dimensions and its porch, windows, and side chambers are described—The LORD promises, “I will dwell among the children of Israel”—Walls and floors are covered with cedar, olive wood, carved cherubim, palms, and gold—The house is finished in seven years.",
 bg:["The temple’s plan echoes the tabernacle of Exodus 25–40, only larger. It had an entry hall, a main room, and an inner room, the “oracle” or most holy place, a perfect cube of twenty cubits, where the ark stood. Many scholars place the start of the work about 966 BC, and the “480th year” of verse 1 is usually treated as a round number of twelve generations, with scholars disagreeing on how literally to count it. The pattern shows that holy space is set apart in steps, with the most sacred place farthest in.","The stone was made ready at the quarry so that no iron tool was heard at the site (verse 7). This recalls the law of the altar in Exodus 20:25 and Deuteronomy 27:5–6, which forbade tooling the stones. Midway through the account the LORD speaks to Solomon (verses 11–13) with a conditional promise that if he walks in the LORD’s statutes, he will dwell among Israel. The temple was never intended as a guarantee, but as a place of covenant."]},
"1kgs.7":{title:"He set up the pillars in the porch of the temple",year:"About 970–931 BC",
 sum:"Solomon builds his own house in thirteen years, along with the house of the forest of Lebanon and the porch of judgment—Hiram of Tyre, a skilled worker in brass, makes two pillars, Jachin and Boaz, and a molten sea on twelve oxen—He also makes ten bases, lavers, and temple vessels—Solomon brings in the things David dedicated.",
 bg:["This Hiram is not the king of Tyre in chapter 5. He is a craftsman whose mother was a widow of the tribe of Naphtali and whose father was from Tyre (verse 14). The two bronze pillars stood at the entrance, and were given names: Jachin, which most scholars take as “he establishes,” and Boaz, whose meaning is less certain and is often rendered “in him is strength.” Taken together, they may have served as a reminder that the LORD makes the house and the dynasty firm.","The “molten sea” (verses 23–26) was a huge bronze basin holding thousands of gallons, resting on twelve oxen in groups of three facing the four directions. It served priests for washing and suggests the twelve tribes. Bronze items were cast in the clay ground of the Jordan valley (verse 46) and were later carried to Babylon when the temple was destroyed (2 Kings 25:13)."]},
"1kgs.8":{title:"Will God indeed dwell on the earth?",year:"About 970–931 BC",
 sum:"The ark is carried into the most holy place and a cloud fills the house—Solomon blesses the people and says the LORD has kept his promise to David—In his dedicatory prayer he asks God to hear prayers made toward this house, to forgive, and to help in war, drought, and captivity—Solomon offers a vast number of sacrifices and keeps the feast.",
 bg:["Solomon’s prayer asks a great question: “Behold, the heaven and heaven of heavens cannot contain thee; how much less this house that I have builded?” (verse 27). The temple is a place where God’s name dwells, not a box that holds him. The prayer lists seven kinds of need, such as sin, defeat, drought, and exile, and asks God to hear from heaven when people pray toward the house, even foreigners (verses 41–43). It also anticipates exile (verses 46–50), which is just what later happened. Latter-day Saints will notice a pattern of temple dedication with a prayer, as at Kirtland (D&C 109).","The Book of Mormon notes that Nephi’s people built a temple “after the manner of the temple of Solomon,” though it was not built of so many precious things (2 Nephi 5:16). The two temples were separated by centuries and an ocean, so we should not press the comparison beyond what the verse says. The cloud that filled the house (verses 10–11) recalls the cloud at the dedication of the tabernacle in Exodus 40:34–35."]},
"1kgs.9":{title:"I have hallowed this house, which thou hast built",year:"About 970–931 BC",
 sum:"The LORD appears to Solomon a second time and says he has heard his prayer—He promises to establish the throne if Solomon walks with integrity, but warns of exile if Israel serves other gods—Solomon gives Hiram twenty cities in Galilee, builds fortified cities, and puts the remaining Canaanites to forced labor—He builds a fleet and brings gold from Ophir.",
 bg:["The LORD’s reply to Solomon’s dedicatory prayer is both warm and serious. He sets his name in the house “for ever,” and promises David’s line will continue, but adds that if Israel turns away, Israel will be “a proverb and a byword among all people,” and the house itself will astonish all who pass by (verses 7–8). The promise to the dynasty depends on faithfulness, a pattern that appears often in scripture (compare 1 Nephi 2:20).","Several details in the chapter show the cost of Solomon’s building. Hiram was not pleased with the twenty Galilean towns, and called them Cabul (verse 13), a word whose meaning is uncertain. The “levy of bondservice” (verses 15–22) was not placed on Israelites in this passage, but the burden grew heavier in other accounts, and it fed the revolt of chapter 12. Ophir was a source of gold whose location is unknown, with suggestions ranging from southern Arabia to East Africa or India."]},
"1kgs.10":{title:"The half was not told me",year:"About 970–931 BC",
 sum:"The queen of Sheba hears of Solomon’s fame and comes to Jerusalem with spices, gold, and precious stones to test him with hard questions—She finds that “the half was not told me” and blesses the LORD—They exchange gifts—Solomon’s wealth in gold, ivory, ships, and horses surpasses all kings of the earth.",
 bg:["Sheba is usually identified with a kingdom in southern Arabia, in the region of modern Yemen, a center of the incense and spice trade. Her journey of roughly a thousand miles was probably part trade mission and part diplomacy, and the “hard questions” were riddles or tests of wisdom. Her words in verse 7 and her praise of the LORD, “Blessed be the LORD thy God,” make her a figure of outsiders who recognize God’s hand. Jesus later says “the queen of the south” will rise in judgment, because she came to hear Solomon’s wisdom and “a greater than Solomon is here” (Matthew 12:42).","The second half of the chapter shows that wealth, too, can be a snare. The king gathers gold by the ton, builds a great ivory throne, and imports horses from Egypt (verses 26–29), things the law of the king warned against in Deuteronomy 17:16–17. The chapter is the high point of the reign, and the writer places it just before the account of Solomon’s fall in chapter 11."]},
"1kgs.11":{title:"His wives turned away his heart",year:"About 970–931 BC",
 sum:"Solomon loves many foreign women, and they turn his heart after other gods—The LORD is angry and says the kingdom will be torn away, though not in Solomon’s days, for David’s sake—Adversaries rise up: Hadad and Rezon—The prophet Ahijah tears his garment in twelve pieces and gives ten to Jeroboam—Solomon dies after reigning forty years.",
 bg:["The chapter explains the tragic shape of Solomon’s life. Deuteronomy 17:16–17 told the king not to multiply horses, wives, or silver and gold, and Solomon is shown doing all three. Verse 3 says he had “seven hundred wives, princesses, and three hundred concubines,” many of them from marriages that sealed treaties. Latter-day Saint scripture speaks plainly about this as well: “David and Solomon truly had many wives and concubines, which thing was abominable before me, saith the Lord” (Jacob 2:24).","Ahijah’s torn garment (verses 29–31) is a prophetic sign: ten pieces for the northern tribes, with one tribe left to David’s line “for David my servant’s sake, and for Jerusalem’s sake.” Jeroboam’s name is often explained as meaning something like “may the people increase” or “the people contend,” and scholars are not certain which. Solomon’s name, “peace,” carries a sad irony here, as his reign ends with enemies on every side."]},
"1kgs.12":{title:"Behold thy gods, O Israel",year:"About 931–850 BC",
 sum:"Rehoboam goes to Shechem, where the people ask for relief from the heavy yoke—He rejects the elders’ counsel and answers harshly, “My little finger shall be thicker than my father’s loins”—Ten tribes cry, “To your tents, O Israel,” and make Jeroboam king—Jeroboam sets up golden calves at Beth-el and Dan and appoints priests who are not Levites.",
 bg:["Rehoboam’s name means “he enlarges the people,” a bitter irony, since his harsh words made his kingdom smaller. The people’s plea in verse 4 was about forced labor and taxes. The quarrel was partly old tribal rivalry between north and south, and Shechem, where the nation had renewed the covenant in Joshua’s time, was a fitting place to make a new king. The chapter says the division was from the LORD (“the cause was from the LORD,” verse 15), fulfilling what Ahijah said.","Jeroboam’s calves (verses 28–33) were meant to stop northern pilgrims from going to the temple in Jerusalem. His words, “Behold thy gods, O Israel, which brought thee up out of the land of Egypt,” almost repeat Aaron’s words in Exodus 32:4. The calves were probably intended as thrones or pedestals for the LORD, but the people were led into idol worship and the text treats this as “the sin of Jeroboam” that later northern kings are judged by."]},
"1kgs.13":{title:"O altar, altar, thus saith the LORD",year:"About 931–850 BC",
 sum:"A man of God from Judah cries against the altar at Beth-el and names a future king, Josiah—Jeroboam’s hand withers when he points at the prophet, and the altar is torn apart—The prophet refuses a meal, but an old prophet deceives him and he eats—A lion slays him on the road, and the old prophet buries him.",
 bg:["The unnamed prophet declares that a king named Josiah will one day defile this altar (verse 2). The Bible says this was fulfilled about three hundred years later, in 2 Kings 23:15–16. The visible signs, the split altar and the withered and restored hand, show that God’s word to Jeroboam was real, and Jeroboam’s reaction is to cry out for the prophet’s prayers (verse 6).","The second half is hard to read. The man of God had been told not to eat or drink in that place, but he was convinced by an older prophet who said an angel told him otherwise (verses 18–19). The text says the old man “lied unto him,” and the man of God died for disobeying a clear command. The story seems to teach that a plain word from the LORD must not be set aside because someone else claims a later revelation, and that even a prophet is accountable to God’s word."]},
"1kgs.14":{title:"I am sent to thee with heavy tidings",year:"About 931–850 BC",
 sum:"Jeroboam’s son Abijah falls ill, and the king sends his wife in disguise to the prophet Ahijah—Ahijah foretells that Jeroboam’s house will be cut off because he made “other gods, and molten images”—The child dies as she enters the city—Rehoboam’s Judah does evil, and Shishak king of Egypt takes the treasures of the temple.",
 bg:["Ahijah is blind with age yet knows who is at the door, so the disguise fails at once (verses 4–6). The prophet who had once promised Jeroboam a lasting house now announces that his house will be swept away “as a man taketh away dung” (verse 10), a harsh image meant to be remembered. The child alone is said to have “some good thing toward the LORD,” and is allowed an honorable burial (verse 13).","In verses 25–26, Shishak (Egyptian Shoshenq I) came against Jerusalem in Rehoboam’s fifth year, around 925 BC. Shoshenq left a victory list at the temple of Karnak in Egypt naming many towns in Israel and Judah, which is a useful outside confirmation of the biblical account. Rehoboam replaced the gold shields with bronze ones (verses 27–28), a sign of how far his kingdom had fallen from Solomon’s glory."]},
"1kgs.15":{title:"Asa’s heart was perfect with the LORD all his days",year:"About 931–850 BC",
 sum:"Abijam reigns three years in Judah and walks in his father’s sins—Asa reigns forty-one years, removes idols and his mother’s idol, and “did that which was right in the eyes of the LORD”—Asa pays Ben-hadad of Syria to break his treaty with Baasha—Nadab and Baasha reign in Israel, Baasha killing Nadab and all Jeroboam’s house.",
 bg:["From here, the writer follows the two kingdoms by comparing kings: for each he gives the other nation’s year, length of reign, and a short verdict. The Hebrew uses “perfect” (shalem) in the sense of whole or undivided (verse 14), and does not mean sinless, for Asa left the high places and later relied on a Syrian alliance rather than on the LORD. Verse 4 repeats that God kept a “lamp” for David in Jerusalem, a figure for a continuing line of descendants.","The queen mother, Maachah, held a powerful role and was removed from office when Asa found her idol (verse 13). The Syrian treaty (verses 18–20) bought Judah a moment of relief at the cost of a more dangerous neighbor, and 2 Chronicles 16:7–9 shows a prophet rebuking Asa for it. Israel meanwhile fell into repeated bloodshed, as one family took the throne by killing the last."]},
"1kgs.16":{title:"Ahab the son of Omri did evil in the sight of the LORD above all that were before him",year:"About 931–850 BC",
 sum:"Baasha, Elah, Zimri, and Omri rule in a rapid sequence of murders and civil war—Omri builds Samaria—Ahab marries Jezebel, daughter of Ethbaal of the Zidonians, and serves Baal—He builds an altar for Baal and makes a grove—Hiel rebuilds Jericho at the cost of his sons, as Joshua had said.",
 bg:["Israel has six rulers in about forty-five years, and Zimri reigns seven days. Omri was a strong king, though the Bible gives him only a few verses. Assyrian records later called Israel “the land of Omri,” and the Moabite king Mesha’s stone, found in 1868, speaks of Omri oppressing Moab. Omri built Samaria on a hill he bought from Shemer (verse 24), and it was the northern capital until 722 BC.","Ahab’s marriage to Jezebel was an alliance with Phoenicia. Her father’s name, Ethbaal, means “Baal is with him,” and the name Jezebel is of uncertain meaning, perhaps linked to Baal. Her influence brought Baal worship into Israel’s royal court. Hiel’s rebuilding of Jericho (verse 34) fulfilled Joshua 6:26, a curse on rebuilding it, which fits the chapter’s picture of people ignoring what God had said."]},
"1kgs.17":{title:"The barrel of meal shall not waste",year:"About 931–850 BC",
 sum:"Elijah the Tishbite tells Ahab there will be no dew or rain—Ravens feed him at the brook Cherith—He goes to Zarephath, where a widow shares her last meal and “the barrel of meal wasted not”—Her son dies, and Elijah restores him to life, so that she says, “the word of the LORD in thy mouth is truth.”",
 bg:["The name Elijah means “my God is the LORD,” a statement against Baal, the storm and rain god of Ahab’s wife Jezebel. The drought answers Baal’s claim, since the rain is withheld by the LORD’s word (verse 1). Zarephath was on the Phoenician coast, in Jezebel’s own homeland, so Elijah finds help from a poor foreign widow while Israel’s king seeks his life. Jesus used this widow as an example when his hometown rejected him (Luke 4:25–26).","The widow’s flour and oil were replenished day by day, not all at once (verses 14–16), and the account of the child’s death and restoration is the first resurrection-like miracle in the Bible’s narrative. Latter-day Saints connect Elijah with the sealing power, which he conferred on Joseph Smith and Oliver Cowdery in the Kirtland Temple on April 3, 1836 (D&C 110:13–16), fulfilling Malachi 4:5–6."]},
"1kgs.18":{title:"How long halt ye between two opinions?",year:"About 931–850 BC",
 sum:"In the third year of drought Elijah meets Obadiah and then Ahab—On Mount Carmel he asks Israel, “How long halt ye between two opinions?”—Baal’s prophets call from morning to evening with no answer—Elijah rebuilds the LORD’s altar, and fire falls—The people say, “The LORD, he is the God,” and rain comes after Elijah prays.",
 bg:["The Hebrew for “halt” means to limp or hop back and forth, like someone who cannot decide which way to go. The contest on Carmel was a test of who controls fire and rain, which Baal, as a storm god, was thought to rule. Elijah’s mocking in verse 27 fits the ancient Near Eastern idea that gods could be asleep or away, and his prayer, much shorter than the prophets’ cries, asks only that people “know that thou art the LORD God” (verse 37). Twelve stones for twelve tribes (verses 31–32) remind Israel it is still one people.","The chapter also shows Obadiah, a believing official who hid a hundred prophets in caves (verse 4), so Elijah is not as alone as he feels (compare chapter 19). After the fire falls and the prophets of Baal are put to death, Elijah prays for rain seven times, until a cloud “like a man’s hand” appears (verse 44)."]},
"1kgs.19":{title:"A still small voice",year:"About 931–850 BC",
 sum:"Jezebel threatens Elijah, who flees to the wilderness and asks to die—An angel feeds him, and he goes forty days to Horeb—The LORD is not in the wind, earthquake, or fire, but in “a still small voice”—He is told to anoint Hazael, Jehu, and Elisha, and that seven thousand in Israel have not bowed to Baal—Elisha leaves his oxen to follow Elijah.",
 bg:["After his great victory, Elijah is afraid and worn out, and the LORD’s first response is to feed him and let him sleep (verses 5–8). On Mount Horeb, the same mountain where Moses met God, wind, earthquake, and fire pass by, and the LORD speaks in “a still small voice” (verse 12). The Hebrew phrase is hard to translate and is also rendered “a sound of thin silence” or “a gentle whisper.” Latter-day Saint scripture uses the same phrase for the way the Spirit speaks (D&C 85:6).","Elijah’s mantle thrown over Elisha (verse 19) is a call to follow and to take up a prophet’s work. Elisha’s name means “God is salvation.” He slaughters his oxen and uses the plow for fuel, a clean break with his old life. The “seven thousand” who have not bowed to Baal (verse 18) show that God had a faithful remnant Elijah did not know about."]},
"1kgs.20":{title:"Let not him that girdeth on his harness boast himself",year:"About 931–850 BC",
 sum:"Ben-hadad of Syria besieges Samaria and demands Ahab’s silver, gold, wives, and children—A prophet promises victory, and Israel defeats the Syrians—When Syria returns, saying, “The LORD is God of the hills, but he is not God of the valleys,” Israel wins again—Ahab spares Ben-hadad, and a prophet rebukes him.",
 bg:["Ben-hadad means “son of Hadad,” Hadad being the storm god of Syria. Ahab’s proverb in verse 11, which says that one who is putting on armor should not boast like one who is taking it off, is the most remembered line of the chapter. Although Ahab is a wicked king, the LORD sends a prophet to help Israel win, “thou shalt know that I am the LORD” (verse 13). The Syrians’ claim that the LORD is only a god of the hills was an insult the LORD answers in verse 28.","In the end, Ahab makes a treaty and calls Ben-hadad his “brother,” sparing a man the LORD had devoted to destruction (verse 42). The prophet’s disguise with a bandage over his eyes is used to bring Ahab to pronounce a verdict against himself, a method like Nathan’s parable to David (2 Samuel 12). The chapter shows a king who wins by God’s help and then bargains with God’s enemy."]},
"1kgs.21":{title:"Hast thou killed, and also taken possession?",year:"About 931–850 BC",
 sum:"Ahab wants Naboth’s vineyard, and Naboth says, “The LORD forbid it me, that I should give the inheritance of my fathers unto thee”—Jezebel writes letters in Ahab’s name, and two false witnesses accuse Naboth, who is stoned—Elijah meets Ahab in the vineyard and foretells that dogs will lick his blood—Ahab humbles himself.",
 bg:["Naboth’s refusal rests on the law that family land was a trust from God and was not for sale (Leviticus 25:23; Numbers 36:7). Ahab behaves like a spoiled child, going to bed, turning his face to the wall, and refusing to eat (verse 4), but Jezebel treats the throne as above the law. She arranges a fake trial with two “sons of Belial” (worthless men) as witnesses, using the legal requirement for two witnesses to make a murder look lawful. Naboth’s sons were also killed, according to 2 Kings 9:26, which ensured no heir.","Elijah’s question, “Hast thou killed, and also taken possession?” sums up the crime. Jezreel, where Naboth’s vineyard lay, was near Ahab’s second palace. When Ahab tears his clothes and goes about in sackcloth, the LORD postpones the judgment on his house to his son’s days (verses 27–29), a sign that even this king’s repentance is noticed."]},
"1kgs.22":{title:"What the LORD saith unto me, that will I speak",year:"About 931–850 BC",
 sum:"Jehoshaphat of Judah joins Ahab against Syria at Ramoth-gilead—Four hundred prophets promise victory, but Jehoshaphat asks for a prophet of the LORD—Micaiah says, “What the LORD saith unto me, that will I speak,” and foretells defeat—Ahab disguises himself, but is mortally wounded by an arrow “at a venture,” and dogs lick his blood.",
 bg:["The four hundred prophets all say the same thing, and Zedekiah son of Chenaanah makes iron horns to show how Ahab will push the Syrians (verse 11). Micaiah says only what he is given by the LORD, and his vision of the host of heaven and a “lying spirit” sent into the prophets’ mouths (verses 19–23) is one of the hardest passages of the book. Scholars understand it in different ways, and the point of the story seems to be that Ahab hears only what he wants to hear.","Ahab’s attempt to cheat the prophecy by disguising himself fails when “a certain man drew a bow at a venture” and the arrow found a gap in the armor (verse 34). His blood was washed from his chariot by the pool of Samaria, and the dogs licked it, as Elijah had said (verses 37–38; compare 21:19). The final verses introduce Jehoshaphat, who reigned twenty-five years and generally did right, and Ahaziah, who “did evil.”"]},
"ezra.1":{title:"The LORD God of heaven hath given me all the kingdoms of the earth",year:"About 538–457 BC",
 sum:"The LORD stirs up the spirit of Cyrus king of Persia to proclaim that the Jews may return and build the house of the LORD in Jerusalem—Those whose spirit God raised prepare to go up, and neighbors give them silver, gold, and goods—Cyrus returns five thousand four hundred temple vessels to Sheshbazzar, prince of Judah.",
 bg:["The chapter opens in about 538 BC, after Cyrus conquered Babylon, and the words repeat the last two verses of 2 Chronicles. The prophets had foretold it: Jeremiah named seventy years (Jeremiah 29:10), and Isaiah named Cyrus as God’s “shepherd” and “anointed” long before (Isaiah 44:28; 45:1). A clay cylinder found in Babylon, the Cyrus Cylinder, tells how Cyrus let captive peoples go home and restored their shrines, a policy that fits this decree. The cylinder does not mention the Jews by name, though.","Sheshbazzar, “prince of Judah,” is given the vessels Nebuchadnezzar took. His name is Babylonian and its meaning is uncertain, and he is probably the same person as the governor mentioned in Ezra 5:14. Many Jews chose to stay in Babylon, and the chapter says that “whose spirit God had raised” went up, meaning the return was voluntary and depended on faith."]},
"ezra.2":{title:"Till there stood up a priest with Urim and with Thummim",year:"About 538–457 BC",
 sum:"The people who return are listed by family, town, and priestly or Levite line—Zerubbabel and Jeshua lead them—The total congregation is “forty and two thousand three hundred and threescore,” besides servants and singers—Some cannot prove their ancestry, and priests who lack records are barred from the priesthood “till there stood up a priest with Urim and with Thummim.”",
 bg:["The list, which also appears in Nehemiah 7, shows how much care was given to family records. Descent mattered because the land was divided by tribe and the priesthood passed from father to son. The numbers differ in places between Ezra and Nehemiah, which is generally explained by copying differences over the centuries or by lists made at different times. Zerubbabel’s name, probably “offspring of Babylon,” tells that he was born in exile, and he was a descendant of David and a governor of Judah.","Verse 63 says some priests would wait until a priest could consult the Urim and Thummim, the sacred means of revelation attached to the high priest’s breastplate (Exodus 28:30). The text does not say whether this ever happened, and Urim and Thummim are not mentioned again in the Old Testament. The freewill gifts for the house of God at the end of the chapter (verses 68–69) set a tone of eager generosity."]},
"ezra.3":{title:"They set the altar upon his bases",year:"About 538–457 BC",
 sum:"In the seventh month Jeshua and Zerubbabel build the altar and offer sacrifices, though “fear was upon them because of the people of those countries”—They keep the feast of tabernacles—In the second year they lay the temple’s foundation—The people praise the LORD, and many shout for joy while the old men who had seen the first house weep.",
 bg:["The first thing the returned exiles build is the altar, before the temple itself. Sacrifice and worship come first, in the seventh month, a season of holy days that includes the Day of Atonement and the feast of tabernacles (Leviticus 23). Zerubbabel (“offspring of Babylon”) leads with Jeshua the high priest, the same man called Joshua in Haggai and Zechariah. Workers were hired using cedar from Tyre, just as in Solomon’s time (verse 7).","The scene at the foundation is unforgettable: praise, “because he is good, for his mercy endureth for ever toward Israel,” and then a mix of shouting and weeping (verses 11–13). The old men who remembered Solomon’s temple wept, perhaps in grief, perhaps for the smaller scale, and the text says the sound could not be told apart. The chapter does not explain their tears, and Haggai 2:3 suggests the new house seemed small by comparison."]},
"ezra.4":{title:"Then ceased the work of the house of God",year:"About 538–457 BC",
 sum:"The adversaries of Judah offer to help build, but Zerubbabel and Jeshua refuse—The people of the land discourage the builders and hire counselors against them—Letters are sent to Ahasuerus and Artaxerxes complaining about Jerusalem—The king orders the work to cease until the second year of Darius.",
 bg:["The “adversaries” claim to have worshiped the LORD since Esarhaddon of Assyria brought them to the land (verse 2). The returnees say the temple is their task alone, a refusal that made enemies. Scholars differ on who the opponents were, though many connect them to the people later called Samaritans, mixed groups settled by Assyria in the old northern kingdom, and the text itself gives only their words and actions.","Verses 6–23 jump ahead in time. They record later complaints under Ahasuerus (Xerxes) and Artaxerxes about rebuilding Jerusalem’s walls, not the temple, and are placed here to show a pattern of long opposition. From verse 8 through 6:18, the book is written in Aramaic, the common language of the Persian empire, rather than Hebrew. Verse 24 returns to the temple and its halt until the second year of Darius, about 520 BC."]},
"ezra.5":{title:"The eye of their God was upon the elders of the Jews",year:"About 538–457 BC",
 sum:"The prophets Haggai and Zechariah prophesy, and Zerubbabel and Jeshua resume building—Tatnai the governor asks who gave the command—The elders answer by citing Cyrus’s decree—Tatnai sends a letter to Darius asking that the royal archives be searched.",
 bg:["Haggai and Zechariah gave their messages in 520 BC, and the books that bear their names show what they said. Haggai urged people to stop living in paneled houses while the LORD’s house lay in ruins (Haggai 1:4), and Zechariah promised that the temple would be finished “not by might, nor by power, but by my spirit” (Zechariah 4:6). The prophets did not replace the builders but stirred them, and the text says they were “helping them” (verse 2).","Tatnai was governor of the province “beyond the river,” the land west of the Euphrates. His letter in verses 7–17 is fair and orderly: he reports what the Jews say, quotes their answer, and asks the king to check the records, and he does not stop the work meanwhile (verse 5). The elders’ answer is both humble and bold, saying they are “the servants of the God of heaven and earth” and that an earlier great king of Israel built the house."]},
"ezra.6":{title:"This house was finished on the third day of the month Adar",year:"About 538–457 BC",
 sum:"Darius’s archives turn up Cyrus’s decree at Achmetha—Darius orders Tatnai to let the work go on and to pay for it from the royal treasury—The elders build and finish the temple with the help of Haggai and Zechariah—They dedicate it with joy and keep the Passover.",
 bg:["The decree was found in Achmetha (Ecbatana), the old Median capital, which shows how carefully Persia kept records. Darius not only confirms the decree but pays for it from taxes and warns that anyone who changes his order will be impaled (verse 11). The writer sees God’s hand behind this: “the LORD . . . turned the heart of the king of Assyria unto them” (verse 22), using a title for Persia’s ruler as heir of the old Assyrian lands.","The temple was finished on the third of Adar, in Darius’s sixth year, which is about March 516 BC, roughly seventy years after the first temple was destroyed. The dedication was modest compared with Solomon’s: a hundred bulls rather than twenty-two thousand (verse 17; compare 1 Kings 8:63). The Passover that followed was kept by priests, Levites, and those who had “separated themselves unto them from the filthiness of the heathen of the land” (verse 21). From verse 19 the book returns to Hebrew."]},
"ezra.7":{title:"Ezra had prepared his heart to seek the law of the LORD",year:"About 538–457 BC",
 sum:"Ezra, a priest descended from Aaron and “a ready scribe in the law of Moses,” goes up to Jerusalem in the seventh year of Artaxerxes—“according to the good hand of his God upon him”—The king’s letter gives silver, gold, and supplies, tax exemption for temple workers, and authority to teach and judge—Ezra blesses the LORD for his favor.",
 bg:["Ezra’s name probably means “help,” and may be short for a name such as “the LORD helps.” Verse 10 gives the heart of his life: he “prepared his heart to seek the law of the LORD, and to do it, and to teach in Israel,” in that order. As a “scribe” he was a student and teacher of the written law, and his work helped make scripture central to Jewish life after the exile.","The “seventh year” of Artaxerxes I was about 458 BC, about 58 years after the temple’s completion in chapter 6. A few scholars place Ezra under Artaxerxes II (about 398 BC), and the text alone does not settle the question. The king’s letter in verses 12–26 is in Aramaic and gives Ezra authority to appoint judges, so that anyone who will not obey God’s law and the king’s law faces consequences. Ezra gives credit to the LORD for putting this in the king’s heart (verse 27)."]},
"ezra.8":{title:"We fasted and besought our God for this",year:"About 538–457 BC",
 sum:"The heads of families who go up with Ezra are listed—At the river Ahava Ezra finds no Levites and sends for some—He proclaims a fast to ask “a right way for us, and for our little ones, and for all our substance”—He entrusts the silver, gold, and vessels to twelve priests and delivers them in Jerusalem.",
 bg:["The journey from Babylon to Jerusalem was about 900 miles and took roughly four months (compare 7:9). Ezra says he “was ashamed to require of the king a band of soldiers and horsemen” to protect them, because he had told the king that “the hand of our God is upon all them for good” (verse 22). So the fast at Ahava (verses 21–23) was his way of putting that statement to the test, and verse 23 says, “he was intreated of us.”","The caravan carried a large treasure, weighed out to twelve priests and weighed again on arrival (verses 25–34), which was a careful way of protecting against both theft and accusation. Only about 1,500 men and their households went with Ezra, and he waited at Ahava for Levites, who were few (verse 15). Afterward the group made burnt offerings of twelve bullocks, ninety-six rams, and more, for all Israel."]},
"ezra.9":{title:"A little reviving in our bondage",year:"About 538–457 BC",
 sum:"Leaders tell Ezra that the people, priests, and Levites have married women of the surrounding peoples—Ezra tears his garment and sits appalled until the evening sacrifice—He prays in shame: “our iniquities are increased over our head”—He recalls God’s mercy, “a nail in his holy place” and “a little reviving”—He confesses that the people have again broken the commandments.",
 bg:["This is a hard chapter, and it helps to know how it was understood at the time. The concern is stated as religious more than ethnic: the people had “not separated themselves” from the peoples’ “abominations,” and the earlier law warned that foreign marriages would lead Israel to serve other gods (Deuteronomy 7:3–4). Marriages with outsiders did not always carry this charge elsewhere. Ruth, a Moabite woman, married into Israel and became the great-grandmother of David because she said, “thy God my God” (Ruth 1:16).","The leaders themselves were mainly guilty (verse 2), and Ezra responds with grief, not anger. He speaks as though he shares the guilt, saying “our iniquities,” and he thanks the LORD for “a nail in his holy place,” a picture of a small stake of security in a time of weakness, and for “a little reviving,” a small renewal after the exile (verses 8–9)."]},
"ezra.10":{title:"Let us make a covenant with our God to put away all the wives",year:"About 538–457 BC",
 sum:"While Ezra weeps before the house of God, a great congregation weeps with him—Shecaniah proposes a covenant to put away the foreign wives and their children, “according to the counsel of my lord”—The people are summoned to Jerusalem, and the assembly stands trembling in a heavy rain—A commission examines each case, and the names of those involved are recorded.",
 bg:["This is one of the hardest chapters in the Old Testament, and it should be read with care. The men are asked to send away foreign wives and their children, which today sounds cruel, and the text itself notes that some opposed the plan (verse 15). The reason given is a fear that Judah, having just returned from exile for idolatry, would be absorbed into surrounding religions and lose its covenant identity. The text speaks of idolatry and the covenant, not of race, and it records the people’s own agreement to it (verse 12). Scripture does not say that every later generation must do the same, and other texts such as Ruth show another path.","The matter was handled slowly: the people met in the ninth month in the rain, a commission worked from the first day of the tenth month for three months, and by the first day of the first month the cases were done (verses 16–17). The names of the priests, Levites, and others who had married foreign wives are listed (verses 18–43), a public record that includes leaders. Nehemiah faces a related problem in Nehemiah 13:23–27, and Malachi also speaks of faithfulness in marriage (Malachi 2:10–16)."]},
"2kgs.1":{title:"Is it not because there is not a God in Israel",year:"About 850–586 BC",
 sum:"Moab rebels after Ahab’s death—King Ahaziah falls through a lattice and sends to Baal-zebub of Ekron—Elijah meets the messengers: “Is it not because there is not a God in Israel?”—Fire from heaven consumes two captains of fifty and their men, but the third captain pleads for his life—Ahaziah dies as Elijah foretold, and Jehoram reigns in his stead.",
 bg:["2 Kings continues the story of 1 Kings, and the two books were originally one scroll. The name Baal-zebub means “lord of the flies”; it is probably a Hebrew twist on the name Baal-zebul, “Baal the prince.” The New Testament name Beelzebub comes from the same title.","Elijah’s name means “my God is the LORD,” and his message here is exactly that: Ahaziah has sent to a foreign god when the God of Israel is right there. The fire from heaven recalls Elijah’s earlier contest on Mount Carmel (1 Kings 18). The story is told briefly and does not explain every detail, but it stresses that the third captain humbled himself and was spared."]},
"2kgs.2":{title:"Where is the LORD God of Elijah?",year:"About 850–586 BC",
 sum:"Elijah and Elisha travel from Gilgal to Bethel, Jericho, and the Jordan—Elijah divides the waters with his mantle, and Elisha asks for a double portion of his spirit—A chariot and horses of fire part them, and Elijah goes up by a whirlwind into heaven—Elisha takes the mantle, divides Jordan, and the sons of the prophets bow to him—He heals the waters of Jericho with salt, and young men at Bethel who mock him are judged.",
 bg:["Elisha’s name means “God is salvation.” His request for “a double portion” of Elijah’s spirit (verse 9) echoes the law of the firstborn, who received a double share of the inheritance (Deuteronomy 21:17), so he is asking to be Elijah’s successor. Elijah is one of those who did not taste death in the usual way; the Latter-day Saint scriptures also speak of Elijah returning later (Malachi 4:5–6; Doctrine and Covenants 110:13–16).","The incident with the youths at Bethel (verses 23–24) is hard to read. The Hebrew words describe young men rather than small children, and the text does not tell us more than that they mocked the prophet and he cursed them “in the name of the LORD.” It shows how seriously the text treats disrespect toward God’s prophet, but it does not explain every detail."]},
"2kgs.3":{title:"Make this valley full of ditches",year:"About 850–586 BC",
 sum:"Jehoram of Israel allies with Jehoshaphat of Judah and the king of Edom against Moab—The armies run short of water in the wilderness, and Jehoshaphat asks for a prophet of the LORD—Elisha, out of respect for Jehoshaphat, says, “Make this valley full of ditches,” and water comes without wind or rain—Moab mistakes the water for blood and is defeated, and Israel strikes the land.",
 bg:["Moab’s king Mesha is known outside the Bible. The Mesha Stone (also called the Moabite Stone), found in 1868 in Jordan, is a Moabite inscription from the 800s BC in which Mesha says he threw off Israel’s rule. It is a rare case of a king from the Bible’s story speaking in his own words, though from the other side.","Verse 27 is difficult. When the king of Moab offers his eldest son on the wall, “there was great indignation against Israel,” and the attackers withdraw. The text does not say whose anger this was, and scholars read it in different ways. Child sacrifice is condemned elsewhere in the law (Leviticus 18:21)."]},
"2kgs.4":{title:"Is it well with the child?",year:"About 850–586 BC",
 sum:"A widow’s small pot of oil is multiplied to pay her debts—A woman of Shunem who has cared for Elisha is promised a son, and the child is born—When the boy dies, Elisha comes, prays, and restores him to his mother—Elisha heals the deadly pottage at Gilgal—Twenty barley loaves feed a hundred men, with some left over.",
 bg:["These miracles resemble ones done by Elijah in 1 Kings 17: oil and flour that do not run out, and a widow’s son raised from the dead. Elisha’s name (“God is salvation”) fits the pattern, because in each story ordinary people in need are rescued. The loaves in verses 42–44 are barley loaves, as in the feeding of the five thousand (John 6:9), where the Gospel writer may be echoing this story, though the Gospels do not say so.","The woman answers, “It is well,” while her son lies dead. The Hebrew word is shalom, which means peace and wholeness, and her answer shows faith that does not wait for the outcome to be clear. Shunem was a village in the Jezreel Valley, on Elisha’s route between Samaria and Mount Carmel."]},
"2kgs.5":{title:"Go and wash in Jordan seven times",year:"About 850–586 BC",
 sum:"Naaman, captain of the Syrian army, is a leper, and a captive Israelite girl tells of a prophet in Samaria—Elisha tells Naaman to wash in Jordan seven times, and Naaman at first goes away in anger—His servants persuade him, and he is cleansed and confesses the God of Israel—Elisha refuses a reward, but Gehazi takes one by deceit and is struck with leprosy.",
 bg:["Naaman’s name means “pleasant,” and it is the name of a man whose skin disease is anything but. Jesus refers to this story in Luke 4:27: “many lepers were in Israel in the time of Eliseus the prophet, and none of them was cleansed, saving Naaman the Syrian.” (Eliseus is the Greek form of Elisha.) His point is that God’s mercy reached a foreigner. Gehazi’s name has an uncertain meaning, and the text does not explain it.","The “leprosy” of the Bible is a Hebrew word (tsaraath) for a range of skin conditions and was not necessarily the modern disease of that name. The little servant girl, the unnamed servants, and the foreign general are all examples of the quiet instruments God uses in these chapters. Naaman’s “two mules’ burden of earth” (verse 17) shows he thought of the LORD as tied to the land of Israel, which is why he wished to take some of its soil home."]},
"2kgs.6":{title:"They that be with us are more than they that be with them",year:"About 850–586 BC",
 sum:"Elisha makes an iron axe head float—He reveals the king of Syria’s plans to the king of Israel—At Dothan, Elisha’s servant sees horses and chariots of fire around them, and the Syrian army is struck with blindness and led to Samaria—Israel feeds the soldiers and sends them home—Later Ben-hadad besieges Samaria, and a terrible famine follows.",
 bg:["The prophet’s servant is afraid until his eyes are opened, and then he sees the “chariots of fire” (verse 17). The idea that unseen help surrounds God’s people is also found in Doctrine and Covenants 84:88, which speaks of angels round about the Lord’s servants. The feeding and release of the captured Syrian soldiers (verses 21–23) shows mercy toward enemies, and “the bands of Syria came no more into the land of Israel” for a time.","The siege of Samaria (verses 24–33) is described honestly and hard to read, with famine so bad that a donkey’s head was sold for a high price and mothers resorted to terrible measures. These were the very consequences the law had warned of for covenant-breaking (Deuteronomy 28:53–57). The king blames Elisha, and the next chapter shows the answer."]},
"2kgs.7":{title:"If the LORD would make windows in heaven",year:"About 850–586 BC",
 sum:"Elisha promises that tomorrow a measure of fine flour will sell for a shekel at the gate of Samaria—A lord scoffs, “If the LORD would make windows in heaven, might this thing be?”—Four lepers go to the Syrian camp and find it empty, for the LORD had made the Syrians hear the sound of a great host—The city is plundered and the famine ends—The scoffing lord is trampled in the gate and dies.",
 bg:["The four lepers lived outside the city gate because the law kept people with skin disease apart (Leviticus 13:46), so they were the first to find the camp. Their words, “We do not well: this day is a day of good tidings, and we hold our peace” (verse 9), show that good news is meant to be shared. A “measure” of flour at “a shekel” was a very low price compared with the famine prices of the previous chapter.","The scoffing lord is told he will “see it with thine eyes, but shalt not eat thereof,” and verse 20 says that is exactly how he died. The text presents God’s word as fully reliable, even when it seems impossible. The “Hittites” and “Egyptians” the Syrians feared (verse 6) show how many powers were in the region at that time."]},
"2kgs.8":{title:"Is thy servant a dog, that he should do this great thing?",year:"About 850–586 BC",
 sum:"Elisha warns the Shunammite woman of a seven-year famine, and her land is later restored by the king—Elisha goes to Damascus and tells Hazael he will be king of Syria—Hazael smothers Ben-hadad and reigns—Jehoram of Judah, who married Ahab’s daughter, reigns eight years and does evil—Edom rebels, and Ahaziah, his son, reigns.",
 bg:["Hazael is known from outside the Bible. Assyrian inscriptions name him as a king of Damascus, and an Aramaic inscription found at Tel Dan in northern Israel, written in the 800s BC and widely thought to come from the time of Hazael or his son, mentions the “house of David.” In verse 13, “a dog” was a common self-insult meaning a person of no importance, and Hazael’s surprise is not shared by Elisha, who weeps.","The marriage of Jehoram of Judah to a daughter of Ahab (verse 18) joined Judah’s royal house with Israel’s. The text says it led Judah to follow “the way of the house of Ahab.” It is a reminder that who we join our lives with matters. The text also notes the LORD preserved Judah “for David his servant’s sake” (verse 19)."]},
"2kgs.9":{title:"The driving is like the driving of Jehu",year:"About 850–586 BC",
 sum:"Elisha sends a young prophet to anoint Jehu king over Israel and to charge him with ending the house of Ahab—Jehu’s fellow officers proclaim him king—He rides to Jezreel and kills Joram of Israel at the field of Naboth—Ahaziah of Judah is also struck down—Jezebel is thrown from a window and trampled, and dogs eat her as Elijah had said.",
 bg:["Jehu’s name is commonly explained as “the LORD is he” (or “Yah is he”). The text says his chariot driving was famous, “he driveth furiously.” Jehu appears outside the Bible too: the Black Obelisk of the Assyrian king Shalmaneser III, found in Nimrud, shows a figure labeled as Jehu, son of Omri, or his envoy, bowing and bringing tribute.","The killing of Joram at Naboth’s field (verses 25–26) fulfills Elijah’s words in 1 Kings 21:19–24, where Ahab’s house was warned for the murder of Naboth and the seizure of his vineyard. Jehu’s violence is told plainly, and later Hosea says that God would “avenge the blood of Jezreel upon the house of Jehu” (Hosea 1:4), so the Bible does not treat everything Jehu did as righteous."]},
"2kgs.10":{title:"Come with me, and see my zeal for the LORD",year:"About 850–586 BC",
 sum:"Jehu has Ahab’s seventy sons beheaded in Samaria—He kills the relatives of Ahaziah of Judah, forty-two men—He meets Jehonadab the son of Rechab: “Come with me, and see my zeal for the LORD”—By a trick Jehu gathers the worshippers of Baal and slays them, destroying the house of Baal—Jehu does not keep the law with all his heart, and Hazael begins to cut Israel short.",
 bg:["This is one of the most violent chapters in the Bible, and the text reports it without softening. Jehu removes Ahab’s family and the worship of Baal that Jezebel had promoted, and the LORD promises that his sons will sit on the throne to the fourth generation (verse 30). But verse 31 says that “Jehu took no heed to walk in the law of the LORD God of Israel with all his heart,” and he kept Jeroboam’s golden calves at Bethel and Dan.","Jehonadab (or Jonadab) son of Rechab, who rides with Jehu, is the ancestor of the Rechabites, who in Jeremiah 35 refuse wine and houses as a family tradition. That passage gives the only other detail of this family. The Bible gives Jehu’s reign about twenty-eight years (verse 36), and his dynasty held the throne for about a century."]},
"2kgs.11":{title:"They made him king, and anointed him",year:"About 850–586 BC",
 sum:"Athaliah, mother of Ahaziah, destroys the royal seed of Judah and seizes the throne—Jehosheba hides baby Joash in the temple for six years—The priest Jehoiada sets guards around the young king and crowns him—Athaliah cries, “Treason, Treason,” and is put to death—Jehoiada makes a covenant between the LORD, the king, and the people, and the house of Baal is torn down.",
 bg:["Athaliah was a daughter of Ahab (or granddaughter of Omri; the text uses both, 8:18 and 8:26) and the only woman to rule Judah on her own. Her rule nearly ended the line of David, which God had promised to keep (2 Samuel 7:16). Jehosheba, a sister of the former king and the wife of Jehoiada the priest (2 Chronicles 22:11), is the one who saved the child.","Joash’s name is sometimes spelled Jehoash and probably means “the LORD has given” or “the LORD’s gift,” though scholars are not certain. He was seven years old when he was crowned (verse 21). The “covenant” at the temple (verse 17) renews the old relationship between God, the king, and the people, much like Josiah’s later covenant in 2 Kings 23."]},
"2kgs.12":{title:"Jehoiada the priest took a chest, and bored a hole in the lid of it",year:"About 850–586 BC",
 sum:"Joash does right in the sight of the LORD while Jehoiada instructs him—The priests are told to use temple money to repair the breaches, but nothing is done for twenty-three years—Jehoiada sets a chest with a hole in its lid by the altar, and honest workmen repair the house—Hazael of Syria threatens Jerusalem, and Joash buys him off with temple treasures—Joash’s servants kill him.",
 bg:["This is a story of financial honesty. Once the money is put in the chest, it goes directly to the carpenters, builders, and masons, who “dealt faithfully” (verse 15), and no accounting was demanded of them. The same story is told in 2 Chronicles 24, which adds that after Jehoiada died Joash turned away from the LORD (2 Chronicles 24:17–22).","The ending is sad: the king who had rebuilt the temple used its treasures to pay off Hazael (verses 17–18), and was later killed by his own servants. Joash had been saved as a baby by faithful people, but the story shows that a good start did not keep him faithful without the guidance of Jehoiada."]},
"2kgs.13":{title:"The LORD was gracious unto them",year:"About 850–586 BC",
 sum:"Jehoahaz and Jehoash of Israel reign and do evil, and Hazael of Syria oppresses Israel—Jehoahaz prays and the LORD sends a deliverer—Elisha, dying, tells King Jehoash to shoot an arrow and strike the ground with arrows, promising victories over Syria—A dead man cast into Elisha’s tomb revives when he touches the prophet’s bones—The LORD is gracious because of his covenant with Abraham, Isaac, and Jacob.",
 bg:["Elisha’s last act is to promise Jehoash victory over Syria. The king strikes the ground only three times, and the prophet is angry because it shows he does not have full faith (verses 18–19). The cry “O my father, my father, the chariot of Israel, and the horsemen thereof” (verse 14) is the same words Elisha spoke when Elijah was taken up (2:12).","Verse 23 gives the reason God did not destroy Israel yet: “because of his covenant with Abraham, Isaac, and Jacob.” The story of the revived man (verse 21) is the only miracle in the Bible connected with a prophet’s bones, and the text offers no further explanation."]},
"2kgs.14":{title:"He restored the coast of Israel",year:"About 850–586 BC",
 sum:"Amaziah reigns in Judah and does right, but not like David—He avenges his father’s murder but spares the murderers’ children, as the law of Moses requires—Amaziah defeats Edom, then challenges Jehoash of Israel and is defeated at Beth-shemesh—Jeroboam II of Israel restores Israel’s borders according to the word of Jonah the son of Amittai—Amaziah is killed at Lachish.",
 bg:["Verse 6 follows Deuteronomy 24:16: “every man shall be put to death for his own sin.” Amaziah did not punish the sons of his father’s killers. Jeroboam II reigned about forty years (verse 23) and Israel became prosperous and large, but the prophets Amos and Hosea show that it was also a time of injustice and idol worship.","Verse 25 names “Jonah, the son of Amittai,” the same prophet who is the subject of the book of Jonah. He is from Gath-hepher in Galilee. The name Jonah means “dove.” Jehoash’s parable of the thistle and the cedar (verse 9) is a way of saying that a small king should not provoke a great one."]},
"2kgs.15":{title:"The LORD smote the king, so that he was a leper",year:"About 850–586 BC",
 sum:"Azariah (Uzziah) reigns in Judah and is struck with leprosy, and his son Jotham rules for him—Zachariah, Shallum, Menahem, Pekahiah, Pekah, and Hoshea follow one another in Israel in quick, violent succession—Menahem pays Pul, king of Assyria, a thousand talents of silver—Tiglath-pileser carries away people from northern Israel—Jotham reigns in Judah, and Ahaz his son after him.",
 bg:["“Pul” (verse 19) is another name for Tiglath-pileser III, who ruled Assyria about 745–727 BC. Assyrian records mention Menahem of Samaria paying tribute. Six kings of Israel in this chapter reigned over a period of roughly thirty years, and four of them died by assassination, which shows how unstable the northern kingdom had become just before its end.","Azariah is called Uzziah in Isaiah and 2 Chronicles. Isaiah’s great vision of the LORD in the temple came “in the year that king Uzziah died” (Isaiah 6:1), so the prophet’s call fits this time. The text here says only that the LORD struck the king with leprosy; 2 Chronicles 26:16–21 gives the reason, that he tried to burn incense in the temple, which was the work of the priests."]},
"2kgs.16":{title:"I am thy servant and thy son: come up, and save me",year:"About 850–586 BC",
 sum:"Ahaz of Judah walks in the ways of the kings of Israel and even makes his son pass through the fire—Syria and Israel attack him, and he sends silver and gold from the temple to Tiglath-pileser, saying, “I am thy servant and thy son”—Assyria takes Damascus—Ahaz sees an altar at Damascus and has a copy built in Jerusalem, rearranging the temple furnishings—Ahaz dies, and Hezekiah his son reigns.",
 bg:["The prophet Isaiah met Ahaz during this crisis and urged him to trust God rather than Assyria (Isaiah 7), offering the sign of Immanuel. Ahaz refused, and instead he made himself Assyria’s vassal. Matthew 1:23 and 2 Nephi 17:14 both quote Isaiah’s words about the child Immanuel, and the Book of Mormon includes Isaiah 7 in 2 Nephi 17.","Ahaz’s changes to the temple (verses 10–18) set an Assyrian-style altar in place of the bronze one Solomon had made. The text does not say exactly why he did this, and scholars disagree on whether it was meant as worship of Assyria’s gods or as a political sign. “Passing a son through the fire” (verse 3) is condemned in the law (Deuteronomy 18:10)."]},
"2kgs.17":{title:"The LORD removed Israel out of his sight",year:"About 850–586 BC",
 sum:"Hoshea, last king of Israel, rebels against Assyria, and the king of Assyria takes Samaria in the ninth year and carries Israel away to Halah, Habor, and the cities of the Medes—The text explains that Israel sinned against the LORD, followed the nations’ customs, and ignored the prophets—The king of Assyria settles foreign peoples in Samaria—They “feared the LORD, and served their own gods.”",
 bg:["Samaria fell in about 722 BC. Assyrian records of Sargon II claim the capture and say that nearly 27,000 people were deported, though historians debate whether Shalmaneser V or Sargon finished the job. The text says Israel was taken to Assyria; the phrase “ten tribes” does not appear in this chapter, but it comes from 1 Kings 11:31, where ten tribes were given to Jeroboam. Judah, the other tribe, remained (verse 18). The Latter-day Saint scriptures speak of the return of those in the north countries (Doctrine and Covenants 133:26–34) and the Articles of Faith state belief in “the restoration of the Ten Tribes” (Articles of Faith 1:10).","Verses 7–23 give a long explanation, one of the Bible’s clearest summaries of why the kingdom ended. The foreign settlers (verses 24–41) mixed worship of the LORD with their own gods. Later Jews called their descendants Samaritans, a group that appears in the New Testament, for example in John 4."]},
"2kgs.18":{title:"He trusted in the LORD God of Israel",year:"About 850–586 BC",
 sum:"Hezekiah becomes king of Judah, removes the high places, and breaks in pieces the brasen serpent, calling it Nehushtan—“He trusted in the LORD God of Israel; so that after him was none like him”—Hezekiah rebels against Assyria, and Sennacherib takes the fenced cities of Judah—Hezekiah pays heavy tribute, and the Rabshakeh mocks Hezekiah’s trust in the LORD before the walls of Jerusalem.",
 bg:["Hezekiah’s name means “the LORD strengthens.” Nehushtan (verse 4) is a play on the Hebrew word for serpent and for bronze. Moses had made the bronze serpent (Numbers 21:9), and it later became an object of worship, so Hezekiah destroyed it. Jesus used the same serpent as a sign of himself in John 3:14, and Nephi also taught about it in 1 Nephi 17:41 and Alma 33:19–22.","Sennacherib’s own record, the Taylor Prism (held in the British Museum), claims he shut Hezekiah up in Jerusalem “like a bird in a cage” and took forty-six walled cities; carved wall reliefs from his palace at Nineveh show the siege of Lachish. The Rabshakeh (verse 17) is an Assyrian title for a high officer, and his speech in Hebrew is also found in Isaiah 36, nearly word for word."]},
"2kgs.19":{title:"LORD, bow down thine ear, and hear",year:"About 850–586 BC",
 sum:"Hezekiah tears his clothes, goes to the house of the LORD, and sends to Isaiah—Isaiah says not to fear, for the king of Assyria will hear a rumor and return to his own land—Sennacherib sends a threatening letter, and Hezekiah spreads it before the LORD and prays—Isaiah answers with a prophecy against Assyria—The angel of the LORD smites 185,000 Assyrians in one night, and Sennacherib is killed by his sons in the house of Nisroch.",
 bg:["This chapter is nearly identical to Isaiah 37, and the Book of Mormon does not quote either chapter. The Assyrian king’s own prism does not mention the loss of an army but does admit Hezekiah was never captured, which fits the Bible’s account. Sennacherib was killed in 681 BC, and the Assyrian records confirm that he was murdered by his sons, about twenty years after this campaign.","The prayer in verses 15–19 is a model of turning to God with a problem: Hezekiah lays the threatening letter “before the LORD.” Isaiah’s answer (verses 21–28) is a taunt aimed at Assyria. The text does not describe how the 185,000 died, saying only that “the angel of the LORD went out” (verse 35)."]},
"2kgs.20":{title:"I have heard thy prayer, I have seen thy tears",year:"About 850–586 BC",
 sum:"Hezekiah is sick unto death, and Isaiah tells him to set his house in order—He turns his face to the wall and prays, and the LORD adds fifteen years to his life—The shadow goes back ten degrees on the sundial of Ahaz as a sign—Envoys of Berodach-baladan of Babylon come, and Hezekiah shows them all his treasures—Isaiah foretells that these things will be carried to Babylon.",
 bg:["Berodach-baladan is the Babylonian ruler Marduk-apla-iddina II (Merodach-baladan), who sought allies against Assyria. Hezekiah’s showing him the treasures (verses 12–19) led to Isaiah’s warning that Babylon would one day carry everything away, a prophecy that came true about a century later (2 Kings 24–25). This chapter is paralleled by Isaiah 38–39.","Verse 20 refers to the “pool, and a conduit” that Hezekiah made. This is the Siloam (Hezekiah’s) tunnel in Jerusalem, a rock-cut channel of about 530 meters (about 1,750 feet) that brought water from the Gihon spring inside the walls. An ancient Hebrew inscription describing the end of its digging was found in the tunnel in 1880, and most scholars connect it with Hezekiah."]},
"2kgs.21":{title:"Manasseh seduced them to do more evil",year:"About 850–586 BC",
 sum:"Manasseh, twelve years old, reigns fifty-five years and rebuilds the high places, sets up altars to Baal, and sacrifices his son—He puts an idol in the temple and sheds much innocent blood—The LORD announces that He will bring such evil on Jerusalem that “whosoever heareth of it, both his ears shall tingle”—Manasseh dies, and his son Amon reigns two years and is killed by his servants.",
 bg:["Manasseh’s fifty-five years (about 697–642 BC) were the longest reign in Judah, and he was a loyal vassal of Assyria; Assyrian records list him among the kings who paid tribute. Verse 9 says he “seduced” the people to do worse than the nations who had been driven out. 2 Chronicles 33:10–19 adds that he was taken to Babylon, humbled himself, and was restored, but 2 Kings does not mention this.","The LORD’s statement in verses 10–15, that He would wipe Jerusalem “as a man wipeth a dish,” is presented as the point of no return. It was Manasseh’s long reign, more than any other, that this book names as the cause of Judah’s fall (see also 2 Kings 23:26 and 24:3). Josiah, his grandson, would try to turn things back."]},
"2kgs.22":{title:"I have found the book of the law in the house of the LORD",year:"About 850–586 BC",
 sum:"Josiah, eight years old, begins to reign and does what is right—In his eighteenth year he orders the temple repaired—Hilkiah the high priest finds the book of the law, and Shaphan reads it to the king—Josiah tears his clothes and sends to inquire of the LORD—The prophetess Huldah says judgment will come, but that Josiah will be gathered in peace because his heart was tender.",
 bg:["Josiah’s name means “the LORD supports” (or “the LORD heals”; scholars are not sure). The “book of the law” found in the temple is thought by many scholars to be all or part of the book of Deuteronomy, because Josiah’s reforms in chapter 23 match its commands about one central place of worship and the Passover. The text itself does not give the book’s name. The date of this event is about 622 BC.","Huldah (verse 14) was a prophetess who lived in Jerusalem, and the king’s men went to her rather than to Jeremiah or Zephaniah, who were also active then. Her words show that the discovery of the law did not cancel Judah’s earlier sins, but it brought mercy to Josiah himself. Lehi lived in Jerusalem a generation after this, and the Book of Mormon records that his brass plates held the law and the prophets (1 Nephi 5:11–13)."]},
"2kgs.23":{title:"The king made a covenant before the LORD",year:"About 850–586 BC",
 sum:"Josiah reads the book of the covenant to all the people and makes a covenant before the LORD—He removes idols and high places from the temple, Jerusalem, Judah, and Bethel, fulfilling the word of the man of God—He keeps a Passover such as had not been held since the judges—There was no king like Josiah, but the LORD does not turn from His anger against Judah—Josiah dies at Megiddo, and Jehoahaz and Jehoiakim reign.",
 bg:["The altar at Bethel that Josiah breaks (verses 15–18) was the one that a man of God had warned about three hundred years earlier, in 1 Kings 13:2, where Josiah is named long before his birth. Josiah’s Passover (verses 21–23) is described in more detail in 2 Chronicles 35. The reform was thorough, and it shows how much idolatry had grown in the temple itself.","Josiah was killed at Megiddo in 609 BC when he met Pharaoh Neco, who was marching north to help Assyria against Babylon (verse 29). Jeremiah began to prophesy in Josiah’s thirteenth year (Jeremiah 1:2), so these reforms and Jeremiah’s early ministry overlap. Jehoiakim, the king placed on the throne by Egypt, reversed much of Josiah’s work."]},
"2kgs.24":{title:"He carried away all Jerusalem",year:"About 850–586 BC",
 sum:"Jehoiakim serves Nebuchadnezzar of Babylon and then rebels—The LORD sends bands against Judah, as He had spoken by His prophets—Jehoiachin reigns three months and surrenders to Babylon, and the temple treasures and about ten thousand captives are taken—Nebuchadnezzar makes Mattaniah king and changes his name to Zedekiah—Zedekiah does evil and rebels against Babylon.",
 bg:["Nebuchadnezzar’s name comes from the Babylonian Nabu-kudurri-usur, usually understood as “Nabu, protect my heir (or boundary).” A Babylonian chronicle records the capture of Jerusalem in March 597 BC, matching this chapter. Jehoiachin (“the LORD establishes”) was taken to Babylon together with the prophet Ezekiel (Ezekiel 1:1–2). Zedekiah means “righteousness of the LORD,” and the new name given by Babylon was a sign that he was now a subject king.","Lehi’s story begins in this period: “in the commencement of the first year of the reign of Zedekiah, king of Judah” (1 Nephi 1:4), about 600 BC, and he prophesied that Jerusalem would be destroyed (1 Nephi 1:13). The first deportation described here, and Jeremiah’s warnings to Zedekiah, form the setting for Lehi leaving Jerusalem."]},
"2kgs.25":{title:"They slew the sons of Zedekiah before his eyes",year:"About 850–586 BC",
 sum:"Nebuchadnezzar besieges Jerusalem from the ninth year of Zedekiah, and famine fills the city—Zedekiah flees, is captured at Jericho, sees his sons killed, and is blinded and taken to Babylon—Nebuzar-adan burns the house of the LORD, the king’s house, and the walls, and carries away the people and the temple vessels—Gedaliah is made governor and is murdered—In the thirty-seventh year of captivity, Jehoiachin is released from prison.",
 bg:["Jerusalem fell in 586 BC. The date given for the temple’s burning is the seventh day of the fifth month (verse 8), while Jeremiah 52:12 says the tenth day. The text does not explain the difference. The account closely matches Jeremiah 39 and 52, and Jeremiah’s own Lamentations mourn this event. Babylonian ration tablets found in Babylon list “Yaukin, king of Judah,” among those receiving food, which fits the ending in verses 27–30.","The Book of Mormon speaks of this time from another side. 1 Nephi 1:13 says Lehi saw that Jerusalem would be destroyed, and Omni 1:15–16 says that Mulek, “a son of Zedekiah,” was brought out by the hand of the Lord and came to the land of Zarahemla. The Bible does not mention Mulek, and 2 Kings 25:7 says only that Zedekiah’s sons were killed. 2 Kings ends with a note of hope: the exiled line of David is still alive."]},
"song.1":{title:"I am black, but comely, O ye daughters of Jerusalem",year:"Date uncertain",
 sum:"The woman longs for her beloved: “thy love is better than wine”—The daughters of Jerusalem join in—She says she is dark but lovely, and that she was made keeper of vineyards while her own vineyard was neglected—She asks where he feeds his flock—They exchange words of admiration, and the bed is green with the cedars and fir.",
 bg:["The book’s Hebrew title, “Song of songs,” means the best of songs, in the same way “holy of holies” means the holiest place. Solomon’s name is in the title and in a few verses, but scholars disagree about whether he wrote it or whether the title means “for” or “about” Solomon. The poem is read in more than one way: as a love poem celebrating marriage, and, in Jewish and Christian tradition, as an image of God’s love for His people.","The Latter-day Saint edition of the King James Bible includes a note on the chapter heading of Song of Solomon that says, from the Joseph Smith Translation, “The Songs of Solomon are not inspired writings.” Latter-day Saints therefore read the book with care, and this summary does not claim more than the text says."]},
"song.2":{title:"I am the rose of Sharon, and the lily of the valleys",year:"Date uncertain",
 sum:"The woman calls herself the rose of Sharon and the lily of the valleys—He answers that she is a lily among thorns—She sits under his shadow, and his banner over her is love—He calls her to come away, for the winter is past and the flowers appear—“My beloved is mine, and I am his.”",
 bg:["Sharon is the fertile coastal plain west of the hills of Judea. The Hebrew word translated “rose” (chavatselet) is a flower whose identity is uncertain; it may be a crocus or similar low spring flower rather than the modern rose. The point of the image is that she describes herself modestly and he responds by calling her a lily among thorns.","Verses 10–13 are a famous spring song: “the winter is past, the rain is over and gone.” The “little foxes, that spoil the vines” (verse 15) are often read as small troubles that damage something precious, and some readers apply that to love and marriage. The poem does not explain the image, and readers have taken it in different ways."]},
"song.3":{title:"I found him whom my soul loveth",year:"Date uncertain",
 sum:"By night on her bed, the woman seeks her beloved and cannot find him—She goes through the city, asks the watchmen, and finds him—She brings him to her mother’s house and asks the daughters of Jerusalem not to awaken love until it pleases—A procession comes from the wilderness, with Solomon in his litter—The daughters of Zion are told to behold the king on the day of his espousals.",
 bg:["The search in the night (verses 1–5) may be a dream or a memory; the poem does not say. The refrain “I charge you, O ye daughters of Jerusalem … that ye stir not up, nor awake my love, till he please” appears three times in the book (2:7, 3:5, 8:4) and is often understood as meaning that love should come at its proper time.","The wedding procession (verses 6–11) pictures a litter, an ornamented carriage, carried by sixty armed men, with Solomon wearing a crown “in the day of his espousals” (verse 11). Verse 10 says the interior was “paved with love,” and the Hebrew is difficult, so translators differ. Some Jewish and Christian readers see in the wedding an image of God’s covenant with His people."]},
"song.4":{title:"Thou art all fair, my love",year:"Date uncertain",
 sum:"The man praises the woman’s beauty with images of doves, goats, pomegranates, and towers—He says, “Thou art all fair, my love; there is no spot in thee”—He calls her “my spouse” and says she has ravished his heart—She is a garden inclosed, a spring shut up, with pleasant fruits and spices—She invites the north wind and south wind to blow on her garden and invites her beloved in.",
 bg:["The comparisons in this chapter come from the world of ancient Israel: flocks of goats on Mount Gilead, a tower of David, mountains of myrrh, and the cedars and spices of Lebanon. They are poetry, not literal description, and many seem strange today. The point is wholehearted delight.","The “garden inclosed” and “spring shut up” (verse 12) picture a bride who is faithful and kept for her husband. Jewish and Christian tradition have also read the garden as an image of God’s people or of the soul. The word “spouse” here means bride or wife, and it first appears in verse 8 as the lover calls her from Lebanon."]},
"song.5":{title:"I sleep, but my heart waketh",year:"Date uncertain",
 sum:"The man comes to the door and asks her to open—She hesitates, and by the time she opens he has gone—She searches the city, and the watchmen beat her and take her veil—She asks the daughters of Jerusalem to tell him she is sick with love—They ask what makes her beloved special, and she describes him: “yea, he is altogether lovely.”",
 bg:["Like chapter 3, this is a night scene that may be a dream. The woman hesitates and then finds that her beloved has withdrawn, which makes her search the city. This is one of the few places where the poem has a hint of sorrow, and readers have often seen in it the longing of someone who has been slow to respond.","Verses 10–16 are the woman’s praise of her beloved from head to feet, with images such as gold, ivory, and sapphires. “White and ruddy” (verse 10) means healthy and radiant. “Altogether lovely” is a phrase often taken from this verse in Christian hymns and writings where the beloved is read as Christ, but the text itself is addressed to the daughters of Jerusalem about her friend."]},
"song.6":{title:"I am my beloved’s, and my beloved is mine",year:"Date uncertain",
 sum:"The daughters of Jerusalem ask where her beloved has gone, and she says he has gone down to his garden—“I am my beloved’s, and my beloved is mine”—He praises her as beautiful as Tirzah, comely as Jerusalem, and terrible as an army with banners—He speaks of the nut garden and of her chariots—The people call, “Return, return, O Shulamite.”",
 bg:["Tirzah and Jerusalem (verse 4) were two great capitals, Tirzah the early capital of the northern kingdom and Jerusalem of Judah, so he is comparing her to the finest cities. “Terrible as an army with banners” means awe-inspiring. “Who is she that looketh forth as the morning, fair as the moon, clear as the sun?” (verse 10) is a poetic way of saying she shines.","The name Shulamite (verse 13) appears only here. The meaning is uncertain: it may be the female form of Solomon (Shelomo), or it may point to a woman from Shunem (the Hebrew would be Shulem), a village in the Jezreel Valley. The phrase “company of two armies” (Hebrew mahanaim) may refer to a dance or may be a place name, and translators differ."]},
"song.7":{title:"How fair and how pleasant art thou, O love, for delights",year:"Date uncertain",
 sum:"The man praises the woman from her feet upward, with images of jewels, a tower, pools, and Carmel—He says that the king is held captive in the tresses—He compares her stature to a palm tree and her breasts to clusters of grapes—She answers that she is her beloved’s and his desire is toward her—She invites him to the field and the vineyards, where she will give him her love.",
 bg:["This chapter is the most openly romantic part of the poem, and the language is poetic and tender. It is a reminder that the Bible includes love between husband and wife as a good gift, a view that fits the Church’s teaching that marriage is ordained of God (Doctrine and Covenants 49:15). Readers who see the book as an image of God’s love read the chapter as a picture of closeness between God and His people.","The mandrakes (verse 13) were a plant believed in the ancient world to help with love and having children, and they are also mentioned in Genesis 30:14–16. The woman says “I am my beloved’s, and his desire is toward me” (verse 10)."]},
"song.8":{title:"Many waters cannot quench love",year:"Date uncertain",
 sum:"The woman wishes her beloved were like a brother she could kiss in public—She asks him to set her as a seal upon his heart, for love is strong as death—“Many waters cannot quench love, neither can the floods drown it”—Her brothers speak of their little sister—She says her own vineyard is hers, and calls her beloved to come away.",
 bg:["Verse 6, “love is strong as death,” compares two powerful things: neither can be stopped. The “seal” was a stamp or signet worn on a cord on the chest or arm to show who owned something, so asking to be set “as a seal” is asking to be near and belong. The words “a most vehement flame” are in Hebrew shalhevetyah, which can mean “flame of the LORD” or simply a very great flame; scholars differ, and the King James translators chose the second.","The final verses (8–14) are hard to read and scholars disagree about who is speaking. Her brothers, her vineyard, and the one thousand pieces of silver for Solomon’s vineyard (verses 11–12) seem to say that she is not for sale and her love is freely given. The book ends with her call, “Make haste, my beloved,” and readers have taken it as longing for love, and, in Christian tradition, longing for Christ’s coming."]},
"1chr.1":{title:"Adam, Sheth, Enosh",year:"Genealogies from Adam",
 sum:"The line runs from Adam through Seth to Noah and his sons Shem, Ham, and Japheth—The nations descend from the sons of Japheth, Ham, and Shem—Peleg is named “because in his days the earth was divided”—The line narrows from Shem to Abram, “the same is Abraham,” and his sons Isaac and Ishmael—Esau’s descendants, the kings and dukes of Edom.",
 bg:["Chronicles was probably written after the exile in Babylon, when Israel needed to remember who they were. Starting with Adam tells the returning community that their story belongs to the whole human family, and the long lists of names (much of this chapter overlaps Genesis 5, 10, 11, 25, and 36) were also records of land, tribe, and priestly service. Peleg means “division” (1:19), and the text links it to a time when the earth was divided; Genesis 10:25 says the same, and the text does not explain what the division was. Latter-day Saints also read the early line of patriarchs in Doctrine and Covenants 107:41–52.","The chapter moves quickly past Abraham’s other sons (Ishmael and Keturah’s sons) to follow Isaac’s line, then lists Esau’s descendants in Edom, including “kings that reigned in the land of Edom before any king reigned over the children of Israel” (1:43). Chronicles names Edom’s rulers without comment, simply showing Israel’s place among its neighbors before the story narrows to Jacob."]},
"1chr.2":{title:"These are the sons of Israel",year:"Genealogies from Adam",
 sum:"The twelve sons of Israel are named—Judah’s line through Tamar, Pharez, and Hezron is traced, “the troubler of Israel” among them—The line passes through Ram, Nahshon, Boaz, and Jesse to David—David’s sisters and the great houses of Jerahmeel and Caleb are listed.",
 bg:["Verses 1–2 list Jacob’s twelve sons, the tribes that held the land. Judah comes first of the later detail because the writer’s main interest is David’s family, the royal line. The chapter openly mentions Tamar’s part in the story (2:4; Genesis 38), and the line from Pharez to Boaz, Obed, and Jesse (2:5–15) is the same one Matthew 1:3–6 follows to Jesus.","“Achar, the troubler of Israel” (2:7) is the man called Achan in Joshua 7. Chronicles changes the name slightly, and “Achar” sounds like the Hebrew word for “trouble,” the same word used in the Valley of Achor, where he was judged. The long Caleb and Jerahmeel lists were records of family and land in southern Judah, and scholars are not certain how every name connects."]},
"1chr.3":{title:"The sons of David, which were born unto him in Hebron",year:"Genealogies from Adam",
 sum:"David’s sons born in Hebron and in Jerusalem are named, Solomon among them—The line of kings of Judah follows from Solomon down to Josiah’s sons—The exile and Jeconiah’s descendants are traced—Zerubbabel and his sons carry the line past Babylon.",
 bg:["This chapter shows that David’s family line did not end with the exile. It follows the kings (3:10–16) to Jeconiah, who was taken to Babylon, and then lists his descendants for several generations, including Zerubbabel (3:19), the leader who later helped lead the return from exile (Ezra 3:2; Haggai 1:1). To a people who had lost their king, this list quietly says the promise to David was still alive.","Verses 1–9 parallel 2 Samuel 3:2–5 and 5:13–16, and verse 5 gives Solomon’s mother as Bath-shua, a form of Bathsheba. Many of the names after Jeconiah are not known from other books, so scholars differ on how some of the generations should be counted. Matthew 1:12–13 follows part of this line through Zerubbabel."]},
"1chr.4":{title:"Jabez called on the God of Israel",year:"Genealogies from Adam",
 sum:"More of Judah’s families are listed, including the potters and workers who served the king—Jabez is “more honourable than his brethren,” named for his mother’s sorrow—He prays: “Oh that thou wouldest bless me indeed, and enlarge my coast”—God grants his request—The sons of Simeon and their wide, quiet pasture lands.",
 bg:["Jabez (4:9) sounds like the Hebrew word for “pain” or “sorrow,” which is why his mother said, “I bare him with sorrow.” His short prayer, asking for blessing, more land, God’s hand, and protection from evil, is the only story in a long list of names, and the text does not say who he was or when he lived. “And God granted him that which he requested” (4:10).","Verse 23 mentions potters who “dwelt with the king for his work,” a reminder that ordinary craftsmen served the royal household. Verses 28–43 list Simeon’s towns in the far south, around Beer-sheba, and say some Simeonites later moved to find pasture and drove out the Amalekite remnant on Mount Seir (4:39–43); this account is found only in Chronicles."]},
"1chr.5":{title:"The birthright was Joseph’s",year:"Genealogies from Adam",
 sum:"Reuben loses the birthright because he defiled his father’s bed, and it goes to Joseph’s sons—“Judah prevailed above his brethren,” and from him came the chief ruler—Reuben, Gad, and half of Manasseh east of the Jordan win a battle because “they cried to God”—They later transgress and are carried away by the king of Assyria.",
 bg:["Verses 1–2 explain a puzzle in Jacob’s family. Reuben was the firstborn, but because of his sin (Genesis 35:22; 49:3–4) the birthright, which included a double inheritance, went to Joseph through his sons Ephraim and Manasseh (Genesis 48), while the royal line came through Judah. Latter-day Saints connect Ephraim’s role with the gathering of Israel (see Doctrine and Covenants 133:30–34), and the Book of Mormon also speaks of Joseph’s descendants (2 Nephi 3).","The tribes east of the Jordan were the first to go into exile. Verse 26 names “Pul” and “Tilgath-pilneser” as kings of Assyria, and most scholars take these as two names for the same king, Tiglath-pileser III (about 745–727 BC); see also 2 Kings 15:29. Chronicles says plainly that they went away because they stopped trusting God (5:25), and that earlier they had been helped when they did (5:20)."]},
"1chr.6":{title:"Whom David set over the service of song",year:"Genealogies from Adam",
 sum:"The line of Levi runs through Aaron’s sons down to Jehozadak, who went into captivity under Nebuchadnezzar—The Levite families of Gershom, Kohath, and Merari are listed—David sets Heman, Asaph, and Ethan over the service of song—Aaron’s sons alone offer on the altars and make atonement—The Levites receive their cities.",
 bg:["Levi’s tribe had no land of its own. Instead the Levites received towns among the other tribes (6:54–81; Joshua 21), and Chronicles lists them because the temple, the priesthood, and the music were central to how the writer thought Israel should live. Priests came only from Aaron’s line (6:49), and the high priestly list ends with Jehozadak “into captivity” (6:15), whose son Joshua helped rebuild the temple (Haggai 1:1).","The singers get special honor here. Heman, Asaph, and Ethan (also called Jeduthun) led the music that David set up “after that the ark had rest” (6:31), and several psalms carry their names (for example, Psalm 50, 73–83 for Asaph, and Psalm 88 for Heman the Ezrahite). Heman is called a grandson of Samuel (6:33; 1 Samuel 8:2), so the prophet’s family served in sacred song."]},
"1chr.7":{title:"Valiant men of might in their generations",year:"Genealogies from Adam",
 sum:"Genealogies of Issachar, Benjamin, and Naphtali are given, with the numbers of mighty men in David’s days—Manasseh’s and Ephraim’s descendants are listed, and Ephraim mourns his slain sons—Beriah is named “because it went evil with his house”—Joshua appears at the end of Ephraim’s line—Asher’s sons and their numbers close the chapter.",
 bg:["These lists, with their counts of fighting men, probably came from military and census records, and they show how many warriors each tribe could muster. They were useful in the days after the exile because land and standing in Israel depended on tribe and family.","Ephraim’s grief (7:21–23) is a tender moment inside a list. After some of his sons were killed in a cattle raid at Gath, he mourned for many days, and a new son was named Beriah, which sounds like the Hebrew “in evil” or “in trouble.” Verse 27 gives Joshua’s line (“Non his son, Jehoshua his son”), where his name appears as Jehoshuah."]},
"1chr.8":{title:"Ner begat Kish, and Kish begat Saul",year:"Genealogies from Adam",
 sum:"Benjamin’s sons and the families who lived in Geba, Ono, and Lod are listed—Some leaders drive out others and settle in new towns—Ner’s line leads to Kish and to Saul and his sons, Jonathan among them—Jonathan’s son Merib-baal and the many descendants of Saul’s house are named.",
 bg:["Benjamin was the tribe of the first king, Saul, and Chronicles records his family even though the book’s attention then turns to David. The list is repeated in 9:35–44 with small differences. The tribe was also close to Jerusalem and, along with Judah, stayed faithful to David’s line after the kingdom divided (1 Kings 12:21).","Some names here include the word “baal,” such as Esh-baal (8:33) and Merib-baal (8:34). Baal means “lord,” and in earlier times it could be used of the LORD himself, but later writers often replaced it with the Hebrew word for “shame,” which is why the same men appear as Ish-bosheth and Mephibosheth in 2 Samuel 2:8 and 4:4. Scholars generally take these to be the same persons."]},
"1chr.9":{title:"All Israel were reckoned by genealogies",year:"Genealogies from Adam",
 sum:"All Israel is reckoned by genealogy, and Judah is carried away to Babylon for transgression—The first to resettle in Jerusalem are Israelites, priests, Levites, and “Nethinims”—The gatekeepers and their duties, chosen by David and Samuel the seer, are described—The singers serve day and night—Saul’s genealogy from chapter 8 is repeated.",
 bg:["Verses 1–2 sum up the whole reason for the genealogies. Israel was exiled “for their transgression,” and the first people to settle again in their own possessions were laypeople, priests, Levites, and the temple servants (“Nethinims”). The lists in 9:3–34 closely match Nehemiah 11:3–19, and they probably describe the people who lived in Jerusalem after the return.","The porters or gatekeepers (9:17–27) did a serious job: they “lodged round about the house of God” and opened it every morning (9:27), guarding the rooms, vessels, and treasures. Chronicles traces their office back to David and Samuel the seer (9:22). The chapter ends by repeating Saul’s family line, which leads straight into the story of Saul’s death in chapter 10."]},
"1chr.10":{title:"Saul died for his transgression",year:"About 1010 BC",
 sum:"The Philistines defeat Israel on Mount Gilboa, and Saul’s sons are killed—Saul, badly wounded, asks his armorbearer to kill him, and when he refuses Saul falls on his own sword—The Philistines display Saul’s body and armor, but the men of Jabesh-gilead rescue and bury it—“Saul died for his transgression,” and the kingdom turns to David.",
 bg:["This chapter retells 1 Samuel 31 almost word for word, but it ends with something the Samuel version leaves out: a plain explanation (10:13–14). Saul died because he did not keep the word of the LORD and sought a medium (1 Samuel 28), and “therefore [the LORD] turned the kingdom unto David the son of Jesse.” Chronicles skips Saul’s reign and David’s years of exile, which Samuel covers, and moves right to David as king.","The Philistines hung Saul’s armor in the house of their gods and fastened his head in the temple of Dagon (10:9–10); 1 Samuel 31:10 says his body was fastened to the wall of Beth-shan. Brave men of Jabesh-gilead took the bodies and buried them, remembering how Saul had once saved their city (1 Samuel 11)."]},
"1chr.11":{title:"We are thy bone and thy flesh",year:"About 1010–970 BC",
 sum:"All Israel gathers to David at Hebron, anoints him king, and he takes Jerusalem, the city of David—Joab leads the assault and becomes chief—David’s mighty men are named, and three break through the Philistine lines for water from Bethlehem’s well—David pours the water out to the LORD, refusing to drink the blood of these men.",
 bg:["Verses 1–9 parallel 2 Samuel 5:1–10, and verses 10–47 parallel the list of heroes in 2 Samuel 23:8–39. Chronicles leaves out David’s seven years as king of Judah alone in Hebron and begins with all Israel joining him, which suits the writer’s picture of a united people around one king. Jerusalem (here still called Jebus, 11:4) was a Jebusite stronghold that David captured and made his capital.","David’s refusal to drink the water (11:17–19) shows how he treated sacrifice. Because the three men had risked their lives, he treated the water as too holy to drink and poured it out “to the LORD.” The writer notes at the start that the mighty men “strengthened themselves with him… to make him king, according to the word of the LORD” (11:10)."]},
"1chr.12":{title:"Thine are we, David, and on thy side",year:"About 1010–970 BC",
 sum:"Warriors from Benjamin, Gad, and Manasseh join David at Ziklag and in the wilderness stronghold—Amasai declares, “Thine are we, David… for thy God helpeth thee”—Men from every tribe come armed to Hebron, Issachar’s men having “understanding of the times”—All Israel feasts for three days, “of one heart to make David king.”",
 bg:["This chapter has no parallel in Samuel or Kings. It shows David’s support growing even while Saul was alive, as skilled men, including left-handed slingers and archers from Saul’s own tribe (12:2), came to him at Ziklag. Gad’s warriors had “whose faces were like the faces of lions” and were “swift as the roes upon the mountains” (12:8).","The Issachar men “that had understanding of the times, to know what Israel ought to do” (12:32) are quoted often, though the text does not say how they knew it. The chapter ends with a feast at Hebron (12:38–40) and the phrase “of one heart,” a theme of unity that Chronicles uses to describe the best days of the kingdom."]},
"1chr.13":{title:"The LORD had made a breach upon Uzza",year:"About 1010–970 BC",
 sum:"David consults the leaders and proposes to bring the ark of God from Kirjath-jearim—The ark is carried on a new cart with music and rejoicing—When the oxen stumble, Uzza puts out his hand to steady the ark and dies—David is displeased and afraid, and the place is called Perez-uzza—The ark rests three months in the house of Obed-edom, whom the LORD blesses.",
 bg:["The ark of the covenant, the sacred chest in the tabernacle, had been out of the central worship since the Philistines captured it (1 Samuel 4–7), and David wanted it in Jerusalem. This chapter parallels 2 Samuel 6:1–11. The law said only Levites were to carry the ark on poles (Numbers 4:15; 7:9), and putting it on a cart was the problem, as David himself admits in 15:13.","Perez-uzza (13:11) means “breach of Uzza” or “breaking out against Uzza.” Obed-edom the Gittite, who kept the ark for three months and was blessed, may have been from Gath or a Levite living there (see 15:18, 24, and 26:4–5), and his name means “servant of Edom.” Scholars differ on his background."]},
"1chr.14":{title:"God hath broken in upon mine enemies",year:"About 1010–970 BC",
 sum:"Hiram of Tyre sends cedar and craftsmen to build David a house—David’s family grows, and he knows the LORD has established his kingdom—The Philistines come up, and David defeats them at Baal-perazim—They return, and God tells David to wait for “a sound of going in the tops of the mulberry trees”—The fame of David goes into all lands.",
 bg:["The chapter parallels 2 Samuel 5:11–25. Verses 1–7 show David’s kingdom growing in strength and in standing among his neighbors, and verse 2 says it was “lifted up on high, because of his people Israel.” In both victories David first asks God what to do (14:10, 14), a habit that stands in contrast with Saul, who “inquired not of the LORD” (10:14).","Baal-perazim means “lord of breakings” or “master of the breakthrough.” David said God had “broken in upon mine enemies… like the breaking forth of waters” (14:11), a rushing flood of a kind well known in the dry hills near Jerusalem. Verse 12 says David ordered the gods the Philistines left behind to be burned; 2 Samuel 5:21 says David and his men carried them off."]},
"1chr.15":{title:"None ought to carry the ark of God but the Levites",year:"About 1010–970 BC",
 sum:"David prepares a tent for the ark and gathers the Levites to sanctify themselves—He admits they did not seek God “after the due order” the first time—Levites carry the ark on their shoulders, with singers and instruments leading—The ark comes into the city of David with shouting and music—Michal despises David in her heart.",
 bg:["After the sad event of chapter 13, David studied the law and did it the way God had instructed. The priests and Levites made themselves holy (15:12, 14), and the Levites bore the ark “upon their shoulders with the staves thereon, as Moses commanded” (15:15; Exodus 25:14). This chapter has more detail than 2 Samuel 6:12–23 about who carried and who sang.","Verse 29 mentions Michal watching David dance; Samuel (2 Samuel 6:16–23) tells more of her reaction. Chronicles emphasizes the Levites’ part, naming leaders such as Kenaniah, who “instructed about the song” (15:22). The order matters here: David has not changed what he wants, but now seeks it in the right way."]},
"1chr.16":{title:"O give thanks unto the LORD; for he is good",year:"About 1010–970 BC",
 sum:"The ark is placed in its tent, offerings are made, and David blesses the people and shares out bread—Levites are appointed to minister, record, thank, and praise—David’s psalm calls Israel to remember his marvelous works and covenant with Abraham, Isaac, and Jacob—“Worship the LORD in the beauty of holiness”—Priests and singers are assigned to Jerusalem and Gibeon.",
 bg:["David’s psalm (16:8–36) is a mix of psalms found later in the Psalter: verses 8–22 are close to Psalm 105:1–15, verses 23–33 to Psalm 96, and verses 34–36 to Psalm 106:1, 47–48. It is not in 2 Samuel 6. Chronicles may be showing it as the first public form of what became the hymn tradition of the temple.","The refrain “for his mercy endureth for ever” (16:34, and again in 16:41) appears in many psalms as a response sung by the people. Verses 37–42 say Asaph and others served before the ark in Jerusalem while Zadok and the priests served the tabernacle at Gibeon, since the ark and the older tabernacle were in two places for a time (see 21:29)."]},
"1chr.17":{title:"Thou shalt not build me an house",year:"About 1010–970 BC",
 sum:"David, living in a house of cedar, wants to build a house for the ark—Nathan first says to go ahead, then the word of God comes that night saying David shall not build it—God promises to make David a house, to raise up a son who will build the temple, and to establish his throne for ever—David sits before the LORD and offers a prayer of humble thanksgiving.",
 bg:["This chapter parallels 2 Samuel 7 and is called the Davidic covenant. The word “house” has two meanings: David wants to build God a house (a temple), but God says he will build David a house (a dynasty, a family line). Nathan’s first answer shows that even a prophet could speak before he had heard from the LORD.","The promise reaches beyond Solomon: “I will settle him in mine house and in my kingdom for ever: and his throne shall be established for evermore” (17:14). Compare 2 Samuel 7:14, which also mentions correction for sin; Chronicles leaves that out and stresses the promise of mercy. Latter-day Saints, like other Christians, see Jesus Christ as the heir of David’s throne (see Luke 1:32–33)."]},
"1chr.18":{title:"David reigned over all Israel",year:"About 1010–970 BC",
 sum:"David defeats the Philistines, Moab, and Hadarezer of Zobah, and takes tribute from Syria—King Tou of Hamath sends his son with gifts—David dedicates the silver, gold, and brass to the LORD, and the LORD preserves him wherever he goes—David reigns over all Israel with judgment and justice, and his officers are listed.",
 bg:["The chapter closely follows 2 Samuel 8, showing how David’s kingdom grew from a small tribal state into a regional power, reaching toward the Euphrates in the north and taking Edom in the south. Verse 8 says that Solomon later used the brass from Tibhath and Chun to make the temple’s bronze sea, pillars, and vessels, a detail found only here.","“So David reigned over all Israel, and executed judgment and justice among all his people” (18:14). That is the writer’s summary of what makes a good king. The list of officers at the end (18:15–17) names Joab as commander, Zadok and Abimelech (Ahimelech) as priests, and Benaiah over the king’s guard, the Cherethites and Pelethites."]},
"1chr.19":{title:"Let the LORD do that which is good in his sight",year:"About 1010–970 BC",
 sum:"David sends messengers to comfort Hanun, king of Ammon, on his father’s death—Hanun’s princes suspect spies, and he shaves the men’s beards and cuts off their garments—Ammon hires Syrian chariots and soldiers—Joab and Abishai divide the army, and Joab says, “Be of good courage… and let the LORD do that which is good”—Israel defeats the Syrians, who make peace with David.",
 bg:["This chapter parallels 2 Samuel 10. David’s kindness was an ordinary diplomatic custom, but Hanun treated it as a scheme. Cutting half the beard and the clothes was a deliberate public shame to men who represented a king; in that world, beards were a sign of manhood and dignity, and David told the men to wait in Jericho until they grew back (19:5).","Hanun’s act led to war because the insult was an act against David himself. Joab’s words before battle (19:13) show a faith that is not just confidence in strength: he does his best and then leaves the result with the LORD."]},
"1chr.20":{title:"Joab smote Rabbah, and destroyed it",year:"About 1010–970 BC",
 sum:"In the spring Joab leads the army against Ammon and besieges Rabbah, while David stays at Jerusalem—Joab destroys the city, and David takes the king’s crown of gold and precious stones and much spoil—Giants of the Philistines are slain at Gezer and Gath by David’s men.",
 bg:["The chapter retells 2 Samuel 11:1 and 12:26–31, and then 2 Samuel 21:18–22. It leaves out the story of David, Bathsheba, and Uriah that lies between these verses (2 Samuel 11–12), though the words “David tarried at Jerusalem” (20:1) hint at where it happened. The writer’s purpose is not to tell everything about David but to present his reign as a model for the temple’s builders.","Verse 5 says Elhanan killed Lahmi, brother of Goliath, while 2 Samuel 21:19 reads as if Elhanan killed Goliath himself. Scholars explain the difference by pointing to how the Hebrew was copied; the text does not settle it. These giants were descendants of a people called the Rephaim, with a spear shaft “like a weaver’s beam,” the same phrase used of Goliath in 1 Samuel 17:7."]},
"1chr.21":{title:"Satan stood up against Israel",year:"About 1010–970 BC",
 sum:"Satan provokes David to number Israel, and Joab protests but obeys—God is displeased, and David confesses, “I have done very foolishly”—David chooses to “fall now into the hand of the LORD; for very great are his mercies,” and pestilence falls on Israel—The angel stops at the threshing floor of Ornan, and David buys it for the full price—He builds an altar, and fire answers from heaven.",
 bg:["This chapter parallels 2 Samuel 24 with some differences. Samuel says the anger of the LORD moved David, while Chronicles names Satan. Samuel gives 800,000 men for Israel and 500,000 for Judah, but Chronicles has 1,100,000 and 470,000 (21:5). Samuel says David paid 50 shekels of silver for the threshing floor and oxen, but here he pays 600 shekels of gold for the whole place (21:25). Scholars explain these differences in different ways, and the text itself does not explain them.","Ornan is a form of the name Araunah in Samuel; both name the Jebusite who owned the threshing floor. David’s words, “I will not take that which is thine for the LORD, nor offer burnt offerings without cost” (21:24), express a principle of sacrifice. This hill is where the temple was built, which 2 Chronicles 3:1 identifies with Mount Moriah, the place connected to Abraham’s offering of Isaac in Genesis 22:2."]},
"1chr.22":{title:"A man of rest",year:"About 1010–970 BC",
 sum:"David declares that this is the site for the house of the LORD and gathers materials—He tells Solomon the LORD said, “Thou hast shed blood abundantly… thou shalt not build an house unto my name”—A son shall be born who will be a man of rest, and his name shall be Solomon—David charges Solomon to be strong and build, and charges the princes to help.",
 bg:["Chapters 22 and 28–29 are Chronicles’ account of David’s preparation for the temple; it has no parallel in Samuel or Kings. David could not build because he had been a man of war, and he gathered stone, iron, bronze, and cedar, along with skilled workmen, so Solomon would have what he needed. David’s gift figures (22:14) are very large, and scholars note that the exact weight of the “talents” is uncertain.","“Solomon” is related to the Hebrew shalom, “peace,” and the text links his name to “peace and quietness” (22:9). The word translated “a man of rest” is also from a root meaning “rest” or “quiet.” David’s charge to Solomon, “Be strong, and of good courage; dread not, nor be dismayed” (22:13), after telling him to obey the statutes of Moses,, is much like Moses’ words to Joshua in Deuteronomy 31:6–8."]},
"1chr.23":{title:"The Levites were numbered from twenty years old and above",year:"About 1010–970 BC",
 sum:"David, old and full of days, makes Solomon king—The Levites are counted and assigned: some to the work of the house, some as officers and judges, some as porters, some as singers—The families of Gershon, Kohath, and Merari are listed—The Levites no longer need to carry the tabernacle, and now serve in the temple, in the courts, and in morning and evening praise.",
 bg:["Because the temple would stand in one place, the Levites’ old job of carrying the tabernacle and its furniture (Numbers 4) was over, and David reorganized their work (23:25–26). They were to help the priests, care for the rooms and offerings, keep order, and lead praise “every morning… and likewise at even” (23:30). Verse 27 says the age was lowered from thirty (23:3, as in Numbers 4:3) to twenty, which suggests the work now needed more hands.","The totals are big: 38,000 Levites, of whom 24,000 oversaw the work, 6,000 were officers and judges, 4,000 were porters, and 4,000 praised with instruments (23:3–5). The chapter shows that the temple was served not just by priests but by a whole community with many kinds of work, including music and gatekeeping."]},
"1chr.24":{title:"The divisions of the sons of Aaron",year:"About 1010–970 BC",
 sum:"Nadab and Abihu die childless, leaving Eleazar and Ithamar to serve as priests—David, with Zadok and Ahimelech, divides the priests into twenty-four courses by lot—Sixteen go to Eleazar’s house and eight to Ithamar’s—The remaining Levites are also listed by lot, “the principal fathers over against their younger brethren.”",
 bg:["With so many priests, David arranged them into twenty-four courses so each family served at the temple in turn (later Jewish tradition says a week at a time). Using lots (24:5, 31) was a way to make the assignments impartial and let the LORD decide. The writer says Nadab and Abihu “died before their father” (24:2), a reference to Leviticus 10.","Abijah’s course was the eighth (24:10). Luke 1:5 says Zacharias, the father of John the Baptist, belonged to “the course of Abia,” which shows the system was still in use a thousand years later. The chapter ends with the Levites cast by lot with “the principal fathers over against their younger brethren” (24:31), meaning the head of a family and his youngest relative were treated alike."]},
"1chr.25":{title:"Who should prophesy with harps, with psalteries, and with cymbals",year:"About 1010–970 BC",
 sum:"David and the captains set apart the sons of Asaph, Heman, and Jeduthun to prophesy with harps, psalteries, and cymbals—Their sons are named under their fathers’ direction—All are trained in the songs of the LORD, 288 in number—Twenty-four courses are chosen by lot, small and great, teacher and scholar alike.",
 bg:["Music here is called prophesying (25:1–3). Asaph, Heman, and Jeduthun, who also appear in 6:31–47 and 2 Chronicles 5:12, led singers and players who gave thanks and praise to the LORD, and verse 5 calls Heman “the king’s seer in the words of God.” The word “prophesy” seems to mean speaking or singing under inspiration, though the text does not describe exactly how.","There were twenty-four courses of singers, matching the twenty-four courses of priests in chapter 24, and the lots were cast “as well the small as the great, the teacher as the scholar” (25:8). The chapter says Heman had fourteen sons and three daughters, all under their father’s hand for song in the house of the LORD (25:5–6)."]},
"1chr.26":{title:"For God blessed him",year:"About 1010–970 BC",
 sum:"The gatekeepers are divided among the Korhites, including Meshelemiah’s sons, and Obed-edom’s family, “for God blessed him”—Each gate is assigned by lot, young and old—Other Levites keep the treasuries and the dedicated things, such as gifts from Samuel, Saul, Abner, and Joab—Officers and judges are appointed outside the temple for the LORD’s business and the king’s.",
 bg:["Obed-edom (26:4–8) is the man whose house sheltered the ark for three months (13:14). Here his eight sons and many descendants served as gatekeepers, and “God blessed him” (26:5) points back to that earlier time. His name means “servant of Edom,” and the text does not explain the link, although in 15:18 he is listed among the Levites.","The “dedicated things” (26:20, 26–28) were spoils of war and gifts that had been set apart for the LORD’s house, and Levites guarded them. Some of this treasure had been given long before the temple was built, by Samuel, Saul, Abner, and Joab. Verses 29–32 show Levites also served as officers and judges across Israel, “for every matter pertaining to God, and affairs of the king.”"]},
"1chr.27":{title:"The children of Israel after their number",year:"About 1010–970 BC",
 sum:"Twelve army divisions of 24,000 each serve month by month—Twelve tribal leaders are named, and David does not count those under twenty because the LORD promised to increase Israel like the stars—Joab begins a count but does not finish it, for wrath fell on Israel—David’s stewards of treasuries, fields, vineyards, and flocks are named, along with his counselors.",
 bg:["The monthly rotation of 24,000 men (27:1–15) gave David a standing force of 288,000 across the year, with each division serving one month and then going home, so the kingdom was always ready without keeping the whole army from their farms. Each division’s commander, such as Jashobeam, Dodai, and Benaiah, was one of the mighty men from chapter 11.","Verses 23–24 refer back to the census in chapter 21: David did not count the men under twenty, trusting God’s promise to make Israel as many as the stars (see Genesis 15:5), and the count was not entered in the official record. The chapter ends with Ahithophel as David’s counselor (27:33), who later turned against him (2 Samuel 15–17), and with Hushai the Archite, “the king’s companion.”"]},
"1chr.28":{title:"Be strong and of good courage, and do it",year:"About 1010–970 BC",
 sum:"David assembles Israel’s leaders and says he wanted to build a house of rest for the ark, but God said, “Thou shalt not build an house for my name”—God chose Judah, David’s house, and Solomon—David charges Solomon to “know thou the God of thy father, and serve him with a perfect heart”—He gives him the pattern of the temple and its vessels, “all this… by his hand upon me”—“Be strong and of good courage, and do it.”",
 bg:["David says that God had shown him the plan in writing: “All this… the LORD made me understand in writing by his hand upon me, even all the works of this pattern” (28:19). The pattern in this chapter covers the temple’s porch, rooms, treasuries, and the place of the mercy seat, much as Moses received a pattern for the tabernacle (Exodus 25:9, 40). The text does not say how David received the plan, only that it came from the LORD.","The promise to Solomon is conditional: “if thou seek him, he will be found of thee; but if thou forsake him, he will cast thee off for ever” (28:9). The great Davidic promise of chapter 17 is a gift of God, but each king still had to choose faithfulness. “The LORD searcheth all hearts” (28:9) shows that David’s concern was the heart as well as the building."]},
"1chr.29":{title:"Of thine own have we given thee",year:"About 1010–970 BC",
 sum:"David tells the congregation the work is great and gives his own treasure to the house of the LORD—The leaders offer willingly, and the people rejoice—David’s prayer: “Thine, O LORD, is the greatness, and the power, and the glory”—Solomon is made king a second time and anointed, and David dies in a good old age.",
 bg:["David’s prayer (29:10–19) is one of the great prayers of the Bible. It praises God as the source of all wealth and power, and it confesses, “Who am I… that we should be able to offer so willingly after this sort? for all things come of thee, and of thine own have we given thee” (29:14). Many Christians know verse 11 from the ending of the Lord’s Prayer in some Bible manuscripts, though the oldest manuscripts of Matthew 6 do not include it, but the Book of Mormon’s version of the prayer includes it (3 Nephi 13:13).","David’s prayer says, “we are strangers before thee, and sojourners, as were all our fathers” (29:15), a reminder that even Israel’s land and temple were gifts. The second anointing of Solomon (29:22) refers to the public ceremony after the quieter one in 1 Kings 1:32–40, and Zadok is anointed as priest. Verse 29 names three earlier works, the records of Samuel, Nathan, and Gad, from which Chronicles drew its account of David."]},
"2chr.1":{title:"Give me now wisdom and knowledge",year:"About 970–931 BC",
 sum:"Solomon and the assembly worship at the tabernacle in Gibeon, where he offers a thousand burnt offerings—God appears and says, “Ask what I shall give thee”—Solomon asks for “wisdom and knowledge” to judge the people—God grants these and adds riches and honor—Solomon gathers chariots and horsemen and trades in horses and silver.",
 bg:["Second Chronicles retells the story of Judah’s kings, with the temple at the center, and it often parallels 1–2 Kings. This chapter parallels 1 Kings 3:4–15 and 10:26–29. Solomon’s name is related to the Hebrew shalom, “peace,” and 1 Chronicles 22:9 says he was to be a man of rest and peace.","The tabernacle Moses built was still at Gibeon (1:3–6), while the ark of the covenant was in Jerusalem (1:4). Chronicles stresses that Solomon went to the proper altar and the proper tent to worship, and that his request for wisdom, not riches, pleased the Lord."]},
"2chr.2":{title:"The house which I build is great",year:"About 970–931 BC",
 sum:"Solomon determines to build a house for the name of the LORD—He sends to Huram king of Tyre for cedar, skilled workmen and craftsmen—“The house which I build is great: for great is our God above all gods”—Huram agrees and sends timber by sea and a skilled man named Huram-abi—Solomon numbers the strangers in the land to carry burdens and hew stone.",
 bg:["This chapter parallels 1 Kings 5. Chronicles calls the Phoenician king Huram, while Kings calls him Hiram; both are forms of the same name. Tyre was a great seafaring city on the coast of what is now Lebanon, famous for its cedar, craftsmen and trade.","The cedars were cut in Lebanon and floated down the coast to Joppa, where Solomon’s workers took them up to Jerusalem (2:16). Solomon admits that no house can truly contain God (2:6), a thought he develops in his prayer in chapter 6."]},
"2chr.3":{title:"Solomon began to build the house of the LORD at Jerusalem in mount Moriah",year:"About 970–931 BC",
 sum:"Solomon begins the temple on Mount Moriah, on the threshing floor of Ornan—The dimensions of the house and the porch are given—The most holy place is overlaid with fine gold and two great cherubim spread their wings—The veil is made of blue, purple and crimson—Two pillars stand before the house, named Jachin and Boaz.",
 bg:["Mount Moriah is the same region where Abraham was told to offer Isaac (Genesis 22:2). Chronicles alone connects the temple site with David’s purchase of Ornan’s (Araunah’s) threshing floor (see 2 Samuel 24:18–25; 1 Chronicles 21–22). Parallels are in 1 Kings 6–7.","The two bronze pillars were named Jachin and Boaz (3:17). Jachin probably means “he establishes”; the meaning of Boaz is less certain, and is often explained as “in him is strength.” Together they were a reminder that the Lord is the one who makes his house and people stand."]},
"2chr.4":{title:"A molten sea of ten cubits from brim to brim",year:"About 970–931 BC",
 sum:"Solomon makes the brass altar, the great molten sea resting on twelve oxen, and ten lavers for washing—He makes ten golden candlesticks and tables, and the courts and doors—Huram finishes the pillars, pots, shovels and basins—All the vessels are of brass too many to weigh, and the golden vessels are set in the house.",
 bg:["This chapter parallels 1 Kings 7:23–51. The “molten sea” was a huge bronze basin, about fifteen feet across, where the priests washed (4:6). Chronicles explains its purpose; Kings only describes its size.","The sea stood on twelve oxen, three facing each direction (4:4), which many readers understand to represent the twelve tribes. Latter-day Saint temples have baptismal fonts resting on twelve oxen in the same pattern."]},
"2chr.5":{title:"The house was filled with a cloud, even the house of the LORD",year:"About 970–931 BC",
 sum:"Solomon brings in the silver, gold and vessels his father David had dedicated—The priests carry the ark into the most holy place under the wings of the cherubim—Singers and trumpeters praise God as with one voice, “for he is good; for his mercy endureth for ever”—The house is filled with a cloud, and the priests cannot stand to minister because of the glory of God.",
 bg:["This chapter parallels 1 Kings 8:1–11. The ark was carried into the temple at the feast in the seventh month (5:3), which is probably the Feast of Tabernacles. Inside the ark were only the two tablets of stone that Moses had put there at Horeb (5:10).","The cloud of the glory of the LORD recalls the cloud that filled the tabernacle in Moses’ day (Exodus 40:34–35). Chronicles adds the detail of the singers and instruments joining together “as one,” showing that music is part of worship in the temple."]},
"2chr.6":{title:"Will God in very deed dwell with men on the earth?",year:"About 970–931 BC",
 sum:"Solomon blesses the people and thanks God for keeping his promise to David—He kneels before the congregation and asks, “Will God in very deed dwell with men on the earth?”—He prays that God will hear the people’s prayers made toward this house, in wrongs, defeat, drought and as strangers—He closes with “Now therefore arise, O LORD God, into thy resting place.”",
 bg:["This long prayer parallels 1 Kings 8:12–53. Solomon names several situations, such as wrongs between neighbors, defeat in war, famine, and even a stranger from a far country, and in each asks God to hear “from heaven” when people pray toward this house. The temple is a place of prayer for all nations.","Verses 41–42 are not found in Kings; they echo Psalm 132:8–10. Latter-day Saints also know a dedicatory prayer for a temple, given by revelation at the Kirtland Temple in 1836 (Doctrine and Covenants 109)."]},
"2chr.7":{title:"If my people, which are called by my name, shall humble themselves",year:"About 970–931 BC",
 sum:"Fire comes down from heaven to consume the sacrifices, and the glory of the LORD fills the house—All the people bow and praise God—Solomon and Israel keep a dedication feast for seven days and the Feast of Tabernacles for seven more—The LORD appears by night: “If my people … shall humble themselves, and pray, and seek my face … then will I hear from heaven”—The promise is conditional: if they forsake him, the house will become a byword.",
 bg:["This chapter parallels 1 Kings 8:62–9:9, but verses 1–3 (the fire from heaven) and 13–15 (the promise of 7:14) are found only in Chronicles. The fire echoes the fire that consumed the sacrifices at the tabernacle’s dedication (Leviticus 9:24).","The Lord’s promise in 7:14 was given to Israel as a nation, as a path of healing after drought, locusts or plague (7:13). The pattern it teaches is plain: humble yourselves, pray, seek the Lord’s face and turn from wickedness, and he will hear and forgive."]},
"2chr.8":{title:"He built Tadmor in the wilderness",year:"About 970–931 BC",
 sum:"After twenty years, Solomon builds up the cities Huram gave him, Tadmor and store cities in Hamath—He puts the remaining Canaanite peoples to forced labor, though the Israelites are not made slaves—He moves Pharaoh’s daughter out of the city of David—Offerings are kept according to Moses and David’s order of priests and Levites—Solomon’s ships bring gold from Ophir.",
 bg:["This chapter parallels 1 Kings 9:10–28. Tadmor is traditionally identified with the later desert city of Palmyra in Syria, though 1 Kings 9:18 has “Tamar,” and scholars debate the original reading. Solomon’s building shows how large and wealthy the kingdom had become.","Solomon moved Pharaoh’s daughter because the places where the ark had been were holy (8:11). Chronicles takes care to show that he kept the temple worship as David had commanded, which is a main theme of the book."]},
"2chr.9":{title:"The one half of the greatness of thy wisdom was not told me",year:"About 970–931 BC",
 sum:"The queen of Sheba comes to Jerusalem with spices, gold and precious stones to test Solomon with hard questions—She sees his wisdom, house, table and burnt offerings, and says “the one half … was not told me”—Solomon gives her all she desires—His gold, ivory and navy of Tarshish make him richer than all kings—Solomon reigns forty years and sleeps with his fathers.",
 bg:["This chapter parallels 1 Kings 10 and 11:41–43. Sheba was probably in southwest Arabia (modern Yemen), a land famous for spices and incense. Jesus later called her “the queen of the south” and said she would rise up in judgment because she came from far to hear Solomon’s wisdom, while his hearers did not listen to one greater than Solomon (Matthew 12:42).","Solomon’s wealth is described in huge numbers: 666 talents of gold in a year (9:13). Chronicles does not repeat the account in 1 Kings 11 of Solomon’s foreign wives and the idolatry that followed, since it focuses on the temple and the line of David."]},
"2chr.10":{title:"I will put more to your yoke",year:"About 931–913 BC",
 sum:"Israel gathers at Shechem to make Rehoboam king and asks him to lighten the heavy yoke Solomon put on them—The old men advise gentleness, but the young men say, “My little finger shall be thicker than my father’s loins”—Rehoboam answers harshly—Israel cries, “To your tents, O Israel,” and Jeroboam is made king of the ten tribes.",
 bg:["This chapter parallels 1 Kings 12:1–19. Shechem was an old, central city in the hill country of Ephraim where Israel had gathered to renew covenants (Joshua 24). Solomon’s heavy taxes and forced labor were the cause of the complaint, and Rehoboam’s foolish answer broke the kingdom in two about 931 BC.","The text says plainly that the people’s turning was “of God” (10:15), fulfilling what the prophet Ahijah had said (1 Kings 11:29–39). Jeroboam’s name is often explained as “the people contend,” though the meaning is debated."]},
"2chr.11":{title:"For three years they walked in the way of David and Solomon",year:"About 931–913 BC",
 sum:"Rehoboam gathers an army, but the prophet Shemaiah tells him not to fight his brethren, for “this thing is done of me”—He fortifies cities in Judah and Benjamin—Priests and Levites leave the north because Jeroboam has set up his own priests and calves, and come to Jerusalem—Rehoboam takes many wives, loving Maachah above them all.",
 bg:["Most of this chapter is found only in Chronicles, with a short parallel in 1 Kings 12:21–24. It shows how the temple kept Judah strong: priests, Levites and faithful people from all tribes came south “such as set their hearts to seek the LORD” (11:16).","Jeroboam had set up golden calves at Bethel and Dan and made priests who were not Levites (1 Kings 12:28–31). Rehoboam’s family shows a king whose household grew large, with eighteen wives, sixty concubines, and many sons and daughters (11:21)."]},
"2chr.12":{title:"The princes of Israel and the king humbled themselves",year:"About 931–913 BC",
 sum:"When Rehoboam is strong he forsakes the law of the LORD, and Shishak king of Egypt comes against Jerusalem with a great army—The prophet Shemaiah says, “Ye have forsaken me, and therefore have I also left you”—The king and princes humble themselves, and the LORD grants some deliverance—Shishak takes the temple treasures, and Rehoboam replaces the gold shields with brass.",
 bg:["This chapter parallels 1 Kings 14:21–31. Shishak is the Pharaoh Shoshenq I, and a list of cities he claimed to conquer appears on a wall of the temple at Karnak in Egypt, which is one of the earliest firm links between the Bible and an outside written source. His raid took place about 925 BC.","Chronicles adds the prophet’s message and Rehoboam’s repentance (12:5–8, 12). Notice the pattern: when the king and his people humbled themselves, “the wrath of the LORD turned from him” (12:12). The brass shields suggest a kingdom that had lost much of its glory."]},
"2chr.13":{title:"God himself is with us for our captain",year:"About 913–911 BC",
 sum:"Abijah begins to reign over Judah and goes to war with Jeroboam—From Mount Zemaraim he reminds Israel that the LORD gave the kingdom to David by “a covenant of salt”—He says Judah has kept the LORD’s priests and offerings, while Jeroboam has made golden calves—Judah cries to the LORD, and God smites Jeroboam, giving Judah a great victory.",
 bg:["This chapter parallels 1 Kings 15:1–8, but Kings has only a short, negative summary of Abijam (the same king), while Chronicles preserves this speech and victory. A “covenant of salt” (13:5) means a lasting, binding agreement; salt was used in sacrifices and in sharing meals to seal promises (Numbers 18:19).","The numbers of soldiers in verses 3 and 17 (400,000 against 800,000, with 500,000 killed) are very large, and scholars discuss whether they are exact or a way of showing how great the victory was. The main point of the story is clear: Judah won because they “relied upon the LORD God of their fathers” (13:18)."]},
"2chr.14":{title:"LORD, it is nothing with thee to help, whether with many, or with them that have no power",year:"About 911–870 BC",
 sum:"Asa does what is good and right, removes the altars of strange gods and commands Judah to seek the LORD—The land has rest, and Asa builds fortified cities—Zerah the Ethiopian comes with an army of a million—Asa cries to the LORD, “Help us, O LORD our God; for we rest on thee”—God smites the Ethiopians, and Judah takes great spoil.",
 bg:["Chronicles gives much more of King Asa’s story than 1 Kings 15:9–24 does. The “Ethiopian” Zerah (Hebrew Cushite) came from Cush, south of Egypt; the battle was at Mareshah, southwest of Jerusalem. His army’s size of “a thousand thousand” may be an exaggeration, or a large conventional number.","Asa’s prayer is a model of trust: he did not say the odds were good, but that for God it makes no difference whether he helps the mighty or the weak (14:11). The chapter says “the land was quiet” because Asa had sought the LORD (14:6–7)."]},
"2chr.15":{title:"The LORD is with you, while ye be with him",year:"About 911–870 BC",
 sum:"The Spirit of God comes on Azariah the son of Oded, who says, “The LORD is with you, while ye be with him; and if ye seek him, he will be found of you”—Asa takes courage and removes idols from Judah, Benjamin and Ephraim—The people covenant to seek the LORD with all their heart—Asa removes Maachah his mother from being queen because of her idol—There is no war until the thirty-fifth year.",
 bg:["This chapter parallels 1 Kings 15:9–15. The prophetic word through Azariah is found only in Chronicles. His sermon looks back on the dark times of the judges, when Israel had “without the true God, and without a teaching priest” (15:3), and it promises that seeking the Lord will bring help.","The covenant took place in the third month in Asa’s fifteenth year, perhaps at the Feast of Weeks (15:10). Asa’s mother, Maachah, probably held the title of “queen mother,” a position of great power; Asa’s willingness to remove her showed his commitment (15:16)."]},
"2chr.16":{title:"The eyes of the LORD run to and fro throughout the whole earth",year:"About 911–870 BC",
 sum:"Baasha of Israel fortifies Ramah, so Asa takes silver and gold from the temple to hire Ben-hadad of Syria—Hanani the seer says, “Herein thou hast done foolishly,” and that “the eyes of the LORD run to and fro … to shew himself strong in the behalf of them whose heart is perfect toward him”—Asa puts Hanani in prison—Asa is diseased in his feet, and “yet in his disease he sought not to the LORD, but to the physicians.”",
 bg:["This chapter parallels 1 Kings 15:16–24, but the prophet’s warning, the imprisonment of Hanani and the note about Asa’s disease are found only in Chronicles. Hanani may be the father of the prophet Jehu (see 19:2 and 1 Kings 16:1). Asa’s earlier prayer (14:11) trusted God against an army of a million; here he trusts a foreign king.","The point is not that doctors are wrong. It is that Asa’s heart had turned from the Lord, who is always searching for those who trust him “whose heart is perfect toward him.” The story is a sobering end to a mostly good reign."]},
"2chr.17":{title:"His heart was lifted up in the ways of the LORD",year:"About 870–848 BC",
 sum:"Jehoshaphat strengthens Judah and puts garrisons in the cities—The LORD is with him because he walks in David’s early ways and seeks the God of his father—He sends princes, Levites and priests with “the book of the law” to teach throughout Judah—Fear of the LORD falls on the nations, and the Philistines and Arabians bring tribute—Jehoshaphat grows great, with captains and mighty men.",
 bg:["Jehoshaphat’s name means “the LORD judges” (or “has judged”), and his reign is a time of teaching and justice. Only Chronicles tells of the travelling teachers (17:7–9); Kings has little about Jehoshaphat except 1 Kings 15:24; 22:41–50.","Sending teachers with the book of the law through every city was a kind of early public religious education. The chapter ties Judah’s peace and wealth to knowing the law of the Lord. “Lifted up” in verse 6 is positive here, meaning his heart was encouraged and boldly devoted."]},
"2chr.18":{title:"I saw the LORD sitting upon his throne",year:"About 870–848 BC",
 sum:"Jehoshaphat joins Ahab king of Israel against Ramoth-gilead—Four hundred prophets promise victory, but Micaiah says, “I saw the LORD sitting upon his throne, and all the host of heaven standing”—A lying spirit is in the mouth of Ahab’s prophets—Micaiah is imprisoned—Ahab is hit by an arrow “at a venture” and dies.",
 bg:["This chapter parallels 1 Kings 22:1–40. Micaiah’s name is usually explained as “Who is like the LORD?” In his vision (18:18–22) the LORD allows a spirit to speak falsehood through Ahab’s prophets, since Ahab refused to listen to truth. The Bible does not explain every detail of how this works.","Jehoshaphat was Judah’s good king, but he joined himself by marriage to Ahab’s family, which later brought trouble (see 2 Chronicles 21:6; 22:2–3). A random arrow found Ahab even though he had disguised himself, and he died at evening, as Micaiah had said."]},
"2chr.19":{title:"Take heed what ye do: for ye judge not for man, but for the LORD",year:"About 870–848 BC",
 sum:"Jehoshaphat returns to Jerusalem, where Jehu the seer rebukes him for helping the ungodly, yet says “there are good things found in thee”—The king goes out among the people and brings them back to the LORD—He appoints judges in every city and tells them there is no iniquity or respect of persons with the LORD—He sets up a court in Jerusalem and says, “Deal courageously.”",
 bg:["This chapter has no parallel in Kings. It shows Jehoshaphat’s reforms of the courts: local judges in the fortified cities and a higher court of priests, Levites and family heads in Jerusalem (19:8). Justice, including a ban on bribes, is said to be a matter of the fear of the LORD.","Jehu was the son of Hanani, the seer who had been imprisoned under Asa (16:7–10). The chapter reminds us that rebuke and encouragement can come together: Jehu told the king both what he had done wrong and what was good in him."]},
"2chr.20":{title:"The battle is not yours, but God’s",year:"About 870–848 BC",
 sum:"Moab, Ammon and others come against Jehoshaphat, who proclaims a fast and prays, “We have no might against this great company … neither know we what to do: but our eyes are upon thee”—Jahaziel says, “Be not afraid … for the battle is not yours, but God’s”—Singers go before the army praising the LORD, and the enemies destroy one another—Judah gathers the spoil in the valley of Berachah.",
 bg:["This story is found only in Chronicles. Jehoshaphat’s prayer (20:6–12) is one of the best examples in the Old Testament of praying in a crisis: remember who God is, remember what he has done, tell him your need, and say that “our eyes are upon thee.” The invaders came from the east, around the Dead Sea.","Berachah means “blessing” (20:26), the name given to the valley where Judah blessed the LORD. Verses 35–37 tell of a failed shipping partnership with Ahaziah of Israel, parallel to 1 Kings 22:48–49, and the prophet Eliezer’s warning."]},
"2chr.21":{title:"He walked in the way of the kings of Israel",year:"About 848–841 BC",
 sum:"Jehoram becomes king and kills his six brothers and some princes—He walks as the house of Ahab, for “he had the daughter of Ahab to wife”—Edom and Libnah revolt—A letter from Elijah warns of judgment—Philistines and Arabians take his goods and sons, and he dies of a sore disease, “departed without being desired.”",
 bg:["This chapter parallels 2 Kings 8:16–24. The letter from Elijah (21:12–15) is found only in Chronicles. Many scholars note that Elijah seems to have been taken up (2 Kings 2) before Jehoram’s reign, so the timing is debated; the Bible does not explain it.","Jehoram’s wife, Athaliah, was a daughter of Ahab and Jezebel, and her influence brought Baal worship into Judah’s royal house. The end of Jehoram’s story is grim: no one mourned him, and he was not buried with the kings (21:20)."]},
"2chr.22":{title:"She arose and destroyed all the seed royal of the house of Judah",year:"About 841–835 BC",
 sum:"Ahaziah becomes king, but his mother Athaliah counsels him to do wickedly—He joins Joram of Israel and is slain by Jehu—Athaliah destroys all the royal seed of Judah—Jehoshabeath, the king’s daughter, steals the infant Joash and hides him six years in the house of God.",
 bg:["This chapter parallels 2 Kings 8:25–29; 9:27–28; 11:1–3. The KJV says Ahaziah was forty-two years old (22:2), but 2 Kings 8:26 says twenty-two, and many scholars think forty-two is a copying error since he was the youngest son of a man who died at forty (21:20). Ahaziah is also called Jehoahaz (21:17) and Azariah (22:6 in some manuscripts).","Athaliah killed her own grandchildren to seize power, so that the line of David nearly ended. Jehoshabeath (also called Jehosheba in 2 Kings 11:2) was the wife of the priest Jehoiada, and her bravery kept alive the line through which promises to David would continue."]},
"2chr.23":{title:"God save the king",year:"About 835–796 BC",
 sum:"In the seventh year Jehoiada the priest makes a covenant with the captains—Levites and priests guard the temple and the young Joash is shown, crowned and anointed—The people shout “God save the king”—Athaliah comes in, cries “Treason, Treason,” and is slain—Jehoiada makes a covenant that the people shall be the LORD’s, and Baal’s house is torn down.",
 bg:["This chapter parallels 2 Kings 11. Chronicles gives special notice to the Levites and gatekeepers who guarded the temple, since the revolt began at the house of the LORD. Joash’s name probably means “the LORD has given.”","Jehoiada acted carefully and with great courage, and he did not allow Athaliah to be killed in the temple itself (23:14). The covenant made between the LORD, the king and the people (23:16) restored the line of David and turned Judah again toward the LORD."]},
"2chr.24":{title:"Why transgress ye the commandments of the LORD",year:"About 835–796 BC",
 sum:"Joash does right all the days of Jehoiada, and he repairs the house of God, collecting money in a chest at the gate—After Jehoiada dies, the princes lead Judah into idols—Zechariah the son of Jehoiada asks, “Why transgress ye the commandments of the LORD, that ye cannot prosper?”—The king has him stoned in the court—Syria attacks, and Joash is slain by his servants.",
 bg:["This chapter parallels 2 Kings 12, but the account of Joash’s fall after Jehoiada’s death and the death of Zechariah is mostly found in Chronicles. The chest placed at the gate (24:8–10) was a way for everyone to give, and the work was done with honest workmen.","Jesus spoke of the blood of “Zacharias” killed between the temple and the altar (Matthew 23:35; Luke 11:51), and many see a link to this event, which was the last murder of a prophet in the Hebrew order of the Old Testament, since Chronicles was last. Scholars discuss whether this is the same Zechariah, and the Bible does not say."]},
"2chr.25":{title:"The LORD is able to give thee much more than this",year:"About 796–767 BC",
 sum:"Amaziah begins well but does not do right with a perfect heart—He hires a hundred thousand men of Israel, but a man of God says, “The LORD is able to give thee much more than this,” and he sends them home—He defeats Edom but worships Edom’s gods—Joash of Israel answers his challenge with a parable of a thistle and a cedar—Amaziah is defeated and later killed at Lachish.",
 bg:["This chapter parallels 2 Kings 14:1–20. The hundred talents of silver he spent on hired soldiers (25:6) were a large amount, but the prophet said that God could give him more. Edom was in the south, and the Valley of Salt was near the Dead Sea.","The Hebrew text is clear that Amaziah’s worship of the gods of people he had just defeated was foolish (25:14–15). Joash’s parable of the thistle and the cedar (25:18) warns that pride goes before a fall; Amaziah did not listen, “for it came of God” (25:20)."]},
"2chr.26":{title:"His heart was lifted up to his destruction",year:"About 792–740 BC",
 sum:"Sixteen-year-old Uzziah reigns in Jerusalem, seeking God in the days of Zechariah—God makes him prosper against the Philistines and Arabians, and he builds towers and an army—“His name spread far abroad; for he was marvellously helped, till he was strong”—But his heart is lifted up, and he burns incense in the temple—Azariah and eighty priests withstand him, and leprosy rises in his forehead.",
 bg:["This chapter parallels 2 Kings 15:1–7, where Uzziah is called Azariah (the two names are probably alternate forms). Uzziah’s name means “the LORD is my strength” (or “my strength is the LORD”). The sad turn of the story is that when he was “strong,” he forgot where his strength came from.","Only priests could burn incense in the temple (Numbers 16:40; 18:7). Uzziah lived in a separate house until he died, and his son Jotham governed (26:21). Isaiah’s great vision in the temple was “in the year that king Uzziah died” (Isaiah 6:1), and Amos mentions an earthquake in his days (Amos 1:1)."]},
"2chr.27":{title:"Jotham became mighty, because he prepared his ways before the LORD",year:"About 750–735 BC",
 sum:"Jotham, age twenty-five, does that which is right but does not enter the temple as his father did—He builds the high gate of the house of the LORD and the wall of Ophel, and towns and castles in Judah—He fights the Ammonites and receives tribute—“Jotham became mighty, because he prepared his ways before the LORD his God.”",
 bg:["This short chapter parallels 2 Kings 15:32–38. Jotham’s name is usually explained as “the LORD is perfect” or “the LORD is upright,” though the exact meaning is debated. He was a good king who learned from his father’s mistake by not entering the temple to burn incense.","Even so, “the people did yet corruptly” (27:2), and the high places remained (2 Kings 15:35). The prophets Isaiah, Hosea and Micah began their ministries in the days of Jotham and his successors (Isaiah 1:1; Hosea 1:1; Micah 1:1)."]},
"2chr.28":{title:"The LORD brought Judah low because of Ahaz king of Israel",year:"About 735–715 BC",
 sum:"Ahaz walks in the ways of Israel’s kings, makes molten images to Baalim, and burns his children in fire—Syria and Israel defeat Judah, and Israel takes 200,000 captives—The prophet Oded persuades them to return the captives, who are clothed, fed and sent to Jericho—Ahaz asks Assyria for help and strips the temple—He shuts the temple doors and makes altars in every corner.",
 bg:["This chapter parallels 2 Kings 16. It was during this crisis that Isaiah gave Ahaz the sign of Immanuel (Isaiah 7), which Isaiah offered because Ahaz refused to ask for a sign. Instead of trusting, Ahaz turned to Assyria, which led to Judah’s becoming a servant.","The story of Oded (28:9–15) is found only in Chronicles. Northern Israelites cared for their Judahite captives, clothing them, giving them food, and carrying the feeble on donkeys to Jericho, the “city of palm trees.” Many readers see in this a forerunner of the good Samaritan (Luke 10:30–37), though Jesus does not mention the connection."]},
"2chr.29":{title:"Hear me, ye Levites, sanctify now yourselves",year:"About 715–686 BC",
 sum:"Hezekiah, age twenty-five, opens and repairs the temple doors in the first month of his reign—He tells the priests and Levites to sanctify themselves and carry out the filth—They cleanse the temple in sixteen days—Sin offerings are made for the kingdom, and singers and musicians praise the LORD with David’s instruments—The people bring sacrifices and thank offerings.",
 bg:["This chapter parallels 2 Kings 18:1–8, but Chronicles tells far more about Hezekiah’s temple reform, which Kings summarizes in a single verse. Hezekiah’s name is usually explained as “the LORD strengthens” (or “my strength is the LORD”). His father Ahaz had closed the temple (28:24), so the first task was to open it and make it clean.","Hezekiah’s reform was a matter of the heart. The priests and Levites had to be sanctified before they could sanctify the house (29:15–19). Music was a central part of the restored worship, in the manner of David and the prophets Gad and Nathan (29:25)."]},
"2chr.30":{title:"Turn again unto the LORD God of Abraham, Isaac, and Israel",year:"About 715–686 BC",
 sum:"Hezekiah sends letters throughout Israel and Judah inviting all to keep the Passover at Jerusalem—Some laugh and mock, but others humble themselves and come—They keep the feast for seven days with great gladness and then add seven more—Hezekiah prays, “The good LORD pardon every one” who prepares his heart to seek God—There is great joy in Jerusalem.",
 bg:["This Passover is found only in Chronicles. It was held in the second month, as the law allowed for those who were unclean (Numbers 9:10–11). Hezekiah’s letter invited the northern tribes, whose kingdom had fallen to Assyria about 722 BC, to “turn again” so that God might return to the remnant who had escaped.","The most important thing here is that many people came unprepared and still ate the Passover, and Hezekiah prayed that God would pardon them because they had set their hearts to seek him. “The LORD hearkened to Hezekiah, and healed the people” (30:20). The chapter says there had not been such joy since Solomon (30:26)."]},
"2chr.31":{title:"Hezekiah wrought that which was good and right and truth before the LORD his God",year:"About 715–686 BC",
 sum:"The people go out and break the pillars, cut down the groves and throw down the high places—Hezekiah appoints courses for the priests and Levites and gives from his own goods—The people bring tithes of corn, wine, oil, honey and the increase of the field, in great heaps—Priests in the cities share the portions—“In every work that he began … he did it with all his heart, and prospered.”",
 bg:["This chapter has little in Kings, except the single verse 2 Kings 18:4 on removing the high places. Chronicles shows that the temple worship needed the support of the people, and the people responded: the priest Azariah said they had eaten enough and still left plenty, “for the LORD hath blessed his people” (31:10).","The giving of tithes (the tenth) follows the law of Moses (Leviticus 27:30–33; Numbers 18:21). The people gave in such abundance that the storehouses had to be prepared in the temple (31:11). Latter-day Saints remember that the Lord promised to “open you the windows of heaven” to those who pay tithes (Malachi 3:10; 3 Nephi 24:10)."]},
"2chr.32":{title:"With us is the LORD our God to help us, and to fight our battles",year:"About 715–686 BC",
 sum:"Sennacherib king of Assyria invades Judah, and Hezekiah stops the water outside the city and strengthens the walls—He says, “With us is the LORD our God to help us, and to fight our battles”—Sennacherib’s servants mock Hezekiah’s trust—Hezekiah and Isaiah pray, and an angel cuts off the Assyrian army—Hezekiah’s sickness, pride and humbling, and his riches.",
 bg:["This chapter parallels 2 Kings 18:13–19:37 and Isaiah 36–37, though it tells the story more briefly. Hezekiah’s preparation included stopping the springs outside Jerusalem and bringing “the upper watercourse of Gihon” into the city (32:30). This is probably the tunnel about 1,750 feet (533 m) long, cut through the rock; an inscription found in it describes how the diggers met in the middle.","Sennacherib’s own record, the Taylor Prism, says he shut Hezekiah up “like a bird in a cage,” and does not claim to have taken Jerusalem. The Bible tells that he returned home, and that his sons killed him (32:21). Notice that after healing, Hezekiah’s heart was lifted up, but he humbled himself (32:25–26); the envoys from Babylon are mentioned in verse 31 and told in 2 Kings 20."]},
"2chr.33":{title:"Then Manasseh knew that the LORD he was God",year:"About 697–642 BC",
 sum:"Manasseh, age twelve, rebuilds the high places, sets up altars for Baal and the host of heaven, passes his sons through fire and puts an idol in the house of God—The LORD speaks, but they do not listen—Assyrians bind Manasseh with fetters and carry him to Babylon—He humbles himself greatly and prays, and God restores him to Jerusalem—Then Manasseh knew that the LORD he was God.",
 bg:["This chapter parallels 2 Kings 21:1–18, but Manasseh’s repentance (33:10–19) is found only in Chronicles. Manasseh reigned fifty-five years, the longest of any king of Judah. His name means “causing to forget,” from the name of Joseph’s son (Genesis 41:51), who was named because God had made Joseph forget his trouble.","Assyrian records list Manasseh as a king who paid tribute, which fits the story that he was under Assyrian control. Verses 18–19 mention a prayer of Manasseh, but its text is not preserved in the Bible (a later work by that name is not part of the Latter-day Saint scriptures). This story shows that even a king who did very great evil could be forgiven when he humbled himself."]},
"2chr.34":{title:"I have found the book of the law in the house of the LORD",year:"About 640–609 BC",
 sum:"Josiah, age eight, begins to reign and in his eighth year begins to seek the God of David—In his twelfth year he purges Judah and Jerusalem of idols—As the temple is repaired, Hilkiah the priest finds “a book of the law of the LORD given by Moses”—Josiah tears his clothes and sends to Huldah the prophetess, who says judgment will come, but not in his days—He gathers the people and makes a covenant.",
 bg:["This chapter parallels 2 Kings 22:1–23:3. Josiah’s name is often explained as “the LORD supports” or “the LORD heals,” though the exact meaning is debated. The book found in the temple was probably Deuteronomy or a part of it, which warns of the curses that would come to a people who forsook the covenant. The prophet Jeremiah began his work in Josiah’s thirteenth year (Jeremiah 1:2).","Huldah the prophetess (34:22) lived in Jerusalem, and the king’s officers consulted her rather than other prophets, which shows how widely her word was trusted. About nine years after Josiah’s death, Lehi’s family lived in Jerusalem in “the first year of the reign of Zedekiah” (1 Nephi 1:4), when Josiah’s sons and grandson ruled."]},
"2chr.35":{title:"There was no passover like to that kept in Israel",year:"About 640–609 BC",
 sum:"Josiah keeps a Passover in Jerusalem, and the Levites teach all Israel and prepare the lamb—The people give thousands of lambs and bullocks—“There was no passover like to that kept in Israel from the days of Samuel the prophet”—Pharaoh Necho goes up to Carchemish, and Josiah goes out to fight him—Josiah is wounded by archers at Megiddo and dies, and Jeremiah and all Judah mourn him.",
 bg:["This chapter parallels 2 Kings 23:21–30. Chronicles gives much more detail about the Passover; Kings only says that none like it had been kept since the days of the judges. Necho (Pharaoh Necho II) was marching to help Assyria against Babylon, and Josiah, perhaps to protect Judah’s independence, tried to stop him at Megiddo in 609 BC.","Josiah “disguised himself” before battle (35:22), as Ahab had (18:29), and ignored a warning that came “from the mouth of God” through Necho. Jeremiah wrote laments for Josiah, and singers still spoke of him (35:25). Josiah’s death ended Judah’s last period of faithful reform."]},
"2chr.36":{title:"All the kingdoms of the earth hath the LORD God of heaven given me",year:"About 609–538 BC",
 sum:"Four of Josiah’s family reign in turn—Jehoiakim, Jehoiachin and Zedekiah do evil, and Zedekiah stiffens his neck against the prophet Jeremiah—Nebuchadnezzar burns the house of God and takes the people to Babylon, and the land enjoys her sabbaths—In the first year of Cyrus the king of Persia, a proclamation says the LORD has charged him to build him a house in Jerusalem.",
 bg:["This chapter covers 2 Kings 23:30–25:30 and Jeremiah 52. Four names stand out: Jehoahaz (also Shallum), Jehoiakim (named “the LORD raises up” by Pharaoh Necho in place of Eliakim), Jehoiachin, and Zedekiah (named “righteousness of the LORD” by Nebuchadnezzar in place of Mattaniah). Jerusalem fell in 586 BC. The “seventy years” are from Jeremiah (25:11–12; 29:10).","The last two verses (36:22–23) repeat the beginning of Ezra (Ezra 1:1–3), so the story of Chronicles ends with hope: Cyrus of Persia allowed the exiles to return in 538 BC. Many Latter-day Saints will also remember that the Book of Mormon says that Mulek, a son of Zedekiah, escaped and came to the promised land (Omni 1:15–16)."]},
"neh.1":{title:"I sat down and wept, and mourned certain days",year:"About 445–430 BC",
 sum:"Nehemiah, cupbearer to the king in Shushan, asks Hanani about Jerusalem—The wall is broken down and the gates burned—He weeps, fasts, and prays—He confesses the sins of Israel and recalls the promise to gather the scattered who turn to the Lord—“Grant him mercy in the sight of this man.”",
 bg:["Nehemiah’s name means “the LORD comforts.” His brother Hanani’s name is usually understood as “gracious,” a short form of “the LORD has been gracious.” Shushan is Susa, one of the Persian kings’ capitals, and a cupbearer was a trusted royal servant who stood close to the king.","Nehemiah’s prayer leans on God’s promise through Moses that if scattered Israel would turn back, he would gather them again (compare Deuteronomy 30:1–4). “The wall … is broken down” most likely refers to the destruction by Babylon in 586 BC, though some scholars think a more recent setback may also be in view (compare Ezra 4:7–23). The book itself does not say."]},
"neh.2":{title:"Let us rise up and build",year:"About 445–430 BC",
 sum:"The king sees Nehemiah’s sad face and asks his request—Nehemiah prays and asks to rebuild Jerusalem—The king grants letters and timber, “according to the good hand of my God upon me”—Sanballat and Tobiah are grieved—Nehemiah secretly surveys the wall by night—“Let us rise up and build.”",
 bg:["The twentieth year of Artaxerxes I was about 445 BC. Sanballat’s name is Babylonian and probably means “Sin (the moon god) has given life.” Tobiah’s name means “the LORD is good,” a Hebrew name that shows he was connected to Israel’s faith in some way. Geshem the Arabian (2:19) is probably the same Geshem known from ancient inscriptions as a ruler of an Arab people south of Judah.","Notice the short prayer in verse 4: “So I prayed to the God of heaven,” right in the middle of the conversation with the king. Nehemiah’s night ride around the broken wall let him see the damage for himself before he told anyone his plan."]},
"neh.3":{title:"Next unto him repaired",year:"About 445–430 BC",
 sum:"Eliashib the high priest and the priests build the Sheep Gate—Families, rulers, goldsmiths, perfumers, and men from many towns repair the wall and gates one section at a time—Shallum works “he and his daughters”—The nobles of Tekoa “put not their necks to the work of their Lord.”",
 bg:["The list reads like a work schedule: priests, merchants, town rulers, and families each took the stretch of wall nearest their own place. The Hebrew verb often translated “repaired” comes from a root meaning “to be strong,” the same root as Nehemiah’s prayer “strengthen my hands” in 6:9. Eliashib’s name probably means “God restores.”","The gates named here (Sheep, Fish, Old, Valley, Dung, Fountain, Water, Horse, East) let you trace the circuit of the city. The people of Tekoa repaired two sections (3:5, 27), while their nobles refused to help. Scripture does not say why."]},
"neh.4":{title:"Our God shall fight for us",year:"About 445–430 BC",
 sum:"Sanballat and Tobiah mock the builders—Nehemiah prays, and “the people had a mind to work”—Enemies plot to attack, so the people pray and set a watch—Half the men work while half hold spears—“Be not ye afraid of them: remember the Lord”—Every builder keeps his sword at his side.",
 bg:["Sanballat spoke for Samaria to the north, Tobiah for Ammon to the east, “the Arabians” to the south, and the Ashdodites to the west. That means Judah was ringed by neighbors who did not want a strong Jerusalem. Archaeologists generally agree the Jerusalem of this period was small, little more than the old ridge of the City of David, which helps explain how the wall could be finished so quickly.","Nehemiah’s response was both faith and work: “we made our prayer unto our God, and set a watch” (4:9). He also gave the people a rally point, saying that when they heard the trumpet they should gather, “our God shall fight for us” (4:20)."]},
"neh.5":{title:"Ye exact usury, every one of his brother",year:"About 445–430 BC",
 sum:"Poor families cry out of hunger, mortgaged fields, and children sold into bondage—Nehemiah is very angry and rebukes the nobles—They agree to give back lands and stop charging interest—Nehemiah shakes out his lap as a warning—He did not take the governor’s bread, for twelve years, “because of the fear of God.”",
 bg:["The law of Moses forbade charging interest to a poor Israelite (Exodus 22:25; Leviticus 25:35–37). The Hebrew word for interest, neshek, comes from a verb meaning “to bite.” The “hundredth part” in verse 11 was probably a monthly charge, though the text is not completely clear.","Nehemiah did not ask of others what he would not do himself. He and his household lent freely, and for the twelve years he was governor he did not collect the food allowance that governors were entitled to (5:14–18). His shaking out of his lap (5:13) was a sign: may God shake out of house and work anyone who breaks this promise."]},
"neh.6":{title:"I am doing a great work, so that I cannot come down",year:"About 445–430 BC",
 sum:"Sanballat, Tobiah, and Geshem invite Nehemiah to meet at Ono, planning harm—He answers, “I am doing a great work”—An open letter falsely says he plans to be king—A hired prophet urges him to hide in the temple, but he will not—“So the wall was finished … in fifty and two days.”",
 bg:["The wall was completed on the twenty-fifth of Elul, the sixth month, “in fifty and two days” (6:15). Even Nehemiah’s enemies had to admit that “this work was wrought of our God.” Gashmu (6:6) seems to be another form of the name Geshem.","Shemaiah’s advice was a trap. Only priests were allowed to enter the temple’s holy place, so a layman like Nehemiah who hid there would have broken the law and given his enemies something to say against him (compare 2 Chronicles 26:16–21). Nehemiah saw that “God had not sent him.”"]},
"neh.7":{title:"He was a faithful man, and feared God above many",year:"About 445–430 BC",
 sum:"With the wall built, Nehemiah appoints gatekeepers, singers, and Levites—He puts Hanani and Hananiah in charge of Jerusalem and sets the hours of the gates—The city is large, but the people are few—He finds the register of those who first returned with Zerubbabel and lists them by family.",
 bg:["The list in this chapter closely matches the list in Ezra 2. Both give a total of 42,360 (Nehemiah 7:66; Ezra 2:64), though some of the individual numbers differ. Scholars think the differences come from copying and counting over time. Hananiah’s name means “the LORD has been gracious,” a close match to his colleague Hanani’s.","The city had walls and gates, but not enough people: “the city was large and great: but the people were few therein, and the houses were not builded” (7:4). The next chapters show how Nehemiah and Ezra built up the people as well as the wall."]},
"neh.8":{title:"The joy of the LORD is your strength",year:"About 445–430 BC",
 sum:"All the people gather at the Water Gate, and Ezra reads the law from morning until midday—The Levites “gave the sense,” helping them understand—The people weep, but are told, “the joy of the LORD is your strength”—They find the command to dwell in booths and keep the Feast of Tabernacles with great gladness.",
 bg:["The people gathered on the first day of the seventh month, the day set apart in Leviticus 23:24. Ezra’s reading is one of the earliest scenes of public Bible teaching. “Gave the sense” may mean the Levites explained the meaning, or translated the Hebrew into Aramaic, the language many had grown up speaking. Ezra’s own journey to Jerusalem is told in Ezra 7.","The people made booths of branches (8:15) and lived in them for seven days, remembering the wilderness (Leviticus 23:39–43). Verse 17 says Israel had not kept the feast like this “since the days of Jeshua.” A Book of Mormon scene with a similar feel is King Benjamin’s people gathering with their tents facing the temple to hear the word (Mosiah 2:5–6)."]},
"neh.9":{title:"Thou art a God ready to pardon",year:"About 445–430 BC",
 sum:"Israel gathers in fasting, sackcloth, and confession—The Levites bless God as Creator, who chose Abram and delivered Israel from Egypt—He gave the law at Sinai and fed them in the wilderness forty years—Though they rebelled, he was “ready to pardon, gracious and merciful, slow to anger”—The people make a sure covenant and write it.",
 bg:["This long prayer retells Israel’s story from Abram to the return from exile, much as Psalms 78, 105, and 106 do. It is a pattern of covenant and failure and mercy, repeated again and again. In verse 21 God sustained Israel in the wilderness so that “their clothes waxed not old, and their feet swelled not.” Nephi gives a similar retelling of the exodus in 1 Nephi 17.","Verse 7 notes that God “gavest him the name of Abraham.” Abram means “exalted father,” and Abraham is explained in Genesis 17:5 as “a father of many nations.” The prayer ends honestly: “we are servants this day” (9:36), living in the promised land but under foreign kings."]},
"neh.10":{title:"We will not forsake the house of our God",year:"About 445–430 BC",
 sum:"Nehemiah, the priests, the Levites, and the chiefs seal the covenant—The people promise to walk in God’s law, keep the sabbath and seventh year, and not intermarry with the peoples around them—They tax themselves for the temple, supply wood, and bring firstfruits and tithes—“We will not forsake the house of our God.”",
 bg:["The title “Tirshatha” (10:1) is a Persian word, generally understood as “governor” or “His Excellency.” The one-third shekel for the temple (10:32) differs from the half shekel in Exodus 30:13; the text does not explain why, and scholars suggest changed circumstances in a poorer community.","The promise about marriage (10:30) echoes Deuteronomy 7:3–4, where the concern is that God’s people would be led away from him. It is a worry about faith, not about family line alone: the Bible also honors Ruth the Moabite as David’s ancestor (Ruth 4:13–22; Matthew 1:5). The promise about tithes and offerings is about caring for the temple, its priests, and its worship."]},
"neh.11":{title:"The people blessed all the men, that willingly offered themselves",year:"About 445–430 BC",
 sum:"The rulers live in Jerusalem, and the people cast lots to bring one in ten to dwell in “the holy city”—The people bless those who volunteer—The chief men of Judah and Benjamin, the priests, the Levites, and the gatekeepers are listed—The rest live in the towns of Judah and Benjamin.",
 bg:["Few wanted to live in Jerusalem. It was crowded with ruins, exposed to enemies, and far from farmland. So Israel drew lots, one household in ten, which they treated as God’s means of choosing, and those who offered themselves without waiting for the lot were blessed (11:1–2). The Nethinims (11:3) were temple servants whose name means “the given ones.”","Verse 30 says the people settled “from Beer-sheba unto the valley of Hinnom,” showing that Judah’s territory in Persian times stretched from the Negev to Jerusalem. “The holy city” (11:1) is a title used again in Isaiah 52:1."]},
"neh.12":{title:"To keep the dedication with gladness",year:"About 445–430 BC",
 sum:"The priests and Levites who returned with Zerubbabel and Jeshua are listed, with the line of high priests—The Levites are sought out to keep the dedication of the wall with thanksgiving and singing—Two great choirs walk on the wall in opposite directions—They offer great sacrifices, “for God had made them rejoice with great joy.”",
 bg:["The list of high priests (12:10–11) runs from Jeshua through Eliashib to Jaddua. Jaddua may have lived well after Nehemiah’s day, so scholars think this part was added to the record later. Verse 22 also mentions “Darius the Persian,” and the book itself does not tell us which Darius.","At the dedication, two large choirs walked in opposite directions along the top of the new wall and met at the temple (12:31, 38–40). “The joy of Jerusalem was heard even afar off” (12:43). Compare the joy and shouting when the temple foundation was laid (Ezra 3:10–13)."]},
"neh.13":{title:"Remember me, O my God, for good",year:"About 445–430 BC",
 sum:"The law shows Ammonites and Moabites are to be kept out—Nehemiah returns from the king and finds Eliashib has given Tobiah a room in the temple—He throws out Tobiah’s goods, restores the tithes for the Levites, and keeps the sabbath—He opposes marriages to foreign women—“Remember me, O my God, for good.”",
 bg:["Nehemiah returned to the king in the thirty-second year of Artaxerxes, about 433 BC, and came back to find the covenant of chapter 10 broken. The room Eliashib gave Tobiah (Tobiah’s name means “the LORD is good”) was a storage room for the temple’s offerings. Some scholars connect these problems with what the prophet Malachi says about tithes and temple worship (Malachi 3:8–10, which also appears in 3 Nephi 24:8–10).","Nehemiah’s response in verses 23–28 was forceful, and this is hard reading today. He reminded them that Solomon’s wisdom was undone by foreign wives (13:26; 1 Kings 11:1–8). His prayer “Remember me, O my God, for good” (13:14, 22, 31) is the book’s last line, a humble request that his faithful work not be forgotten."]},
"eccl.1":{title:"Vanity of vanities, saith the Preacher",year:"Date uncertain",
 sum:"The Preacher, son of David, king in Jerusalem, declares “all is vanity”—Generations come and go while the sun, wind, and rivers repeat their rounds—“There is no new thing under the sun”—He gives his heart to seek wisdom and finds it a “sore travail”—“In much wisdom is much grief.”",
 bg:["The speaker is called Qoheleth in Hebrew, from a word related to “assembly,” so it is often translated “the Preacher” or “the Gatherer.” Tradition says Solomon wrote the book, since the speaker is “king in Jerusalem” and “son of David,” but the text never names him. Many scholars think its language and style are later than Solomon’s time and date it after the exile.","“Vanity” translates the Hebrew hevel, meaning “breath” or “vapor,” something real but passing. It is the same word as the name of Abel (Hevel), whose life was so brief (Genesis 4:2). “Vanity of vanities” is a Hebrew way of saying “the most hevel,” as “Song of Songs” means the best song. This book’s sceptical voice puzzles many readers. It is usually read as wisdom literature that presses a hard question: what is truly lasting?"]},
"eccl.2":{title:"There was no profit under the sun",year:"Date uncertain",
 sum:"The Preacher tests mirth and wine, then builds houses, vineyards, gardens, and pools, gathering servants, silver, gold, and singers—He holds back no joy from his heart, yet finds “no profit under the sun”—Wisdom excels folly, but the same event happens to both—He must leave his labor to one who comes after—Eating, drinking, and enjoying labor are gifts “from the hand of God.”",
 bg:["The project in this chapter sounds royal, with parks, pools for watering trees, servants, singers, and treasure, much like the wealth described for Solomon in 1 Kings 4–10. The speaker tries every pleasure as a test and reports honestly that none of them satisfied. Wisdom beats folly “as far as light excelleth darkness” (2:13), yet death comes to both.","The chapter ends with a calmer note: enjoying food and work is “from the hand of God” (2:24–26). The Book of Mormon, in a different setting and with different wording, also teaches that joy is part of God’s purpose for us: “men are, that they might have joy” (2 Nephi 2:25). Treat this as an echo of the idea, not a quotation."]},
"eccl.3":{title:"To every thing there is a season",year:"Date uncertain",
 sum:"“To every thing there is a season”—A time to be born and to die, to weep and laugh, to mourn and dance, to love and hate, to be silent and to speak—God “hath made every thing beautiful in his time” and set eternity in human hearts—Enjoy life as God’s gift—God will judge the righteous and the wicked—Man and beast both return to dust.",
 bg:["The poem in verses 1–8 has fourteen pairs of opposites, twenty-eight “times” in all. It does not say every event is good, but that life has its seasons, and wisdom means knowing which one you are in. Many will know it from a 1960s folk-rock song that borrowed its words. Verse 11, “he hath set the world in their heart,” uses the Hebrew olam, which can mean “world,” “eternity,” or “a sense of long time,” and translators disagree.","Verses 18–21 compare humans and animals, asking “who knoweth the spirit of man that goeth upward?” Here the Preacher is honest about how little can be seen “under the sun.” For a Latter-day Saint link on the use of this life, see Alma 34:32, “this life is the time for men to prepare to meet God.”"]},
"eccl.4":{title:"A threefold cord is not quickly broken",year:"Date uncertain",
 sum:"The Preacher sees the tears of the oppressed, who have “no comforter”—He sees envy driving much labor, and the fool who folds his hands—A man alone with no child or brother labors without end—“Two are better than one”—“A threefold cord is not quickly broken”—Even a popular new king will pass from memory.",
 bg:["The most loved lines in this chapter are in verses 9–12. A rope braided from three strands is much stronger than one, and the lesson is that friends, spouses, and fellow workers help each other when one falls. The text does not say who the third strand is, and readers have applied the image in many ways.","In the covenant of baptism, Alma’s people promised “to mourn with those that mourn … and comfort those that stand in need of comfort” (Mosiah 18:8–9), an answer to the oppressed who had “no comforter” in verse 1. The chapter ends honestly: even a poor wise youth who becomes king will be forgotten by those who come after."]},
"eccl.5":{title:"Let thy words be few",year:"Date uncertain",
 sum:"Be careful in the house of God and slow to speak—“God is in heaven, and thou upon earth: therefore let thy words be few”—Pay a vow rather than make one and break it—Do not be amazed at oppression, for there is One higher—He who loves silver is never satisfied—Naked we came, and naked we go—Enjoying one’s labor is “the gift of God.”",
 bg:["Verses 1–7 are close to the law on vows in Deuteronomy 23:21–23: a promise made to God is serious, and it is better to make fewer promises than to break one. “Before the angel” (5:6) uses a Hebrew word that means “messenger,” and scholars debate whether it means a priest or temple messenger or a heavenly one.","Verses 10–17 say that wealth does not satisfy: “The sleep of a labouring man is sweet.” “As he came forth of his mother’s womb, naked shall he return” (5:15) is close to Job 1:21 and 1 Timothy 6:7. The chapter ends with the same calmer point as chapter 2: eating, working, and enjoying are God’s gift."]},
"eccl.6":{title:"All the labour of man is for his mouth",year:"Date uncertain",
 sum:"A man given riches and honor, but no power to enjoy them, has “an evil disease”—A man with a hundred children and many years but no good is no better off—“All the labour of man is for his mouth, and yet the appetite is not filled”—Who knows what is good for man, “all the days of his vain life … as a shadow”?",
 bg:["This is a hard chapter. The statement that “an untimely birth is better” than such a man (6:3) is a bitter comparison, not a command or a teaching of God. The Preacher says a life without enjoyment or contentment is worse than one that never knew the sorrow. The Hebrew word for “appetite” in verse 7 is nefesh, usually translated “soul,” so the verse hints that the soul is hungry for something that food cannot give.","“Better is the sight of the eyes than the wandering of the desire” (6:9) means that enjoying what is in front of you is better than chasing what is out of reach. The final questions, “who knoweth what is good for man in this life?” and “who can tell a man what shall be after him?” are left open in this chapter."]},
"eccl.7":{title:"A good name is better than precious ointment",year:"Date uncertain",
 sum:"A good name is better than precious ointment—The house of mourning teaches more than the house of feasting—Patience is better than pride, and anger rests “in the bosom of fools”—Wisdom is a defense—“Be not righteous over much”—“There is not a just man upon earth, that doeth good, and sinneth not”—God made man upright, but they sought many inventions.",
 bg:["Verse 1 is a pun in Hebrew: tov shem mishemen tov, “A good name is better than precious ointment.” Shem means “name” and shemen means “oil,” so the line sounds like what it says. Verse 10, “Say not thou, What is the cause that the former days were better than these?” is advice against always praising the past.","Verse 16, “Be not righteous over much; neither make thyself over wise,” puzzles readers. Most take it as a warning against proud, self-made righteousness that thinks it needs no mercy, which fits verse 20, “there is not a just man upon earth, that doeth good, and sinneth not” (compare Romans 3:23). The advice to “fear God” in verse 18 is what the Preacher offers to guard the balance."]},
"eccl.8":{title:"A wise man’s heart discerneth both time and judgment",year:"Date uncertain",
 sum:"Wisdom makes a man’s face shine—Keep the king’s commandment, for his word has power—No man has power over the day of death—The wicked are buried and forgotten, and justice is slow—“It shall be well with them that fear God”—Yet the just sometimes suffer as the wicked should, so the Preacher commends joy—No one can find out all God’s work.",
 bg:["In the ancient world a king’s word was law, and the advice to obey him (8:2–5) is the practical counsel of a court official. “The oath of God” probably means a vow of loyalty made in God’s name, though the text does not say exactly. The line “sentence against an evil work is not executed speedily” (8:11) says that delayed justice makes people bolder in wrongdoing.","Verse 14 names the puzzle that fills Job, Psalm 73, and Habakkuk 1: sometimes the righteous seem to get what the wicked deserve. The Preacher does not solve it. He holds to “it shall be well with them that fear God” (8:12) and admits that “a man cannot find out the work that is done under the sun” (8:17)."]},
"eccl.9":{title:"Whatsoever thy hand findeth to do, do it with thy might",year:"Date uncertain",
 sum:"The righteous and the wise are in the hand of God, yet one event comes to all—“A living dog is better than a dead lion”—The dead know nothing of this life—Eat bread with joy, live happily with your wife, and do whatever your hand finds “with thy might”—“The race is not to the swift”—A poor wise man saves a city but is forgotten.",
 bg:["“The dead know not any thing” (9:5) reflects the Old Testament’s view of Sheol as a dim place where earthly work stops (compare Psalm 88:10–12; Isaiah 38:18). Dogs were looked down on in Israel, and the lion was a symbol of royalty, so “a living dog is better than a dead lion” is a proverb. Later revelation teaches more about life after death, for example Alma 40:11–12 and Doctrine and Covenants 138.","Verses 7–10 give advice to enjoy life now: bread, wine, white garments, and the love of a spouse are all gifts. Verse 11, “time and chance happeneth to them all,” says that skill and strength do not guarantee success. The poor wise man who saves the city (9:13–18) is forgotten, but the Preacher still says “wisdom is better than strength.”"]},
"eccl.10":{title:"Wisdom is profitable to direct",year:"Date uncertain",
 sum:"Dead flies spoil the ointment, and a little folly spoils a wise man’s name—Rulers’ errors raise folly and bring down the worthy—He that digs a pit shall fall into it—A dull axe needs more strength, but “wisdom is profitable to direct”—A fool is full of words—Woe to the land whose king is a child.",
 bg:["This chapter reads like the book of Proverbs, a string of short sayings. “He that diggeth a pit shall fall into it” (10:8) is close to Proverbs 26:27. The picture in verse 10 is of an axe: sharpening the blade takes time, but it saves effort. The picture in verse 1 is of flies spoiling a perfumer’s ointment, and one small folly can spoil a good name.","Verse 16, “Woe to thee, O land, when thy king is a child,” probably means a king who is immature, not simply young. Verse 20 says even private grumbling about rulers can get out, since “a bird of the air shall carry the voice.” That is an old proverb for how secrets travel."]},
"eccl.11":{title:"Cast thy bread upon the waters",year:"Date uncertain",
 sum:"“Cast thy bread upon the waters: for thou shalt find it after many days”—Give a portion to seven, and also to eight—Do not wait for perfect weather to sow and reap—You cannot know the works of God—The light is sweet—“Rejoice, O young man, in thy youth,” but God will bring every action into judgment.",
 bg:["Scholars are not sure what “cast thy bread upon the waters” means. Some read it as generosity (kindness will come back to you), others as taking a risk in trade by sea. Either way the point of verses 1–6 is that you cannot know what will happen, so act with generosity and diligence anyway: “in the morning sow thy seed, and in the evening withhold not thine hand.”","In verse 5, the Hebrew word ruach means both “wind” and “spirit,” so some translations read “As thou knowest not what is the way of the spirit” and others “the way of the spirit.” Either way, no one can fully understand how new life comes into the world. Verses 9–10 encourage the young to rejoice, and to remember that their choices matter."]},
"eccl.12":{title:"Remember now thy Creator in the days of thy youth",year:"Date uncertain",
 sum:"“Remember now thy Creator in the days of thy youth”—Old age is pictured as a darkening house, a failing cord, and a broken bowl—“The dust return to the earth … and the spirit shall return unto God who gave it”—The wise teach knowledge—“Fear God, and keep his commandments: for this is the whole duty of man”—God will bring every work into judgment.",
 bg:["The picture in verses 2–7 is usually read as a poem about growing old: the “keepers of the house” are the arms, the “strong men” the legs, the “grinders” the teeth, and those who “look out of the windows” the eyes. The “almond tree” may stand for white hair. Scholars disagree about some details, and some see a storm or a funeral picture instead. Verse 7 echoes Genesis 2:7 and 3:19. In the Book of Mormon, Alma 40:11 says the spirits of all people “are taken home to that God who gave them life,” much like the wording here.","Verses 9–14 sound like a different voice speaking about the Preacher in the third person, gathering his words as “goads” and “nails … given from one shepherd.” Many scholars think an editor added this closing, and it gives the book’s main answer to “what is truly lasting?”: “Fear God, and keep his commandments.” The Hebrew behind “whole duty of man” is literally “this is the whole duty of man.”"]},
"job.1":{title:"The LORD gave, and the LORD hath taken away",year:"Date uncertain",
 sum:"Job, a perfect and upright man in the land of Uz, is blessed with seven sons, three daughters, and great wealth—Satan, among the sons of God, says Job serves God only because he is protected—The Lord permits Satan to test Job’s possessions, and in one day raiders, fire, wind, and a great storm take his livestock, servants, and children—Job tears his robe and worships: “Blessed be the name of the LORD.”",
 bg:["The book begins in “the land of Uz” (1:1). Its exact location is uncertain, though many scholars place it east or southeast of Israel, in the region of Edom. The setting feels like the patriarchal age, since Job himself offers sacrifices for his family, but scholars debate when the book was written. Even the meaning of the name Job is uncertain; some propose “persecuted” or “where is my father?”","In verse 6 Satan comes among the “sons of God.” The Hebrew is ha-satan, “the adversary” or “the accuser,” a title as much as a name. The Hebrew of parts of Job is difficult, so translations differ in places. James 5:11 later holds up Job as an example of patience: “Ye have heard of the patience of Job, and have seen the end of the Lord.”"]},
"job.2":{title:"Shall we receive good at the hand of God, and shall we not receive evil?",year:"Date uncertain",
 sum:"Satan again appears before the Lord and asks leave to touch Job’s body—Job is struck with painful boils from head to foot—His wife says, “Curse God, and die,” but Job calls her words foolish—“In all this did not Job sin with his lips”—Three friends, Eliphaz, Bildad, and Zophar, come to comfort him and sit with him seven days in silence.",
 bg:["The three friends come from different places: Eliphaz the Temanite, Bildad the Shuhite, and Zophar the Naamathite (2:11). Teman was associated with Edom and was known for wisdom. Their names are not explained in the text; Eliphaz is often understood as “my God is fine gold” or similar, but meanings for all three are uncertain.","The friends do their best work here, before they speak. They tear their robes, sit on the ground, and say nothing for seven days because they see that Job’s grief is very great (2:13). The book’s later chapters show how quickly their comfort changes to argument."]},
"job.3":{title:"Let the day perish wherein I was born",year:"Date uncertain",
 sum:"After seven days of silence, Job breaks it with a lament—He curses the day of his birth and wishes he had died in the womb—“Why died I not from the womb?”—He longs for the rest of the grave, where “the weary be at rest”—Why is light given to the miserable, whose way is hid and whom God has hedged in?",
 bg:["This poem opens the long poetic section of the book, which runs to 42:6. Job does not curse God; he curses the day of his birth and asks why. Many readers find comfort that scripture lets a faithful man speak this honestly in his pain.","Verse 8 mentions Leviathan, a sea creature of ancient poetry associated with chaos, which comes back in chapter 41. Verse 13 and following speak of Sheol, the Hebrew word for the realm of the dead, pictured here as a place of quiet. The Hebrew of this chapter is hard in places, and translations vary."]},
"job.4":{title:"Shall mortal man be more just than God?",year:"Date uncertain",
 sum:"Eliphaz the Temanite speaks first, gently—He reminds Job that he has comforted others and should trust his own integrity—“Who ever perished, being innocent?”—Eliphaz describes a night vision in which a spirit asks, “Shall mortal man be more just than God?”—Even God’s servants and angels are not beyond fault.",
 bg:["Eliphaz begins the first of three rounds of speeches. His argument, shared by all three friends, is that suffering comes from sin: “they that plow iniquity, and sow wickedness, reap the same” (4:8). That belief is stated confidently here but is later rebuked by the Lord (42:7).","Verses 12–21 describe a hushed, frightening vision at night. Eliphaz treats it as a message from beyond, but the text does not say it came from God, and the Lord later says Eliphaz did not speak rightly. The friends are a mixed example: they say some true things about God, but apply them wrongly to Job."]},
"job.5":{title:"Man is born unto trouble, as the sparks fly upward",year:"Date uncertain",
 sum:"Eliphaz continues—Fools are consumed by their own wrath, but trouble does not spring from the dust—“I would seek unto God”—Happy is the man whom God corrects, for “he maketh sore, and bindeth up”—In six troubles and seven God will deliver, and Job’s house shall be at peace.",
 bg:["Verse 7 is well known: “Man is born unto trouble, as the sparks fly upward.” The Hebrew phrase for the sparks is literally “sons of Resheph,” which some scholars take as sparks or flames and others as flying creatures; the general sense is the same.","Eliphaz’s counsel in verses 17–18, “despise not thou the chastening of the Almighty,” sounds helpful, and parts of it are echoed elsewhere in scripture (Proverbs 3:11–12; Hebrews 12:5–6). But he assumes Job’s pain is a punishment, and that is the point on which the book proves him wrong."]},
"job.6":{title:"How forcible are right words!",year:"Date uncertain",
 sum:"Job answers Eliphaz—His grief is heavier than the sand of the sea, and he wishes God would end his life—He asks for pity from his friends, but they are “as a brook” that dries up when needed—“How forcible are right words!”—He asks them to show him where he has erred.",
 bg:["Job compares his friends to winter streams in desert valleys (6:15–20). They rush with water in the cold season, but in summer heat they vanish just when travelers need them most. It is a vivid picture of friends who offer words instead of help.","Job does not deny that he is suffering, and he does not claim to be sinless. He only asks to be shown a specific wrong, and he says plainly that “to him that is afflicted pity should be shewed from his friend” (6:14). Some of the Hebrew in this chapter is difficult."]},
"job.7":{title:"My days are swifter than a weaver’s shuttle",year:"Date uncertain",
 sum:"Job continues, speaking of the hard service and sleepless nights of a hired servant—“My days are swifter than a weaver’s shuttle, and are spent without hope”—He turns from his friends to God and asks why he is watched so closely—“What is man, that thou shouldest magnify him?”—If he has sinned, why does God not pardon him?",
 bg:["In verses 17–18 Job echoes Psalm 8:4 but turns it around. The psalm marvels that God remembers man; Job asks why God will not leave him alone. Job is speaking to God here, not only to his friends.","A weaver’s shuttle is the tool that darts back and forth across the loom, a fitting picture of how quickly life passes. The word for “hope” in verse 6 can also mean “thread,” so some think Job may be playing on both ideas."]},
"job.8":{title:"Doth God pervert judgment?",year:"Date uncertain",
 sum:"Bildad the Shuhite speaks—He asks, “Doth God pervert judgment? or doth the Almighty pervert justice?”—He suggests Job’s children died because of their own sin and urges Job to seek God—Ask the former generations: the hypocrite’s hope is like a spider’s web—God will not cast away a perfect man.",
 bg:["Bildad relies on tradition (“inquire, I pray thee, of the former age,” 8:8). He is the harshest of the friends so far, since he says Job’s children died because “cast them away for their transgression” (8:4). In the end the Lord shows this reasoning to be wrong.","Verse 14 compares the trust of the godless to a spider’s house, which seems solid until it is leaned on. The papyrus and flag of verse 11 are Egyptian-style marsh plants that grow tall only where there is water."]},
"job.9":{title:"How should man be just with God?",year:"Date uncertain",
 sum:"Job replies that no one can win an argument with God, who is wise in heart and mighty in strength—God moves mountains, shakes the earth, and makes Arcturus, Orion, and Pleiades—Job cannot answer him, and God is not a man to meet in court—“Neither is there any daysman betwixt us”—Job longs for someone to mediate.",
 bg:["Verse 9 names “Arcturus, Orion, and Pleiades.” The Hebrew words (Ash, Kesil, Kimah) are star groups that translators identify differently, so the names vary from Bible to Bible. The point is that God made the heavens.","A “daysman” (9:33) is an old English word for an umpire or mediator who can lay a hand on both sides. Job feels he needs one because God is not a man he can summon to court. Latter-day Saints may think here of Jesus Christ as the Mediator (see 1 Timothy 2:5)."]},
"job.10":{title:"My soul is weary of my life",year:"Date uncertain",
 sum:"Job speaks his complaint in bitterness of soul—He asks God not to condemn him but to show him why God contends with him—“Thine hands have made me and fashioned me”—Why bring him forth from the womb to suffer?—He asks for a little comfort before he goes to “the land of darkness and the shadow of death.”",
 bg:["Verses 8–12 are a beautiful piece of poetry about the formation of the body: “Hast thou not poured me out as milk, and curdled me like cheese?” Job says God knit him together with “bones and sinews” and watched over his spirit. That makes his suffering feel all the more puzzling to him.","The “land of darkness” in verses 21–22 is Sheol, the ancient Hebrew word for the place of the dead. Job’s view of it here is bleak; later in the book he reaches toward hope beyond it (14:14; 19:25–27)."]},
"job.11":{title:"Canst thou by searching find out God?",year:"Date uncertain",
 sum:"Zophar the Naamathite speaks, bluntly—He says Job’s many words should not go unanswered and that God exacts less than Job’s iniquity deserves—“Canst thou by searching find out God?”—God’s wisdom is higher than heaven and deeper than hell—If Job puts away iniquity and prepares his heart, he will be secure and the wicked will fail.",
 bg:["Zophar is the third friend and the sharpest. He claims that God knows “vain men” and that Job has been punished less than he deserves (11:6). The book’s ending shows this to be false.","His statement that God’s wisdom is deeper than anything we can search out (11:7–9) is true and is later echoed by the Lord. His mistake is using it to condemn a man who is not guilty. Some Hebrew in verse 12 is obscure and translators render it differently."]},
"job.12":{title:"No doubt but ye are the people, and wisdom shall die with you",year:"Date uncertain",
 sum:"Job answers with sarcasm: “No doubt but ye are the people, and wisdom shall die with you”—Even the beasts, birds, and earth teach that God’s hand has done this—With God are wisdom, strength, and counsel; he breaks down, deposes princes, and increases nations—He deprives leaders of understanding and brings deep things out of darkness.",
 bg:["Job begins a long reply that runs through chapter 14. He is not saying the friends know nothing; he is saying that what they offer is common knowledge, and that “I am not inferior to you” (12:3). He wants to speak to God, not trade proverbs.","Verses 13–25 are a poem on God’s power over nations and rulers. Job agrees with his friends that God is great, but he points out that this power is sometimes used in ways people cannot explain, and that suffering is not always punishment."]},
"job.13":{title:"Though he slay me, yet will I trust in him",year:"Date uncertain",
 sum:"Job tells his friends he knows as much as they do and calls them “forgers of lies” and “physicians of no value”—He would rather argue his case before God—“Though he slay me, yet will I trust in him”—He will defend his ways and asks God to remove his hand and answer him—Why does God hide his face and count him an enemy?",
 bg:["The Hebrew of 13:15 is debated. The traditional Hebrew text can be read, “He will slay me; I have no hope,” and some modern Bibles translate it that way. The KJV reading, “Though he slay me, yet will I trust in him,” is the one most familiar to Latter-day Saints and expresses the faith that runs through the whole book.","Job insists on speaking directly to God, and he says the friends “speak wickedly for God” (13:7) when they defend God with false arguments. That prepares for the Lord’s verdict in 42:7."]},
"job.14":{title:"If a man die, shall he live again?",year:"Date uncertain",
 sum:"Man is born of woman, few of days and full of trouble, like a flower that is cut down—His days are numbered, and a tree has more hope than a man, for it may sprout again—“If a man die, shall he live again?”—Job longs to be hidden in the grave until God’s wrath passes, and says, “all the days of my appointed time will I wait, till my change come.”",
 bg:["Verse 14 is one of the great questions of the Old Testament: “If a man die, shall he live again?” Job asks it and hopes for a yes, saying, “Thou shalt call, and I will answer thee: thou wilt have a desire to the work of thine hands” (14:15). Latter-day Saints see the Resurrection as the answer (see 1 Corinthians 15:20–22).","The tree in verses 7–9 is a picture of hope: a stump may sprout again “through the scent of water.” Job says man does not seem to have even that, yet the question he asks keeps pointing forward."]},
"job.15":{title:"What is man, that he should be clean?",year:"Date uncertain",
 sum:"Eliphaz speaks again, now sharply—He accuses Job of empty knowledge and of making himself wise—“What is man, that he should be clean?”—The heavens are not clean in God’s sight—The wicked man suffers pain all his days and trusts in vanity, but he will not escape.",
 bg:["The second round of speeches begins here and the friends grow harsher. Eliphaz now says Job’s own words condemn him (15:6), and his picture of the wicked man (15:20–35) is aimed squarely at Job without naming him.","Eliphaz appeals to the wisdom of old men and to what “wise men have told from their fathers” (15:18). The book respects tradition, but its ending shows that tradition applied without compassion can still be wrong."]},
"job.16":{title:"Miserable comforters are ye all",year:"Date uncertain",
 sum:"Job answers: “Miserable comforters are ye all”—If the friends were in his place he could speak as they do, but he would rather strengthen them—God has worn him out, torn him, and set him as a mark—Yet “my witness is in heaven, and my record is on high”—His eyes pour out tears to God, and he asks that someone plead for him.",
 bg:["In verses 19–21 Job says his “witness” and “record” are in heaven, and that his “friend” will plead with God for him. Scholars debate whom he means; some see God himself as Job’s advocate against God, which is a daring paradox, and Latter-day Saints may see in it a pointer to Christ as advocate (see D&C 45:3–5).","The picture of God as an enemy (16:9–14) is Job’s honest feeling, not a statement of doctrine. The book later shows God answering him, which shows that Job’s honesty was not rebuked."]},
"job.17":{title:"Where is now my hope?",year:"Date uncertain",
 sum:"Job says his breath is corrupt, his days are extinct, and the graves are ready for him—He asks God to be his surety, since the friends cannot help—He is a byword of the people and his eyes are dim with grief—“Where is now my hope? as for my hope, who shall see it?”—He expects the pit, yet the righteous hold on to their way.",
 bg:["To be a “surety” (17:3) is to put up a pledge or guarantee for another. Job asks God to stand as the guarantee of his innocence, since no human can. The thought continues the search for a mediator from chapter 9.","Job’s despair is real but is not the last word. Verse 9 says that “the righteous also shall hold on his way, and he that hath clean hands shall be stronger and stronger,” which is the quiet faith that carries through the whole book."]},
"job.18":{title:"How long will it be ere ye make an end of words?",year:"Date uncertain",
 sum:"Bildad speaks a second time, offended at Job’s words—“Shall the earth be forsaken for thee?”—He describes the fate of the wicked: the light of the wicked is put out, snares and traps surround him—He is driven from light into darkness and has no son or nephew among his people.",
 bg:["Bildad’s long description of the wicked (18:5–21) uses images of a lamp going out, a trap closing, and a house falling. He is describing what Job seems to be, and he makes it hurtful by saying the wicked man leaves no “son nor nephew” (18:19), which Job’s grief makes especially painful.","The “king of terrors” in verse 14 is a poetic name for death. The Hebrew of several verses in this chapter is difficult, and translations differ in the details."]},
"job.19":{title:"I know that my redeemer liveth",year:"Date uncertain",
 sum:"Job says his friends are tormenting him and that God has overthrown him and removed his hope—His brethren, servants, wife, and even young children turn from him—“Have pity upon me, O ye my friends”—“I know that my redeemer liveth, and that he shall stand at the latter day upon the earth”—“In my flesh shall I see God.”",
 bg:["“Redeemer” is the Hebrew go’el, a kinsman whose duty was to protect a family member in trouble, buy back lost property, or avenge a wrong (compare Ruth 3:9–13). Job is certain that a defender will rise for him. Latter-day Saints, like many Christians, see this as a testimony of Jesus Christ, and of resurrection; the words are famously set in Handel’s Messiah.","The Hebrew of verses 25–27 is difficult, and translations differ, especially on whether Job sees God “in my flesh” or “apart from my flesh.” Either way, he expresses confidence that he will see God and that his case will be vindicated."]},
"job.20":{title:"The triumphing of the wicked is short",year:"Date uncertain",
 sum:"Zophar speaks again, feeling insulted by Job’s reproach—“The triumphing of the wicked is short, and the joy of the hypocrite but for a moment”—Though wickedness is sweet in his mouth, it turns to the gall of asps within him—He must give back what he took by violence, and heaven shall reveal his iniquity.",
 bg:["Zophar repeats the friends’ main teaching: the wicked flourish only briefly and then are brought down (20:5, 29). It is often true in the long run, but it is not a rule that explains every case, and Job answers it in the next chapter.","The image of wickedness as food that is sweet at first and then poison (20:12–16) is vivid. The Hebrew of this chapter is among the harder parts of the book."]},
"job.21":{title:"Wherefore do the wicked live, become old?",year:"Date uncertain",
 sum:"Job asks his friends to listen, since that would be their consolation—“Wherefore do the wicked live, become old, yea, are mighty in power?”—They prosper and die in peace, and say to God, “Depart from us”—The friends’ claim that God punishes the wicked in this life fails the test of experience—Their comfort is vain, “seeing in your answers there remaineth falsehood.”",
 bg:["This is Job’s direct answer to the friends’ whole theory. He points to wicked people who seem to prosper (21:7–13) and says plainly that life does not always match the friends’ rule. The book itself agrees with Job on this point (42:7).","Verse 26 says that rich and poor alike “lie down alike in the dust.” Job recognizes that death is the same for all, so only God can finally set things right."]},
"job.22":{title:"Acquaint now thyself with him, and be at peace",year:"Date uncertain",
 sum:"Eliphaz speaks a third time and now accuses Job of great sins—He says Job has taken pledges, denied bread to the hungry, and sent widows away empty—Does God see through the dark cloud?—“Acquaint now thyself with him, and be at peace”—If Job returns to the Almighty and puts away iniquity, he shall lay up gold and pray, and God will hear him.",
 bg:["Eliphaz has no evidence for the sins he lists (22:5–9). He invents them because his theory demands that Job must be guilty. This is the clearest example of how the friends’ view goes wrong when it is applied to a real person.","Verses 21–30 contain good advice in themselves: draw near to God, receive his law, and pray. The tragedy is that Job is already doing this, and has been all along (see Job 1:1, 5)."]},
"job.23":{title:"When he hath tried me, I shall come forth as gold",year:"Date uncertain",
 sum:"Job replies that his complaint is bitter and that he longs to find God and set his cause in order before him—He goes forward and backward but cannot see him—“But he knoweth the way that I take: when he hath tried me, I shall come forth as gold”—His foot has held God’s steps, and he has treasured God’s words more than his food—Yet he is terrified before him.",
 bg:["“I shall come forth as gold” (23:10) is a refiner’s image: fire removes the dross and leaves the pure metal. Job does not claim to be perfect, but he is confident that God’s testing will show he has been faithful.","Job’s experience of not being able to find God, “Behold, I go forward, but he is not there” (23:8–9), is honest and familiar to many believers. D&C 121–122 teaches that the Lord is still near even when he seems silent, and that trials can be for our good (D&C 122:7)."]},
"job.24":{title:"Men groan from out of the city",year:"Date uncertain",
 sum:"Job asks why those who know God do not see his days of judgment—The wicked remove landmarks, seize the flocks, and drive away the ass of the fatherless—The poor are forced out of the way and go about naked—“Men groan from out of the city, and the soul of the wounded crieth out”—Yet God does not charge them with folly; the wicked are in the end brought low.",
 bg:["This chapter is a list of injustices in the world that seem to go unpunished: moving boundary stones (24:2), taking a widow’s ox, and leaving the poor without shelter. The Old Testament law forbade moving landmarks (Deuteronomy 19:14).","Some of the verses, especially 24:18–25, sound more like the friends’ teaching than Job’s earlier words. Scholars debate whether the text has been disturbed or whether Job is quoting or answering the friends. The Hebrew is also hard here."]},
"job.25":{title:"How then can man be justified with God?",year:"Date uncertain",
 sum:"Bildad gives his third and shortest speech—Dominion and fear are with God, who makes peace in his high places—“Is there any number of his armies?”—“How then can man be justified with God? or how can he be clean that is born of a woman?”—Even the moon and stars are not pure in his sight, much less man, who is a worm.",
 bg:["This speech is only six verses long. The friends are running out of arguments. Some scholars think part of the missing speech from Bildad or Zophar has become mixed into chapters 24 and 26–27, but the text as we have it does not say so.","Bildad’s point that no man is perfectly pure before God is true. But he uses it to say Job’s suffering is deserved, and the Lord rejects that reasoning at the end of the book."]},
"job.26":{title:"Hangeth the earth upon nothing",year:"Date uncertain",
 sum:"Job answers Bildad with irony: “How hast thou helped him that is without power?”—He speaks of God’s power over the dead below, who tremble—God stretches out the north over empty space and “hangeth the earth upon nothing”—He binds up the waters in thick clouds and divides the sea with his power—“These are parts of his ways: but how little a portion is heard of him?”",
 bg:["“hangeth the earth upon nothing” (26:7) is a poetic way of saying that God holds the world in place without visible support. The ancient authors were not giving a science lesson, but the line is often noticed because it fits what we now know about the earth in space.","“Dead things” in verse 5 translates the Hebrew Rephaim, the shades of the dead in Sheol. “Rahab” in verse 12 is another name, like Leviathan, for the chaos sea monster in ancient poetry. Both are used to say that God is stronger than anything frightening."]},
"job.27":{title:"Till I die I will not remove mine integrity from me",year:"Date uncertain",
 sum:"Job swears by the living God that his lips will not speak wickedness—“Till I die I will not remove mine integrity from me”—He refuses to say his friends are right and holds his righteousness fast—The portion of the wicked and oppressor is destruction, though their wealth may grow for a time—Their children and riches will not last.",
 bg:["No third speech of Zophar appears in the book, and 27:13–23 sounds much like what the friends have been saying about the wicked. Scholars debate whether Job is quoting his friends, whether the text was disturbed, or whether Job is stating that he does believe the wicked ultimately fall but denies that he is one of them.","Job’s refusal to say he is guilty when he is not is an example of integrity. The Hebrew word tom (“integrity”) is the same one used by God of Job in 1:8 and 2:3."]},
"job.28":{title:"Where shall wisdom be found?",year:"Date uncertain",
 sum:"Man digs mines and brings silver, gold, iron, and sapphires out of the earth—“But where shall wisdom be found? and where is the place of understanding?”—It cannot be bought with gold or onyx, and it is hid from the eyes of all living—God understands the way thereof—“Behold, the fear of the Lord, that is wisdom; and to depart from evil is understanding.”",
 bg:["This beautiful poem about wisdom stands apart from the arguments around it. It is not labeled with a speaker, and scholars debate whether it is Job speaking, the narrator, or a later addition. Its message is that human ingenuity can dig deep into the earth, but true wisdom comes only from God.","Verse 28 gives the book’s answer: “the fear of the Lord, that is wisdom.” The same idea appears in Proverbs 9:10. In scripture, “fear” of the Lord means reverence and obedience, not terror."]},
"job.29":{title:"Oh that I were as in months past",year:"Date uncertain",
 sum:"Job speaks again and longs for the days when God watched over him and his candle shone upon his head—Young men hid themselves and princes were silent when he came to the gate—He delivered the poor and fatherless, and “I was eyes to the blind, and feet was I to the lame”—He expected to die in his nest, with honor and with counsel waiting for him.",
 bg:["Chapters 29–31 are Job’s final defense, in three parts: how he used to be (29), how he is treated now (30), and his solemn oath of innocence (31). He is speaking now to God and to the readers, not to the friends.","The “gate” (29:7) was the center of a city’s life, where elders heard disputes and made decisions. Job’s honored seat there shows how much of his earlier good life was spent helping others, which makes his sudden fall all the harder."]},
"job.30":{title:"And now my soul is poured out upon me",year:"Date uncertain",
 sum:"Now Job is mocked by those younger than he, whose fathers he would not have set with his dogs—He is a byword; they spit in his face and set up against him—“And now my soul is poured out upon me”—He cries to God and God does not answer; he looked for good and evil came—His skin is black upon him, and his harp is turned to mourning.",
 bg:["The first half of the chapter describes the contempt of outcasts who now mock Job. In the ancient world honor and shame were everything, and to lose them was a second kind of suffering beside the physical pain.","Verses 20–23 are some of Job’s most painful words, accusing God of becoming cruel to him. The book allows this complaint to stand, and the Lord’s later response does not scold Job for sincerity. Some Hebrew in the chapter is obscure."]},
"job.31":{title:"I made a covenant with mine eyes",year:"Date uncertain",
 sum:"Job swears an oath of innocence—“I made a covenant with mine eyes; why then should I think upon a maid?”—He has not walked with vanity, nor deceived, nor wronged his servants, nor withheld from the poor, widow, or fatherless—He has not trusted in gold or worshipped the sun and moon—“Oh that one would hear me! behold, my desire is, that the Almighty would answer me”—“The words of Job are ended.”",
 bg:["This chapter is a long list of “if I have...” oaths, a kind of formal self-curse that ancient people used to swear innocence. It shows Job’s standards: not only avoiding sins in act, but also in thought (31:1), honesty, kindness to servants (31:13–15), and care for the poor.","In verse 35 Job signs his case: “my desire is, that the Almighty would answer me.” The word for “desire” is literally his “mark” or “signature” (tav). The next speakers in the book are Elihu and then God, who does answer."]},
"job.32":{title:"Great men are not always wise",year:"Date uncertain",
 sum:"The three friends cease answering because Job is righteous in his own eyes—Elihu the son of Barachel the Buzite, a younger man, is angry with Job and with the friends—He waited because they were older, but “there is a spirit in man: and the inspiration of the Almighty giveth them understanding”—“Great men are not always wise”—He is full of matter and must speak.",
 bg:["Elihu appears suddenly and is not mentioned in the opening or closing chapters. His name means “he is my God,” and his speeches (32–37) are the part of the book scholars most often debate, some seeing them as a later addition and others as an original part of the design.","Verse 8 says, “there is a spirit in man: and the inspiration of the Almighty giveth them understanding.” Latter-day Saints may think of D&C 88:11–13, which speaks of the light of Christ that gives understanding to all, though Elihu is only talking about understanding coming from God, not about that doctrine."]},
"job.33":{title:"The Spirit of God hath made me",year:"Date uncertain",
 sum:"Elihu invites Job to answer him—“The Spirit of God hath made me, and the breath of the Almighty hath given me life”—He quotes Job’s claim of innocence and replies that God is greater than man—God speaks in dreams and through pain to turn man from his purpose and save his soul from the pit—If an interpreter, one among a thousand, is with him, God is gracious and says, “Deliver him from going down to the pit: I have found a ransom.”",
 bg:["Elihu offers a different explanation of suffering than the friends. It is not just a punishment but can be a warning and a way God draws a person back (33:14–30). That is a thoughtful idea, but the Lord’s speeches later show that Job’s case still goes beyond this.","The “interpreter” and “ransom” (33:23–24) are often read by Christians as pointing toward Christ. The text itself does not explain who the interpreter is, and scholars debate its meaning."]},
"job.34":{title:"Far be it from God, that he should do wickedness",year:"Date uncertain",
 sum:"Elihu calls on the wise to hear him and says Job has claimed, “I am righteous: and God hath taken away my judgment”—“Far be it from God, that he should do wickedness”—God judges all people without partiality, and he sees every step—He cannot be taken to court by anyone, for he is above kings and princes—Elihu accuses Job of adding rebellion to his sin.",
 bg:["Elihu defends God’s justice, and in verses 10–12 states one of the book’s central truths: “Yea, surely God will not do wickedly, neither will the Almighty pervert judgment.” This is true, and the Lord later agrees that God is just.","But Elihu also misrepresents Job, saying he drinks up scorning “like water” (34:7), and he treats Job’s honest questions as rebellion. As with the friends, he applies correct ideas in the wrong way."]},
"job.35":{title:"Who giveth songs in the night",year:"Date uncertain",
 sum:"Elihu says Job has claimed his righteousness is more than God’s—He tells Job to look at the heavens: if he sins, what does he do to God, and if he is righteous, what does he give him?—People cry out under oppression but do not say, “Where is God my maker, who giveth songs in the night?”—God does not hear empty cries.",
 bg:["“Songs in the night” (35:10) is a beautiful phrase for the comfort and praise that God can give even in dark times. Elihu uses it to suggest that people cry out in pain but forget to seek God himself.","Elihu argues that God is too high to be harmed by our sins or helped by our righteousness (35:6–7), which is true in a sense, but the Bible elsewhere stresses that God cares about what happens to us (compare Moses 1:39)."]},
"job.36":{title:"Behold, God is mighty, and despiseth not any",year:"Date uncertain",
 sum:"Elihu says he has more to say on God’s behalf—“Behold, God is mighty, and despiseth not any”—He preserves the afflicted and judges the wicked, and he opens their ear to discipline—Elihu warns Job against turning to iniquity because of affliction—God teaches, and who can teach like him?—He lifts the waters into the clouds and rains upon men abundantly.",
 bg:["Elihu now turns from arguing with Job to praising God’s greatness. In 36:22–33 he moves into poetry about rain, clouds, and lightning that prepares for the storm in which the Lord speaks in chapter 38.","Verse 15 says, “He delivereth the poor in his affliction, and openeth their ears in oppression.” Elihu teaches that God uses suffering to open people’s hearts. Latter-day Saints may compare this with the Lord’s counsel to Joseph Smith in D&C 122:7."]},
"job.37":{title:"Stand still, and consider the wondrous works of God",year:"Date uncertain",
 sum:"Elihu’s heart trembles at God’s thunder, which he directs under the whole heaven—He bids the snow fall upon the earth and the great rain come, so that all men may know his work—“Stand still, and consider the wondrous works of God”—Can Job spread out the sky or teach God what to say?—“Touching the Almighty, we cannot find him out: he is excellent in power.”",
 bg:["Elihu’s final speech is full of weather: thunder, snow, rain, clouds, ice, and the south wind. Ancient people saw these as signs of God’s power. This sets the stage for the Lord’s speech out of the whirlwind that follows.","Verses 21–24 describe the bright sky after a storm, and a fearful splendor “with God.” The Hebrew is hard to translate and the verses can be read in different ways. They bring the section to a close with the thought that God’s ways are beyond us, which the Lord then says himself."]},
"job.38":{title:"Where wast thou when I laid the foundations of the earth?",year:"Date uncertain",
 sum:"The Lord answers Job out of the whirlwind—“Where wast thou when I laid the foundations of the earth? declare, if thou hast understanding”—When the morning stars sang together, “all the sons of God shouted for joy”—He set bounds for the sea, commands the morning, and knows the gates of death—He asks about snow, rain, constellations, and who provides for the raven.",
 bg:["This is the first of the Lord’s two speeches from the whirlwind (38:1–40:2 and 40:6–41:34). He does not explain why Job suffered, and he does not accuse him of sin. He asks questions that reveal how much of creation Job, and all of us, did not witness and cannot control.","In verse 7 the “sons of God” shout for joy at creation. The text does not explain who they are; the same phrase appears in 1:6. Some Latter-day Saints connect it with the premortal life, but this is an interpretation, not something Job states. Verse 31 names the Pleiades and Orion, as in 9:9, with the same uncertainty about the Hebrew terms."]},
"job.39":{title:"Hast thou given the horse strength?",year:"Date uncertain",
 sum:"The Lord continues with wild creatures: do you know when the mountain goats bear young, or who set the wild ass free?—The unicorn will not serve you or bed at your crib—The ostrich leaves her eggs, because God has deprived her of wisdom—“Hast thou given the horse strength?”—The hawk and the eagle fly and nest by his command.",
 bg:["These are all animals that live free of human control. The point is that God cares for creatures that Job has never tended, and that the world does not revolve around human understanding. The Hebrew re’em (“unicorn” in KJV, 39:9) is likely a wild ox, or aurochs, a large animal now extinct.","The war horse in verses 19–25 is one of the most vivid passages in the book: he “saith among the trumpets, Ha, ha” and “smelleth the battle afar off.” The ostrich passage (39:13–18) is hard to translate and the ancient Hebrew is obscure in places."]},
"job.40":{title:"Behold now behemoth, which I made with thee",year:"Date uncertain",
 sum:"The Lord asks whether the one who contends with the Almighty will instruct him—Job answers, “Behold, I am vile; what shall I answer thee? I will lay mine hand upon my mouth”—The Lord speaks again out of the whirlwind and asks if Job can have an arm like God’s—“Behold now behemoth, which I made with thee”—Behemoth eats grass like an ox and is chief of the ways of God.",
 bg:["Behemoth is a Hebrew plural form of behemah, “beast,” and here the word seems to mean a great and powerful animal. Scholars debate what is meant: some suggest the hippopotamus, others a mythic creature symbolizing strength. The text only says that God made it and that it is beyond Job’s control.","Job’s response in 40:3–5 is brief: he puts his hand on his mouth and says no more. The Hebrew word translated “vile” means “small” or “of little weight.” He feels how little he knows, and does not yet say more until chapter 42."]},
"job.41":{title:"Canst thou draw out leviathan with an hook?",year:"Date uncertain",
 sum:"The Lord describes Leviathan and asks whether Job can draw it out with a hook or make a covenant with it—None is so fierce that dare stir him up—His scales are shut up together, out of his mouth go burning lamps, and in his neck remaineth strength—Nothing on earth is like him: “he is a king over all the children of pride.”",
 bg:["Leviathan appears elsewhere in scripture (Psalm 74:14; 104:26; Isaiah 27:1) as a sea creature or a symbol of chaos. Scholars debate what is meant here: it may be the crocodile, a whale, or a mythic sea monster, or all of these at once. The point is that this being is powerful and terrifying, yet God made it.","The Lord’s point is that if Job cannot stand before Leviathan, he cannot stand before the One who made it (41:10). It is not meant as a threat, but as an invitation to trust a God whose wisdom and power go beyond human understanding."]},
"job.42":{title:"The LORD gave Job twice as much as he had before",year:"Date uncertain",
 sum:"Job answers: “I know that thou canst do every thing”—“I have heard of thee by the hearing of the ear: but now mine eye seeth thee”—The Lord says Eliphaz and his friends have not spoken of him “the thing that is right, as my servant Job hath”—Job prays for his friends and the Lord turns his captivity and gives him twice as much as before—He has seven sons and three daughters and lives 140 years more.",
 bg:["In 42:7 the Lord rebukes the three friends for not speaking rightly of him as Job did. The friends’ view, that suffering always means punishment, is clearly rejected. Job’s repentance in verse 6 is hard to translate (“abhor myself” can also mean “retract” or “despise my words”), and scholars debate the exact sense. James 5:11 says we have seen “the end of the Lord,” that he is “very pitiful, and of tender mercy.”","The daughters’ names in 42:14 are Jemima, Keziah, and Keren-happuch. They are commonly given as “dove,” “cassia” (a fragrant spice, like cinnamon), and “horn of eye-paint” (a beauty product). Unusually, Job gave them an inheritance among their brothers (42:15). D&C 121:7–8 and 122:7–9, the Lord’s words to Joseph Smith in Liberty Jail, teach that adversity can be “but a small moment” and can give experience and be for our good."]},
"ps.1":{title:"Like a tree planted by the rivers of water",year:"Date uncertain",
 sum:"“Blessed is the man” who does not walk with the ungodly but delights in the law of the LORD—He is “like a tree planted by the rivers of water,” fruitful and green—The ungodly are like chaff the wind drives away—“The LORD knoweth the way of the righteous: but the way of the ungodly shall perish.”",
 bg:["Psalm 1 is a wisdom psalm and serves as a doorway to the whole book of Psalms. Hebrew poetry rests on parallel lines, where the second line repeats, deepens or contrasts the first, rather than on rhyme. Notice the progression in verse 1: walking, standing, then sitting with the scornful.","The word for “blessed” (Hebrew ashre) is often translated “happy” and describes a settled well-being. The psalm has no title, so its author and date are unknown. Its two roads, one that stands and one that “shall perish,” echo Lehi’s teaching of two ways in 2 Nephi 2:27."]},
"ps.2":{title:"Thou art my Son; this day have I begotten thee",year:"Date uncertain",
 sum:"The nations rage against “the LORD, and against his anointed”—He who sits in the heavens laughs and speaks in wrath—“Yet have I set my king upon my holy hill of Zion”—The Lord’s Son is given the nations as his inheritance—“Kiss the Son”—“Blessed are all they that put their trust in him.”",
 bg:["This is a royal psalm, probably used when a new king of David’s line was crowned. “Anointed” translates the Hebrew mashiach (Messiah), and Zion is the hill of Jerusalem where the temple stood. The Hebrew of “Kiss the Son” in verse 12 is hard to translate, and some versions render it differently, but the call is to submit to the king.","The New Testament applies this psalm to Jesus Christ. Psalm 2:1–2 is quoted in Acts 4:25–26, and Psalm 2:7 is quoted in Acts 13:33 and Hebrews 1:5 (also Hebrews 5:5). The “rod of iron” of verse 9 reappears in Revelation 2:27."]},
"ps.3":{title:"Thou, O LORD, art a shield for me",year:"Attributed to David",
 sum:"David, fleeing from Absalom, laments that many rise against him and say there is no help for him in God—The LORD is “a shield for me; my glory, and the lifter up of mine head”—“I laid me down and slept; I awaked; for the LORD sustained me”—“Salvation belongeth unto the LORD.”",
 bg:["The title ties this psalm to David’s flight from his son Absalom’s rebellion, told in 2 Samuel 15–18. It is a lament that turns to trust, and it is traditionally read as a morning prayer because of verse 5. Verse 7 speaks of striking enemies on the “cheek bone,” a vivid way of saying God defeats them, and the Psalms often speak this way in poetry.","Selah is a Hebrew word of unknown meaning. It is probably a pause or a musical direction, and it appears three times in this psalm."]},
"ps.4":{title:"I will both lay me down in peace, and sleep",year:"Attributed to David",
 sum:"David asks God to hear him, since he “hast enlarged me when I was in distress”—He warns those who turn his glory to shame to “stand in awe, and sin not”—He asks for the light of God’s countenance and finds more gladness than in harvest—“I will both lay me down in peace, and sleep.”",
 bg:["The title directs the psalm “to the chief Musician on Neginoth.” Neginoth comes from a Hebrew word for stringed instruments, so the psalm was sung with harp or lyre accompaniment. It is an evening prayer, the companion to Psalm 3’s morning prayer.","Verse 4, “Stand in awe, and sin not,” can also be translated “Be ye angry, and sin not,” and Paul quotes the Greek version in Ephesians 4:26. “Leasing” in verse 2 is an old English word for lies."]},
"ps.5":{title:"In the morning will I direct my prayer unto thee",year:"Attributed to David",
 sum:"David prays at morning for God to hear his cry—God hates wickedness and destroys liars and the bloody and deceitful—David comes into God’s house in his mercy—He asks to be led in righteousness and that those who trust in God rejoice—“With favour wilt thou compass him as with a shield.”",
 bg:["The title names Nehiloth, a word that probably refers to flutes or wind instruments, though its meaning is uncertain. David’s words about enemies in verses 9–10, including “Destroy thou them, O God,” are honest cries to God from someone being lied about, not a pattern for taking revenge. He leaves the judgment to God.","The phrase “their throat is an open sepulchre” (verse 9) is quoted by Paul in Romans 3:13 when he describes humanity’s need for the gospel."]},
"ps.6":{title:"O LORD, rebuke me not in thine anger",year:"Attributed to David",
 sum:"David, weak and in anguish, asks the LORD not to rebuke him in anger—“I water my couch with my tears”—He asks for deliverance because the dead do not give thanks in the grave—He becomes confident that “the LORD hath heard the voice of my weeping.”",
 bg:["This is a lament, and church tradition since ancient times has counted it among the seven “penitential psalms” (6, 32, 38, 51, 102, 130, 143). The Bible does not say what David’s trouble was. In verse 5 he says the dead cannot praise God, which reflects the limited view of the afterlife common in the earlier Old Testament psalms.","Sheminith in the title means “the eighth.” It may refer to an eight-stringed instrument or a musical pitch, but scholars are not sure. Neginoth means stringed instruments."]},
"ps.7":{title:"The righteous God trieth the hearts and reins",year:"Attributed to David",
 sum:"David, pursued by an enemy “like a lion,” asks God to judge him if he has done wrong—He calls on the LORD to arise and judge the peoples—“God judgeth the righteous”—The wicked fall into the pit they dug—“I will praise the LORD according to his righteousness.”",
 bg:["Shiggaion is a Hebrew word of uncertain meaning, perhaps a passionate or wandering song. The title mentions “Cush the Benjamite,” who is not named anywhere else in the Bible. Many readers connect the psalm to David’s troubles with Saul’s Benjamite household (1 Samuel 24 or 26), but the psalm itself does not say.","This is a prayer of someone falsely accused. His request that evil “return upon his own head” (verse 16) is an honest cry for justice, and he gives the judging to God. “Reins” in verse 9 means kidneys, ancient shorthand for a person’s inner feelings."]},
"ps.8":{title:"What is man, that thou art mindful of him?",year:"Attributed to David",
 sum:"“O LORD our Lord, how excellent is thy name in all the earth”—Strength is ordained “out of the mouth of babes and sucklings”—Looking at the heavens, David asks, “What is man?”—Man is crowned with glory and given dominion over the works of God’s hands.",
 bg:["This is a hymn of praise. Gittith in the title may mean “of Gath” or relate to a winepress, so it may be a tune or instrument, but the meaning is uncertain. The first “LORD” is the divine name YHWH and the second “Lord” is Adonai, “master.”","The New Testament uses this psalm in several places. Jesus quotes verse 2 in Matthew 21:16, and Psalm 8:4–6 is quoted in Hebrews 2:6–8, where it is applied to Jesus. The thought of man given dominion over the animals echoes Genesis 1:26–28. The Hebrew for “angels” in verse 5 is elohim, which can also mean God or gods."]},
"ps.9":{title:"I will praise thee, O LORD, with my whole heart",year:"Attributed to David",
 sum:"David praises the LORD with his whole heart for upholding his cause—The LORD rebukes the nations and reigns forever as judge—He is “a refuge for the oppressed”—“Sing praises to the LORD, which dwelleth in Zion”—“The needy shall not alway be forgotten.”",
 bg:["Psalms 9 and 10 were probably once a single poem, since together they follow, with some gaps, the Hebrew alphabet from line to line (an acrostic). Muth-labben in the title is probably “death of the son,” but nobody knows what it refers to. “Higgaion” in verse 16 probably means a meditation or the sound of a musical instrument, and “Selah” is a Hebrew word of unknown meaning, probably a pause.","Zion was the name of the hill in Jerusalem where David placed the ark. Calling it “the daughter of Zion” (verse 14) was a way of speaking of the city and its people."]},
"ps.10":{title:"Why standest thou afar off, O LORD?",year:"Date uncertain",
 sum:"The psalmist asks why the LORD hides in times of trouble—The proud wicked persecute the poor and think God does not see—The wicked ambush the helpless—“Arise, O LORD; O God, lift up thine hand: forget not the humble”—“The LORD is King for ever and ever.”",
 bg:["This psalm has no title, which fits with its being the second half of the alphabetic poem begun in Psalm 9. It is a lament about injustice, and it is honest about the feeling that God is far away. It asks God to “break thou the arm of the wicked” (verse 15), a plea for him to stop oppression, not a call to take revenge into our own hands.","The psalm ends in confidence that God hears “the desire of the humble” and cares for the fatherless and oppressed (verses 17–18). Throughout the Bible, God shows special concern for the poor and powerless."]},
"ps.11":{title:"In the LORD put I my trust",year:"Attributed to David",
 sum:"David trusts in the LORD when friends advise him to flee like a bird to the mountains—The wicked shoot at the upright in secret—“If the foundations be destroyed, what can the righteous do?”—The LORD is in his holy temple and tries the righteous—“His countenance doth behold the upright.”",
 bg:["This is a psalm of trust. Friends tell David to “flee as a bird to your mountain,” while David chooses to rely on God, who sees everything from his heavenly temple. The image of “fire and brimstone” in verse 6 recalls the destruction of Sodom in Genesis 19:24.","Psalm 11 does not say which crisis lay behind it. A well-known idea in the Old Testament is that God tests people, as in verse 5, in order to purify them, as one tests metal."]},
"ps.12":{title:"The words of the LORD are pure words",year:"Attributed to David",
 sum:"David cries, “Help, LORD; for the godly man ceaseth”—Flattering and proud lips deceive the neighbor—The LORD will arise for the oppression of the poor—“The words of the LORD are pure words: as silver tried in a furnace”—The LORD will preserve his people.",
 bg:["This is a lament about a society where truth has broken down: people speak “with flattering lips and with a double heart” (the Hebrew says “with a heart and a heart”). God answers directly in verse 5, saying he will rise up to protect the poor. The title mentions Sheminith, “the eighth,” a musical term of uncertain meaning.","Verse 6 compares God’s words to silver refined in a furnace “seven times,” a way of saying completely pure."]},
"ps.13":{title:"How long wilt thou forget me, O LORD?",year:"Attributed to David",
 sum:"David asks four times, “How long?” as God seems to forget him—He asks God to consider and lighten his eyes lest he sleep the sleep of death—He turns to trust, saying, “I have trusted in thy mercy”—“I will sing unto the LORD, because he hath dealt bountifully with me.”",
 bg:["This short psalm is a clear example of a lament. It moves in three steps: complaint (verses 1–2), request (verses 3–4), and trust and praise (verses 5–6). The Hebrew word for “how long” is repeated four times, which gives the feeling of waiting in pain.","The psalm shows that faith can speak honestly to God about feeling forgotten, and that praise can follow even before the answer comes. “Lighten mine eyes” in verse 3 refers to the renewal of strength and hope."]},
"ps.14":{title:"The fool hath said in his heart, There is no God",year:"Attributed to David",
 sum:"The fool says in his heart that there is no God, and humanity has become corrupt—The LORD looks down to see if any seek God—“There is none that doeth good, no, not one”—The workers of iniquity devour God’s people—“Oh that the salvation of Israel were come out of Zion”",
 bg:["In the Bible a “fool” (Hebrew nabal) is not someone who lacks intelligence but someone who lives as though God does not matter. The verse says the fool says it “in his heart,” which refers to how he lives more than to a debate. Psalm 14 is repeated almost word for word in Psalm 53, with some changes in the divine name.","Paul quotes verses 1–3 in Romans 3:10–12 to show that all people need Christ. Zion in verse 7 is Jerusalem, where God’s help was expected to come from."]},
"ps.15":{title:"LORD, who shall abide in thy tabernacle?",year:"Attributed to David",
 sum:"The psalmist asks who may dwell in the LORD’s tabernacle and holy hill—The one who walks uprightly, speaks truth and does not slander his neighbor—He honors those who fear the LORD, keeps his oath even to his hurt and takes no bribes—“He that doeth these things shall never be moved.”",
 bg:["This is an entrance psalm of the kind used by pilgrims who came to worship at the sanctuary. It asks about character, not ritual. Listing ten qualities, it speaks of honest speech, loyalty to a promise, fairness with money and refusing bribes. “Usury” in verse 5 meant charging interest to a poor neighbor, which the law of Moses forbade (Exodus 22:25).","Psalm 24:3–5 asks a similar question. Latter-day Saints often connect these psalms with temple worship, where covenant promises are made and kept."]},
"ps.16":{title:"Thou wilt shew me the path of life",year:"Attributed to David",
 sum:"David trusts in God and delights in the saints—“The LORD is the portion of mine inheritance and of my cup”—“The lines are fallen unto me in pleasant places”—“I have set the LORD always before me”—“Thou wilt not leave my soul in hell; neither wilt thou suffer thine Holy One to see corruption”—“In thy presence is fulness of joy.”",
 bg:["Michtam in the title is a Hebrew word of uncertain meaning. Some think it means “a golden poem,” others “an engraved inscription.” The “lines” of verse 6 are the measuring cords used to divide the promised land among the tribes, so David is saying that God himself is his inheritance.","Peter quotes Psalm 16:8–11 in Acts 2:25–28, and Paul quotes verse 10 in Acts 13:35, applying it to the resurrection of Jesus Christ, whose body did not see corruption. “Hell” in verse 10 translates Sheol, the place of departed spirits."]},
"ps.17":{title:"Keep me as the apple of the eye",year:"Attributed to David",
 sum:"David prays for God to hear his right cause and test his heart—He has kept from the paths of the destroyer and asks to be held in God’s paths—“Keep me as the apple of the eye, hide me under the shadow of thy wings”—The wicked surround him like a lion—“I shall be satisfied, when I awake, with thy likeness.”",
 bg:["This is the prayer of a person who is falsely attacked and appeals to God as judge. The “apple of the eye” translates a Hebrew phrase meaning “the little man of the eye,” the pupil, the part we protect most instinctively. The “shadow of thy wings” is the image of a mother bird sheltering her young.","Verse 15 closes with hope to see God’s face and to awake in his likeness. Many readers see here a hope for life after death, and the psalm does not explain further."]},
"ps.18":{title:"The LORD is my rock, and my fortress, and my deliverer",year:"Attributed to David",
 sum:"David praises the LORD as his rock, fortress and high tower—When he cried in distress, the earth shook and God came down in storm and fire to deliver him—He recounts walking in the LORD’s ways—God girds him with strength and makes his way perfect—“The LORD liveth; and blessed be my rock.”",
 bg:["The title says David sang this song “in the day that the Lord delivered him from the hand of all his enemies, and from the hand of Saul.” The same song appears, with small variations, in 2 Samuel 22. It is a thanksgiving and royal psalm, and its storm imagery (verses 7–15) shows God coming to rescue like a great thunderstorm.","“Horn of my salvation” (verse 2) pictures the horn of a strong animal as a symbol of strength. David’s claim of righteousness in verses 20–24 is not a claim of being sinless. It speaks of his loyalty to God’s covenant during the conflict with Saul."]},
"ps.19":{title:"The heavens declare the glory of God",year:"Attributed to David",
 sum:"The heavens and firmament declare God’s glory, and their voice goes out to all the earth—The sun is like a bridegroom coming out of his chamber—The law of the LORD is perfect, converting the soul, and more desirable than gold—David asks to be cleansed of secret faults and presumptuous sins—“Let the words of my mouth, and the meditation of my heart, be acceptable in thy sight.”",
 bg:["Hebrew poetry rests on parallel lines, and this psalm shows it well: “Day unto day uttereth speech, and night unto night sheweth knowledge.” The psalm has two halves, creation speaking without words (verses 1–6) and the law of the LORD speaking with words (verses 7–14). It is both praise and wisdom.","Paul quotes verse 4 in Romans 10:18 about the gospel going to all the earth. The word “law” is Hebrew torah, which means “instruction.” The psalm’s final verse is often used as a prayer before speaking."]},
"ps.20":{title:"Some trust in chariots, and some in horses",year:"Attributed to David",
 sum:"The people pray that the LORD will hear the king in the day of trouble and send help out of Zion—They ask that he remember his offerings and fulfill his petitions—“Now know I that the LORD saveth his anointed”—“Some trust in chariots, and some in horses: but we will remember the name of the LORD”—“Save, LORD: let the king hear us.”",
 bg:["This is a royal psalm, apparently sung before a king went to battle. The people sing the first part to the king, and the king or priest answers in verse 6. “Zion” is the hill of Jerusalem where the sanctuary stood, and “the name of the God of Jacob” means his power and presence.","Chariots and horses were the strongest military technology of the day, so verse 7 is a deliberate contrast: trust God, not weapons. Selah in verse 3 is a Hebrew word of unknown meaning, probably a pause or musical direction."]},
"ps.21":{title:"The king shall joy in thy strength, O LORD",year:"Attributed to David",
 sum:"The king rejoices in the LORD’s strength and salvation—God gave him his heart’s desire, a crown of pure gold and length of days—“For the king trusteth in the LORD”—The king’s enemies will be consumed—“Be thou exalted, LORD, in thine own strength.”",
 bg:["This royal psalm is a thanksgiving, perhaps paired with Psalm 20, that was sung after a victory or on a king’s coronation anniversary. “Preventest” in verse 3 is old English for “come to meet,” so the line means God met the king with good things.","The psalm gives the credit to God, not the king. Latter-day Saints often read royal psalms as also pointing to Jesus Christ, the ultimate King, though this psalm never says so itself. Its language about enemies in verses 8–12 belongs to ancient royal poetry and describes God’s justice, not a pattern for personal revenge."]},
"ps.22":{title:"My God, my God, why hast thou forsaken me?",year:"Attributed to David",
 sum:"David cries, “My God, my God, why hast thou forsaken me?”—Though the fathers trusted and were delivered, he is “a worm, and no man”—Enemies mock him, pierce his hands and feet, and part his garments—“Be not thou far from me, O LORD”—He promises to declare God’s name and all nations will turn to the LORD.",
 bg:["Aijeleth Shahar in the title means “hind of the dawn,” probably the name of a tune. Jesus spoke the opening words from the cross, recorded in Matthew 27:46 and Mark 15:34. The Gospels also echo other parts of the psalm: dividing the garments and casting lots (verse 18, in John 19:24), and the mockery and head-shaking (verses 7–8, in Matthew 27:39, 43).","The Hebrew wording of verse 16 (“they pierced my hands and my feet”) is debated by scholars, but early Greek translations read it as “pierced.” The psalm moves from agony to praise, and Hebrews 2:12 quotes verse 22 as the words of Christ."]},
"ps.23":{title:"The LORD is my shepherd; I shall not want",year:"Attributed to David",
 sum:"“The LORD is my shepherd; I shall not want”—He makes me lie down in green pastures, leads beside still waters and restores my soul—“Though I walk through the valley of the shadow of death, I will fear no evil”—A table is prepared and my cup runs over—“I will dwell in the house of the LORD for ever.”",
 bg:["David had been a shepherd, and the psalm uses his experience. The “rod” protected the flock and the “staff” guided it. “Valley of the shadow of death” could also be translated “valley of deep darkness,” and scholars disagree on the best rendering. In the second half the picture changes to a host who welcomes a guest and anoints his head with oil.","In John 10:11–16 Jesus calls himself “the good shepherd.” “For ever” in the last line literally means “for length of days.”"]},
"ps.24":{title:"Who shall ascend into the hill of the LORD?",year:"Attributed to David",
 sum:"“The earth is the LORD’s, and the fulness thereof”—Who may ascend the hill of the LORD? “He that hath clean hands, and a pure heart”—He shall receive the Lord’s blessing—“Lift up your heads, O ye gates … and the King of glory shall come in”—The LORD of hosts is the King of glory.",
 bg:["This psalm was probably sung as worshipers and the ark approached the sanctuary on Mount Zion, with one group asking the questions and another answering at the gates. It is a temple entrance psalm, and verses 3–4 describe the character needed to stand in God’s holy place. Latter-day Saints often connect it with the temple, where covenants are made.","Paul quotes verse 1 in 1 Corinthians 10:26. Christians have long read the “King of glory” entering the gates as Christ’s triumph. “LORD of hosts” (verse 10) means the commander of heavenly armies. Selah is a Hebrew word of unknown meaning, probably a pause."]},
"ps.25":{title:"Unto thee, O LORD, do I lift up my soul",year:"Attributed to David",
 sum:"David lifts up his soul to the LORD and asks to be taught God’s ways—He asks God to remember mercy and not the sins of his youth—“Good and upright is the LORD: therefore will he teach sinners in the way”—The meek are guided—He asks for pardon, deliverance and integrity—“Redeem Israel, O God, out of all his troubles.”",
 bg:["Psalm 25 is an acrostic: each verse begins with the next letter of the Hebrew alphabet (with a few irregularities), which suggests it was meant to be memorized. In English this is not visible. The prayer is a mix of lament and trust, asking for guidance and forgiveness.","Verse 14, “The secret of the LORD is with them that fear him,” says that God shares his counsel with those who respect him. “Fear” here means reverence, and not terror."]},
"ps.26":{title:"I will wash mine hands in innocency",year:"Attributed to David",
 sum:"David asks the LORD to judge him, for he has walked in integrity and trusted in the LORD—He asks to be examined and says he has not sat with the vain or the wicked—He will wash his hands in innocency and walk around God’s altar—“LORD, I have loved the habitation of thy house”—“My foot standeth in an even place.”",
 bg:["This is a prayer of someone who asks to be examined by God. It is not boasting. David is saying that he has been loyal to God’s covenant and is asking God to confirm it. The phrase “try my reins and my heart” uses “reins,” which means kidneys, an ancient way of speaking of inner feelings and desires.","Washing the hands before approaching the altar was an old sign of being clean to worship, and the psalm moves toward the temple. Verse 12 closes with confidence that the psalmist will bless the LORD in the congregations."]},
"ps.27":{title:"The LORD is my light and my salvation; whom shall I fear?",year:"Attributed to David",
 sum:"“The LORD is my light and my salvation; whom shall I fear?”—Though an army encamps against him, his heart will not fear—“One thing have I desired”: to dwell in the house of the LORD and behold his beauty—He asks God not to hide his face—“Wait on the LORD: be of good courage, and he shall strengthen thine heart.”",
 bg:["The psalm has two moods: bold confidence in verses 1–6 and a humble prayer for help in verses 7–14. Verse 4 shows the heart of the psalm, a longing to be in God’s house and to “behold the beauty of the LORD.” The “tabernacle” and “pavilion” of verse 5 are images of shelter in the sanctuary.","Verse 10 says, “When my father and my mother forsake me, then the LORD will take me up.” It says that God remains faithful even when family cannot. “Wait” in verse 14 means to hope and expect, not only to stand still."]},
"ps.28":{title:"The LORD is my strength and my shield",year:"Attributed to David",
 sum:"David cries to the LORD, his rock, and lifts his hands toward the holy oracle—He asks not to be drawn away with the wicked—He asks that they be given according to their deeds—“Blessed be the LORD, because he hath heard the voice of my supplications”—“Save thy people, and bless thine inheritance: feed them also, and lift them up for ever.”",
 bg:["“Holy oracle” in verse 2 translates the Hebrew debir, the innermost room of the temple. Lifting the hands was a common posture of prayer. The request that the wicked be repaid in verses 3–5 is an honest prayer to God, who alone judges, and is not a model for taking revenge.","The psalm turns at verse 6 from a plea to praise, as though the answer had come. The final words, “feed them also,” use the Hebrew for “shepherd them,” a picture also found in Psalm 23."]},
"ps.29":{title:"The voice of the LORD is powerful",year:"Attributed to David",
 sum:"The mighty are called to give the LORD glory and to worship in “the beauty of holiness”—The voice of the LORD is over the waters, breaks the cedars of Lebanon and shakes the wilderness—In his temple everyone speaks of his glory—“The LORD sitteth King for ever”—“The LORD will bless his people with peace.”",
 bg:["This hymn describes a thunderstorm moving from the Mediterranean Sea, across Lebanon, and south to the wilderness of Kadesh. “The voice of the LORD” appears seven times, which is how thunder is described. Sirion is the Sidonian name for Mount Hermon (Deuteronomy 3:9). Some scholars think the psalm borrows the style of neighboring storm-god poems and uses it to say that YHWH alone rules the storm.","“The beauty of holiness” in verse 2 can also be translated “holy splendor,” meaning worship in holy clothing or in a holy place. The psalm begins with “glory” and ends with “peace.”"]},
"ps.30":{title:"Weeping may endure for a night, but joy cometh in the morning",year:"Attributed to David",
 sum:"David extols the LORD, who lifted him up and healed him—The LORD’s anger is for a moment, “in his favour is life”—“Weeping may endure for a night, but joy cometh in the morning”—In prosperity David had said he would never be moved, but when God hid his face, he was troubled—“Thou hast turned for me my mourning into dancing.”",
 bg:["The title calls this a song “at the dedication of the house of David.” The Hebrew word for dedication, chanukkah, is the one later used for the Jewish festival of Hanukkah. Scholars are not sure which house is meant: David’s palace (2 Samuel 5:11), the site of the temple (1 Chronicles 21–22), or the temple itself.","This is a thanksgiving psalm for being rescued from sickness or near death. David admits that he became overconfident in his “prosperity” (verse 6) and then learned to depend on God again. “Sackcloth” (verse 11) was a rough cloth worn when mourning."]},
"ps.31":{title:"Into thine hand I commit my spirit",year:"Attributed to David",
 sum:"David takes refuge in the Lord, his “strong rock”—“Into thine hand I commit my spirit”—He is troubled by enemies, grief, and the scorn of neighbors, yet says, “My times are in thy hand”—He praises God’s goodness stored up for those who fear him—“Be of good courage, and he shall strengthen your heart.”",
 bg:["This is a lament that turns to thanksgiving. Jesus prayed the first line of verse 5 from the cross: “Father, into thy hands I commend my spirit” (Luke 23:46). In the psalm, David says it as a trusting prayer during danger; Jesus said it as he gave up his life.","The psalm piles up pictures of safety: a rock, a fortress, a “large room” (verse 8), and a “secret of thy presence” (verse 20). The Hebrew word for “rock” (sela) can also mean a cliff or crag, a real place where people in Judah’s hills could hide from enemies."]},
"ps.32":{title:"Blessed is he whose transgression is forgiven",year:"Attributed to David",
 sum:"“Blessed is he whose transgression is forgiven, whose sin is covered”—David’s bones waxed old while he kept silence, but when he acknowledged his sin the Lord forgave—Let the godly pray in time of trouble—“I will instruct thee and teach thee in the way which thou shalt go”—Be not like the horse or the mule; rejoice, ye righteous.",
 bg:["The title calls this a Maschil, probably meaning a teaching or instructive psalm. It is also one of the church’s traditional “penitential” psalms, and many readers connect it with David’s repentance, though the psalm itself does not name the sin. Paul quotes verses 1–2 in Romans 4:6–8 to show that forgiveness is a gift from God.","Three Hebrew words for sin appear in verses 1–2: transgression (rebelling), sin (missing the mark), and iniquity (twisting what is right). Three words for forgiveness answer them: forgiven (lifted off), covered, and not counted against us. Selah, found here three times, is a word of unknown meaning, probably a pause or musical direction."]},
"ps.33":{title:"By the word of the LORD were the heavens made",year:"Date uncertain",
 sum:"The righteous are called to “sing unto him a new song” with harp and psaltery—The word of the Lord is right, and by it the heavens were made—He gathers the waters, and the nations’ counsels come to nought—“Blessed is the nation whose God is the LORD”—No king is saved by an army; the Lord watches over those who fear him, and our hope is in him.",
 bg:["This hymn of praise has no title in the Hebrew, which is unusual in this part of the book. It has exactly 22 verses, the number of letters in the Hebrew alphabet, though the lines do not begin with those letters as in a true acrostic. Some scholars think it was written to follow Psalm 32 as a song of the forgiven.","The “psaltery” and the “instrument of ten strings” (verse 2) were stringed instruments, like a harp or lyre. Verse 6 pairs “word” and “breath” (the Hebrew ruach also means spirit), echoing the creation account, where God speaks and things come to be (Genesis 1)."]},
"ps.34":{title:"O taste and see that the LORD is good",year:"Attributed to David",
 sum:"David will “bless the LORD at all times”—“This poor man cried, and the LORD heard him”—“The angel of the LORD encampeth round about them that fear him”—Taste and see that the Lord is good; the young lions lack, but those who seek the Lord lack no good thing—The Lord is near “unto them that are of a broken heart,” and “keepeth all his bones.”",
 bg:["The title points to 1 Samuel 21:10–15, when David fled from Saul to Gath and pretended to be insane before the Philistine king. That king is called Achish there but Abimelech here; Abimelech was probably a royal title for Philistine kings, as in Genesis 20 and 26. This is a thanksgiving psalm, and its lines begin in order with the Hebrew alphabet (an acrostic), with minor irregularities.","John 19:36 sees verse 20, “He keepeth all his bones: not one of them is broken,” fulfilled when soldiers did not break Jesus’s legs on the cross. Peter also borrows from this psalm: 1 Peter 2:3 echoes verse 8, and 1 Peter 3:10–12 quotes verses 12–16."]},
"ps.35":{title:"Plead my cause, O LORD, with them that strive with me",year:"Attributed to David",
 sum:"David asks the Lord to “plead my cause” and fight those who fight him—He prays that his pursuers be put to shame, and rejoices in the Lord who delivers the poor—False witnesses repay him evil for good, though he had mourned for them in their sickness—“They that hate me without a cause”—He asks God to vindicate him, that his tongue may speak of God’s righteousness all the day long.",
 bg:["This is a lament with courtroom and battlefield language side by side: David asks God to be both his lawyer and his warrior. Like other so-called imprecatory psalms (psalms that call down trouble on enemies), it is an honest cry to God for justice. It is not a model for taking revenge ourselves; David leaves the outcome with the Lord.","In John 15:25 Jesus says the words “They hated me without a cause” (compare verse 19) were fulfilled in how he was treated. The same phrase appears in Psalm 69:4, so the New Testament may be drawing on either psalm."]},
"ps.36":{title:"With thee is the fountain of life",year:"Attributed to David",
 sum:"The wicked person has no fear of God, flatters himself, and plans mischief even on his bed—God’s mercy reaches “in the heavens” and his faithfulness “unto the clouds”—People trust “under the shadow of thy wings” and drink “the river of thy pleasures”—“In thy light shall we see light”—David prays that pride may not remove him.",
 bg:["The title calls David “the servant of the Lord,” a description also used of Moses and of David elsewhere. The psalm sets two pictures side by side: a person who has shut God out (verses 1–4) and the wide, steadfast love of God (verses 5–9). The Hebrew word for God’s love here, chesed, means loyal covenant love and is often translated “lovingkindness.”","The “fountain of life” (verse 9) would have been a powerful picture in a dry land, where a spring meant survival. Verse 8 says God’s people feast on the “fatness of thy house,” likely a reference to the rich offerings and meals shared at the temple."]},
"ps.37":{title:"Fret not thyself because of evildoers",year:"Attributed to David",
 sum:"“Fret not thyself because of evildoers”—Trust in the Lord, delight in him, commit thy way unto him, and “rest in the LORD, and wait patiently”—“The meek shall inherit the earth”—The wicked plot, but the Lord laughs, for their day is coming—“I have been young, and now am old; yet have I not seen the righteous forsaken”—Mark the perfect man; the salvation of the righteous is of the Lord.",
 bg:["This is a wisdom psalm, and it is an acrostic: roughly every second verse begins with the next letter of the Hebrew alphabet, which made it easier to memorize. Its message is steady and simple: the success of the wicked does not last, so do not envy them. Verse 11 is the source of Jesus’s beatitude “Blessed are the meek: for they shall inherit the earth” (Matthew 5:5), which also appears in 3 Nephi 12:5.","Here “the earth” may also be translated “the land,” and in the Old Testament that meant the promised land, the inheritance God gave to Israel. The psalm repeats the promise that the righteous will “inherit the land” (verses 9, 11, 22, 29, 34)."]},
"ps.38":{title:"O LORD, rebuke me not in thy wrath",year:"Attributed to David",
 sum:"David asks the Lord not to rebuke him in wrath—His sin weighs “as an heavy burden” and his body is sick, bowed down, and sore—Friends and kinsmen stand afar off while enemies lay snares—“For in thee, O LORD, do I hope”—“I will declare mine iniquity; I will be sorry for my sin”—He prays, “Make haste to help me, O Lord my salvation.”",
 bg:["This is a lament of illness and guilt, one of the traditional seven “penitential psalms” (6, 32, 38, 51, 102, 130, 143). Ancient Israelites often connected suffering with sin, as the speaker does here, though the Book of Job shows that is not always the case. The title, “to bring to remembrance,” may mean the psalm was used as a prayer asking God to remember the worshipper.","Notice the loneliness in verses 11–14. The speaker’s friends keep their distance and he stays silent, “as a deaf man,” while waiting for God to answer instead of defending himself. The only hope he names is the Lord."]},
"ps.39":{title:"Lord, make me to know mine end",year:"Attributed to David",
 sum:"David resolves to guard his tongue, but his silence only stirs his sorrow—“Lord, make me to know mine end, and the measure of my days”—“Thou hast made my days as an handbreadth,” and every man is vanity—“And now, Lord, what wait I for? my hope is in thee”—He asks for deliverance and for God to turn away his gaze, “before I go hence, and be no more.”",
 bg:["The title names Jeduthun, a Levite musician appointed by David to lead music in the worship of Israel (1 Chronicles 16:41–42; 25:1–3). Psalm 62 and Psalm 77 also mention him. The Hebrew word for “vanity” (hevel) means a breath or vapor, and is the key word of Ecclesiastes.","This psalm is honest about how short and fragile life feels. Its prayer is not for escape but for perspective: the speaker asks to understand his days and to put his hope in God. Verse 12, calling himself “a stranger with thee, and a sojourner, as all my fathers were,” echoes how Abraham and the patriarchs lived as guests in the land."]},
"ps.40":{title:"I waited patiently for the LORD",year:"Attributed to David",
 sum:"“I waited patiently for the LORD”—he lifted me from the “horrible pit” and set my feet upon a rock, and put “a new song” in my mouth—Sacrifice and offering thou didst not desire; “I delight to do thy will, O my God”—David has declared God’s righteousness in the great congregation—Troubles beyond number have surrounded him—“Thou art my help and my deliverer; make no tarrying, O my God.”",
 bg:["Hebrews 10:5–7 quotes verses 6–8 and applies them to Jesus Christ, who came to do God’s will and offer himself once for all. Hebrews follows the ancient Greek translation, “a body hast thou prepared me,” where the Hebrew (and our KJV) says “mine ears hast thou opened.” Both versions point to a person fully given to God’s service.","The “miry clay” (verse 2) recalls a muddy cistern or pit, a danger David and Israel’s prophets knew well. The last five verses (13–17) appear again, with small changes, as Psalm 70, which suggests the poem was reused as an independent prayer for help."]},
"ps.41":{title:"Blessed is he that considereth the poor",year:"Attributed to David",
 sum:"“Blessed is he that considereth the poor”—the Lord will preserve and strengthen him on his sickbed—David, ill, asks for mercy and healing, while enemies wish him dead and whisper against him—“Mine own familiar friend … hath lifted up his heel against me”—He knows the Lord favors him, and upholds him “in mine integrity.”",
 bg:["In John 13:18 Jesus quotes verse 9, “He that eateth bread with me hath lifted up his heel against me,” in connection with Judas, who shared the table with him and then betrayed him. To “eat bread” with someone was a sign of friendship and trust in the ancient Near East, which made betrayal at a meal especially painful.","Verse 13 (“Blessed be the LORD God of Israel from everlasting, and to everlasting. Amen, and Amen”) is not part of the psalm’s own story. It marks the close of Book I of the Psalms (Psalms 1–41), as each of the five books ends with a similar blessing."]},
"ps.42":{title:"As the hart panteth after the water brooks",year:"Attributed to the sons of Korah",
 sum:"“As the hart panteth after the water brooks, so panteth my soul after thee, O God”—The speaker’s tears are meat day and night, and he remembers going to the house of God with the multitude—“Why art thou cast down, O my soul?… hope thou in God”—“Deep calleth unto deep” as God’s waves go over him—He remembers God in the land of Jordan and Hermon, and asks again, “Why go I mourning?”",
 bg:["This opens Book II of the Psalms. The sons of Korah were temple singers and gatekeepers, descended from a Levite family (1 Chronicles 6:31–37; 9:19). Their ancestor Korah led a rebellion against Moses (Numbers 16), yet his family line did not end, and Numbers 26:11 notes that “the children of Korah died not.” Maschil probably means a skillful or teaching psalm.","A “hart” is a male deer. The speaker, apparently far from Jerusalem “from the land of Jordan, and of the Hermonites” (verse 6), longs for the temple the way a thirsty animal longs for water. Psalms 42 and 43 share a refrain (42:5, 42:11, 43:5) and probably were once one poem."]},
"ps.43":{title:"O send out thy light and thy truth",year:"Date uncertain",
 sum:"The speaker asks God to “plead my cause” against an ungodly nation and the deceitful—“Why dost thou cast me off? why go I mourning?”—“O send out thy light and thy truth: let them lead me”—He will go to the altar of God, “unto God my exceeding joy,” and praise him on the harp—“Why art thou cast down, O my soul?… hope in God.”",
 bg:["This short psalm has no title of its own, and many scholars think it is the final part of Psalm 42, because it repeats the same refrain: “Why art thou cast down, O my soul?” Many Hebrew manuscripts actually join the two. Together they make one lament with three stanzas ending in the same words of self-encouragement.","Light and truth are pictured as two guides sent from God to lead the speaker back to “thy holy hill, and to thy tabernacles” (verse 3), meaning Zion, where the temple stood. Zion was the hill in Jerusalem where David’s city and later the temple were located."]},
"ps.44":{title:"Awake, why sleepest thou, O Lord?",year:"Attributed to the sons of Korah",
 sum:"The people remember what God did for their fathers—driving out the heathen and planting Israel—“For they got not the land in possession by their own sword”—Now God has cast them off, scattered them, and made them a reproach, though they have not forgotten him—“For thy sake are we killed all the day long”—They cry, “Awake, why sleepest thou, O Lord?… Arise for our help.”",
 bg:["This is a national lament, with the community speaking as “we.” What makes it unusual is that the people say they have been faithful (verses 17–22) and still suffered defeat. It gives honest words to those who feel confused by hardship and who keep trusting God while they ask hard questions.","Paul quotes verse 22 in Romans 8:36 and then adds the answer: nothing “shall be able to separate us from the love of God, which is in Christ Jesus our Lord” (Romans 8:39). The psalm’s cry “Awake, why sleepest thou?” is a figure of speech; the Lord does not literally sleep (see Psalm 121:4)."]},
"ps.45":{title:"Thy throne, O God, is for ever and ever",year:"Attributed to the sons of Korah",
 sum:"The poet’s “heart is inditing a good matter” as he speaks of the king—The king is fairer than the children of men, with sword, glory, truth, meekness, and righteousness—“Thy throne, O God, is for ever and ever”—The king’s daughter is “all glorious within,” and she is led to the king in clothing wrought with gold—“I will make thy name to be remembered in all generations.”",
 bg:["This is a royal wedding song, as the title calls it, “A Song of loves.” “Shoshannim” means “lilies,” probably the name of a tune. The scene is an Israelite king and his bride, including a bride from Tyre (verse 12), and some scholars think the lines were written for one of the kings of Judah or Israel.","Hebrews 1:8–9 quotes verses 6–7 and applies them to Jesus Christ as the Son whose throne endures forever and who loved righteousness and hated iniquity. In the psalm the wording “O God” is addressed to the king, whose throne is God’s gift, and the New Testament sees its fullest meaning in Christ."]},
"ps.46":{title:"God is our refuge and strength",year:"Attributed to the sons of Korah",
 sum:"“God is our refuge and strength, a very present help in trouble”—we will not fear though the earth be removed and the mountains be carried into the sea—There is a river whose streams make glad the city of God, and God is in the midst of her—The nations raged, but the Lord of hosts is with us—“Be still, and know that I am God.”",
 bg:["The title says “A Song upon Alamoth,” and alamoth means “young women,” probably a note that the song was sung in a high voice. Martin Luther’s hymn “A Mighty Fortress Is Our God” is based on this psalm. Selah, which comes after verses 3, 7, and 11, is a word of unknown meaning, probably a pause or musical direction.","In D&C 101:16 the Lord uses the same words to comfort the Saints in a time of persecution: “Therefore, let your hearts be comforted; for all flesh is in mine hands; be still and know that I am God.” The Hebrew word for “be still” can also mean “let go” or “stop striving,” an invitation to stop fighting and trust."]},
"ps.47":{title:"O clap your hands, all ye people",year:"Attributed to the sons of Korah",
 sum:"All peoples are called to clap their hands and shout to God with the voice of triumph—The Lord Most High is “a great King over all the earth”—He subdues peoples and chooses an inheritance for Israel—“God is gone up with a shout”—Sing praises, for God reigneth over the heathen, and the shields of the earth belong to him.",
 bg:["This is a praise psalm, and it declares God’s kingship over every nation, not only Israel. Verse 9 pictures the “princes of the people” gathering as “the people of the God of Abraham,” a hint that all nations may someday share in the blessing promised to Abraham (Genesis 12:3). Some scholars think it was sung at a festival celebrating God’s rule, perhaps when the ark was carried up to Jerusalem.","“God is gone up with a shout” (verse 5) may refer to the ark of the covenant being brought up to Zion, accompanied by shouting and trumpets, as in 2 Samuel 6:15. Scholars are not certain which event the psalm had in mind."]},
"ps.48":{title:"Great is the LORD, and greatly to be praised",year:"Attributed to the sons of Korah",
 sum:"“Great is the LORD, and greatly to be praised” in the city of our God—Mount Zion is beautiful, “the city of the great King,” where God is known as a refuge—Kings who came against it were troubled and fled—“Walk about Zion, and go round about her: tell the towers thereof”—“This God is our God for ever and ever: he will be our guide even unto death.”",
 bg:["This is a “song of Zion,” praising Jerusalem and its temple hill. Zion was originally the fortified hill of the Jebusites that David captured (2 Samuel 5:7); later the name came to stand for the whole city, the temple mount, and God’s people. The phrase “on the sides of the north” translates the Hebrew word Zaphon, a name for a sacred northern mountain in neighboring cultures, here applied to Zion.","“Ships of Tarshish” (verse 7) were large merchant ships that sailed to Tarshish, a distant western port whose exact location is uncertain, often linked to Spain. The line pictures even the strongest ships being broken by God’s power, just as the proud kings were."]},
"ps.49":{title:"God will redeem my soul from the power of the grave",year:"Attributed to the sons of Korah",
 sum:"All people, “both low and high, rich and poor,” are called to hear wisdom—Those who trust in wealth cannot redeem a brother, for “the redemption of their soul is precious”—The wise die as the foolish do and leave their wealth to others—“Man being in honour abideth not”—“But God will redeem my soul from the power of the grave: for he shall receive me.”",
 bg:["This is a wisdom psalm. It opens with the speaker calling his teaching a “dark saying,” a riddle or hard truth, which he sets “upon the harp” (verse 4). The problem is why the wealthy and powerful seem to thrive, and the answer is that no amount of money can buy off death (verses 7–9).","Verse 15 is a quiet statement of hope that God will “receive” the speaker beyond death. The Hebrew verb is the same one used of Enoch being “taken” by God in Genesis 5:24, though scholars differ on how much the psalmist understood about life after death. Jesus’s parable of the rich fool in Luke 12:16–21 makes a similar point."]},
"ps.50":{title:"Offer unto God thanksgiving",year:"Attributed to Asaph",
 sum:"The mighty God calls the earth from the rising of the sun to its setting, and “out of Zion, the perfection of beauty, God hath shined”—He gathers his saints, those who made a covenant by sacrifice—He needs no sacrifice, for the cattle upon a thousand hills are his—“Offer unto God thanksgiving; and pay thy vows unto the most High”—He warns the wicked who forget him, and promises salvation to the one who orders his way aright.",
 bg:["Asaph (“he gathers”) was a Levite and chief musician under David (1 Chronicles 15:16–19; 16:4–5), and Psalms 50 and 73–83 carry his name. Here God acts as judge in a courtroom scene, calling heaven and earth as witnesses (verse 4). Unlike a psalm of praise, this one is mostly spoken by God himself.","The point is that worship is more than ritual. God says he does not need animals to eat (verses 12–13); what he wants is thanksgiving, kept promises, and a call to him “in the day of trouble” (verses 14–15). The prophets said similar things, for example Micah 6:6–8."]},
"ps.51":{title:"Create in me a clean heart, O God",year:"Attributed to David",
 sum:"David pleads for mercy and asks to be washed from his iniquity—“Against thee, thee only, have I sinned”—“Purge me with hyssop, and I shall be clean”—“Create in me a clean heart, O God; and renew a right spirit within me”—“Take not thy holy spirit from me”—“The sacrifices of God are a broken spirit: a broken and a contrite heart.”",
 bg:["The title ties this psalm to David’s repentance after Nathan the prophet confronted him over his sin with Bath-sheba and his arranging of Uriah’s death (2 Samuel 11–12). It is the best known of the penitential psalms. The Hebrew uses three words for sin and three for cleansing (“blot out,” “wash,” “purge”), a picture of dirty clothes being scrubbed clean, and “hyssop” (verse 7) was a plant used in cleansing rituals (Leviticus 14; Numbers 19).","The “broken heart and a contrite spirit” of verse 17 is a key idea in the Book of Mormon, where it replaces animal sacrifice after Christ’s atonement: see 2 Nephi 2:7 and 3 Nephi 9:20, and also D&C 59:8. Verses 18–19 speak of rebuilding Jerusalem’s walls, and scholars disagree on whether David or a later editor added them."]},
"ps.52":{title:"The goodness of God endureth continually",year:"Attributed to David",
 sum:"The mighty man boasts in mischief, and his tongue is “like a sharp rasor, working deceitfully”—He loves evil more than good and lies more than truth—God will destroy him for ever and pluck him out of his dwelling—The righteous shall see it and laugh—“But I am like a green olive tree in the house of God.”",
 bg:["The title points to 1 Samuel 22:9–19, where Doeg the Edomite told King Saul that the priest Ahimelech had helped David. Saul then ordered Doeg to kill the priests of Nob, and 85 died. Doeg’s name is Hebrew and meaning is uncertain, though the story shows that his tongue led to terrible harm. David may have written this psalm in grief after hearing the news (see 1 Samuel 22:20–23).","The psalm contrasts two ends. The one who “trusted in the abundance of his riches” (verse 7) is uprooted; the one who trusts in “the mercy of God for ever and ever” (verse 8) is a flourishing olive tree, a tree known for long life and for producing oil used in worship."]},
"ps.53":{title:"The fool hath said in his heart, There is no God",year:"Attributed to David",
 sum:"“The fool hath said in his heart, There is no God”—People have become corrupt and “there is none that doeth good”—God looks down from heaven to see if any understand and seek him; all have gone aside—The workers of iniquity “eat up my people as they eat bread”—“Oh that the salvation of Israel were come out of Zion!”",
 bg:["This psalm is almost a copy of Psalm 14, with a few changes, chiefly in verse 5 and in using the name God (Elohim) instead of the LORD (Yahweh). The Hebrew word for “fool” is nabal, someone morally careless instead of mentally dull; it is the same word as the name of Nabal in 1 Samuel 25. The title includes Mahalath, a word whose meaning is uncertain (perhaps a tune name or a reference to sickness), and Maschil, likely a teaching psalm.","Paul uses lines from Psalm 14 (and so Psalm 53) in Romans 3:10–12, “There is none righteous, no, not one,” to show that all need Christ."]},
"ps.54":{title:"Behold, God is mine helper",year:"Attributed to David",
 sum:"David asks God to “save me … by thy name” and hear his prayer—Strangers rise up against him and oppressors seek his soul—“Behold, God is mine helper: the Lord is with them that uphold my soul”—He asks that evil return on his enemies—He will freely sacrifice and praise God’s name, for he hath delivered him from all trouble.",
 bg:["The title refers to 1 Samuel 23:19–29 (see also 26:1), when the Ziphites, people from the town of Ziph in the hill country of Judah, told Saul where David was hiding. This was especially painful because they were of David’s own tribe. Neginoth means “stringed instruments.”","This is one of the shortest laments, only seven verses. It goes from fear to confidence in a few lines, with David praying “by thy name,” meaning by God’s character and power. His promise to offer a “freewill offering” (verse 6) shows he expects deliverance."]},
"ps.55":{title:"Oh that I had wings like a dove",year:"Attributed to David",
 sum:"David prays as fear and trembling overtake him—“Oh that I had wings like a dove! for then would I fly away, and be at rest”—He sees violence and strife in the city—“It was not an enemy that reproached me … But it was thou, a man mine equal”—He will call upon God morning, noon, and night—“Cast thy burden upon the LORD, and he shall sustain thee.”",
 bg:["The psalm does not name the betrayer. Many readers connect it with the time when David’s trusted counselor Ahithophel joined Absalom’s rebellion (2 Samuel 15–17), but the text does not say so. The pain of verses 12–14, being betrayed by a close friend with whom “we took sweet counsel together,” is one of the clearest pictures in Scripture of a broken friendship.","Verse 15 asks God to bring judgment on David’s enemies, in language that is hard to read today. Such psalms are honest prayers, handed over to God for judgment, and the New Testament later teaches us to love our enemies (Matthew 5:44) and leave vengeance with God (Romans 12:19). Verse 22 is echoed in 1 Peter 5:7, “Casting all your care upon him; for he careth for you.”"]},
"ps.56":{title:"What time I am afraid, I will trust in thee",year:"Attributed to David",
 sum:"David asks for mercy because men would swallow him up—“What time I am afraid, I will trust in thee”—His enemies twist his words and watch his steps—“Put thou my tears into thy bottle: are they not in thy book?”—“In God have I put my trust: I will not be afraid what man can do unto me”—God has delivered his soul from death, that he may walk before God in the light of the living.",
 bg:["The title refers to 1 Samuel 21:10–15, when David fled to Gath, a Philistine city, and was recognized as the man who had killed Goliath, who came from there. “Michtam” is a word of uncertain meaning, perhaps related to a Hebrew root for “gold” or “inscription.” The tune name, “Jonath-elem-rechokim,” may mean “the dove of the silence of far-off ones.”","Verse 8 pictures God collecting David’s tears in a bottle (a small leather or clay container) and recording them in a book, a vivid way to say that God notices every sorrow. In many ancient cultures, mourners did sometimes save their tears, though scholars are not sure of this practice in Israel."]},
"ps.57":{title:"In the shadow of thy wings will I make my refuge",year:"Attributed to David",
 sum:"David asks God to be merciful, for his soul trusts “in the shadow of thy wings … until these calamities be overpast”—His soul is among lions, and his enemies have prepared a net—“Be thou exalted, O God, above the heavens; let thy glory be above all the earth”—“My heart is fixed, O God”—He will wake the dawn with song and praise God among the nations.",
 bg:["The title says David wrote it “when he fled from Saul in the cave.” Two cave stories fit: the cave of Adullam (1 Samuel 22:1) and the cave at En-gedi (1 Samuel 24). The psalm does not say which. “Al-taschith” means “Destroy not,” probably the name of a tune; it also appears in the titles of Psalms 58 and 59.","The psalm has a refrain in verses 5 and 11, “Be thou exalted, O God, above the heavens.” It is a prayer of praise even while in danger. Verses 7–11 are used again at the start of Psalm 108, joined with the end of Psalm 60."]},
"ps.58":{title:"Verily he is a God that judgeth in the earth",year:"Attributed to David",
 sum:"David asks whether rulers truly “speak righteousness” and “judge uprightly”—He says they work wickedness in heart and violence with their hands—Their poison is like a serpent’s, “like the deaf adder that stoppeth her ear”—He prays that God would break their teeth and make them melt away—The righteous will rejoice in justice, and men will say, “Verily he is a God that judgeth in the earth.”",
 bg:["This is the hardest of the imprecatory psalms, with images such as breaking teeth and washing feet “in the blood of the wicked” (verse 10). These are not instructions for us to act, but cries to God from someone who has seen unjust rulers cruelly harm the weak and has no power to stop them. The singer gives the matter to God, who judges, and Paul later says “Vengeance is mine; I will repay, saith the Lord” (Romans 12:19).","The Hebrew word translated “congregation” in verse 1 is elem, which some scholars think should be read “gods” or “rulers,” meaning corrupt judges; the meaning is debated. The “deaf adder” is a snake that does not respond to a charmer, and the image describes people who ignore every call to justice."]},
"ps.59":{title:"Deliver me from mine enemies, O my God",year:"Attributed to David",
 sum:"David asks God to deliver him from enemies who “lie in wait for my soul”—They return at evening “like a dog” and go round about the city—“Because of his strength will I wait upon thee: for God is my defence”—He asks God not to slay them lest his people forget, but to bring them down and show that God ruleth in Jacob—“But I will sing of thy power; yea, I will sing aloud of thy mercy in the morning.”",
 bg:["The title points to 1 Samuel 19:11–17, when Saul sent men to watch David’s house and kill him in the morning, and David’s wife Michal helped him escape through a window. Wild dogs in ancient cities scavenged at night in packs, which is the picture in verses 6 and 14. The psalm has a refrain in verses 9 and 17: “God is my defence,” using the Hebrew word for a high, safe place.","Like other imprecatory psalms, it prays for hard things (verses 11–13), but note verse 11: David asks God not to kill his enemies outright, so the lesson would not be forgotten. As elsewhere, the prayer ends in praise, and the speaker leaves judgment with God."]},
"ps.60":{title:"Through God we shall do valiantly",year:"Attributed to David",
 sum:"After a defeat, the people say God has cast them off and made the earth tremble—“Thou hast given a banner to them that fear thee”—God claims Shechem, Succoth, Gilead, Manasseh, Ephraim, and Judah, and says “Moab is my washpot”—“Give us help from trouble: for vain is the help of man”—“Through God we shall do valiantly: for he it is that shall tread down our enemies.”",
 bg:["The title connects this national lament to David’s wars with the Arameans (“Aram-naharaim,” the land between the rivers in the north, and Zobah) and with Edom in the “valley of salt,” south of the Dead Sea; see 2 Samuel 8:3–14 and 1 Chronicles 18. The numbers differ among accounts: the title says 12,000 Edomites were killed, while 2 Samuel 8:13 says 18,000; scholars have long noted this difference. “Michtam” is of uncertain meaning, and “Shushan-eduth” means “lily of testimony.”","In verses 6–8, God speaks and names regions as if dividing property: Moab is a “washpot” (a basin for washing feet), and over Edom God will “cast out my shoe,” probably a sign of claiming ownership of land (compare Ruth 4:7). Verses 5–12 are repeated in Psalm 108:6–13."]},
"ps.61":{title:"Lead me to the rock that is higher than I",year:"Attributed to David",
 sum:"A prayer of David from “the end of the earth,” when his heart is overwhelmed—“Lead me to the rock that is higher than I”—God has been a shelter and a strong tower, and he will abide in his tabernacle and trust “in the covert of thy wings”—A prayer for the king’s long life, and a vow of daily praise.",
 bg:["This is a psalm of trust and petition. The title says it is for the chief Musician “upon Neginah,” which most scholars take to mean accompaniment by stringed instruments. “Selah” in verse 4 is a Hebrew word whose meaning is uncertain; it is usually understood as a pause or musical interlude.","The “rock that is higher than I” is a picture of safety: a cliff or high ground an enemy cannot reach. The “covert of thy wings” is the image of a mother bird sheltering her young, used elsewhere in Psalms (see 17:8; 91:4). Verses 6–7 pray for “the king,” which could be David praying for himself in the third person or a prayer offered on behalf of the king."]},
"ps.62":{title:"My soul waiteth upon God: from him cometh my salvation",year:"Attributed to David",
 sum:"“Truly my soul waiteth upon God: from him cometh my salvation”—Enemies are like “a bowing wall” and “a tottering fence,” blessing with their mouths while cursing inwardly—“Trust in him at all times; ye people, pour out your heart before him”—Neither the great nor the lowly are worth trusting, and wealth must not hold the heart—Power and mercy both belong to God, who rewards every man “according to his work.”",
 bg:["The title names Jeduthun, one of the Levites David appointed to lead music in worship (see 1 Chronicles 16:41–42; 25:1). The psalm is a quiet song of confidence, and the words “only” and “alone” (the same Hebrew word) ring through it: God alone is my rock, my salvation and my defence (verses 1, 2, 5, 6).","Verse 11 uses a Hebrew teaching device: “God hath spoken once; twice have I heard this.” It is a way of saying that a truth is certain and dependable. “Selah,” in verses 4 and 8, is usually taken as a pause or musical break, though its exact meaning is not known."]},
"ps.63":{title:"My soul thirsteth for thee, in a dry and thirsty land",year:"Attributed to David",
 sum:"A psalm of David “in the wilderness of Judah”—“Early will I seek thee: my soul thirsteth for thee” in a land where no water is—God’s lovingkindness is “better than life”; the soul is satisfied as with a feast while remembering him in the night watches—Those who seek to destroy the psalmist will fall by the sword, but the king will rejoice in God.",
 bg:["The title ties the psalm to David’s time “in the wilderness of Judah,” the dry, rocky hill country east of Jerusalem and Hebron. The Bible records David in this region when fleeing from Saul (1 Samuel 23–24) and, later, from Absalom (2 Samuel 15–17); the psalm itself does not say which. The word “early” in verse 1 can also be read as “earnestly.”","The psalm moves from thirst to satisfaction, using the image of a rich meal (“marrow and fatness,” verse 5). The “lovingkindness” of verse 3 translates the Hebrew chesed, God’s loyal, covenant love. Verses 9–11 speak of the downfall of enemies, in the plain language of ancient royal prayer."]},
"ps.64":{title:"Who whet their tongue like a sword",year:"Attributed to David",
 sum:"David prays for protection from the secret counsel of the wicked—They sharpen their tongues like swords and shoot “bitter words” like arrows at the innocent, asking, “Who shall see them?”—God will shoot at them “suddenly,” and their own tongue will fall upon themselves—All men shall fear and declare the work of God, and the righteous shall be glad in him.",
 bg:["This is a lament, a prayer of someone under attack, and the attack is made of words. The psalm pictures slander as weapons: tongues sharpened like swords and words shot from hiding like arrows (verses 3–4). The same picture appears in Psalm 57:4 and Proverbs 12:18.","The turn comes in verse 7, where the same arrow image is used against the wicked: God shoots “suddenly,” just as they did. Verse 8 describes justice in which their own speech brings them down, much like the proverb of the one who digs a pit and falls into it (see Psalm 7:15)."]},
"ps.65":{title:"Thou crownest the year with thy goodness",year:"Attributed to David",
 sum:"“Praise waiteth for thee, O God, in Sion,” the one who hears prayer and purges away transgressions—Blessed is he whom God brings near to dwell in his courts—God stills the seas and the tumult of the nations, and his tokens move the far ends of the earth to awe—He waters the earth with “the river of God,” and the year is crowned with goodness as pastures, flocks and valleys shout for joy.",
 bg:["The title calls this “A Psalm and Song of David.” It is a hymn of thanksgiving, probably sung around the harvest. “Sion” is the KJV spelling of Zion, the hill of Jerusalem where the temple stood; the name came to stand for the whole city and for God’s people.","The psalm moves from the temple (verses 1–4) to the whole earth (verses 5–8) to the farmland (verses 9–13). “The river of God, which is full of water” (verse 9) is poetry for the rains and streams that God supplies. In a land that depended on rainfall rather than great rivers, a good harvest was seen as a gift of God."]},
"ps.66":{title:"Come and see the works of God",year:"Date uncertain",
 sum:"“Make a joyful noise unto God, all ye lands”—Come and see his works: he turned the sea into dry land—God has proved his people “as silver is tried,” bringing them through fire and water into a wealthy place—The psalmist will pay his vows with offerings in God’s house—“Come and hear, all ye that fear God, and I will declare what he hath done for my soul.”",
 bg:["The title gives no author. The psalm begins with the whole earth (verses 1–12, speaking as “we”) and then turns to one person giving thanks in the temple (verses 13–20, speaking as “I”). Verse 6 recalls the Red Sea and perhaps the Jordan crossing, so ancient Israel’s story is sung as one the singer takes part in: “there did we rejoice in him.”","Verse 10 uses the picture of a silversmith: silver is heated until the impurities rise to the top. Verse 18 does not say that God hears only the perfect, but that he will not hear “if I regard iniquity in my heart,” meaning if I hold on to it and refuse to let it go."]},
"ps.67":{title:"God be merciful unto us, and bless us",year:"Date uncertain",
 sum:"A short prayer: “God be merciful unto us, and bless us; and cause his face to shine upon us”—That God’s way may be known on earth and his “saving health” among all nations—“Let all the people praise thee”—God judges the peoples righteously, the earth yields her increase, and the ends of the earth shall fear him.",
 bg:["The opening lines echo the priestly blessing God gave Aaron’s sons to say over Israel: “The LORD make his face shine upon thee, and be gracious unto thee” (Numbers 6:24–26). The psalm borrows that blessing, then asks that it flow beyond Israel so that all nations may know God.","The psalm is built around the refrain “Let the people praise thee, O God; let all the people praise thee” (verses 3 and 5), with “Selah” marking a pause after verses 1 and 4. “Saving health” in verse 2 simply means salvation. The psalm fits the idea, found throughout the Old Testament, that Israel was blessed in order to bless others (see Genesis 12:3)."]},
"ps.68":{title:"Let God arise, let his enemies be scattered",year:"Attributed to David",
 sum:"“Let God arise, let his enemies be scattered”—He is “a father of the fatherless, and a judge of the widows” and sets the solitary in families—He marched through the wilderness, Sinai shook, and he sent rain on his weary inheritance—“Thou hast ascended on high, thou hast led captivity captive”—God dwells on Zion, and kings and kingdoms are called to sing to him.",
 bg:["This is one of the hardest psalms in the Bible to translate, and scholars disagree about its setting; it may be a processional hymn for bringing the ark to Zion. It begins with words close to those Moses spoke when the ark set out in the wilderness (Numbers 10:35). “JAH” (verse 4) is a short form of the divine name, as in “Hallelu-jah.” Verses 7–8 recall Sinai and have wording much like the Song of Deborah (Judges 5:4–5).","Paul quotes verse 18 in Ephesians 4:8, applying it to Christ’s ascension, but writes “gave gifts unto men” where the Hebrew has “received gifts for men.” Both ideas fit the picture of a conquering king who takes tribute and then shares it. Verses 21–23 describe the defeat of enemies in vivid, warlike language that belongs to ancient victory songs; Latter-day Saints will want to read it as God’s power over opposition, and not as a pattern for personal revenge."]},
"ps.69":{title:"The zeal of thine house hath eaten me up",year:"Attributed to David",
 sum:"“Save me, O God; for the waters are come in unto my soul”—David sinks in deep mire, hated “without a cause,” a stranger to his brethren—“The zeal of thine house hath eaten me up”—He asks to be delivered from the deep waters and, in his sorrow, calls down judgment on his persecutors—He praises God, who hears the poor, and who will save Zion.",
 bg:["This psalm is quoted more in the New Testament than almost any other lament. John 2:17 applies verse 9 to Jesus cleansing the temple, Romans 15:3 applies the second half of verse 9 to Christ bearing reproach for others, and John 15:25 applies verse 4 (“hate me without a cause”) to those who rejected Jesus. The Gospels also tell of vinegar being offered to Jesus on the cross (for example Matthew 27:48), recalling verse 21, and Paul uses verses 22–23 in Romans 11:9–10. “Shoshannim” in the title means “lilies,” probably the name of a tune.","Verses 22–28 are one of the Bible’s imprecatory sections, prayers asking God to bring judgment on enemies. They are best read honestly as the raw cry of someone who has suffered deeply and who hands the matter to God instead of taking revenge himself; Jesus later taught his followers to love and pray for their enemies (Matthew 5:44). The psalm ends in praise and hope (verses 30–36)."]},
"ps.70":{title:"Make haste, O God, to deliver me",year:"Attributed to David",
 sum:"A brief, urgent prayer: “Make haste, O God, to deliver me”—Let those who seek his hurt, and who mock, “Aha, aha,” be put to shame—Let all who seek God rejoice and say continually, “Let God be magnified”—“I am poor and needy: make haste unto me, O God.”",
 bg:["Psalm 70 is nearly the same as Psalm 40:13–17, with small differences in wording (for example, “God” here where Psalm 40 has “the LORD”). It appears the psalm was kept as a short prayer that could be used on its own when help was needed quickly. The title, “to bring to remembrance,” is a Hebrew phrase whose exact meaning is uncertain; it may refer to a memorial offering or to bringing the petitioner to God’s remembrance.","“Aha, aha” was the sound of scorn and gloating from those who enjoyed seeing the psalmist in trouble. The psalm’s last line, “make no tarrying,” is a plain request, and the shortness of the psalm shows how brief a prayer can be and still be heard."]},
"ps.71":{title:"Cast me not off in the time of old age",year:"Date uncertain",
 sum:"“In thee, O LORD, do I put my trust”—God has been the psalmist’s hope and trust “from my youth,” holding him up from the womb—“Cast me not off in the time of old age”; his enemies say that God has forsaken him—He will hope continually and tell of God’s righteousness and strength “unto this generation, and … to every one that is to come”—He will sing with the harp to “the Holy One of Israel.”",
 bg:["This psalm has no title. It is the prayer of someone who has grown old and who looks back over a lifetime of trusting God, while still facing enemies. Its first verses are very close to Psalm 31:1–3, and it also echoes Psalm 22:9–10 on God’s care from birth. The psalmist seems to have known earlier psalms well and to have used their words as his own prayer.","Verses 9 and 18 speak to older people in particular: the fear of being set aside when strength fades and the desire to pass on faith to the next generation. “The Holy One of Israel” (verse 22) is a favorite title for God in the book of Isaiah, and it appears in Psalms only a few times."]},
"ps.72":{title:"He shall judge thy people with righteousness",year:"Attributed to Solomon",
 sum:"A prayer for the king: “Give the king thy judgments, O God”—He shall judge the poor with righteousness and break the oppressor in pieces—He comes down “like rain upon the mown grass,” and righteousness and peace flourish—His dominion reaches from sea to sea, and kings bring gifts—He delivers the needy, “and precious shall their blood be in his sight”—His name shall endure, and all nations shall call him blessed.",
 bg:["This is a royal psalm, a prayer for the king of Israel on the day of his coronation or at other times. The Hebrew title can mean “for Solomon” or “of Solomon.” The name Solomon is related to the Hebrew word shalom, “peace,” and the psalm keeps returning to that word and to justice for the poor. The note in verse 20, “The prayers of David the son of Jesse are ended,” closes the second of the five “books” into which the Psalter is divided.","Verse 17 (“men shall be blessed in him: all nations shall call him blessed”) echoes the promise to Abraham in Genesis 22:18. The psalm describes an ideal king who no human king fully matched, so later readers, Jewish and Christian, have long seen in it a picture of the Messiah. Sheba (verses 10, 15) was a kingdom in southern Arabia, and Tarshish is a distant western port whose location is uncertain."]},
"ps.73":{title:"Until I went into the sanctuary of God",year:"Attributed to Asaph",
 sum:"“Truly God is good to Israel”—Yet Asaph’s feet “were almost gone” when he envied the prosperity of the proud and the wicked who say, “How doth God know?”—He felt he had cleansed his heart in vain—“Until I went into the sanctuary of God; then understood I their end”—Now he sees his own folly and says, “Whom have I in heaven but thee?”—“It is good for me to draw near to God.”",
 bg:["Psalm 73 opens Book III of the Psalter and the group of Asaph psalms (73–83). Asaph was a Levite whom David appointed to lead temple music (1 Chronicles 15:17; 16:5). His name is generally thought to come from a Hebrew word meaning “he gathered,” though that is not certain. In these psalms Asaph often asks hard questions about God’s justice.","The psalm is honest about doubt. The turning point is verse 17: he came into the sanctuary, God’s house, and saw things from God’s point of view. The text does not say exactly what he saw, only that he understood “their end,” and that he found that being near God was worth more than anything the wicked possessed. “Afterward receive me to glory” (verse 24) is a hopeful line whose exact meaning is debated, since the Old Testament says less about life after death than the New Testament does."]},
"ps.74":{title:"Why hast thou cast us off for ever",year:"Attributed to Asaph",
 sum:"“O God, why hast thou cast us off for ever?”—Remember Zion: the enemy has burned and hacked down the sanctuary, and “there is no more any prophet”—The psalmist recalls that God divided the sea, broke the heads of the dragons and leviathan, and made day and night—“Arise, O God, plead thine own cause”—Remember the covenant, and do not forget the poor.",
 bg:["This is a communal lament, probably written after the destruction of the temple. Many scholars think of the Babylonian conquest of Jerusalem in 586 BC, but the psalm does not name the enemy, so some place it at another time of attack. “Maschil” in the title is a Hebrew word of uncertain meaning, perhaps “instruction” or a skillful psalm. In verse 8 the KJV has “synagogues,” a word for any appointed meeting places for worship.","Verses 12–17 recall God’s power over the sea, in language that draws on ancient pictures of the waters as a chaotic monster: dragons and leviathan. In the Bible these are always defeated by God, and here they remind the suffering people that the Creator who tamed chaos can also act now. The psalmist asks God to “have respect unto the covenant” (verse 20), which is the heart of his plea."]},
"ps.75":{title:"God is the judge: he putteth down one, and setteth up another",year:"Attributed to Asaph",
 sum:"“Unto thee, O God, do we give thanks”—God speaks: “When I shall receive the congregation I will judge uprightly,” and he bears up the pillars of a shaking earth—Boasters are told not to lift up the horn, since promotion comes neither from east nor west—“God is the judge: he putteth down one, and setteth up another”—The cup of his wine is poured out for the wicked, and the psalmist will sing praises to the God of Jacob.",
 bg:["“Al-taschith” in the title means “Do not destroy,” and probably names a tune; it also appears in Psalms 57–59. In this psalm a “horn” stands for strength and pride, as in an animal that lifts up its horns. The words “lift not up your horn on high” (verses 4–5) tell the proud not to boast. Verse 7 has a similar message to Hannah’s song in 1 Samuel 2:7–8, that God lowers one and raises another.","The “cup” in verse 8 is a familiar biblical picture of God’s judgment (see Isaiah 51:17; Jeremiah 25:15), which the wicked must drink down to the dregs. This answers the question raised in Psalm 73: those who seem to succeed are not outside God’s reach. “Selah” in verse 3 is generally taken as a pause or musical interlude."]},
"ps.76":{title:"In Judah is God known",year:"Attributed to Asaph",
 sum:"“In Judah is God known: his name is great in Israel”—His dwelling is in Salem and Zion, where he broke the arrow, shield, sword and battle—The stouthearted sleep and the chariot and horse are cast into “a dead sleep” at his rebuke—Who may stand before him when he is angry?—He arose to judgment “to save all the meek of the earth”—Vow and pay unto the Lord.",
 bg:["This is one of the “Songs of Zion,” celebrating God’s protection of Jerusalem. “Salem” (verse 2) is an old name for Jerusalem; it is the city of which Melchizedek was king (Genesis 14:18), and the name is related to shalom, “peace.” Many readers connect the psalm with the sudden end of the Assyrian siege of Jerusalem under Hezekiah (2 Kings 19:35), but the text does not name the event.","Verse 10 can be read two ways: that even human anger ends up praising God, or that God holds back whatever anger would go beyond his purposes. The psalm ends with an invitation to vow and bring gifts to the Lord, which is a fitting response to the God who “to save all the meek of the earth.”"]},
"ps.77":{title:"I will remember the works of the LORD",year:"Attributed to Asaph",
 sum:"“I cried unto God with my voice”—In the night his soul refuses comfort, and he asks, “Will the Lord cast off for ever?”—“Hath God forgotten to be gracious?”—He turns his mind to the years of the right hand of the Most High: “I will remember the works of the LORD”—God redeemed his people at the sea, when the waters saw him and were afraid, and led them “like a flock by the hand of Moses and Aaron.”",
 bg:["Like Psalm 62, this psalm is addressed to Jeduthun, one of David’s chief musicians (1 Chronicles 25:1). It begins in distress and then changes tone at verse 10, when the writer decides to remember. The remembering turns toward the Exodus: the sea that parted (verses 16–19) and the leading of Israel by Moses and Aaron (verse 20).","The psalm gives a model for facing doubt: it asks the hard questions of verses 7–9 plainly and does not pretend to feel better. Verse 19, “thy footsteps are not known,” suggests that God’s path through the sea left no trace, as if his ways are beyond tracking. In verse 15, “Jacob and Joseph” stand for all Israel, as Joseph’s sons Ephraim and Manasseh were important tribes."]},
"ps.78":{title:"I will open my mouth in a parable",year:"Attributed to Asaph",
 sum:"“Give ear, O my people, to my law”—The works of the Lord are to be told to the children so they will not be a stubborn and rebellious generation—God divided the sea, led them by cloud and fire, and gave water from the rock and manna, yet they tempted him again and again—He forgave, being “full of compassion,” but they provoked him with idols and he forsook Shiloh—He chose Judah, Zion and David, who fed Israel “according to the integrity of his heart.”",
 bg:["This is the second-longest of the historical psalms and is a “Maschil,” a teaching psalm, retelling Israel’s history from Egypt to David in order to teach each generation not to forget. Matthew 13:35 quotes verse 2 (“I will open my mouth in a parable”) as a description of Jesus’ teaching in parables. “Zoan” (verses 12, 43) was an Egyptian city in the Nile delta, usually identified with Tanis, where Pharaoh’s court was.","The psalm records both God’s mighty acts and Israel’s repeated unbelief, and also his patience: “many a time turned he his anger away” (verse 38). Shiloh, where the tabernacle stood in the days of Eli, was left in ruins (verses 60–61; see 1 Samuel 4 and Jeremiah 7:12). The shift from the tribe of Ephraim to Judah (verses 67–68) shows why the psalm ends with David, taken “from following the ewes” to shepherd Israel (compare 2 Samuel 7:8)."]},
"ps.79":{title:"O God, the heathen are come into thine inheritance",year:"Attributed to Asaph",
 sum:"The heathen have entered God’s inheritance, defiled his temple, and laid Jerusalem on heaps; his servants lie unburied—“How long, LORD? wilt thou be angry for ever?”—The people ask God to pour out his wrath on the nations that have not known him—“O remember not against us former iniquities”—Help us “for the glory of thy name”—Then the people, “sheep of thy pasture,” will give thanks forever.",
 bg:["This is a lament over the destruction of Jerusalem and its temple, which most scholars connect with the Babylonian invasion of 586 BC. The scene it describes is painful: unburied bodies, mocking neighbors and a defiled temple. Unlike some psalms, this one admits that the people’s own sins played a part (verses 8–9).","Verses 6–7 and 12 are an imprecatory prayer, asking God to punish the nations. Read in its setting, it is the plea of survivors who have lost everything and who place judgment in God’s hands. The Bible elsewhere teaches that vengeance belongs to the Lord (Deuteronomy 32:35; Romans 12:19) and, in the New Testament, that those who have been wronged are to love their enemies (Matthew 5:44)."]},
"ps.80":{title:"Turn us again, O God, and cause thy face to shine",year:"Attributed to Asaph",
 sum:"“Give ear, O Shepherd of Israel, thou that leadest Joseph like a flock”—A refrain three times: “Turn us again, O God, and cause thy face to shine; and we shall be saved”—God has given his people “the bread of tears” to eat—He brought a vine out of Egypt and it filled the land, but now its hedges are broken down and it is burned—Let thy hand be upon “the man of thy right hand,” and “we will call upon thy name.”",
 bg:["“Shoshannim-Eduth” in the title probably means “lilies of testimony” and may name a tune. The refrain in verses 3, 7 and 19 grows with each repetition: “O God,” then “O God of hosts,” then “O LORD God of hosts.” Ephraim, Benjamin and Manasseh (verse 2) were northern tribes, so many scholars think the psalm was written after the northern kingdom of Israel fell to Assyria in about 722 BC, though the psalm does not say so.","The vine is a favorite picture of Israel in the Bible, as in Isaiah 5:1–7. Jesus said, “I am the true vine” (John 15:1), and Latter-day Saints will also know the olive tree allegory in Jacob 5. “Dwellest between the cherubims” (verse 1) refers to the ark of the covenant, over which the golden cherubim stood. Who “the man of thy right hand” is in verse 17 is not made clear: it could be the king, Israel, or a future deliverer."]},
"ps.81":{title:"Open thy mouth wide, and I will fill it",year:"Attributed to Asaph",
 sum:"“Sing aloud unto God our strength”—Blow the trumpet at the new moon and the solemn feast, for this is a statute for Israel—God delivered Israel from the burden in Egypt and proved them at the waters of Meribah—“There shall no strange god be in thee”; “open thy mouth wide, and I will fill it”—But Israel would not hear, and God gave them up to their own counsels—“Oh that my people had hearkened unto me.”",
 bg:["This psalm was sung at a festival, probably the new moon of the seventh month or the Feast of Tabernacles (see Numbers 10:10; 29:1). “Gittith” in the title is of uncertain meaning, perhaps “of Gath” or a reference to a winepress or a tune. In verse 5, “Joseph” stands for all Israel, and the “language that I understood not” seems to be the speech of Egypt.","Meribah (verse 7) means “strife” or “quarrel.” It was where Israel complained about having no water, and the place was also called Massah, “testing” (Exodus 17:1–7; see also Numbers 20:1–13). The midpoint of the psalm turns from song to a divine speech in the first person, with an invitation (“open thy mouth wide”) and a sad refrain: “Oh that my people had hearkened unto me.” God’s “giving them up” in verse 12 shows that he allows his people to choose, even when the choice is harmful."]},
"ps.82":{title:"I have said, Ye are gods; and all of you are children of the most High",year:"Attributed to Asaph",
 sum:"God stands in the congregation and judges among “the gods”—How long will they judge unjustly and favor the wicked?—They must defend the poor and fatherless and deliver the needy—They walk in darkness and the foundations of the earth are out of course—“Ye are gods; and all of you are children of the most High,” yet they shall die like men—“Arise, O God, judge the earth.”",
 bg:["This short psalm pictures God holding court. Scholars disagree about who “the gods” are: they may be human judges and rulers who are called “gods” because they act in God’s name (the Hebrew word elohim can have this sense), or members of a heavenly council. Either way, they are charged with defending the weak and are held accountable.","Jesus quoted verse 6 in John 10:34 when accused of blasphemy, reminding his hearers that their own scripture calls those who received God’s word “gods.” Latter-day Saints often connect “children of the most High” with the teaching that we are God’s children and “heirs of God” (Romans 8:16–17), and with Church teaching about our divine potential; the psalm itself, however, is mostly a call for justice."]},
"ps.83":{title:"Keep not thou silence, O God",year:"Attributed to Asaph",
 sum:"“Keep not thou silence, O God”—His enemies have taken crafty counsel to cut Israel off from being a nation—A league of Edom, the Ishmaelites, Moab, Hagarenes, Gebal, Ammon, Amalek, Philistia, Tyre and Assur is named—The psalmist asks God to do to them as to Midian, Sisera and Jabin—“Fill their faces with shame; that they may seek thy name”—That men may know that his name alone is “JEHOVAH,” the Most High.",
 bg:["This is a national prayer against a coalition of ten peoples, which are named in verses 6–8. No single historical event in the Bible matches this exact league, so the setting is uncertain. The psalmist names Midian (Gideon, Judges 7), Sisera and Jabin (Deborah and Barak, Judges 4–5), and the princes Oreb, Zeeb, Zebah and Zalmunna (Judges 7:25; 8:5), as examples of earlier times when God rescued Israel against great odds.","These verses are imprecatory, and they should be read honestly: they ask for ruin on enemies. Yet verses 16 and 18 state the hoped-for purpose as the enemies’ shame leading them to “seek thy name” and to know that God is the Most High, which keeps the prayer from being only about revenge. “JEHOVAH” in verse 18 translates the divine name YHWH, which the KJV usually renders “LORD” and spells out only a few times (see Exodus 6:3; Isaiah 12:2)."]},
"ps.84":{title:"How amiable are thy tabernacles, O LORD of hosts",year:"Attributed to the sons of Korah",
 sum:"“How amiable are thy tabernacles, O LORD of hosts”—The soul longs and faints for the courts of the Lord; even the sparrow and swallow find a home near his altars—Blessed are those whose strength is in God and who pass through the valley of Baca; they “go from strength to strength”—“A day in thy courts is better than a thousand”—“No good thing will he withhold from them that walk uprightly.”",
 bg:["This is a pilgrim psalm, sung by those travelling to the temple in Jerusalem for the yearly festivals. “Amiable” in the KJV means “lovely,” not “friendly.” The sons of Korah were a family of Levites who served as singers and temple doorkeepers (see 1 Chronicles 6:31–38; 9:19), which is why verse 10 speaks of being “a doorkeeper in the house of my God.” “Gittith” is of uncertain meaning.","The “valley of Baca” is mentioned only here. The Hebrew word may mean “weeping” or may name a kind of balsam tree, and scholars do not agree on whether it was an actual place. Either way, the point is that the pilgrims make their dry or sorrowful road a place of springs. Verse 7 describes them growing stronger as they near Zion, not weaker."]},
"ps.85":{title:"Mercy and truth are met together",year:"Attributed to the sons of Korah",
 sum:"The Lord has been favorable to his land, forgiving iniquity and covering all sin—“Wilt thou not revive us again?”—The psalmist will hear what God will speak: “peace unto his people,” if they do not turn again to folly—“Mercy and truth are met together; righteousness and peace have kissed each other”—“Truth shall spring out of the earth; and righteousness shall look down from heaven.”",
 bg:["The psalm looks back on God’s favor in bringing “back the captivity of Jacob” (verse 1), most likely the return from Babylon in the sixth century BC, and then asks for more help, since conditions were still hard. It moves from remembered forgiveness to present prayer to a promise of peace. The poetry of verses 10–11 gives mercy, truth, righteousness and peace the character of friends who meet and embrace, while truth springs up from below and righteousness looks down from above.","Verse 11 closely resembles the Lord’s words to Enoch in Moses 7:62: “Righteousness will I send down out of heaven; and truth will I send forth out of the earth.” The two passages share the same pairing and direction, and many Latter-day Saints read them side by side, though the psalm is speaking in its own setting."]},
"ps.86":{title:"Thou, Lord, art good, and ready to forgive",year:"Attributed to David",
 sum:"“Bow down thine ear, O LORD”—David is “poor and needy” and pleads for preservation—“Thou, Lord, art good, and ready to forgive; and plenteous in mercy”—There is none among the gods like him, and all nations shall come and worship—“Teach me thy way, O LORD”; “unite my heart to fear thy name”—Great is his mercy, though the proud and violent seek his soul—A prayer for a token of good.",
 bg:["This is the only psalm in Book III attributed to David (the rest are by Asaph and the sons of Korah, or by Heman and Ethan). It borrows heavily from other Scripture, including Exodus 34:6 in verse 15 (“full of compassion, and gracious, longsuffering, and plenteous in mercy and truth”), so the psalmist prays using what God has said about himself.","“Unite my heart to fear thy name” (verse 11) is a prayer for an undivided heart, which is a notable request. “The lowest hell” in verse 13 translates Sheol, the Hebrew word for the realm of the dead, and in the psalm it means a deep place of danger and death from which God has rescued him."]},
"ps.87":{title:"Glorious things are spoken of thee, O city of God",year:"Attributed to the sons of Korah",
 sum:"The Lord’s foundation is in the holy mountains, and he loves the gates of Zion—“Glorious things are spoken of thee, O city of God”—He will record Rahab (Egypt), Babylon, Philistia, Tyre and Ethiopia as among those who know him: “this man was born there”—Of Zion it shall be said that this and that man was born in her—“All my springs are in thee.”",
 bg:["This short “Song of Zion” is brief and compressed, and some of its verses are hard to translate. Its surprise is that nations that were Israel’s enemies or rivals are listed as being “born” in Zion, as though the citizens of Jerusalem included people from all the world. “Rahab” here is a poetic name for Egypt, as in Isaiah 30:7 (the word elsewhere is used of a sea monster, as in Psalm 89:10).","The “book” in verse 6 is the register of citizens. The idea that people from many nations will belong to God’s city fits other prophecies, such as Isaiah 2:2–3 and Isaiah 19:24–25. Zion, the hill in Jerusalem where David’s city and the temple stood, came to mean God’s city and people."]},
"ps.88":{title:"My soul is full of troubles",year:"Attributed to the sons of Korah and to Heman the Ezrahite",
 sum:"“O LORD God of my salvation, I have cried day and night before thee”—The psalmist’s soul is full of troubles, and he is counted with those who go down into the pit—God’s wrath lies hard on him, and friends are far from him—Will God show wonders to the dead or his lovingkindness in the grave?—“Why castest thou off my soul?”—The psalm ends in darkness, with lover and friend put far away.",
 bg:["This is the darkest psalm in the Psalter, and it is the only lament that ends with no word of hope or praise: its last word in Hebrew is “darkness.” Yet it is still a prayer, addressed to “O LORD God of my salvation,” and the psalmist keeps calling “daily” (verses 1, 9, 13). Its place in scripture tells us that honest, unresolved prayer in suffering is welcome to God.","The title joins the sons of Korah with “Heman the Ezrahite,” who is named with Ethan the Ezrahite among famous wise men in 1 Kings 4:31 and as a temple musician in 1 Chronicles 15:19. “Mahalath Leannoth” is of uncertain meaning, perhaps a tune name connected with sickness or affliction. “Maschil” is probably a teaching or skillful psalm. The speaker’s view of death, a silent land of “forgetfulness” where God’s wonders are not known (verses 10–12), reflects the limited understanding of the afterlife in much of the Old Testament."]},
"ps.89":{title:"I will sing of the mercies of the LORD for ever",year:"Attributed to Ethan the Ezrahite",
 sum:"“I will sing of the mercies of the LORD for ever”—God made a covenant with David his servant: his seed and throne shall be established for ever—The heavens praise God’s wonders; he rules the sea and broke Rahab in pieces—David was anointed, and God will make him “my firstborn, higher than the kings of the earth”—If his children forsake God’s law they shall be chastened, but God’s covenant will not be broken—Yet God has now cast off his anointed; “Lord, where are thy former lovingkindnesses?”",
 bg:["Verses 3–4 and 19–37 recall the promise God made to David through Nathan in 2 Samuel 7:8–16: David’s house and throne would endure, and God would be a father to his son. The psalm then turns sharply at verse 38 to say that the king has been defeated and humbled, so that God’s promise seems broken. It is likely written after the fall of a Davidic king, perhaps near the Babylonian exile, but the psalm gives no date. “Anointed” (verses 38, 51) is the Hebrew mashiach, from which we get “Messiah.”","Ethan the Ezrahite is named as a famous wise man in 1 Kings 4:31. “Rahab” in verse 10 is the name of a mythic sea monster, used here as a poetic name for chaos and for God’s enemies (see 87:4, where it stands for Egypt). Mount Tabor and Mount Hermon (verse 12) are two prominent mountains, one in the south and one in the north of the land. Verse 52 closes Book III of the Psalter with “Amen, and Amen.”"]},
"ps.90":{title:"Lord, thou hast been our dwelling place in all generations",year:"Attributed to Moses",
 sum:"“Lord, thou hast been our dwelling place in all generations”—Before the mountains were brought forth, God was from everlasting to everlasting—A thousand years in his sight are as yesterday, and man is like grass that flourishes in the morning and is cut down at evening—Our days are “threescore years and ten”—“So teach us to number our days, that we may apply our hearts unto wisdom”—“Establish thou the work of our hands upon us.”",
 bg:["This is the only psalm attributed to Moses, whom Deuteronomy 33:1 calls “the man of God,” and it opens Book IV of the Psalter. Many have found in it a fitting prayer for Israel’s long years in the wilderness, though the psalm does not say when it was written. The Hebrew word for “dwelling place” suggests a home or refuge. The “watch in the night” (verse 4) was a division of the night, a few hours long, that a guard would stand.","Verse 4, “a thousand years in thy sight are but as yesterday,” is echoed in 2 Peter 3:8, where Peter says that “one day is with the Lord as a thousand years.” The psalm contrasts God’s endless time with the shortness of human life, which it sets at “threescore years and ten,” or seventy, or eighty with strength (verse 10). Its prayer is not for escape from time but for wisdom to use our days well (verse 12) and for God to bless the work of our hands (verse 17)."]},
"ps.91":{title:"He that dwelleth in the secret place of the most High",year:"Date uncertain",
 sum:"He who dwells in the secret place of the Most High abides “under the shadow of the Almighty”—God is refuge and fortress, delivering from the snare, the pestilence and the terror by night—“A thousand shall fall at thy side,” but harm will not come near the faithful—Angels are given charge over thee—“Because he hath set his love upon me, therefore will I deliver him.”",
 bg:["This is a psalm of trust, often called a psalm of protection. It has no title in the KJV, so the author is not named. Its images of shelter are drawn from daily life: the shadow of a king’s house, a fortress, and a mother bird covering her young with “his feathers” and “under his wings” (v. 4).","Satan quoted verses 11–12 to tempt Jesus to throw himself from the temple (Matthew 4:6; Luke 4:10–11). Jesus answered that Scripture also says not to tempt the Lord (Matthew 4:7), showing that trusting God is different from testing him."]},
"ps.92":{title:"It is a good thing to give thanks unto the LORD",year:"Date uncertain",
 sum:"A Psalm or Song for the sabbath day—It is good to give thanks and sing praises, for “thy works” are great and thy thoughts very deep—The brutish man knows not; the wicked flourish as grass but shall be destroyed—“The righteous shall flourish like the palm tree”—They shall still bring forth fruit in old age.",
 bg:["The heading is the only one in the Psalter that names the sabbath. It is a song of thanksgiving, and verses 1–3 mention music on the “instrument of ten strings” and the harp. The Hebrew word translated “sabbath” comes from a root meaning “to rest” or “to cease.”","Hebrew poetry rests on parallel lines, and verses 12–14 pair two pictures: the palm tree, which stays green in the desert, and the cedar of Lebanon, which grows tall and strong. Both are used to describe the righteous who are “planted in the house of the LORD.”"]},
"ps.93":{title:"The LORD reigneth, he is clothed with majesty",year:"Date uncertain",
 sum:"“The LORD reigneth, he is clothed with majesty”—The world is established and cannot be moved; his throne is of old, from everlasting—The floods lift up their voice, but the Lord on high is “mightier than the noise of many waters”—His testimonies are sure, and holiness becometh his house for ever.",
 bg:["Psalms 93 and 95–99 are often grouped as the “enthronement psalms,” which celebrate the Lord as King. This is one of the shortest, with only five verses. In ancient thought the sea and floods stood for chaos and threat, so the poem says that even the greatest forces are small before God.","The Hebrew line “the LORD reigneth” can also be read as a shout of acclamation, “the LORD has become King!” Scholars have debated how these psalms were used in worship, and the text itself does not say."]},
"ps.94":{title:"O LORD God, to whom vengeance belongeth",year:"Date uncertain",
 sum:"The psalmist asks God, the judge of the earth, to render a reward to the proud—The wicked slay the widow, the stranger and the fatherless, and say the Lord will not see—“He that planted the ear, shall he not hear?”—Blessed is the man whom the Lord chastens and teaches—“The LORD is my defence; and my God is the rock of my refuge.”",
 bg:["This is a prayer for justice, sometimes called a psalm of lament. “Vengeance” here means putting wrongs right, which belongs to the one who judges fairly, not to the psalmist (compare Deuteronomy 32:35). The psalmist cries out “how long” but then answers his own doubts with questions about the Creator who made ear and eye.","Paul quotes verse 11 in 1 Corinthians 3:20, “The Lord knoweth the thoughts of the wise, that they are vain.” The psalm has “the thoughts of man,” and Paul applies it to human wisdom."]},
"ps.95":{title:"O come, let us sing unto the LORD",year:"Date uncertain",
 sum:"“O come, let us sing unto the LORD: let us make a joyful noise to the rock of our salvation”—The Lord is a great God and King above all gods; the sea and the dry land are his—“Let us worship and bow down”—“To day if ye will hear his voice, harden not your heart,” as at Meribah and Massah.",
 bg:["This psalm is a call to worship that turns into a warning in verses 7–11. Meribah (“quarreling”) and Massah (“testing”) recall Israel’s complaint for water in the wilderness (Exodus 17:1–7). Hebrews 3:7–4:11 quotes Psalm 95:7–11 and teaches that those who harden their hearts miss the Lord’s “rest.”","The words “make a joyful noise” translate a Hebrew verb for a shout of acclamation, like the cheer for a king. The Hebrew word for “rock” (tsur) is a common title for God as a place of safety."]},
"ps.96":{title:"O sing unto the LORD a new song",year:"Date uncertain",
 sum:"“O sing unto the LORD a new song: sing unto the LORD, all the earth”—Declare his glory among the heathen, for the Lord is great and the gods of the nations are idols—Give unto the Lord the glory due unto his name—“Say among the heathen that the LORD reigneth”—He cometh to judge the earth with righteousness.",
 bg:["First Chronicles 16:23–33 contains nearly the same words, in a song of thanksgiving David appointed when the ark was brought to Jerusalem. This is one of the enthronement psalms, and unusually it calls all nations, not only Israel, to worship.","The Hebrew word for “idols” in verse 5 (elilim) sounds like a word meaning “nothings” or “worthless things.” The line contrasts these with the Lord, who “made the heavens.” Hebrew poetry often does this by setting two lines side by side, as in the closing verses where sea, field and trees all rejoice."]},
"ps.97":{title:"The LORD reigneth; let the earth rejoice",year:"Date uncertain",
 sum:"“The LORD reigneth; let the earth rejoice”—Clouds and darkness are round about him; righteousness and judgment are the habitation of his throne—The heavens declare his righteousness, and all who serve graven images are confounded—“Light is sown for the righteous”—Ye that love the Lord, hate evil and give thanks at the remembrance of his holiness.",
 bg:["This enthronement psalm pictures the Lord’s coming in storm and fire, as at Sinai: “clouds and darkness,” fire, lightning and mountains melting like wax (vv. 2–5). Verse 7 ends, “worship him, all ye gods.” Hebrews 1:6 has a similar line, “let all the angels of God worship him,” though scholars note it may draw on Deuteronomy 32:43 in the Greek version, so the exact source is debated.","“Light is sown for the righteous” (v. 11) is a farming image: light planted like seed, which will come up as joy for “the upright in heart.” “Zion” in verse 8 is a name for Jerusalem and its temple mount, and later also for the people of God."]},
"ps.98":{title:"O sing unto the LORD a new song; for he hath done marvellous things",year:"Date uncertain",
 sum:"A Psalm—“O sing unto the LORD a new song; for he hath done marvellous things”—He hath made known his salvation and remembered his mercy toward the house of Israel—All the ends of the earth have seen it—Make a joyful noise with harp, trumpets and cornet; let the sea, floods and hills rejoice before the Lord, who comes to judge the earth.",
 bg:["The heading is just “A Psalm,” the Hebrew mizmor, a song sung with instrumental music. The phrase “his right hand, and his holy arm” (v. 1) is a picture of God acting in power, as in the exodus.","Verses 7–9 call on the sea, rivers and hills to rejoice, which is a way of poetically saying that all creation welcomes the Lord’s righteous rule. The Church hymn “Joy to the World” by Isaac Watts is based on this psalm."]},
"ps.99":{title:"Exalt ye the LORD our God, and worship at his footstool",year:"Date uncertain",
 sum:"“The LORD reigneth; let the people tremble: he sitteth between the cherubims”—The Lord is great in Zion and “holy,” and loves judgment and equity—Exalt him and worship at his footstool—Moses, Aaron and Samuel called upon the Lord and he answered them—He forgave them, though he took vengeance on their inventions.",
 bg:["The word “holy” appears at verses 3, 5 and 9 like a refrain. “Between the cherubims” refers to the ark of the covenant, whose cover, the mercy seat, was topped with two winged cherubim (Exodus 25:18–22); the ark was God’s “footstool” (compare 1 Chronicles 28:2).","Moses, Aaron and Samuel are named as men who prayed and were answered. Verse 7 says God spoke to them “in the cloudy pillar,” which is told of Moses and Aaron in Numbers 12:5 and Exodus 33:9; the psalm does not say how Samuel heard the Lord (see 1 Samuel 3)."]},
"ps.100":{title:"Make a joyful noise unto the LORD, all ye lands",year:"Date uncertain",
 sum:"A Psalm of praise—“Make a joyful noise unto the LORD, all ye lands”—Serve the Lord with gladness and come before him with singing—“Know ye that the LORD he is God”: it is he that made us, and we are his people and the sheep of his pasture—Enter his gates with thanksgiving and courts with praise—“His mercy is everlasting.”",
 bg:["This is a psalm of thanksgiving, one of the best known and most loved, often sung in Christian worship (the hymn “Old Hundredth,” based on it, takes its name from this psalm’s number). It has only five verses. “Make a joyful noise” translates the same Hebrew verb used for a shout of loyalty to a king.","The KJV heading calls it “A Psalm of praise.” The Hebrew word often translated “praise” here (todah) is also the word for “thanksgiving,” and was used for the thank offering in the temple (Leviticus 7:12). The psalm may have been sung as worshipers entered the temple “gates” and “courts.”"]},
"ps.101":{title:"I will sing of mercy and judgment",year:"Attributed to David",
 sum:"A Psalm of David—“I will sing of mercy and judgment”—I will behave myself wisely in a perfect way and walk within my house with a perfect heart—I will set no wicked thing before mine eyes—A froward heart and a proud look will I not suffer—“Mine eyes shall be upon the faithful.”",
 bg:["This psalm is sometimes called a royal psalm, because it sounds like a king’s promises about how he will rule and who will serve in his court. It is traditionally linked with David, and the text itself does not say when he wrote it. Verse 2 asks, “O when wilt thou come unto me?”, which shows he knew he needed God’s help to keep his promises.","The words “perfect way” and “perfect heart” use a Hebrew word (tamim) meaning whole, complete or blameless, not flawless. The king’s vow in verse 8 to “I will early destroy all the wicked of the land” is the language of justice in a city, not a personal threat."]},
"ps.102":{title:"Hear my prayer, O LORD, and let my cry come unto thee",year:"Date uncertain",
 sum:"A Prayer of the afflicted, when he is overwhelmed—“Hear my prayer, O LORD”; his days are consumed like smoke and he is like a pelican of the wilderness—The Lord shall endure for ever and will arise and have mercy upon Zion—He will regard the prayer of the destitute—The earth and heavens shall perish, “but thou art the same, and thy years shall have no end.”",
 bg:["This is one of the seven psalms the Church has long called penitential or lament psalms. Its heading names no author and says it is the prayer of a person overwhelmed who pours out his complaint. The poem moves from personal suffering (vv. 1–11) to hope for Zion’s restoration (vv. 12–22).","Hebrews 1:10–12 quotes verses 25–27 and applies them to the Son, the Creator who remains the same when the heavens “wax old like a garment.” The “pelican” (v. 6) is uncertain; the Hebrew word may mean a desert bird such as a pelican or an owl, but scholars cannot be sure."]},
"ps.103":{title:"Bless the LORD, O my soul",year:"Attributed to David",
 sum:"A Psalm of David—“Bless the LORD, O my soul, and forget not all his benefits”—He forgiveth all thine iniquities and healeth all thy diseases—“As far as the east is from the west, so far hath he removed our transgressions from us”—Like as a father pitieth his children—Man’s days are as grass, but the Lord’s mercy is from everlasting to everlasting.",
 bg:["This is a psalm of thanksgiving and one of the best-loved in the Bible. “Bless” here means to speak well of or praise God, and the writer talks to his own soul as he begins and ends the psalm. Verse 8 echoes God’s self-description to Moses in Exodus 34:6: “merciful and gracious, slow to anger.”","Some of the psalm’s comparisons are measured by distance: heaven above the earth (v. 11) and east from west (v. 12). Because the east and the west never meet, the picture says that forgiven sins are removed completely."]},
"ps.104":{title:"O LORD my God, thou art very great",year:"Date uncertain",
 sum:"“Bless the LORD, O my soul. O LORD my God, thou art very great”—He stretches out the heavens like a curtain and sets the earth’s foundations—Springs, grass, wine, oil and bread; trees, cattle and wild beasts—“O LORD, how manifold are thy works! in wisdom hast thou made them all”—The Lord’s glory shall endure for ever.",
 bg:["This psalm is a hymn to the Creator, and its order follows the days of creation in Genesis 1: light and heavens, waters and land, plants, sun and moon, animals and humans. Verse 4 is quoted in Hebrews 1:7, about angels as “spirits” and ministers. Some scholars note a likeness to an Egyptian hymn to the sun god Aten, but how the two are related is debated.","“Leviathan” in verse 26 is a sea creature, and the psalm treats it as God’s plaything, not a threat. The psalm calls God’s works “manifold,” meaning many and varied, and ends with the wish that sinners be consumed and a “Praise ye the LORD” (Hebrew Hallelujah)."]},
"ps.105":{title:"O give thanks unto the LORD; call upon his name",year:"Date uncertain",
 sum:"“O give thanks unto the LORD; call upon his name: make known his deeds among the people”—He remembers his covenant with Abraham, Isaac and Jacob—Joseph is sold, imprisoned and exalted in Egypt—Israel grows; Moses and Aaron bring plagues—He leads them out with silver and gold and gives them the lands of the heathen, “that they might observe his statutes, and keep his laws.”",
 bg:["Psalm 105 is a history psalm. It retells the story from Abraham to the conquest of Canaan, and verses 1–15 appear also in 1 Chronicles 16:8–22. The psalm picks out the Lord’s faithfulness, the opposite of the people’s faithlessness told in Psalm 106.","The “land of Ham” (vv. 23, 27) is a name for Egypt, drawn from Ham, a son of Noah (Genesis 10:6). The order of plagues here differs from Exodus 7–12, and darkness comes first, which shows the poet was shaping the story as a song, not copying a record."]},
"ps.106":{title:"We have sinned with our fathers",year:"Date uncertain",
 sum:"“Praise ye the LORD. O give thanks unto the LORD; for he is good”—“We have sinned with our fathers”—Israel provoked him at the Red Sea, lusted in the wilderness and made a calf in Horeb—They joined themselves to Baal-peor and mixed with the heathen—Many times he delivered them; “save us, O LORD our God, and gather us from among the heathen.”",
 bg:["This is a history psalm like Psalm 105, but it tells the people’s failures in the wilderness and in Canaan. Verses 1 and 47–48 appear in 1 Chronicles 16:34–36. Verse 48 closes the fourth section of the Psalter with a blessing, followed by “Amen” and “Praise ye the LORD.”","The psalm names the golden calf at Horeb (Exodus 32), Dathan and Abiram (Numbers 16), the water at Meribah (Numbers 20) and Baal-peor (Numbers 25). Verses 37–38 mention that children were sacrificed to idols, a grim sign of how far some had fallen. It ends with a prayer to be gathered back, which fits the time of exile, though the text does not date it."]},
"ps.107":{title:"Oh that men would praise the LORD for his goodness",year:"Date uncertain",
 sum:"“O give thanks unto the LORD, for he is good”—“Let the redeemed of the LORD say so,” gathered from east, west, north and south—Four groups cry in trouble and are delivered: wanderers in the wilderness, prisoners, the sick and those at sea—“He maketh the storm a calm”—He turns rivers into wilderness and wilderness into pools; “whoso is wise… shall understand.”",
 bg:["This psalm has four stanzas with a refrain, “Then they cried unto the LORD in their trouble, and he delivered them.” Each tells of a different kind of trouble: desert travelers (vv. 4–9), prisoners (vv. 10–16), sick people (vv. 17–22) and sailors in a storm (vv. 23–32). Verse 3 suggests the psalm was sung for people gathered back from exile, though the text does not say which return.","The refrain “Oh that men would praise the LORD for his goodness” uses the Hebrew word chesed, often translated “mercy” or “lovingkindness,” meaning faithful covenant love. Verses 23–30, in which the Lord calms the storm, remind many readers of Jesus stilling the sea (Mark 4:35–41)."]},
"ps.108":{title:"O God, my heart is fixed",year:"Attributed to David",
 sum:"A Song or Psalm of David—“O God, my heart is fixed; I will sing and give praise”—I will awake the dawn and praise thee among the people—Thy mercy is great above the heavens—God has spoken in his holiness; he divides Shechem and Succoth and claims Gilead, Manasseh and Ephraim—“Through God we shall do valiantly: for he it is that shall tread down our enemies.”",
 bg:["This psalm is built from two earlier psalms: verses 1–5 are almost the same as Psalm 57:7–11, and verses 6–13 are Psalm 60:5–12. Whoever put it together joined a morning song of praise with a prayer for victory.","Shechem, Succoth, Gilead, Manasseh, Ephraim and Judah are regions of Israel, and Moab, Edom and Philistia are its neighbors. “Moab is my washpot” and casting a shoe over Edom (v. 9) are vivid pictures of ruling over a place, but scholars are not sure of every detail of the images."]},
"ps.109":{title:"For my love they are my adversaries: but I give myself unto prayer",year:"Attributed to David",
 sum:"To the chief Musician, A Psalm of David—“Hold not thy peace, O God of my praise”—Enemies speak with a lying tongue and repay evil for good—A long series of curses is called down on the accuser—“But do thou for me, O GOD the Lord”—“I will greatly praise the LORD with my mouth,” for he stands at the right hand of the poor.",
 bg:["This is the hardest of the psalms of imprecation, or prayers asking God to judge enemies. The curses in verses 6–19 are stern, and some scholars read them as the words the enemies have said against the psalmist, which he quotes before turning to God in verse 21; the text does not mark this clearly. In either case, the psalm asks God to be the judge instead of taking revenge himself.","Peter quotes verse 8, “let another take his office” (translated “bishoprick” in the KJV of Acts 1:20), when choosing someone to replace Judas. The Hebrew word for “adversary” in verses 4, 6, 20 and 29 is satan, meaning “accuser” or “opponent.”"]},
"ps.110":{title:"The LORD said unto my Lord, Sit thou at my right hand",year:"Attributed to David",
 sum:"A Psalm of David—“The LORD said unto my Lord, Sit thou at my right hand, until I make thine enemies thy footstool”—The rod of strength goes out of Zion and his people are willing in the day of his power—“Thou art a priest for ever after the order of Melchizedek”—The Lord shall strike through kings and judge among the heathen.",
 bg:["This is a royal psalm, and the most often quoted in the New Testament. Jesus used verse 1 to ask how the Messiah could be both David’s son and David’s Lord (Matthew 22:44; also Mark 12:36 and Luke 20:42–43). Peter applied it to Christ’s resurrection and ascension in Acts 2:34–35, and Hebrews 1:13 uses it too.","Verse 4 says the king is a priest “after the order of Melchizedek,” the king and priest of Salem who blessed Abraham (Genesis 14:18–20). Hebrews 5–7 builds on this. In the Doctrine and Covenants, the higher priesthood is called the Melchizedek Priesthood (D&C 107:2–4), and Alma 13:14–19 also speaks of Melchizedek."]},
"ps.111":{title:"The fear of the LORD is the beginning of wisdom",year:"Date uncertain",
 sum:"“Praise ye the LORD. I will praise the LORD with my whole heart”—His works are great, honourable and glorious; the Lord is gracious and full of compassion—He gives meat to them that fear him and is mindful of his covenant—“He sent redemption unto his people”—“The fear of the LORD is the beginning of wisdom.”",
 bg:["This is an acrostic psalm: in Hebrew each half-line starts with the next letter of the alphabet (aleph through tav), so its 22 short lines fit into 10 verses. Psalm 112 is built the same way, and the two are often read together, the first about God’s character and the second about the person who reflects it.","“Praise ye the LORD” translates the Hebrew Hallelujah (hallelu-yah), meaning “praise Yah,” where Yah is a short form of the divine name. “Fear” in verse 10 means reverence and respect for God. The same line appears in Proverbs 9:10."]},
"ps.112":{title:"Blessed is the man that feareth the LORD",year:"Date uncertain",
 sum:"“Blessed is the man that feareth the LORD, that delighteth greatly in his commandments”—His seed shall be mighty and wealth and righteousness are in his house—Unto the upright light arises in darkness—He is gracious, lends and guides his affairs with discretion—“He hath dispersed, he hath given to the poor.”",
 bg:["Like Psalm 111, this is an alphabetical acrostic, and it is a companion portrait of the person who fears the Lord. Many phrases from Psalm 111, such as “his righteousness endureth for ever,” are used here of the righteous person, as if such a person takes on God’s own qualities.","Paul quotes verse 9, “He hath dispersed, he hath given to the poor; his righteousness endureth for ever,” in 2 Corinthians 9:9 when he teaches the Corinthians about cheerful giving. The “horn” lifted up “with honour” in verse 9 is a Hebrew picture of strength and dignity."]},
"ps.113":{title:"Who is like unto the LORD our God",year:"Date uncertain",
 sum:"“Praise ye the LORD. Praise, O ye servants of the LORD”—Blessed be his name from the rising of the sun to its going down—The Lord is high above all nations, yet he humbles himself to behold things in heaven and earth—“He raiseth up the poor out of the dust”—He makes the barren woman “a joyful mother of children.”",
 bg:["Psalms 113–118 are called the Egyptian Hallel (“praise”), sung at Passover and other festivals. By later Jewish tradition, Psalms 113–114 are sung before the Passover meal and 115–118 after. “When they had sung an hymn” in Matthew 26:30 and Mark 14:26 may well refer to these psalms, since Jesus and his disciples had just eaten the Passover meal.","This psalm celebrates the God who is far above everything but who stoops to lift up the poor and the childless. Hannah’s song in 1 Samuel 2:5–8 has similar wording, and Mary’s Magnificat in Luke 1:46–55 also echoes the theme."]},
"ps.114":{title:"When Israel went out of Egypt",year:"Date uncertain",
 sum:"“When Israel went out of Egypt, the house of Jacob from a people of strange language”—Judah was his sanctuary and Israel his dominion—The sea saw it and fled; Jordan was driven back—The mountains skipped like rams and the little hills like lambs—“Tremble, thou earth, at the presence of the Lord,” who turned the rock into a fountain of waters.",
 bg:["This is part of the Egyptian Hallel, sung at Passover, and it recalls the exodus. In poetic form it treats the sea and mountains as if they were people, startled by the Lord’s coming: the sea “fled” (Exodus 14), the Jordan stood back (Joshua 3), and Sinai shook (Exodus 19).","Verse 1 says Israel left “a people of strange language,” meaning a foreign tongue, which is a reminder of how unlike Israel the Egyptians were."]},
"ps.115":{title:"Not unto us, O LORD, not unto us, but unto thy name give glory",year:"Date uncertain",
 sum:"“Not unto us, O LORD, not unto us, but unto thy name give glory”—Our God is in the heavens; he hath done whatsoever he hath pleased—Idols are silver and gold, the work of men’s hands; they have mouths but speak not—“They that make them are like unto them”—Israel, the house of Aaron and all who fear the Lord should trust him; he will bless them and their children.",
 bg:["This is part of the Egyptian Hallel, sung after the Passover meal in later Jewish custom. It is a song of trust and a sharp comparison between the living God and idols. The list of body parts that cannot work (vv. 5–7) is a mocking list, and verse 8 says worshipers become like what they worship; Psalm 135:15–18 repeats the passage.","Verses 9–11 name three groups in turn, Israel, the house of Aaron (the priests) and “ye that fear the LORD.” This structure suggests the psalm was sung in turns, with a priest or choir responding, though the text does not explain how it was performed."]},
"ps.116":{title:"I love the LORD, because he hath heard my voice",year:"Date uncertain",
 sum:"“I love the LORD, because he hath heard my voice and my supplications”—The sorrows of death compassed me; I called upon the Lord and he delivered my soul—“I will take the cup of salvation, and call upon the name of the LORD”—“Precious in the sight of the LORD is the death of his saints”—I will pay my vows in the courts of the Lord’s house.",
 bg:["This is a song of personal thanksgiving for being rescued from death, and one of the Egyptian Hallel psalms sung at Passover. Because it was part of the Passover hymns, Jesus may have sung it the night before his death (Matthew 26:30). The “cup of salvation” (v. 13) may refer to a thank offering of drink; it also reminds readers of the Passover cups.","Paul quotes verse 10, “I believed, therefore have I spoken,” in 2 Corinthians 4:13. In the Greek and Latin Bibles this psalm is numbered differently, because those versions join Psalms 114 and 115 into one and split this one into two."]},
"ps.117":{title:"O praise the LORD, all ye nations",year:"Date uncertain",
 sum:"“O praise the LORD, all ye nations: praise him, all ye people”—For his merciful kindness is great toward us—“The truth of the LORD endureth for ever. Praise ye the LORD.”",
 bg:["This is the shortest chapter in the Bible, with only two verses, and it is the shortest psalm. It is a call for all peoples, not only Israel, to praise the Lord, a theme Paul quotes in Romans 15:11 to show that the Gentiles were always meant to join in praise.","“Merciful kindness” translates chesed, God’s faithful covenant love, and “truth” translates emet, meaning faithfulness or reliability. These two words often come together in the Old Testament to describe God’s character. It is one of the Egyptian Hallel psalms."]},
"ps.118":{title:"The stone which the builders refused is become the head stone of the corner",year:"Date uncertain",
 sum:"“O give thanks unto the LORD; for he is good: because his mercy endureth for ever”—“The LORD is on my side; I will not fear: what can man do unto me?”—Better to trust in the Lord than in princes—“Open to me the gates of righteousness”—“The stone which the builders refused is become the head stone of the corner”—“This is the day which the LORD hath made.”",
 bg:["This is the last of the Egyptian Hallel psalms, sung at Passover and also at other major feasts. The people may have sung it as a procession entering the temple, with “This gate of the LORD” in verse 20. The word “Hosanna” comes from verse 25, “Save now, I beseech thee,” and the crowd shouted words from verses 25–26 as Jesus entered Jerusalem (Matthew 21:9).","Jesus applied the stone the builders rejected (vv. 22–23) to himself in Matthew 21:42, and Peter did the same in Acts 4:11 and 1 Peter 2:7. Jacob in the Book of Mormon uses the same image in Jacob 4:15–17, saying the Jews “rejected the stone” on which they could have built. Paul quotes verse 6 in Hebrews 13:6."]},
"ps.119":{title:"Blessed are the undefiled in the way, who walk in the law of the LORD",year:"Date uncertain",
 sum:"“Blessed are the undefiled in the way, who walk in the law of the LORD”—A young man cleanses his way by taking heed to God’s word—“Thy word is a lamp unto my feet, and a light unto my path”—The psalmist loves the law, hates vain thoughts and hopes in God’s word through affliction—“Thy testimonies are wonderful”—“I have gone astray like a lost sheep; seek thy servant.”",
 bg:["This is the longest chapter in the Bible, with 176 verses. It is an acrostic of 22 stanzas of 8 verses each, one stanza for each letter of the Hebrew alphabet, and all eight verses in a stanza begin with that letter. The KJV headings give the letters in order: Aleph (vv. 1–8), Beth (9–16), Gimel (17–24), Daleth, He, Vau, Zain, Heth, Teth, Jod, Caph, Lamed, Mem, Nun, Samech, Ain, Pe, Tzaddi, Koph, Resh, Schin and Tau (vv. 169–176).","Almost every verse uses a word for God’s teaching, such as law (torah), testimonies, precepts, statutes, commandments, judgments or word. Scholars note these are close in meaning, and the psalmist uses them to show different sides of the same instruction from God. The letter names are also words: Aleph means “ox,” Beth “house,” and Gimel is commonly said to mean “camel.”"]},
"ps.120":{title:"In my distress I cried unto the LORD, and he heard me",year:"Date uncertain (a song of degrees, a pilgrim song)",
 sum:"A Song of degrees—“In my distress I cried unto the LORD, and he heard me”—Deliver my soul from lying lips and a deceitful tongue—Sharp arrows and coals of juniper await the false tongue—“Woe is me, that I sojourn in Mesech”—“I am for peace: but when I speak, they are for war.”",
 bg:["This is the first of fifteen “songs of degrees” (Psalms 120–134). The Hebrew word (ma’alah) means “steps” or “ascents,” and the best-known idea is that pilgrims sang these on the way up to Jerusalem for the yearly feasts; scholars also have other suggestions, such as the steps of the temple. This psalm begins in distress, far from home, and the series ends in the temple.","Meshech and Kedar (v. 5) are far apart. Meshech was a people of the far north, near the Black Sea region, and Kedar was an Arabian desert tribe descended from Ishmael’s son (Genesis 25:13). The psalmist probably names them poetically for the places where he feels like a foreigner among those who hate peace, not as a literal address."]},
"ps.121":{title:"I will lift up mine eyes unto the hills",year:"Date uncertain",
 sum:"A song of degrees—“My help cometh from the LORD, which made heaven and earth”—He that keeps Israel “will not slumber,” and he is a shade on the right hand by day and by night—The LORD will preserve the going out and the coming in, “from this time forth, and even for evermore.”",
 bg:["Psalms 120–134 each begin “A Song of degrees,” the “songs of ascents” that pilgrims sang while going up to Jerusalem for the feasts. Psalm 121 is a traveler’s psalm of trust, written in parallel lines that repeat the key word “keep” (Hebrew shamar, “guard, watch over”) six times in English as keeper, keepeth and preserve.","The first verse can be read as a question (where will my help come from?), which the second verse answers; Hebrew has no punctuation, so scholars read it both ways. The hills may be the hills around Jerusalem, but the psalm says plainly that help comes from the Lord who made them, not from the hills themselves."]},
"ps.122":{title:"I was glad when they said unto me",year:"Attributed to David",
 sum:"A song of degrees of David—“I was glad when they said unto me, Let us go into the house of the LORD”—Jerusalem is “a city that is compact together,” where the tribes go up to give thanks—“Pray for the peace of Jerusalem”—For the sake of brethren and the house of the LORD, the psalmist will seek her good.",
 bg:["This is a pilgrim psalm, sung by those arriving at the gates of Jerusalem for one of the yearly feasts. It plays on the city’s name: the word for “peace” (shalom) appears again and again, and the name Jerusalem is often explained as meaning something like “foundation of peace,” though scholars are not fully certain of its origin.","Verse 4 says the tribes go up “unto the testimony of Israel,” and verse 5 names the thrones of the house of David, where justice was given. The temple and the king’s court were both in Jerusalem, so it was a city of worship and of fair judgment."]},
"ps.123":{title:"So our eyes wait upon the LORD our God",year:"Date uncertain",
 sum:"A song of degrees—“Unto thee lift I up mine eyes, O thou that dwellest in the heavens”—As servants watch their master’s hand, so Israel’s eyes wait on the LORD until he has mercy—The people plead, “Have mercy upon us,” for they are filled with the scorn of the proud.",
 bg:["This is a short community lament, one of the pilgrim songs. The psalm begins with one person saying “I” and then widens to “we,” as the whole group of pilgrims lifts its eyes together.","Servants in the ancient world watched a master’s or mistress’s hand for a signal of what to do or of what would be given. The picture is of patient, attentive trust, waiting for the Lord’s mercy rather than demanding it."]},
"ps.124":{title:"If it had not been the LORD who was on our side",year:"Attributed to David",
 sum:"A song of degrees of David—“If it had not been the LORD who was on our side,” men would have swallowed Israel up—Floods and “proud waters” would have gone over their soul—“Our soul is escaped as a bird out of the snare of the fowlers”—“Our help is in the name of the LORD, who made heaven and earth.”",
 bg:["This is a song of thanksgiving for being rescued. It uses three pictures of danger: being swallowed alive, being swept away by a flood, and being torn by teeth. Winter floods in dry stream beds in the land of Israel could come suddenly and be deadly.","Verse 7 compares the escape to a bird freed from a fowler’s trap. The psalm closes with the same words as Psalm 121:2, “who made heaven and earth,” a phrase that recurs in the pilgrim songs (see also 134:3)."]},
"ps.125":{title:"As the mountains are round about Jerusalem",year:"Date uncertain",
 sum:"A song of degrees—Those who trust in the LORD are like mount Zion, which “abideth for ever”—“As the mountains are round about Jerusalem, so the LORD is round about his people”—The rod of the wicked shall not rest on the lot of the righteous—The LORD does good to the upright, and “peace shall be upon Israel.”",
 bg:["Jerusalem sits on a ridge surrounded by higher hills, so a pilgrim coming up to the city really could see mountains “round about” it. The psalm uses the setting to teach that God surrounds his people the way the hills surround the city.","Zion originally named a hill in Jerusalem (the city of David) and came to stand for the whole city and its people. The “rod of the wicked” in verse 3 probably means foreign rule over Israel’s land, which the psalm hopes will not last long enough to tempt the righteous into wrongdoing."]},
"ps.126":{title:"They that sow in tears shall reap in joy",year:"Date uncertain",
 sum:"A song of degrees—When the LORD turned again the captivity of Zion, “we were like them that dream”—Even the heathen said, “The LORD hath done great things for them”—The people pray, “Turn again our captivity, O LORD, as the streams in the south”—“They that sow in tears shall reap in joy.”",
 bg:["Many scholars connect this psalm with the return of Jewish exiles from Babylon (see Ezra 1), though the phrase “turned again the captivity” could also mean a general restoration of fortunes. The “streams in the south” are dry wadis in the Negev that fill suddenly with rain, a picture of sudden renewal.","Verses 5–6 are a farming image: seed was precious, and sowing it in a hard year could be a sad act of faith. The promise is that the one who weeps while sowing will return “bringing his sheaves with him.” These lines are often quoted to comfort people in sorrow."]},
"ps.127":{title:"Except the LORD build the house, they labour in vain",year:"Attributed to Solomon",
 sum:"A song of degrees for Solomon—Unless the LORD builds the house and keeps the city, human labor and watchfulness are in vain—He gives sleep to his beloved—“Children are an heritage of the LORD”—Like arrows in a warrior’s hand, they bless the man whose quiver is full.",
 bg:["The title says “for Solomon,” and the Hebrew word can mean “by,” “for,” or “about” Solomon, so scholars read it in different ways. “House” can mean a building, such as the temple Solomon built, and also a household or family line, so the psalm moves naturally from building a house to raising children.","Verses 3–5 say children are “an heritage of the LORD” and compare them to arrows. In a city, men met at the gate for business and legal matters, so “speak with the enemies in the gate” pictures a father who has family to stand beside him there. Verse 2’s “he giveth his beloved sleep” is a quiet reminder that rest is also a gift."]},
"ps.128":{title:"Thy children like olive plants round about thy table",year:"Date uncertain",
 sum:"A song of degrees—Blessed is everyone who fears the LORD and walks in his ways—He shall eat the labor of his hands—His wife shall be like a fruitful vine and his children like olive plants round about his table—The LORD blesses out of Zion, and he sees “thy children’s children, and peace upon Israel.”",
 bg:["This is a wisdom-style blessing, often paired with Psalm 127, and was likely sung as pilgrims blessed one another and their households. Vines and olive trees were among the most valued plants in ancient Israel, so they were natural pictures of a flourishing family.","The blessing is addressed to “every one that feareth the LORD.” In the Bible, to “fear” the Lord means reverence and obedience out of trust, not terror. The psalm ends by widening from one home to the whole nation, hoping for peace on Israel."]},
"ps.129":{title:"Many a time have they afflicted me from my youth",year:"Date uncertain",
 sum:"A song of degrees—Israel says, “Many a time have they afflicted me from my youth: yet they have not prevailed against me”—“The plowers plowed upon my back”—The LORD is righteous and has cut asunder the cords of the wicked—May the haters of Zion be like grass on the housetops, which withers before it grows.",
 bg:["Here the nation speaks as one person, “from my youth,” looking back on its long history of being attacked. The “plowers” who “made long their furrows” are a striking picture of cruel treatment, perhaps of whip marks or of the cutting of long furrows, and the psalm says that even so Israel “they have not prevailed against me” (verse 2)","Houses in ancient Israel had flat roofs of packed earth, where seeds sometimes sprouted in the spring and then died quickly in the sun. Such grass gave no harvest and no one gathered it, which is why the psalm uses it to picture the end of those who hate Zion."]},
"ps.130":{title:"Out of the depths have I cried unto thee, O LORD",year:"Date uncertain",
 sum:"A song of degrees—“Out of the depths have I cried unto thee, O LORD”—“If thou, LORD, shouldest mark iniquities, O Lord, who shall stand?” but there is forgiveness with him—The psalmist waits for the LORD more than watchmen wait for the morning—“With the LORD there is mercy, and with him is plenteous redemption.”",
 bg:["This is one of the seven psalms the church has long called penitential (confessing sin and asking mercy), and its first words in Latin, De profundis, became a famous title. “The depths” is a picture of deep water, a sign of trouble and distress. It is also a pilgrim song, one of the songs of ascents.","Watchmen on a city wall waited through the night for the dawn that meant relief, and the psalmist says his waiting is even more eager. The psalm turns on verse 4, “there is forgiveness with thee,” and then speaks to all Israel with the promise of “plenteous redemption.”"]},
"ps.131":{title:"As a child that is weaned of his mother",year:"Attributed to David",
 sum:"A song of degrees of David—“LORD, my heart is not haughty, nor mine eyes lofty”—He does not exercise himself in things too high for him—He has quieted his soul “as a child that is weaned of his mother”—“Let Israel hope in the LORD from henceforth and for ever.”",
 bg:["This is one of the shortest psalms (three verses) and one of the most personal. A weaned child is not crying for food but rests quietly with its mother, so the picture is of a soul that has learned to trust rather than to demand.","“Things too high for me” means matters beyond one’s understanding or place, and the psalmist says he will not strain after them. The first two verses are one person’s humble words, and verse 3 invites the whole nation to share that hope."]},
"ps.132":{title:"Lord, remember David, and all his afflictions",year:"Date uncertain",
 sum:"A song of degrees—The psalmist asks the LORD to remember David, who vowed not to rest until he found “a place for the LORD”—The ark is found and brought up with worship: “Arise, O LORD, into thy rest”—The LORD swears to David that his sons shall sit on his throne if they keep the covenant—The LORD has chosen Zion for his habitation.",
 bg:["The psalm recalls two stories: David’s plan to bring the ark of the covenant to Jerusalem (2 Samuel 6) and the promise that his house would endure (2 Samuel 7). “Ephratah” in verse 6 is a name linked with the Bethlehem area, and “the fields of the wood” is commonly taken to mean Kiriath-jearim, where the ark had been kept (1 Samuel 7:1–2), though scholars are not certain of every detail.","Verse 12 makes the promise conditional: David’s sons would sit on the throne if they kept the covenant. In Acts 2:30 Peter echoes verse 11 when he says God had sworn with an oath to David that one of his descendants would sit on his throne. The “horn of David” and “lamp” in verse 17 are images of strength and of a continuing line."]},
"ps.133":{title:"How good and how pleasant it is for brethren to dwell together in unity",year:"Attributed to David",
 sum:"A song of degrees of David—“Behold, how good and how pleasant it is for brethren to dwell together in unity”—Unity is like the precious ointment on Aaron’s head running down to the skirts of his garments—It is like the dew of Hermon descending on the mountains of Zion—“There the LORD commanded the blessing, even life for evermore.”",
 bg:["The psalm gives two pictures of unity. In Exodus 29:7 and Leviticus 8:12, Aaron was anointed with oil poured on his head, a sign of being set apart; the psalm says unity is like that rich oil, overflowing and sweet. The second picture is dew: Mount Hermon, at about 9,200 feet in the far north, is snowcapped and gives heavy dew, a picture of life-giving refreshment, and “Hermon” is often thought to mean “sacred” or “set apart,” though that meaning is not certain.","In the Book of Mormon, Alma the Elder taught the people to have their “hearts knit together in unity and in love one towards another” (Mosiah 18:21). Brethren in Hebrew can include kin or fellow Israelites, so the psalm may be pictured at the feasts, when families from all the tribes gathered in Jerusalem."]},
"ps.134":{title:"Lift up your hands in the sanctuary, and bless the LORD",year:"Date uncertain",
 sum:"A song of degrees—“Behold, bless ye the LORD, all ye servants of the LORD, which by night stand in the house of the LORD”—“Lift up your hands in the sanctuary, and bless the LORD”—“The LORD that made heaven and earth bless thee out of Zion.”",
 bg:["This is the last of the fifteen songs of ascents, and it may have been a short exchange: pilgrims or priests call the night watchers of the temple to bless the Lord (verses 1–2), and someone answers with a blessing in return (verse 3). Levites and priests kept watch at the temple through the night (1 Chronicles 9:33 mentions Levite singers who were on duty day and night).","Lifting the hands was a common ancient posture of prayer and praise. The psalm says that to “bless” the Lord means to speak good of him in worship, and that his blessing returns to the worshipper “out of Zion.”"]},
"ps.135":{title:"Whatsoever the LORD pleased, that did he",year:"Date uncertain",
 sum:"Praise ye the LORD—The LORD has chosen Jacob for his “peculiar treasure” and is above all gods—He rules the weather and smote Egypt’s firstborn and the kings of Canaan—The idols of the heathen have mouths but do not speak, and those who trust in them become like them—The houses of Israel, Aaron and Levi are called to bless the LORD.",
 bg:["“Praise ye the LORD” is the Hebrew Hallelujah: hallelu means “praise” (a command to many people) and Yah is a short form of the divine name. Psalm 135 borrows heavily from other Scripture, including Exodus (the plagues), Deuteronomy, and Psalm 115:4–8, where the same verses about idols appear almost word for word.","“Peculiar treasure” (verse 4) translates a Hebrew word for a special possession, like a king’s own treasure, as in Exodus 19:5. Sihon and Og (verse 11) were the Amorite kings Israel defeated east of the Jordan (Numbers 21), and the verses on idols say that those who worship lifeless things become like them."]},
"ps.136":{title:"For his mercy endureth for ever",year:"Date uncertain",
 sum:"O give thanks to the LORD, for he is good—Every verse ends with “for his mercy endureth for ever”—He made the heavens, the earth, the sun, moon and stars—He smote Egypt’s firstborn, divided the Red Sea, led his people through the wilderness, and gave them their land—He “remembered us in our low estate” and gives food to all flesh.",
 bg:["This psalm is a litany, a call-and-response: a leader likely sang the first half of each line, and the congregation answered with the refrain. The refrain word, hesed, is one of the richest in the Hebrew Bible, usually translated “mercy,” “lovingkindness,” or “steadfast love,” and it means loyal love that keeps a covenant.","The psalm retells creation (verses 4–9) and the exodus and conquest (verses 10–22), showing the same steady love in the world’s making and in Israel’s history. Psalm 135 tells the same story in less structured form, and the two are often read together."]},
"ps.137":{title:"By the rivers of Babylon, there we sat down",year:"Date uncertain",
 sum:"By the rivers of Babylon the exiles weep when they remember Zion and hang their harps on the willows—Their captors ask for a song of Zion, and they ask, “How shall we sing the LORD’s song in a strange land?”—“If I forget thee, O Jerusalem,” let my right hand forget her cunning—The psalm closes with a cry for justice against Edom and Babylon, in terrible words.",
 bg:["This is the cry of Jewish exiles after Babylon destroyed Jerusalem and the temple in 586 BC, as told in 2 Kings 25. Babylon was the great empire on the Euphrates; “the rivers of Babylon” are probably its canals and rivers. Zion is the temple hill and a name for Jerusalem. The Book of Mormon says Lehi’s family left Jerusalem before this happened and later learned that the Jews would be carried to Babylon (1 Nephi 10:3).","Edom, the nation descended from Esau, took part in or cheered Jerusalem’s fall (see Obadiah 1:10–14). The last verses speak of striking Babylon’s infants, the raw anger of people who had seen their own children killed, and they are not a model for Christian conduct. The psalm shows that Scripture lets us bring even our worst grief and rage to God honestly, and it leaves vengeance to him. Jesus later taught his followers to love their enemies (Matthew 5:44)."]},
"ps.138":{title:"I will praise thee with my whole heart",year:"Attributed to David",
 sum:"A Psalm of David—“I will praise thee with my whole heart” and worship toward the holy temple—“In the day when I cried thou answeredst me”—All the kings of the earth shall praise the LORD—Though high, he has respect to the lowly; “the LORD will perfect that which concerneth me.”",
 bg:["This is a psalm of thanksgiving for answered prayer. “Before the gods” (verse 1) uses the Hebrew word elohim, which can mean gods, angels or judges; the psalmist is declaring his praise boldly in front of every power that others worship, and scholars discuss which meaning is meant here.","In verse 2, “thou hast magnified thy word above all thy name,” the exact sense is debated; many take it to mean that God has honored his promises even beyond what his reputation led people to expect. Verse 6 states a pattern that appears throughout the Bible: God is high yet attends to the humble and sees the proud only “afar off.”"]},
"ps.139":{title:"O LORD, thou hast searched me, and known me",year:"Attributed to David",
 sum:"To the chief Musician, a Psalm of David—“O LORD, thou hast searched me, and known me”—“Whither shall I flee from thy presence?”—Even darkness is as light to him—“I am fearfully and wonderfully made”: he knew the psalmist in the womb—His thoughts are “more in number than the sand”—The psalmist rejects the wicked and asks, “Search me, O God, and know my heart.”",
 bg:["Psalm 139 is the Bible’s great reflection on God’s complete knowledge: he knows each sitting and rising, each thought and word, in every place. “Reins” in verse 13 is an old word for the kidneys, which the Hebrews thought of as the seat of deep feelings. “Fearfully” means with awe, and “wonderfully made” means set apart as something marvelous; the phrase in verse 14 is often cited about the worth of each person.","Verses 19–22 turn suddenly to violent people the psalmist hates because they hate God. Many readers find this jarring after the tender first part, but the psalm ends by turning back to self-examination: “Search me, O God, and know my heart” (verse 23). Latter-day Saint scripture agrees that God “comprehendeth all things, and all things are before him” (D&C 88:41)."]},
"ps.140":{title:"Deliver me, O LORD, from the evil man",year:"Attributed to David",
 sum:"To the chief Musician, a Psalm of David—The psalmist asks to be delivered from violent men who “have sharpened their tongues like a serpent”—He asks to be kept from the snares set by the proud—He says to the LORD, “Thou art my God,” and prays that the wicked will be overtaken by their own mischief—The LORD “will maintain the cause of the afflicted, and the right of the poor.”",
 bg:["This is a prayer for rescue from slander and plots, and it echoes the troubles David faced under Saul and later. Snakes, nets, snares and traps are the psalm’s pictures of hidden danger, and the poisoned tongue in verse 3 is quoted by Paul in Romans 3:13 in a list of human sin.","“Selah” appears here three times. Its meaning is not certain; it is most likely a musical or liturgical direction, perhaps for a pause, an interlude or a lifting up of voices, and most translations leave the word untranslated. The curses in verses 9–11 are in the style of ancient prayers for justice, asking God to let evil fall back on those who planned it."]},
"ps.141":{title:"Let my prayer be set forth before thee as incense",year:"Attributed to David",
 sum:"A Psalm of David—“Let my prayer be set forth before thee as incense; and the lifting up of my hands as the evening sacrifice”—He asks the LORD to set a watch before his mouth and keep his heart from evil—He welcomes correction from the righteous, “an excellent oil”—He trusts in the LORD and asks to be kept from the snares of the wicked.",
 bg:["Incense was burned in the tabernacle and temple, and the evening sacrifice was offered at the end of each day (Exodus 29:38–41; 30:7–8). The psalmist, perhaps unable to be at the temple, asks that prayer take the place of those offerings. In Revelation 5:8 the prayers of the saints are likewise pictured as incense.","Verse 5 is a rare welcome of correction: “let the righteous smite me; it shall be a kindness.” Oil was a sign of honor and healing, so being reproved by a good person is called “excellent oil.” The Hebrew of verses 5–7 is hard, and translators differ about how some of it should be rendered."]},
"ps.142":{title:"I poured out my complaint before him",year:"Attributed to David",
 sum:"Maschil of David, a prayer when he was in the cave—He cries to the LORD with his voice and pours out his complaint—“Refuge failed me; no man cared for my soul”—“Thou art my refuge and my portion in the land of the living”—He prays, “Bring my soul out of prison,” and the righteous will gather round him.",
 bg:["The title connects the psalm to David hiding in a cave, which fits either Adullam (1 Samuel 22:1) or En-gedi (1 Samuel 24:3), when Saul was hunting him. “Maschil” is a Hebrew term of uncertain meaning, probably “instructive poem” or “skillful song.”","The psalm shows that honest complaint is a form of faith: David “poured out” his trouble to God and said, “Thou knewest my path” (verse 3). “Prison” in verse 7 can be literal or can mean any place of confinement or distress, and the psalm does not say which."]},
"ps.143":{title:"In thy sight shall no man living be justified",year:"Attributed to David",
 sum:"A Psalm of David—He asks the LORD to hear and answer in faithfulness—“Enter not into judgment with thy servant: for in thy sight shall no man living be justified”—He is overwhelmed, remembers the days of old, and stretches out his hands as thirsty land—He asks to hear God’s lovingkindness in the morning and to be taught to do his will.",
 bg:["This is the last of the seven penitential psalms in the church’s traditional list. In Romans 3:20 and Galatians 2:16, Paul echoes verse 2 to teach that no one is made righteous before God by works alone, and the psalm itself already says that no one living can claim to be perfectly righteous.","“Thy spirit is good; lead me into the land of uprightness” (verse 10) is a prayer for guidance, and in verse 8 the morning is the time to hear God’s hesed (“lovingkindness,” loyal love). Verses 3–4 describe despair in the image of living in darkness “as those that have been long dead,” and the verses that follow show the psalmist turning to remember and to reach out to God."]},
"ps.144":{title:"Happy is that people, whose God is the LORD",year:"Attributed to David",
 sum:"A Psalm of David—He blesses the LORD, his strength, fortress and deliverer, who teaches his hands to war—“LORD, what is man, that thou takest knowledge of him?”—He asks God to come down and deliver him from foreign foes—“I will sing a new song unto thee”—He prays for flourishing sons and daughters, full storehouses, and peace: “Happy is that people, whose God is the LORD.”",
 bg:["This psalm mixes a king’s war prayer with a hope for peace and plenty. It borrows from older psalms: verses 1–2 resemble Psalm 18, and verses 3–4 echo Psalm 8:4 and Job 7:17. “Strange children” means foreigners.","Verse 12 pictures daughters as “corner stones, polished after the similitude of a palace,” a way of saying they are shapely, strong and valued, like carved pillars in a palace. The “ten strings” of verse 9 is a harp-like instrument with ten strings, and “new song” is a phrase that appears in many psalms (for example 33:3; 96:1; 98:1; 149:1)."]},
"ps.145":{title:"The LORD is gracious, and full of compassion",year:"Attributed to David",
 sum:"David’s Psalm of praise—“I will extol thee, my God, O king”—“Great is the LORD, and greatly to be praised; and his greatness is unsearchable”—“The LORD is gracious, and full of compassion; slow to anger, and of great mercy”—His kingdom is everlasting—“Thou openest thine hand, and satisfiest the desire of every living thing”—“The LORD is nigh unto all them that call upon him.”",
 bg:["This is the only psalm with the title “praise” (tehillah), and it is an acrostic: each verse begins with a successive letter of the Hebrew alphabet, as a way of praising God “from A to Z.” In the traditional Hebrew text the verse for the letter nun is missing, though the Dead Sea Scrolls and the Greek Bible include a line there (“The LORD is faithful in all his words, and holy in all his works”). Many English Bibles do not print it.","Verse 8 repeats the description God gave of himself in Exodus 34:6, “merciful and gracious, longsuffering, and abundant in goodness and truth.” Jewish worship has long included this psalm (with Psalms 84 and 144) in a prayer called Ashrei said three times daily, and it begins the closing group of praise psalms."]},
"ps.146":{title:"Put not your trust in princes",year:"Date uncertain",
 sum:"Praise ye the LORD—“While I live will I praise the LORD”—“Put not your trust in princes, nor in the son of man, in whom there is no help”—Happy is he who has the God of Jacob for his help—The LORD gives justice to the oppressed and food to the hungry, opens the eyes of the blind, and cares for strangers, the fatherless and the widow—“The LORD shall reign for ever.”",
 bg:["Psalms 146–150 each begin and end with “Praise ye the LORD,” which is the Hebrew Hallelujah (hallelu, “praise,” and Yah, a short form of the Lord’s name). These five “Hallelujah” psalms close the Book of Psalms. Psalm 146 is a song of trust that praises the Lord as the true king instead of human rulers.","Verse 6 says the Lord made “heaven, and earth, the sea, and all that therein is,” the same wording the early believers prayed in Acts 4:24. Verses 7–9 list those the Lord cares for, and Jesus later gave sight to the blind and fed the hungry (for example Mark 8:22–25 and John 6:1–14)."]},
"ps.147":{title:"He healeth the broken in heart",year:"Date uncertain",
 sum:"Praise ye the LORD—He builds up Jerusalem and gathers the outcasts of Israel—“He healeth the broken in heart, and bindeth up their wounds”—He tells the number of the stars and calls them by name—He provides rain, food for beasts and ravens—He strengthens Jerusalem and sends his word like snow and frost.",
 bg:["Many scholars think this psalm was written after the exile, when Jerusalem’s walls were being rebuilt (verse 2 and verse 13 mention building up and the bars of the gates). Verses 2–3 join great and small works: the same Lord who gathers a nation also heals individual hearts.","Verse 4 says God counts the stars and “calleth them all by their names,” a way of saying he knows all that he made. The last verses link his word to nature: his “word runneth very swiftly,” sending snow, ice and warm wind, and the same word has been given to Israel as statutes and judgments (verses 19–20)."]},
"ps.148":{title:"Praise ye the LORD from the heavens",year:"Date uncertain",
 sum:"Praise ye the LORD—Angels, hosts, sun, moon, stars and waters above the heavens are called to praise him, for he commanded and they were created—From the earth, sea creatures, fire, hail, snow, mountains, trees, beasts and birds are to praise him—Kings, young men and maidens, old men and children are to praise him—His name alone is excellent.",
 bg:["This psalm is organized as a call to praise from the heavens (verses 1–6) and then from the earth (verses 7–14), moving from the highest things to the lowest and finally to people. The “dragons” in verse 7 translates a Hebrew word (tannin) for large sea creatures or monsters, which the psalm says also praise their Creator.","The psalm makes no distinction between what is thought of as sacred and ordinary: weather, animals, rulers, young and old all have a place in the chorus. Praise is also a cosmic order that “shall not pass” (verse 6), because it rests on God’s command in creation."]},
"ps.149":{title:"Sing unto the LORD a new song",year:"Date uncertain",
 sum:"Praise ye the LORD—“Sing unto the LORD a new song” in the congregation of saints—Let them praise his name with dance, timbrel and harp, for he “will beautify the meek with salvation”—Let high praises be in their mouth and “a twoedged sword in their hand”—Judgment on the nations is an honor for all his saints.",
 bg:["This psalm joins joyful worship with the image of holy war. Verses 1–5 call for singing and dancing with the timbrel (a hand drum) and harp. Latter-day Saint revelation also teaches that “the song of the righteous is a prayer unto me” (D&C 25:12).","Verses 6–9 are hard to read: they speak of a sword, of chains for kings, and of judgment on the nations. Interpreters differ on whether they describe Israel’s actual battles, a hope for God’s final justice, or praise as a kind of spiritual weapon. The psalm says the judgment is “written,” meaning it belongs to God’s word, and it should be read in light of its overall call to praise."]},
"ps.150":{title:"Let every thing that hath breath praise the LORD",year:"Date uncertain",
 sum:"Praise ye the LORD—Praise God in his sanctuary and in the firmament of his power—Praise him for his mighty acts and his excellent greatness—Praise him with trumpet, psaltery, harp, timbrel, dance, stringed instruments, organs and cymbals—“Let every thing that hath breath praise the LORD.”",
 bg:["Psalm 150 closes the whole Book of Psalms with a full orchestra of praise. The word “praise” (hallelu) appears thirteen times, and the psalm answers three questions: where to praise (verse 1), why (verse 2) and how (verses 3–5). The Book of Psalms is divided into five books, and each of the first four ends with a short blessing (see 41:13; 72:19–20; 89:52; 106:48), and Psalm 150 is the grand finale of the fifth.","The instruments named are the trumpet (a ram’s-horn or metal horn), the “psaltery” (a stringed instrument like a harp), harp (lyre), timbrel (hand drum), “organs” (a pipe or flute), and two kinds of cymbals. “Let every thing that hath breath” in verse 6 uses the Hebrew word for breath (neshamah), so every living creature, not only Israel, is invited to praise."]},
"prov.1":{title:"The fear of the LORD is the beginning of knowledge",year:"Date uncertain (Solomon’s era and later)",
 sum:"Proverbs of Solomon give “wisdom and instruction” to the simple and the wise—“The fear of the LORD is the beginning of knowledge”—A son is urged to heed his father and refuse the enticing words of sinners—Wisdom cries aloud in the streets, but those who refuse her call will find her gone in the day of calamity.",
 bg:["The book opens by naming Solomon, whose name probably comes from the Hebrew shalom, “peace.” The word for “proverb,” mashal, can mean a saying, a comparison, or a short poem. Many of the sayings in the book were gathered after Solomon’s time, as later chapters show.","Wisdom (Hebrew chokmah) in Proverbs is not book learning but skill for living well before God, and verse 7 makes reverent trust in the LORD its starting point. In verses 20–33 Wisdom is pictured as a woman calling in the public square. Proverbs are general principles drawn from experience, not promises that every situation will turn out the same way."]},
"prov.2":{title:"If thou criest after knowledge",year:"Date uncertain (Solomon’s era and later)",
 sum:"If a son receives and treasures the words of wisdom, he will understand the fear of the LORD—“The LORD giveth wisdom: out of his mouth cometh knowledge and understanding”—Wisdom will keep him from the way of evil men and from the strange woman—The upright shall dwell in the land.",
 bg:["The chapter is one long sentence in Hebrew style: if you do these things (verses 1–4), then you will find these results (verses 5–22). Notice the verbs: crying out, seeking, searching as for hidden treasure. Wisdom is pictured as something to be sought with effort and also received as a gift from the LORD.","The “strange woman” (verses 16–19) is a recurring figure in chapters 1–9. The Hebrew word means a woman outside the proper bounds, and scholars debate whether she is mainly a literal adulteress, a foreigner, or a picture of Folly. She is the opposite of Wisdom, who also calls out to the young man."]},
"prov.3":{title:"Trust in the LORD with all thine heart",year:"Date uncertain (Solomon’s era and later)",
 sum:"Keep the commandments and let mercy and truth be bound about the neck—“Trust in the LORD with all thine heart; and lean not unto thine own understanding”—Honour the LORD with thy substance, and despise not his chastening—Happy is the man who finds wisdom, for she is “a tree of life”—Do good to thy neighbour and envy not the oppressor.",
 bg:["Verses 5–6 are among the best-loved lines in the Bible. To “direct thy paths” can also be read as making the paths straight or smooth. It is a promise about God’s guidance, though Proverbs as a whole reminds us that wise living improves life without guaranteeing easy outcomes.","The LORD’s correction in verses 11–12 is quoted in Hebrews 12:5–6, and the Lord echoes the thought in Doctrine and Covenants 95:1, “whom I love I also chasten.” Verse 34 is quoted in James 4:6 and 1 Peter 5:5. In verses 19–20 the LORD creates the world “by wisdom,” a theme taken up in chapter 8."]},
"prov.4":{title:"Wisdom is the principal thing",year:"Date uncertain (Solomon’s era and later)",
 sum:"A father passes on what his own father taught him—“Wisdom is the principal thing; therefore get wisdom: and with all thy getting get understanding”—The path of the just is as the shining light, the way of the wicked as darkness—“Keep thy heart with all diligence; for out of it are the issues of life.”",
 bg:["In verses 3–4 the speaker remembers being taught as a child by his own father, so the chapter shows wisdom handed down through the generations of a family. Some readers connect this with David teaching Solomon, though the text does not say so.","In Hebrew thought the “heart” (verse 23) is the center of thought and will, not only of feeling. To “keep” it is to guard it like a watchman. The “way” or “path” is the book’s favourite picture of how a person lives, and the chapter ends by urging the reader to look straight ahead and walk a level path."]},
"prov.5":{title:"Drink waters out of thine own cistern",year:"Date uncertain (Solomon’s era and later)",
 sum:"A father warns his son against the smooth speech of the strange woman, whose end is bitter—Remain far from her door lest strangers be filled with thy wealth—“Drink waters out of thine own cistern”: rejoice with the wife of thy youth—A man’s ways are before the eyes of the LORD.",
 bg:["The chapter speaks briefly and plainly about faithfulness in marriage. Water was precious in a dry land, so a cistern or well (verses 15–18) was a natural picture of something valuable that belongs to you and should be kept for you alone. Verse 18 speaks of rejoicing in “the wife of thy youth,” a warm picture of lasting marriage.","Verse 22 says the wicked are caught in “the cords of his sins,” and the chapter ends with a person who dies for lack of instruction. The book’s warnings about the strange woman, like those in chapters 2, 6, 7 and 9, are written to a young man by a parent who wants to protect him."]},
"prov.6":{title:"Go to the ant, thou sluggard",year:"Date uncertain (Solomon’s era and later)",
 sum:"Warnings against pledging surety for a neighbour and against laziness—“Go to the ant, thou sluggard; consider her ways, and be wise”—A worthless person sows discord—Six things the LORD hates, yea seven are an abomination to him—“The commandment is a lamp; and the law is light”—Adultery destroys the soul.",
 bg:["The ant (verses 6–8) works without a boss and stores food in season. The “six things… yea seven” (verses 16–19) is a Hebrew poetic pattern in which a number is followed by the next number, drawing attention to the last item. Here it is the one who sows discord among brothers.","Verses 20–23 say that the parents’ commands are a lamp and light, a theme the Bible often uses for God’s law, as in Psalm 119:105. The long warning about adultery in verses 24–35 is plain about how it harms a person and a family. Like all proverbs, these describe how life usually goes rather than guarantee exact results."]},
"prov.7":{title:"Keep my commandments, and live",year:"Date uncertain (Solomon’s era and later)",
 sum:"A father urges his son to keep his words as “the apple of thine eye” and bind them on his fingers—From his window he sees a young man void of understanding go toward the house of a strange woman—She entices him with smooth words, and he goes “as an ox goeth to the slaughter”—Her house is “the way to hell.”",
 bg:["This chapter is a short story told by an eyewitness looking through a lattice. The young man goes out at twilight, his heart open to temptation, and the story shows how a person can drift into harm one step at a time. The “apple of thine eye” in verse 2 is the pupil of the eye, something guarded with special care.","The woman speaks of her religious meal and sacrifice (verse 14), probably a peace offering after which part of the meat was eaten, as a way of making her invitation sound respectable. “Hell” in verse 27 translates the Hebrew Sheol, the realm of the dead."]},
"prov.8":{title:"The LORD possessed me in the beginning of his way",year:"Date uncertain (Solomon’s era and later)",
 sum:"Wisdom cries at the gates, offering truth, riches and righteousness—“I love them that love me; and those that seek me early shall find me”—Wisdom was with the LORD before the earth was made, “as one brought up with him”—“Whoso findeth me findeth life.”",
 bg:["In verses 22–31 Wisdom speaks of being with God from the beginning, “rejoicing always before him.” The Hebrew verb in verse 22 (qanah) can mean “possessed,” “acquired,” or “created,” and scholars and early Christian readers have long debated which fits best. Many Christians read the passage together with John 1:1–3 and Colossians 1:15–17 as pointing to Christ, the Word through whom all things were made; that is a Christian reading, not something Proverbs itself states.","Chapter 8 is the high point of the personified Wisdom of chapters 1–9. She is a person in poetry, a way of speaking about God’s own wisdom in creation and in daily life. Her call stands against the call of Folly in chapter 9."]},
"prov.9":{title:"Wisdom hath builded her house",year:"Date uncertain (Solomon’s era and later)",
 sum:"Wisdom builds her house with seven pillars, prepares a feast and invites the simple—A scorner will hate reproof, but a wise man will love it—“The fear of the LORD is the beginning of wisdom”—A foolish woman, loud and simple, calls from the door of her house to those who pass.",
 bg:["The two women of this chapter, Wisdom and Folly, each build a house and each send out invitations to the same simple passers-by. The words of the two invitations are nearly the same (verses 4 and 16), and the difference is in where they lead: life for Wisdom’s guests, the depths of Sheol for Folly’s.","Verse 10 repeats the theme of 1:7, that reverence for the LORD is the starting point of wisdom, and adds that “the knowledge of the holy is understanding.” The seven pillars are often taken as a sign of completeness, though the text does not explain the number. This chapter ends the first section of the book."]},
"prov.10":{title:"A wise son maketh a glad father",year:"Date uncertain (Solomon’s era and later)",
 sum:"The proverbs of Solomon begin—A wise son makes a glad father, and a foolish son is the heaviness of his mother—“The memory of the just is blessed: but the name of the wicked shall rot”—“Love covereth all sins”—The tongue of the just is as choice silver.",
 bg:["With this chapter the book changes style. Chapters 1–9 are longer poems, while 10:1–22:16 is a collection of about 375 short, two-line sayings, as the Hebrew tradition counts them. Most put two things side by side in contrast, such as the wise and the foolish, the diligent and the lazy, the righteous and the wicked.","These sayings are general truths about how life tends to go, not promises for every case; the Bible itself, in the book of Job, shows that righteous people can suffer. Verse 12, “love covereth all sins,” is echoed in 1 Peter 4:8, and verse 7 of this chapter plays on the contrast between a lasting memory and a name that “shall rot.”"]},
"prov.11":{title:"A false balance is abomination to the LORD",year:"Date uncertain (Solomon’s era and later)",
 sum:"A false balance is an abomination, but a just weight is the LORD’s delight—“When pride cometh, then cometh shame: but with the lowly is wisdom”—Righteousness delivers from death—He that is surety for a stranger shall smart—The merciful man does good to his own soul—“He that winneth souls is wise.”",
 bg:["Ancient merchants weighed silver and goods on a balance with stone weights, so a dishonest dealer could cheat by using a heavy stone to buy and a light one to sell. The law of Moses forbids this (Leviticus 19:35–36), and Proverbs repeats that honest dealing matters to God, not just to customers.","Verse 31 asks that if the righteous are repaid on earth, how much more the wicked and the sinner, and it is quoted in 1 Peter 4:18 in its Greek form. Verse 30 reads, “He that winneth souls is wise,” a phrase some read as winning people over by wise living; the Hebrew can also be read as “he who takes lives is wise”, and translators differ."]},
"prov.12":{title:"Whoso loveth instruction loveth knowledge",year:"Date uncertain (Solomon’s era and later)",
 sum:"He that loves instruction loves knowledge, but he that hates reproof is brutish—“A virtuous woman is a crown to her husband”—The wise hearken to counsel, but the way of a fool is right in his own eyes—A righteous man regards the life of his beast—“Lying lips are abomination to the LORD”—Anxiety weighs down the heart, but a good word makes it glad.",
 bg:["The “virtuous woman” in verse 4 is the same Hebrew phrase, eshet chayil, that begins the poem of Proverbs 31:10–31. It means a woman of strength and worth, and it is also used of Ruth in Ruth 3:11. A “crown” suggests honour and joy, while the one who shames her husband is like “rottenness in his bones.”","Verse 10 says a righteous man “regardeth the life of his beast.” In an agricultural world, caring well for animals was part of being just. Verse 25, “heaviness in the heart of man maketh it stoop: but a good word maketh it glad,” still speaks to anyone who has been encouraged by a kind word."]},
"prov.13":{title:"Hope deferred maketh the heart sick",year:"Date uncertain (Solomon’s era and later)",
 sum:"A wise son hears his father’s instruction—He that keeps his mouth keeps his life—“Hope deferred maketh the heart sick: but when the desire cometh, it is a tree of life”—He that walks with wise men shall be wise—The foolish and the slothful meet ruin, but the diligent are made rich—The rod and discipline are signs of love.",
 bg:["Verse 24 is often quoted: “He that spareth his rod hateth his son: but he that loveth him chasteneth him betimes.” The “rod” (shebet) was a shepherd’s staff used to guide and protect, and the verse is about loving parents correcting their children early in a caring way. The Bible elsewhere warns parents not to provoke their children (Ephesians 6:4).","“Tree of life” appears in Proverbs four times (3:18; 11:30; 13:12; 15:4) to describe good things, such as wisdom or a gentle tongue, that give life and strength. It is a picture borrowed from the garden of Eden story in Genesis 2–3."]},
"prov.14":{title:"There is a way which seemeth right unto a man",year:"Date uncertain (Solomon’s era and later)",
 sum:"“Every wise woman buildeth her house: but the foolish plucketh it down with her hands”—There is a way that seems right but its end is death—The simple believes every word, but the prudent looks well to his going—“Righteousness exalteth a nation: but sin is a reproach to any people”—Whoso oppresses the poor reproaches his Maker.",
 bg:["Verse 12 appears word for word again in Proverbs 16:25. The warning is that feeling sure about a path does not make it safe. Proverbs often repeats sayings in the collection of Solomon, and repeating them adds weight.","Verse 4 says, “Where no oxen are, the crib is clean: but much increase is by the strength of the ox.” It is a plain observation that anything worthwhile often comes with some mess and effort. In verse 34 “reproach” translates a word that can mean shame or disgrace for a people."]},
"prov.15":{title:"A soft answer turneth away wrath",year:"Date uncertain (Solomon’s era and later)",
 sum:"“A soft answer turneth away wrath: but grievous words stir up anger”—The eyes of the LORD are in every place, beholding the evil and the good—“A merry heart maketh a cheerful countenance”—Better is a little with the fear of the LORD than great treasure with trouble—“Before honour is humility.”",
 bg:["Verse 1 is among the most practical sayings in the book. The word “soft” can mean gentle or tender, and the verse describes what often happens, not what always does. Verse 23 adds that “a word spoken in due season, how good is it!”","The chapter has many “better is” sayings (verses 16–17), which compare two things and decide what matters more. Verse 8 says the sacrifice of the wicked is an abomination to the LORD, “but the prayer of the upright is his delight,” echoing what the prophets later teach about the heart behind worship."]},
"prov.16":{title:"Pride goeth before destruction",year:"Date uncertain (Solomon’s era and later)",
 sum:"“The preparations of the heart in man, and the answer of the tongue, is from the LORD”—Commit thy works unto the LORD—“Pride goeth before destruction, and an haughty spirit before a fall”—A king’s favour is as a cloud of the latter rain—He that rules his spirit is better than he that takes a city.",
 bg:["Verse 9, “A man’s heart deviseth his way: but the LORD directeth his steps,” holds two ideas together: people make real plans, and God is still over the outcome. Verse 33 speaks of the lot cast into the lap, a way people at that time decided matters, and says the decision is “of the LORD.”","Verse 18 is often quoted in the shortened form “Pride goes before a fall.” In verses 10–15 the chapter turns to kings and what makes a good ruler: righteous lips, honest scales, and a desire to listen. Verse 25 is the same as 14:12."]},
"prov.17":{title:"A friend loveth at all times",year:"Date uncertain (Solomon’s era and later)",
 sum:"“Better is a dry morsel, and quietness therewith, than an house full of sacrifices with strife”—Children’s children are the crown of old men—“A friend loveth at all times, and a brother is born for adversity”—“A merry heart doeth good like a medicine”—Even a fool who holds his peace is counted wise.",
 bg:["Verse 22 reads, “A merry heart doeth good like a medicine: but a broken spirit drieth the bones.” The saying shows that people have long noticed how closely body and spirit are connected. It does not say that cheerfulness cures illness, only that it helps.","Verse 17 is read in more than one way: the friend who loves at all times may be the same person as the brother born for adversity, or the two may be different. Either way it says that real friendship holds up when times are hard. Verse 28 has the saying that even a fool seems wise if he keeps quiet."]},
"prov.18":{title:"The name of the LORD is a strong tower",year:"Date uncertain (Solomon’s era and later)",
 sum:"A man who isolates himself seeks his own desire—A fool takes no delight in understanding—“The name of the LORD is a strong tower: the righteous runneth into it, and is safe”—He that answers before he hears it, it is folly and shame—“Death and life are in the power of the tongue”—“A friend that sticketh closer than a brother.”",
 bg:["In the Bible a person’s “name” stands for who they are, their character and reputation. A strong tower was a place of refuge in a walled town, where people could run in times of danger. The verse says the LORD himself is that refuge for the righteous.","Verse 22 says, “Whoso findeth a wife findeth a good thing, and obtaineth favour of the LORD.” Verse 21 says the tongue holds power over life and death, and verse 13 says answering before hearing is “folly and shame.” These are the lessons of listening before speaking."]},
"prov.19":{title:"He that hath pity upon the poor lendeth unto the LORD",year:"Date uncertain (Solomon’s era and later)",
 sum:"Better is the poor who walks in his integrity than a fool and a liar—“Also, that the soul be without knowledge, it is not good”—Wealth makes many friends, but the poor are left alone—“He that hath pity upon the poor lendeth unto the LORD”—“There are many devices in a man’s heart; nevertheless the counsel of the LORD, that shall stand.”",
 bg:["Verse 17 teaches that kindness to the poor is treated by God as a loan he will repay. Proverbs often speaks about the poor and about God’s concern for them (see also 14:31 and 22:9), and many readers of the Bible see in this a reason to give. It is a principle about God’s care, not a promise of material return.","Verse 21 contrasts the many plans a person makes with the purpose of the LORD that stands. Verse 18, “chasten thy son while there is hope,” is a call to correct a child while it can still make a difference."]},
"prov.20":{title:"Wine is a mocker, strong drink is raging",year:"Date uncertain (Solomon’s era and later)",
 sum:"“Wine is a mocker, strong drink is raging: and whosoever is deceived thereby is not wise”—The sluggard will not plow by reason of the cold—Counsel in the heart is like deep water—“Divers weights, and divers measures, both of them are alike abomination to the LORD”—“The spirit of man is the candle of the LORD.”",
 bg:["Verse 1 warns that wine and strong drink lead people astray. The Hebrew word for “mocker” is the same used for the scorner in earlier chapters, and “raging” means noisy and brawling. Proverbs teaches this lesson in several places, including 23:29–35, and gives a general warning, not a medical lesson.","Verse 27 says that the human spirit (neshamah, “breath”) is a lamp from the LORD that searches the inward parts. Verse 9 asks, “Who can say, I have made my heart clean?” and verses 10 and 23 return to false weights, a theme from chapter 11. Verse 25 warns about rashly vowing something holy and only afterward thinking it over."]},
"prov.21":{title:"The king’s heart is in the hand of the LORD",year:"Date uncertain (Solomon’s era and later)",
 sum:"“The king’s heart is in the hand of the LORD, as the rivers of water: he turneth it whithersoever he will”—Every way of a man is right in his own eyes, but the LORD ponders the heart—“To do justice and judgment is more acceptable to the LORD than sacrifice”—He that stops his ears at the cry of the poor shall cry himself.",
 bg:["Verse 1 says that even a powerful king is under God. Farmers in dry lands dug channels to turn rivers and streams where they wished, and the saying pictures God directing a ruler’s will as easily. Verse 3 adds that doing right and justice matters more to God than sacrifice, a point also made by prophets like Samuel and Hosea.","Verse 13 says that whoever shuts his ears to the cry of the poor will cry himself and not be answered. Verse 31 says the horse is prepared for the day of battle, “but safety is of the LORD,” teaching that human effort and trust in God belong together."]},
"prov.22":{title:"Train up a child in the way he should go",year:"Date uncertain (Solomon’s era and later)",
 sum:"“A good name is rather to be chosen than great riches”—The rich and poor meet together; the LORD is the maker of them all—“Train up a child in the way he should go: and when he is old, he will not depart from it”—The words of the wise begin at verse 17—“Rob not the poor, because he is poor.”",
 bg:["The Hebrew verb for “train up” (chanak) is used for dedicating a house or a temple, so it carries the idea of starting someone on a path. “In the way he should go” can also be read as “according to his way,” meaning his own nature. Verse 6 is a general principle drawn from experience, not a guarantee, since faithful parents sometimes see a child choose another road. Doctrine and Covenants 68:25–28 and 93:40 also speak of parents teaching and bringing up children in light and truth.","The Solomon collection ends at verse 16, and verses 17–24:22 begin a new section, “the words of the wise.” Scholars have long noted close parallels with the Egyptian Instruction of Amenemope; for example, verse 22, “Rob not the poor,” resembles one of its sayings. Scholars still disagree about who borrowed from whom, or whether both drew on a common stock of wisdom, and verse 20’s “excellent things” may refer to thirty sayings."]},
"prov.23":{title:"Labour not to be rich",year:"Date uncertain (Solomon’s era and later)",
 sum:"Be careful when dining with a ruler—“Labour not to be rich: cease from thine own wisdom”—Riches make themselves wings and fly away—Withhold not correction from the child—“Buy the truth, and sell it not”—Woe, sorrow and wounds belong to those who tarry long at the wine.",
 bg:["Verse 5 says riches “make themselves wings; they fly away as an eagle toward heaven.” A comparable image about wealth is found in the Egyptian Instruction of Amenemope, one of the parallels scholars have noted in this section, which is called “the words of the wise” (22:17–24:22). The Bible and Egyptian text both teach that wealth is unreliable.","Verse 7 says of a miser, “as he thinketh in his heart, so is he.” The Hebrew is hard to translate, and many modern versions read it as a host who invites you to eat but is calculating the cost. The warning against drunkenness in verses 29–35 describes its effects with strong images, such as lying down in the midst of the sea."]},
"prov.24":{title:"A just man falleth seven times, and riseth up again",year:"Date uncertain (Solomon’s era and later)",
 sum:"Be not envious of evil men—“Through wisdom is an house builded”—If thou faint in the day of adversity, thy strength is small—Deliver them that are drawn to death—“A just man falleth seven times, and riseth up again”—The sluggard’s field is overgrown with thorns, and poverty comes upon him.",
 bg:["Verse 16 says a righteous person “falleth seven times” and gets up, which is a poetic number for many times. It is not praise for failing; it tells us that being righteous means getting up again after failure. Verses 11–12 add that we must not turn away from those being led to death, since God weighs the heart.","Verses 23–34 are labelled “these things also belong to the wise,” so the “words of the wise” section ends at verse 22 and a short extra collection follows. The picture of the sluggard’s field in verses 30–34 is a story about what one person saw and learned. “A little sleep, a little slumber” also appears at 6:10–11."]},
"prov.25":{title:"A word fitly spoken is like apples of gold",year:"About 700 BC (collected by Hezekiah’s men, 25:1)",
 sum:"These are proverbs of Solomon that “the men of Hezekiah king of Judah copied out”—It is the glory of God to conceal a thing, and the honour of kings to search it out—“A word fitly spoken is like apples of gold in pictures of silver”—“If thine enemy be hungry, give him bread to eat.”",
 bg:["Hezekiah, whose name means “the LORD strengthens,” ruled Judah around 715–686 BC, and 25:1 says his scribes copied more of Solomon’s sayings. This points to a later stage in the book’s formation. Chapters 25–29 are another collection of Solomon’s sayings, with images from nature and from the royal court.","Verses 21–22 are quoted in Romans 12:20: giving an enemy food and drink will “heap coals of fire” on his head. Most scholars read this as a call to kindness that may bring the enemy to shame and a change of heart, though the exact image of the coals is debated. Verse 11 compares a fitting word to apples (or citrus) of gold in settings of silver, a picture of beauty and skill."]},
"prov.26":{title:"As a dog returneth to his vomit, so a fool returneth to his folly",year:"About 700 BC (collected by Hezekiah’s men, 25:1)",
 sum:"“As snow in summer, and as rain in harvest, so honour is not seemly for a fool”—Answer not a fool according to his folly, lest thou be like him; answer a fool according to his folly, lest he be wise in his own conceit—“As a dog returneth to his vomit, so a fool returneth to his folly”—The sluggard turns on his bed like a door on its hinges.",
 bg:["Verses 4–5 seem to contradict each other: do not answer a fool, and yes, do answer a fool. Many readers take them as showing that wisdom requires judgment about when to answer and when to stay silent. The saying about the dog in verse 11 is quoted in 2 Peter 2:22.","The chapter has several short sections on fools, sluggards, and gossips. Verse 27 says that whoever digs a pit will fall into it, a saying that teaches that harm often returns to the one who plans it. These sayings describe patterns, not certain results in every case."]},
"prov.27":{title:"Iron sharpeneth iron",year:"About 700 BC (collected by Hezekiah’s men, 25:1)",
 sum:"“Boast not thyself of to morrow: for thou knowest not what a day may bring forth”—Let another man praise thee, and not thine own mouth—“Faithful are the wounds of a friend”—“Iron sharpeneth iron; so a man sharpeneth the countenance of his friend”—Know the state of thy flocks.",
 bg:["Verse 1 is echoed by James 4:13–14, which warns against making plans as if the future were certain. Verse 10 advises that a neighbour who is near is better than a brother far away, a practical saying about the people who are there when you need them.","Verse 17 compares friends to iron tools that sharpen one another, so honest conversation improves both. The chapter ends in verses 23–27 with advice to a farmer or shepherd to look well to his flocks, because wealth does not last forever without care."]},
"prov.28":{title:"The righteous are bold as a lion",year:"Date uncertain (Solomon’s era and later)",
 sum:"“The wicked flee when no man pursueth: but the righteous are bold as a lion”—When a land transgresses, it has many rulers—“He that covereth his sins shall not prosper: but whoso confesseth and forsaketh them shall have mercy”—He that gives to the poor shall not lack—A man of faithful dealing shall abound with blessings.",
 bg:["Verse 13 is a clear statement about repentance: hiding sin does not lead to success, but confessing and leaving it leads to mercy. The Hebrew word for “forsaketh” means to leave behind, so mercy follows a changed life and not words alone. Latter-day Saints will recall Doctrine and Covenants 58:43, which also pairs confession with forsaking sin.","Chapters 28–29 are generally read as the last part of the second Solomon collection, and they speak of justice, rulers and the poor. Verse 9 says that even the prayer of someone who turns away his ear from the law is an abomination, showing that God cares about how we live as well as how we pray."]},
"prov.29":{title:"Where there is no vision, the people perish",year:"Date uncertain (Solomon’s era and later)",
 sum:"He that is often reproved and hardens his neck shall be destroyed—“When the righteous are in authority, the people rejoice”—“Where there is no vision, the people perish: but he that keepeth the law, happy is he”—“The fear of man bringeth a snare: but whoso putteth his trust in the LORD shall be safe.”",
 bg:["The Hebrew word for “vision” (chazon) refers to a prophetic revelation from God, and “perish” (para) means to be unrestrained or to run wild, as in Exodus 32:25. The verse can be read as saying that when no word from God is heard, the people cast off restraint; the second half says that those who keep the law are happy.","Verse 15 says that the rod and reproof give wisdom, while “a child left to himself bringeth his mother to shame.” Verse 25 contrasts fearing people, which is a snare, with trusting the LORD, which brings safety."]},
"prov.30":{title:"Give me neither poverty nor riches",year:"Date uncertain",
 sum:"The words of Agur, son of Jakeh—“Every word of God is pure: he is a shield unto them that put their trust in him”—“Give me neither poverty nor riches; feed me with food convenient for me”—Four things never satisfied, and four too wonderful to know—The ant, the coney, the locust and the spider are wise.",
 bg:["Nothing is known about Agur son of Jakeh beyond this chapter. The word translated “prophecy” in verse 1 may be a place name, Massa, which is also a son of Ishmael in Genesis 25:14, so some scholars think Agur was from an Arabian tribe; others disagree. “Ithiel” (“God is with me”) and “Ucal” are named in verse 1, though the Hebrew is hard and translations differ on whether these are names at all.","Verses 4–6 ask who has ascended to heaven and gathered the wind, and what his name and his son’s name is. Some Christians read this with Jesus in mind, but that is a later reading. Verse 5 says “every word of God is pure,” and verse 6 warns not to add to his words, as in Deuteronomy 4:2. Verses 8–9 give a short prayer for neither poverty nor riches, so that one neither steals nor forgets God."]},
"prov.31":{title:"Who can find a virtuous woman?",year:"Date uncertain",
 sum:"The words of King Lemuel, which his mother taught him: do not give thy strength to women or to wine, and open thy mouth for the dumb—“Who can find a virtuous woman? for her price is far above rubies”—She works willingly, helps the poor, and “openeth her mouth with wisdom”—“Favour is deceitful, and beauty is vain: but a woman that feareth the LORD, she shall be praised.”",
 bg:["Lemuel means “belonging to God” or “devoted to God,” and he is called a king, but we do not know which king. His mother’s teaching comes first (verses 1–9), then a poem about the “virtuous woman” (verses 10–31). The poem is an acrostic: each verse begins with the next letter of the Hebrew alphabet, a way of saying the whole range of a life’s excellence, A to Z.","The Hebrew phrase eshet chayil is often translated “woman of valour” or “woman of strength,” using a word that is elsewhere used of soldiers and wealthy people. This woman buys a field, trades, and manages a household, and is praised in the city gates. Many scholars see in her a picture of Wisdom herself living out among ordinary people, so it is a portrait of an ideal and not a checklist for every woman."]},
"isa.1":{title:"Come now, and let us reason together",year:"About 740–700 BC",
 sum:"Isaiah sees a vision concerning Judah and Jerusalem—The people have forsaken the Lord; “the ox knoweth his owner, and the ass his master’s crib: but Israel doth not know”—Empty sacrifices and unwashed hands are rejected—“Though your sins be as scarlet, they shall be as white as snow”—Zion shall be redeemed with judgment.",
 bg:["The name Isaiah means “the LORD saves.” His book opens by naming four kings of Judah (Uzziah, Jotham, Ahaz, Hezekiah), so his ministry spanned roughly forty years or more. The Book of Mormon’s long quotation of Isaiah begins with chapter 2 (2 Nephi 12), so this opening chapter is not quoted there.","Scarlet and crimson were deep, fast dyes that were hard to wash out, so Isaiah’s promise that they can become “white as snow” and “as wool” pictures forgiveness that seems impossible. The picture of a ruined land with Zion left “as a cottage in a vineyard” (1:8) is often linked to the Assyrian invasion of about 701 BC."]},
"isa.2":{title:"They shall beat their swords into plowshares",year:"About 740–700 BC",
 sum:"In the last days “the mountain of the LORD’S house shall be established in the top of the mountains”—All nations flow to it to learn the Lord’s ways, and swords become plowshares—Judah is called to walk in the light—Pride, idols, and wealth are brought low—The Lord alone shall be exalted “in that day.”",
 bg:["Isaiah 2 is quoted in 2 Nephi 12, the start of Nephi’s long Isaiah section (2 Nephi 12–24 = Isaiah 2–14), which Nephi explains in 2 Nephi 25–30. The wording is nearly the same, though 2 Nephi 12:16 appears to add “the ships of the sea” before “the ships of Tarshish.” Verses 2–4 also appear in Micah 4:1–3, and many Latter-day Saints see in them the temples of the last days.","A plowshare is the blade of a plow and a pruninghook is a vine-trimming knife, so the image is of weapons melted down into farm tools. The “cedars of Lebanon” and “high mountains” (2:13–14) stand for proud people and nations, tall in their own eyes."]},
"isa.3":{title:"Grind the faces of the poor",year:"About 740–700 BC",
 sum:"The Lord takes away Judah’s leaders, “the stay and the staff,” and the people oppress one another—Jerusalem is ruined because their “tongue and their doings are against the LORD”—The rulers have “eaten up the vineyard” and ground the faces of the poor—The proud daughters of Zion lose their finery and are left in shame.",
 bg:["This chapter is quoted in 2 Nephi 13, with a few small wording differences. Isaiah’s charge in 3:14–15 is aimed first at “the ancients” and princes, the leaders who were supposed to care for the poor.","The long list of ornaments in 3:18–23 (anklets, headbands, mirrors, veils) shows how much fashion mattered to the wealthy women of Jerusalem. Isaiah turns it into a picture of reversal: perfume becomes a stench, a girdle becomes a rope, and beauty becomes branding."]},
"isa.4":{title:"In that day shall the branch of the LORD be beautiful and glorious",year:"About 740–700 BC",
 sum:"In a day of great loss, seven women seek one man to take away their reproach—“The branch of the LORD” shall be beautiful and glorious, and the remnant holy—The Lord washes away the filth of Zion’s daughters by “the spirit of judgment, and by the spirit of burning”—A cloud by day and a flaming fire by night shall cover Zion as a tabernacle and a place of refuge.",
 bg:["This short chapter is quoted in 2 Nephi 14 almost word for word. “Branch” translates a Hebrew word for a sprout or shoot, and many Christians and Latter-day Saints read it as a title of the coming Messiah; others read it as the fruitfulness of the land.","Verse 1 belongs with the end of chapter 3: after war has killed many men, women would be willing to share a husband just to be spared the shame of being childless. The cloud, smoke, and fire in verses 5–6 recall God’s presence with Israel in the wilderness in Exodus 13:21–22."]},
"isa.5":{title:"Woe unto them that call evil good, and good evil",year:"About 740–700 BC",
 sum:"The song of the vineyard: God planted it, fenced it, and looked for grapes, but it brought forth wild grapes—He will lay it waste, for Israel is his vineyard—Six woes fall on greed, drunkenness, mockery, twisting right and wrong, pride, and bribery—The Lord lifts an ensign and a distant nation comes swiftly.",
 bg:["This chapter is quoted in 2 Nephi 15. Isaiah plays on Hebrew sounds in verse 7: God looked for mishpat (“justice”) and saw mispach (“bloodshed”), for tsedaqah (“righteousness”) and heard tse’aqah (“a cry”). Jacob 5 also uses vineyard and olive-tree imagery for the house of Israel, though it is the separate allegory of Zenos.","Vineyards took years of work: clearing stones, building a wall and a watchtower, and digging a winepress. Isaiah first sings it like a love song, and only in verse 7 reveals that the vineyard is God’s people and the “wild grapes” are injustice. The refrain “his hand is stretched out still” (5:25) also returns in chapters 9 and 10."]},
"isa.6":{title:"Here am I; send me",year:"About 740 BC",
 sum:"In the year King Uzziah died, Isaiah sees the Lord on a throne, high and lifted up—Seraphim cry, “Holy, holy, holy, is the LORD of hosts”—A live coal from the altar purges Isaiah’s lips—He answers, “Here am I; send me”—He is told to speak to a people who will not understand, yet a holy seed remains like the stump of a tree.",
 bg:["Uzziah died about 740 BC, which places Isaiah’s call at about that time. Isaiah 6 is quoted in 2 Nephi 16, and in 2 Nephi 11:2–3 Nephi says Isaiah saw his Redeemer; John 12:41 also says Isaiah saw Christ’s glory. Jesus quoted verses 9–10 about people who see but do not perceive (Matthew 13:14–15).","“Seraphim” means “burning ones.” The live coal on Isaiah’s mouth shows that God himself cleanses the prophet before sending him. The “stump” in verse 13 is what remains when a great tree is cut down, yet the “holy seed” is its living shoot, a picture that Isaiah develops in chapter 11."]},
"isa.7":{title:"Behold, a virgin shall conceive, and bear a son",year:"About 735 BC",
 sum:"Syria and Israel march against King Ahaz of Judah, and his heart is shaken “as the trees of the wood are moved with the wind”—Isaiah and his son Shear-jashub meet Ahaz and tell him to be calm—Ahaz refuses to ask for a sign—“A virgin shall conceive, and bear a son, and shall call his name Immanuel”—Assyria will come on the land like a razor.",
 bg:["Isaiah 7 is quoted in 2 Nephi 17, and Matthew 1:22–23 quotes verse 14 about the birth of Jesus. “Immanuel” means “God with us.” The Hebrew word for “virgin” (almah) means a young woman of marriageable age; Matthew uses the Greek parthenos, and in 1 Nephi 11:13–20 Nephi sees Mary called a virgin.","Isaiah’s son’s name Shear-jashub means “a remnant shall return,” so even the boy’s name carried a message of hope and warning. The two kings, Rezin of Syria and Pekah son of Remaliah of Israel, are called “two tails of these smoking firebrands”: burned-out sticks that are no real threat. The “razor” (7:20) hired from beyond the Euphrates is Assyria, which will shave the land bare, a sign of shame."]},
"isa.8":{title:"To the law and to the testimony",year:"About 735–732 BC",
 sum:"Isaiah writes “Maher-shalal-hash-baz” on a great roll, and a son is given that name—Assyria will flood Judah like the river, yet “God is with us”—Do not fear what the people fear; sanctify the Lord as a sanctuary and a stone of stumbling—Do not seek mediums and wizards but go “to the law and to the testimony”—Those who reject the word walk into darkness.",
 bg:["Isaiah 8 is quoted in 2 Nephi 18. Maher-shalal-hash-baz means “hurrying to plunder, hastening to spoil,” a sign that Damascus and Samaria would soon be taken by Assyria. Isaiah’s own name and his children’s names were “for signs and wonders in Israel” (8:18; quoted in Hebrews 2:13), and Jacob 4:14–15 uses the “stone of stumbling” image for those who rejected the Savior.","The “waters of Shiloah” are the gentle, steady stream from the Gihon spring that supplied Jerusalem. Isaiah says the people prefer the small waters, but the Lord will send the great flooding river, which is Assyria, over the land. Even then the promise of Immanuel, “God with us,” stands in verses 8 and 10."]},
"isa.9":{title:"For unto us a child is born",year:"About 740–700 BC",
 sum:"Light comes to Zebulun and Naphtali, “the people that walked in darkness have seen a great light”—The yoke and rod of the oppressor are broken—“Unto us a child is born, unto us a son is given”: Wonderful, Counsellor, The mighty God, The everlasting Father, The Prince of Peace—Of the increase of his government there shall be no end—Because of pride and wickedness, the Lord’s hand is stretched out still against Israel.",
 bg:["Isaiah 9 is quoted in 2 Nephi 19, and Matthew 4:12–16 sees verses 1–2 fulfilled when Jesus began his ministry in Galilee. Latter-day Saints read verse 6 as a prophecy of Jesus Christ. One notable difference is that 2 Nephi 19:3 says “and increased the joy” where the KJV has “and not increased the joy,” which agrees with many modern Bible translations.","“The day of Midian” (9:4) recalls Gideon’s surprise victory in Judges 7, when God, not a large army, delivered Israel. The “battle of the warrior” and the “garments rolled in blood” are burned as fuel, picturing a time when war equipment is no longer needed. The refrain “his hand is stretched out still” (9:12, 17, 21) is a warning that judgment is not finished yet."]},
"isa.10":{title:"The remnant shall return",year:"About 740–700 BC",
 sum:"Woe to those who make unjust laws and rob the poor—Assyria is “the rod of mine anger” but boasts as if it were its own power, so the Lord will punish its pride—A remnant of Israel shall return and lean upon the Lord—Assyria’s army marches toward Jerusalem and is cut down like a forest—The Lord “shall lop the bough with terror.”",
 bg:["This chapter is quoted in 2 Nephi 20. Verses 21–22 repeat the name of Isaiah’s son Shear-jashub, “a remnant shall return,” turning his name into a promise. Verses 28–32 list towns Assyria’s army passes through on its way toward Jerusalem; the exact route is debated by scholars.","In 10:15 God asks whether an axe or saw can boast over the one who uses it. Assyria is only a tool in God’s hand, not the master. The cutting down of the forest in verses 33–34 sets up chapter 11, where a new shoot grows from what looks like a dead stump."]},
"isa.11":{title:"There shall come forth a rod out of the stem of Jesse",year:"About 740–700 BC",
 sum:"A rod comes out of the stem of Jesse, and a Branch grows out of his roots; the Spirit of the Lord rests upon him—He judges the poor with righteousness and slays the wicked with the breath of his lips—The wolf dwells with the lamb, and “the earth shall be full of the knowledge of the LORD”—The Lord sets his hand “the second time” to gather the remnant from the four corners of the earth.",
 bg:["Isaiah 11 is quoted in 2 Nephi 21, and Moroni quoted it to Joseph Smith, saying it was “about to be fulfilled” (Joseph Smith—History 1:40). Doctrine and Covenants 113:1–6 explains that the “stem of Jesse” is Christ and speaks of a servant in his hands, a descendant of Jesse and of Ephraim. Nephi applies the gentle peace of verses 4–9 to the last days in 2 Nephi 30:9–15, and 2 Nephi 25:17 refers to the Lord setting his hand “the second time.”","Jesse was the father of King David, so the “stem” is a family tree cut down to a stump, such as the one in chapter 6, from which a new shoot grows. The wolf with the lamb and the child playing near the cobra picture a world where harm no longer exists; “as the waters cover the sea” means there will be no place without God’s knowledge."]},
"isa.12":{title:"With joy shall ye draw water out of the wells of salvation",year:"About 740–700 BC",
 sum:"“O LORD, I will praise thee: though thou wast angry with me, thine anger is turned away”—“Behold, God is my salvation; I will trust, and not be afraid”—With joy they draw water out of the wells of salvation—Make known his deeds and sing, for “great is the Holy One of Israel in the midst of thee.”",
 bg:["This short song of thanks is quoted in 2 Nephi 22, closing the section that has the same wording as Isaiah 2–12. The Hebrew word for salvation here, yeshuah, comes from the same root as Isaiah’s own name and the name Jesus (Yeshua), so “God is my salvation” is nearly a pun on both.","Wells and springs were precious in a dry land, and drawing water was daily work. Isaiah calls salvation a well from which a person can draw again and again. Jesus used the same image when he called the thirsty to come to him at the Feast of Tabernacles (John 7:37–38)."]},
"isa.13":{title:"Howl ye; for the day of the LORD is at hand",year:"About 740–700 BC",
 sum:"The Lord musters an army of those “sanctified” to carry out his anger against Babylon—“The day of the LORD is at hand”; the sun, moon, and stars are darkened—The Lord punishes the world’s wickedness and humbles the proud—The Medes stir up the attack, and Babylon, “the glory of kingdoms,” becomes like Sodom and Gomorrah.",
 bg:["Isaiah 13 is quoted in 2 Nephi 23, which differs in a few places. For example, 2 Nephi 23:3 reads “mine anger is not upon them that rejoice in my highness,” and 2 Nephi 23:22 adds a promise of mercy to the Lord’s people. Babylon fell to Persia and the Medes in 539 BC, long after Isaiah, and in Latter-day Saint scripture “Babylon” is also a name for the wicked world that people are told to leave (D&C 133:5).","Verses 10 and 13 use cosmic language, with darkened stars and a shaken earth, which is the Old Testament’s usual way of describing a day when God’s judgment changes everything. Scholars differ on how much is meant for Babylon’s fall and how much for the end of the world; the text itself does not separate them."]},
"isa.14":{title:"How art thou fallen from heaven, O Lucifer, son of the morning!",year:"About 740–700 BC",
 sum:"The Lord has mercy on Jacob and gathers Israel to rest—Israel takes up a taunt against the king of Babylon, whose rod is broken—Sheol stirs to greet him—“How art thou fallen from heaven, O Lucifer, son of the morning!” who said, “I will ascend into heaven”—The Lord will cut off Babylon and break Assyria on his mountains.",
 bg:["Isaiah 14 is quoted in 2 Nephi 24. The Hebrew in verse 12 is helel ben shachar, “shining one, son of the dawn,” a name for the morning star; “Lucifer” comes from the Latin Vulgate, where it means “light-bearer.” The taunt is first aimed at a human king of Babylon, and Latter-day Saint scripture also applies the same story of pride and falling to Satan (D&C 76:25–27).","This is a satire in the form of a funeral song. The king who thought he could “ascend into heaven” is greeted in Sheol, the place of the dead, by other fallen kings, and worms are his bed. Verses 24–27 then include Assyria’s fall, since God’s plan is “purposed upon the whole earth.”"]},
"isa.15":{title:"The burden of Moab",year:"About 740–700 BC",
 sum:"In one night Ar and Kir of Moab are laid waste—Moab goes up to its high places to weep, with shaved heads and cut beards—Sackcloth is worn in the streets, and the cry is heard from Heshbon to Jahaz—Even the refugees are not safe, for “the waters of Dimon shall be full of blood.”",
 bg:["Isaiah 15 is not quoted in the Book of Mormon. “Burden” (Hebrew massa) means something lifted up and carried, and it is often used for a prophet’s message or oracle. Moab, east of the Dead Sea, descended from Lot (Genesis 19:37); the towns Nebo, Medeba, and Dibon are also named on the Moabite Stone, a king’s inscription from about 840 BC.","Shaved heads, cut beards, and sackcloth were ordinary signs of deep mourning in the ancient Near East. Isaiah paints Moab’s grief with names of towns crying out one after another, to show that no part of the land escapes."]},
"isa.16":{title:"Let mine outcasts dwell with thee, Moab",year:"About 740–700 BC",
 sum:"Moab is told to send a lamb to the ruler in Zion—Moab’s daughters are like wandering birds at the fords of Arnon—Moab asks Judah to hide the outcasts and be a covert from the spoiler—A throne is established in mercy, yet Moab’s pride brings mourning for its vineyards—Within three years Moab’s glory is brought into contempt.",
 bg:["This chapter is not quoted in the Book of Mormon. Many of its laments and phrases about Moab’s pride and vineyards also appear in Jeremiah 48, so the two prophets seem to share traditions or one borrows from the other.","The phrase “as the years of an hireling” (16:14) means exactly three years, counted carefully, as a hired worker counted days to the last one. Verses 4–5 are a surprising moment of hope in the middle of a judgment oracle: a throne “established in mercy,” sitting “in the tabernacle of David,” offers a refuge, even for foreigners."]},
"isa.17":{title:"At that day shall a man look to his Maker",year:"About 740–700 BC",
 sum:"Damascus is taken away from being a city, and the glory of Ephraim fades—Israel shall be like the gleaning left after a harvest, “two or three berries in the top of the uppermost bough”—“At that day shall a man look to his Maker”—The people have forgotten the God of their salvation—The nations roar like the sea, yet the Lord rebukes them and they flee like chaff.",
 bg:["This chapter is not quoted in the Book of Mormon. Damascus fell to Assyria in 732 BC and Samaria, the capital of Ephraim (northern Israel), in 722 BC, which fits Isaiah’s picture of the two allies falling together.","The harvest picture is of a reaper cutting grain and olives being shaken from the tree. Only a few stalks and two or three berries are left on the topmost branch, which stand for the few survivors. The “pleasant plants” and “strange slips” (17:10) are imported vines planted for fast growth, a picture of looking for help from foreign gods instead of the Rock of salvation."]},
"isa.18":{title:"Woe to the land shadowing with wings",year:"About 740–700 BC",
 sum:"A land beyond the rivers of Ethiopia sends messengers by sea in vessels of bulrushes—Swift messengers go to a nation “scattered and peeled,” tall and smooth-skinned—The Lord lifts an ensign and blows a trumpet, then quietly watches—He cuts down the sprigs with pruning hooks, and in time the people bring a gift to Mount Zion.",
 bg:["Isaiah 18 is not quoted in the Book of Mormon. “Ethiopia” is Cush, the Nubian kingdom south of Egypt, whose pharaohs ruled Egypt around 700 BC; 2 Kings 19:9 names one of them, Tirhakah. The phrase “shadowing with wings” is hard to translate, and scholars differ on whether it means buzzing insects, spread sails, or protective wings.","Vessels of bulrushes means boats made of papyrus reeds. The picture of the Lord “resting” in his dwelling while the harvest ripens (18:4) means that God is neither hurried nor anxious about the powers of the world. At the right moment he cuts off the vine’s branches in a moment, just before the harvest."]},
"isa.19":{title:"Blessed be Egypt my people, and Assyria the work of my hands",year:"About 740–700 BC",
 sum:"The Lord rides on a cloud into Egypt, and its idols and hearts melt—The Nile dries up, and fishers, weavers, and counselors despair—Egypt’s wise men of Zoan and Noph are foolish, and Egypt staggers “as a drunken man staggereth in his vomit”—Five cities speak the language of Canaan, and there is an altar in the midst of Egypt—“Blessed be Egypt my people, and Assyria the work of my hands, and Israel mine inheritance.”",
 bg:["This chapter is not quoted in the Book of Mormon, but its closing promise that Egypt and Assyria will be called God’s people is much like the Lord’s words in 2 Nephi 29:7 that he remembers every nation. Verse 18 mentions “the city of destruction” in many Bible translations, while some old manuscripts read “city of the sun” (Heres); the text does not say which city is meant.","Egypt’s life depended on the Nile, so when Isaiah says the river dries up, the reed beds wither, and the fishermen and flax workers mourn, he is describing an entire economy failing. Verses 23–25 end with a highway running from Egypt to Assyria and Israel as the third partner, a surprising picture of three old enemies sharing one blessing."]},
"isa.20":{title:"Naked and barefoot three years for a sign",year:"About 711 BC",
 sum:"Sargon of Assyria sends his commander against Ashdod—Isaiah is told to loose the sackcloth from his loins and the shoe from his foot, and he walks “naked and barefoot” for three years—It is a sign that Egypt and Ethiopia will be led away captive—Those who trusted in them shall be ashamed.",
 bg:["Sargon II is named only here in the Bible. Assyrian records say his army captured Ashdod in 711 BC, which helps to date this chapter. Isaiah 20 is not quoted in the Book of Mormon.","Prophets sometimes acted out a message, as Jeremiah did with a yoke (Jeremiah 27). “Naked” in Hebrew can mean wearing only an undergarment or without an outer robe, so Isaiah was probably dressed like a prisoner of war, not completely unclothed. Judah’s leaders hoped Egypt and Ethiopia would save them from Assyria, and Isaiah’s walk showed that those hopes were empty."]},
"isa.21":{title:"Watchman, what of the night?",year:"About 740–700 BC",
 sum:"A dreadful vision comes of Elam and Media against Babylon, and Isaiah is pained as a woman in labor—A watchman sees chariots and cries, “Babylon is fallen, is fallen”—A burden of Dumah (Edom): “Watchman, what of the night?”—The watchman answers, “The morning cometh, and also the night”—A burden on Arabia: within a year the glory of Kedar shall fail.",
 bg:["Isaiah 21 is not quoted in the Book of Mormon, but “Babylon is fallen, is fallen” is repeated in Revelation 14:8 and 18:2, and in Doctrine and Covenants 133:5 the Lord calls his people out of Babylon. Scholars differ on who “the desert of the sea” (21:1) is, though verses 2 and 9 point to Babylon.","The watchman standing on the tower all day and night (21:8) is an image Isaiah uses for a prophet who watches for what the Lord will do. The answer in verse 12, “The morning cometh, and also the night,” is intentionally two-sided: relief does not last and the question may be asked again. “Within a year, according to the years of an hireling” (21:16) means exactly one year."]},
"isa.22":{title:"The key of the house of David",year:"About 740–700 BC",
 sum:"The valley of vision, Jerusalem, is full of noise, and its people hide on the housetops while the city is besieged—They gather water and tear down houses to strengthen the wall but do not look to the One who made it—“Let us eat and drink; for to morrow we shall die”—The proud steward Shebna is replaced by Eliakim, who receives “the key of the house of David”—The nail fastened in a sure place shall in time be removed.",
 bg:["Isaiah 22 is not quoted in the Book of Mormon, though the saying “eat, drink, and be merry” in 2 Nephi 28:7–8 echoes verse 13, and Paul quotes it in 1 Corinthians 15:32. Revelation 3:7 applies the “key of David” and “open, and none shall shut” to Christ. The name Eliakim means “God raises up.”","The preparations in verses 9–11 fit what archaeologists have found in Jerusalem: Hezekiah’s tunnel that brought water into the city, and a thick wall built partly over demolished houses. The key on the shoulder (22:22) pictures authority. A key was large, and the chief steward carried it as the one who could admit or exclude people at the king’s house."]},
"isa.23":{title:"Howl, ye ships of Tarshish",year:"About 740–700 BC",
 sum:"The merchant city of Tyre is laid waste, and its ships of Tarshish howl—Zidon and the sea itself are ashamed; “who hath taken this counsel against Tyre, the crowning city?”—The Lord has purposed to stain the pride of all glory—After seventy years Tyre returns to her trade “as an harlot,” but her gain will finally be holiness to the Lord.",
 bg:["Isaiah 23 is not quoted in the Book of Mormon. Tyre was a rich island-city of Phoenicia, and its ships traded across the Mediterranean; “Tarshish” was a distant western port, often thought to be in Spain, though scholars are not sure. Chittim (23:1) is Cyprus.","Calling Tyre a harlot (23:15–17) is a picture of a city that sells itself to every kingdom for profit, so the word is about trade and not mere sin. The chapter ends with a surprising turn: Tyre’s earnings will be “holiness to the LORD,” given to feed and clothe those who serve him."]},
"isa.24":{title:"The earth shall reel to and fro like a drunkard",year:"About 740–700 BC",
 sum:"The Lord makes the earth empty and waste, and priest and people are alike—“The earth also is defiled under the inhabitants thereof; because they have transgressed the laws, changed the ordinance, broken the everlasting covenant”—The earth reels like a drunkard and falls—The Lord punishes the host of the high ones on high, and “the LORD of hosts shall reign in mount Zion.”",
 bg:["Isaiah 24–27 is often called “Isaiah’s Apocalypse” because it describes judgment on the whole earth. Verse 5 closely matches Doctrine and Covenants 1:15, and Doctrine and Covenants 88:87 uses the same drunkard image for the earth in the last days. This chapter is not quoted in the Book of Mormon.","The “windows from on high” opening (24:18) recalls the flood in Genesis 7:11, as if the world were returning to the waters of its beginning. The earth moves like a drunkard and sways like a flimsy hut (a “cottage”) in the wind, pictures of a world whose foundations no longer hold."]},
"isa.25":{title:"He will swallow up death in victory",year:"About 740–700 BC",
 sum:"Isaiah praises the Lord, whose counsels are “faithfulness and truth,” and who has been a strength to the poor—On Mount Zion the Lord prepares a feast of fat things for all people—He destroys the covering cast over all nations and “will swallow up death in victory”—The Lord “will wipe away tears from off all faces”—Moab is trodden down in its pride.",
 bg:["Paul quotes verse 8 in 1 Corinthians 15:54 about the resurrection, and Revelation 21:4 echoes “wipe away tears.” The Book of Mormon uses similar words in Mosiah 16:7–8, where the grave has no victory because death is “swallowed up in Christ.” Isaiah 25 itself is not quoted in the Book of Mormon.","“Wines on the lees well refined” (25:6) means wine left to age on its dregs, then strained clear, rich, and strong, the finest drink for a banquet. The “covering” or veil spread over all nations (25:7) is a picture of the grief and ignorance that death casts over the world, which God removes."]},
"isa.26":{title:"Thou wilt keep him in perfect peace",year:"About 740–700 BC",
 sum:"A song in the land of Judah: salvation is set as walls and bulwarks, and the righteous nation enters—“Thou wilt keep him in perfect peace, whose mind is stayed on thee”—The wicked are brought low, but the Lord’s people long for him in the night—Though Israel has been in pain like a woman in labor, “thy dead men shall live”—The people are told to hide until the indignation is overpast.",
 bg:["In Hebrew, “perfect peace” in verse 3 is simply “peace, peace” (shalom shalom), a doubling that means complete, full peace. Verse 19, “thy dead men shall live, together with my dead body shall they arise,” is one of the Old Testament’s clearest promises of resurrection. This chapter is not quoted in the Book of Mormon, and it should not be confused with 2 Nephi 26, which echoes Isaiah 29.","Verses 17–18 compare the nation’s suffering to a woman in labor who gives birth only to wind, meaning that their pain seems to have produced nothing. Then verse 19 turns that picture around, so the earth itself gives birth to the dead. “The Lord JEHOVAH is everlasting strength” (26:4) translates a Hebrew phrase meaning “rock of ages.”"]},
"isa.27":{title:"Israel shall blossom and bud, and fill the face of the world with fruit",year:"About 740–700 BC",
 sum:"The Lord punishes “leviathan the piercing serpent” and slays the dragon in the sea—“A vineyard of red wine”: the Lord keeps it and waters it every moment—Jacob shall take root, and “Israel shall blossom and bud, and fill the face of the world with fruit”—The Lord smites Israel in measure and removes the sin of Jacob—A great trumpet is blown, and the outcasts come to worship in Jerusalem.",
 bg:["This chapter is not quoted in the Book of Mormon. The vineyard song in verses 2–6 answers the sad vineyard song of Isaiah 5: there the vineyard produced wild grapes, but here the Lord guards it day and night and it fills the world with fruit.","Leviathan was an ancient symbol of chaos and sea monsters, also found in Job 41 and Psalm 74:14, and here it stands for the powers that oppose God. Scholars differ on whether Isaiah meant particular empires or evil in general; the text only says God will defeat them."]},
"isa.28":{title:"Precept must be upon precept, line upon line",year:"About 740–700 BC",
 sum:"Woe to the proud drunkards of Ephraim, whose glory is a fading flower—Priests and prophets stagger with wine, and mock Isaiah’s teaching as “precept upon precept, line upon line”—The people trust in “a covenant with death” and “lies our refuge”—The Lord lays in Zion “a precious corner stone, a sure foundation”—Hail sweeps away lies, and the Lord does “his strange work.”",
 bg:["The drunken mockers repeat the Hebrew sounds tsav latsav, qav laqav, which sound like a child’s baby-talk, as if Isaiah were teaching them like little ones. In reply, the Lord says that they will learn his word only through foreign speakers. Latter-day Saint scripture uses “line upon line, precept upon precept” as a good pattern of learning in 2 Nephi 28:30 and Doctrine and Covenants 98:12, and Doctrine and Covenants 95:4 uses “his strange work.” Isaiah 28 itself is not quoted in the Book of Mormon.","Verse 20 uses a vivid picture: a bed too short to stretch out on and a blanket too narrow to wrap around, meaning that the false comforts of the proud will not cover them. In contrast, Peter applies the “precious corner stone” in verse 16 to Jesus Christ (1 Peter 2:6)."]},
"isa.29":{title:"A marvellous work and a wonder",year:"About 740–700 BC",
 sum:"Woe to Ariel, the city where David dwelt, which is besieged and brought low—Her voice is “out of the ground” like a familiar spirit, and the multitude of her foes becomes like dust—A vision becomes “as the words of a book that is sealed”: the learned cannot read it, and the unlearned cannot read it—The people draw near with their mouths but their hearts are far—“A marvellous work and a wonder”—the deaf hear the words of the book and the meek rejoice.",
 bg:["“Ariel” means either “lion of God” or “altar hearth,” and here it is a name for Jerusalem. Verses 11–12 are echoed in Joseph Smith—History 1:63–65, where Martin Harris showed characters from the plates to Charles Anthon, who said, “I cannot read a sealed book.” Verse 13 is what the Lord told Joseph Smith in the First Vision (Joseph Smith—History 1:19), and verse 14 is echoed in the opening of several revelations in the Doctrine and Covenants, such as D&C 4:1.","Isaiah 29 is echoed and expanded in 2 Nephi 26–27, where Nephi applies the “voice from the dust” to the Book of Mormon (2 Nephi 26:14–17) and 2 Nephi 27 retells the sealed book at greater length. In verse 8, Isaiah compares the enemy nations to a hungry person who dreams of eating but wakes empty, a picture of the armies that dream of conquering Zion and wake with nothing."]},
"isa.30":{title:"In quietness and in confidence shall be your strength",year:"About 740–700 BC",
 sum:"Woe to the rebellious children who go down to Egypt for help—The people say to the seers, “See not,” and prophesy only smooth things—“In returning and rest shall ye be saved; in quietness and in confidence shall be your strength,” but they say, “We will flee upon horses”—The Lord waits to be gracious, and the people will hear a voice saying, “This is the way, walk ye in it”—Tophet is prepared for the king of Assyria.",
 bg:["This chapter is not quoted in the Book of Mormon. “Rahab” in verse 7 is a poetic name for Egypt; it comes from a sea monster of ancient stories and probably means something like “the proud one” or “the stormer.” Isaiah calls Egypt “Rahab that sitteth still,” meaning a boastful power that does nothing for those who trust it.","In verse 14, a broken wall becomes a smashed clay jar: so thoroughly crushed that not one fragment is big enough to carry fire from a hearth or water from a pit. Tophet in verse 33 was a place in the Valley of Hinnom outside Jerusalem, where children were sacrificed in later years (Jeremiah 7:31), and it became a symbol of judgment."]},
"isa.31":{title:"Woe to them that go down to Egypt for help",year:"About 740–700 BC",
 sum:"Woe to those who rely on Egypt’s horses and chariots and do not look to the Holy One of Israel—“The Egyptians are men, and not God; and their horses flesh, and not spirit”—The Lord defends Jerusalem like a lion over its prey and like birds hovering over their young—Israel is told to turn from idols—Assyria falls by a sword “not of a mighty man.”",
 bg:["This chapter is not quoted in the Book of Mormon. Egypt was famous for horses and chariots, and Judah’s leaders hoped these could stop Assyria. Isaiah’s answer is that God, who is spirit, counts for more than an army of flesh.","Verse 4 gives the picture of a lion that will not be frightened by a crowd of shepherds shouting at it, and verse 5 compares the Lord to birds hovering over their nest. Verse 8 foretells that Assyria will fall by a sword “not of a mighty man,” which agrees with the account in 2 Kings 19:35 of the angel who struck the Assyrian camp around 701 BC."]},
"isa.32":{title:"A king shall reign in righteousness",year:"About 740–700 BC",
 sum:"A king shall reign in righteousness, and a man shall be “as an hiding place from the wind, and a covert from the tempest”—Eyes shall see and ears hear, and the vile will no longer be called noble—Women at ease are warned that their fields will fail—“Until the spirit be poured upon us from on high,” the wilderness becomes a fruitful field—“The work of righteousness shall be peace.”",
 bg:["This chapter is not quoted in the Book of Mormon. Scholars differ on whether verse 1 first means King Hezekiah or looks ahead to a future ideal king; Christians read it as a prophecy of Christ, and the text does not say which it is.","Verses 2 and 5–8 use opposites to make a point about character: a “vile person” will no longer be called generous (the Hebrew word for “liberal” means “noble”), and a fool is no longer called wise. The chapter moves from the king’s righteous rule to “quietness and assurance for ever” (32:17), a peace that comes from doing what is right, not from weapons."]},
"isa.33":{title:"The LORD is our judge, the LORD is our lawgiver, the LORD is our king",year:"About 740–700 BC",
 sum:"Woe to the destroyer who deals treacherously, for he will be spoiled—“O LORD, be gracious unto us; we have waited for thee”—Ambassadors of peace weep, for the covenant is broken—“Who among us shall dwell with the devouring fire?” Those who walk righteously—Thine eyes shall see the king in his beauty, and Zion shall be a quiet habitation—“The LORD is our judge, the LORD is our lawgiver, the LORD is our king; he will save us.”",
 bg:["This chapter is not quoted in the Book of Mormon. Many scholars read verses 1 and 7–8 as referring to Assyria breaking a treaty with Hezekiah and sending ambassadors away, though the chapter does not name the king. The phrase “not one of the stakes thereof shall ever be removed” (33:20) helps explain why Latter-day Saints call regional units of the Church “stakes of Zion” (see D&C 82:14).","Zion is described as a tent that will never be taken down, with its stakes fixed and its cords unbroken. In contrast, an enemy ship in verse 23 has loose ropes and cannot even hold up its mast. The question in verse 14, “Who among us shall dwell with the devouring fire?”, is answered in verses 15–16: the one who walks righteously and speaks uprightly."]},
"isa.34":{title:"The indignation of the LORD is upon all nations",year:"About 701 BC",
 sum:"“The indignation of the LORD is upon all nations”—The heavens are rolled together as a scroll—The sword of the LORD comes down upon Idumea for “the day of the LORD’s vengeance”—Her land becomes a wilderness for wild creatures—“Seek ye out of the book of the LORD.”",
 bg:["Idumea (verses 5–6) is the Greek form of Edom, the land south of the Dead Sea settled by the descendants of Esau. Edom often stands for the nations that opposed Israel, and Doctrine and Covenants 1:36 uses “Idumea” to mean “the world.”","Verse 4 describes judgment in cosmic pictures: stars dissolving and the heavens “rolled together as a scroll.” Ancient prophets used such language for the Lord’s power over everything, and Revelation 6:13–14 borrows it. The ruined land filled with owls, pelicans and wild beasts is a common ancient way to picture a city left empty."]},
"isa.35":{title:"The desert shall rejoice, and blossom as the rose",year:"About 701 BC",
 sum:"The wilderness and the solitary place rejoice and blossom—“Strengthen ye the weak hands”—The eyes of the blind are opened, the lame leap, and the tongue of the dumb sings—A highway of holiness—“The ransomed of the LORD shall return, and come to Zion with songs.”",
 bg:["The “rose” of verse 1 is a Hebrew word that probably means a bulb flower such as a crocus or lily; scholars are not certain which. When John the Baptist asked whether Jesus was the one to come, Jesus answered by pointing to the blind seeing and the lame walking (Matthew 11:2–5), echoing verses 5–6. Doctrine and Covenants 49:24–25 also uses the picture of the wilderness blossoming as the rose.","Think of a dry desert road suddenly lined with flowers and springs: that is how Isaiah pictures the return of the Lord’s people. The “highway of holiness” is a safe road where the unclean and the foolish do not wander. Verse 10 appears almost word for word in Isaiah 51:11, which is quoted in 2 Nephi 8:11."]},
"isa.36":{title:"What confidence is this wherein thou trustest?",year:"About 701 BC",
 sum:"Sennacherib of Assyria takes the fenced cities of Judah—His messenger, the Rabshakeh, stands by the conduit of the upper pool—“What confidence is this wherein thou trustest?”—He mocks trust in Egypt and in the LORD, and shouts in the Jews’ language—The people keep silent, as Hezekiah commanded.",
 bg:["Chapters 36–39 repeat the story in 2 Kings 18:13–20:19, with some differences. Sennacherib’s own record, the Taylor Prism, says he shut Hezekiah up in Jerusalem “like a bird in a cage.” Rabshakeh is a title, probably meaning chief cupbearer or chief officer, not a personal name. “The Jews’ language” (verse 11) is Hebrew.","The Rabshakeh calls Egypt a “bruised reed,” a staff that splinters and pierces the hand of anyone who leans on it (verse 6). Hezekiah’s name is generally understood to mean “the LORD strengthens,” and Isaiah’s name “the LORD saves,” both fitting for this story."]},
"isa.37":{title:"O LORD of hosts, God of Israel, that dwellest between the cherubims",year:"About 701 BC",
 sum:"Hezekiah tears his clothes and sends to Isaiah—Isaiah promises that Assyria will hear a rumor and fall—Hezekiah spreads Sennacherib’s letter before the LORD and prays—Isaiah answers that the LORD will defend the city—The angel of the LORD smites 185,000 in the Assyrian camp, and Sennacherib is slain by his sons.",
 bg:["This chapter parallels 2 Kings 19. Hezekiah prays in the house of the LORD, spreading the threatening letter before him, and the cherubim over the ark of the covenant are the picture of God’s throne. Assyrian records of this campaign boast of taking many Judean towns but do not claim to have captured Jerusalem. Sennacherib died in 681 BC.","The Lord says to Sennacherib, “I will put my hook in thy nose, and my bridle in thy lips” (verse 29). Assyrian carvings show captives led by rings and ropes, so the picture is of a proud king pulled back like a beast. The “remnant” and the sign of verse 30 promise that life in Judah will go on."]},
"isa.38":{title:"Set thine house in order: for thou shalt die, and not live",year:"About 701 BC",
 sum:"Hezekiah is sick and told to set his house in order—He turns his face to the wall and weeps—The LORD adds fifteen years to his life—The shadow goes back ten degrees on the sun dial of Ahaz as a sign—Hezekiah’s writing: “The grave cannot praise thee.”",
 bg:["This story is also told in 2 Kings 20:1–11. The “degrees” (verse 8) translate a Hebrew word for steps or stairs, so the device was probably a stairway or similar sundial, though the exact form is not known. The cake of figs laid on the boil (verse 21) was an ordinary ancient poultice.","Hezekiah’s psalm uses vivid images for a life cut short: a shepherd’s tent pulled up and carried away, and a weaver cutting the cloth from the loom (verse 12). He says he chattered “like a crane or a swallow” and mourned like a dove (verse 14). In the Old Testament view of the time, the grave is a silent place where no one praises God (verse 18), which is why he rejoices in more years."]},
"isa.39":{title:"There is nothing among my treasures that I have not shewed them",year:"About 701 BC",
 sum:"Merodach-baladan of Babylon sends letters and a present to Hezekiah—Hezekiah shows the envoys all his treasures and armour—Isaiah foretells that everything will be carried to Babylon—Some of Hezekiah’s sons will be eunuchs in the palace of the king of Babylon—Hezekiah says, “Good is the word of the LORD.”",
 bg:["This chapter parallels 2 Kings 20:12–19. Merodach-baladan was a Babylonian ruler who rebelled against Assyria, so his visit may have been an effort to find an ally. Babylon was not yet the world power it would become, which is why Isaiah’s warning about Babylon (verses 6–7) looks far ahead.","Hezekiah’s closing words may be read as thankfulness for peace in his own days or as a sign of resignation; the text does not say which. This ends the first major part of Isaiah, and chapters 40 onward turn to comfort for people carried away to Babylon."]},
"isa.40":{title:"Comfort ye, comfort ye my people",year:"About 700 BC (see note)",
 sum:"“Comfort ye, comfort ye my people, saith your God”—The voice crying in the wilderness, “Prepare ye the way of the LORD”—“All flesh is grass... the word of our God shall stand for ever”—Zion’s good tidings and the Shepherd who gathers lambs in his arm—No one can be compared to the Creator of the ends of the earth—“They that wait upon the LORD shall renew their strength.”",
 bg:["A note on dating. Many scholars date chapters 40–66 to the time of the Babylonian exile or after (about 540 BC or later), because they speak to people already in exile and name Cyrus, who ruled in the 500s BC. Latter-day Saints also note that the Book of Mormon quotes chapters from this section as the words of Isaiah (1 Nephi 20–21 = Isaiah 48–49; 2 Nephi 7–8 = Isaiah 50–51; Mosiah 14 = Isaiah 53; 3 Nephi 22 = Isaiah 54) in a record that left Jerusalem about 600 BC. Both views are noted here, and the year for these chapters is given as about 700 BC for that reason.","The voice crying in the wilderness (verses 3–5) is applied to John the Baptist in Matthew 3:3 and John 1:23. The picture is of workers leveling a road for a king’s procession, with valleys raised and mountains made low. Verse 31 compares those who wait on the LORD to eagles, and in Hebrew “wait” carries the sense of hoping and looking for him."]},
"isa.41":{title:"Fear thou not; for I am with thee",year:"About 700 BC (see note)",
 sum:"The LORD stirs up one from the east and gives nations before him—Idol makers encourage one another—“Thou, Israel, art my servant... the seed of Abraham my friend”—“Fear thou not; for I am with thee”—Israel as a threshing instrument, and water in the wilderness—The idols are challenged to foretell anything.",
 bg:["Isaiah calls Israel by two names: Jacob, which means “heel-holder” or “supplanter” (Genesis 25:26), and Israel, “he struggles with God” (Genesis 32:28). The Lord also calls Abraham “my friend” (verse 8), and James 2:23 uses the same title. “Thou worm Jacob” (verse 14) pictures how small and helpless Israel feels, yet the LORD calls himself her Redeemer.","In verses 15–16 Israel is made like a threshing sledge, a heavy board studded with teeth that crushes grain; the mountains are the chaff it breaks apart. The one “from the east” in verses 2 and 25 is understood by many as Cyrus of Persia, though he is not named until chapter 44."]},
"isa.42":{title:"Behold my servant, whom I uphold",year:"About 700 BC (see note)",
 sum:"“Behold my servant, whom I uphold; mine elect, in whom my soul delighteth”—He will bring forth judgment gently, not breaking the bruised reed—He is given for a covenant and “a light of the Gentiles”—“Sing unto the LORD a new song”—The blind and deaf servant who did not see or hear—Israel’s sin and the LORD’s anger.",
 bg:["Matthew 12:18–21 quotes verses 1–4 and applies them to Jesus Christ as he healed and taught quietly. Scholars debate who the servant is in each passage, since Isaiah calls Israel the LORD’s servant (41:8) and also speaks of a servant who will gather Israel (49:5–6).","The “bruised reed” and “smoking flax” (verse 3) are fragile things: a cracked reed pipe and a lamp wick nearly out. The servant does not snap the one or snuff the other, but tends them gently. Verses 18–20 then turn to a servant, Israel, who has eyes and ears but does not use them."]},
"isa.43":{title:"Fear not: for I have redeemed thee",year:"About 700 BC (see note)",
 sum:"“Fear not: for I have redeemed thee, I have called thee by thy name; thou art mine”—Through waters and fire the LORD is with Israel—“Ye are my witnesses”—“Beside me there is no saviour”—“Behold, I will do a new thing”—The LORD blots out transgressions and remembers sins no more.",
 bg:["“Redeemed” in Hebrew (ga’al) is the word for a family member who buys back a relative from slavery or debt. Verse 3 says the LORD gives Egypt, Ethiopia and Seba as a ransom; scholars understand this in different ways, and the text does not explain it. Verse 25 says that he blots out transgressions “for mine own sake” and remembers sins no more, a promise echoed in Doctrine and Covenants 58:42.","“When thou passest through the waters... when thou walkest through the fire” (verse 2) recalls the Red Sea and the fiery furnace, and promises that trials will come but will not destroy those who belong to the LORD. The “new thing” of verses 18–19 is rivers in the desert, a second great rescue that will outshine the first, the Exodus."]},
"isa.44":{title:"Fear not, O Jacob, my servant",year:"About 700 BC (see note)",
 sum:"“Fear not, O Jacob, my servant; and thou, Jesurun, whom I have chosen”—The LORD pours water on the thirsty and his Spirit on their offspring—“I am the first, and I am the last”—The folly of making a god of wood—The LORD blots out transgressions—Cyrus is named: “He is my shepherd.”",
 bg:["Jacob here means the whole people, and “Jesurun” (also Jeshurun, see Deuteronomy 32:15; 33:5) is a poetic name for Israel, probably meaning “upright one” (scholars are not certain). The chapter makes fun of idols: half of a log warms a man and cooks his meal, and the other half he bows down to and calls “my god” (verses 14–20).","Verse 28 and Isaiah 45:1 name Cyrus, the Persian king who conquered Babylon in 539 BC and allowed the Jews to go home (Ezra 1:1–3). The Lord calls him “my shepherd,” a title of kings, and “his anointed” in 45:1. His Persian name is often explained as relating to “sun,” though that is uncertain. Scholars who date these chapters to the exile see a prophet speaking in Cyrus’s own time, while Latter-day Saints and others read it as Isaiah naming him in advance, over a century before he reigned."]},
"isa.45":{title:"Unto me every knee shall bow",year:"About 700 BC (see note)",
 sum:"The LORD calls Cyrus his anointed, opens doors before him and gives him treasures of darkness—“I am the LORD, and there is none else”—“Woe unto him that striveth with his Maker”—The LORD did not make the earth in vain—“Look unto me, and be ye saved”—“Unto me every knee shall bow, every tongue shall swear.”",
 bg:["“Anointed” translates the Hebrew mashiach (Messiah). It is used here for a foreign king whom the Lord uses to free his people, which is surprising since Cyrus did not worship the LORD by name (verse 4). In verse 7, “evil” translates a word that means calamity or disaster, set against “peace”; the Lord is saying that he rules over both good times and hard times.","The potter and the clay (verse 9) is a picture that Isaiah, Jeremiah (18:1–6) and Paul (Romans 9:20–21) all use to say that the Maker has the right to shape his work. Verse 23, “every knee shall bow,” is quoted in Philippians 2:10–11 and echoed in Mosiah 27:31."]},
"isa.46":{title:"Bel boweth down, Nebo stoopeth",year:"About 700 BC (see note)",
 sum:"Bel and Nebo, the gods of Babylon, bow down and are carried on beasts—The LORD has borne Israel from the womb to old age—“To whom will ye liken me?”—Idols must be carried and cannot move—The LORD calls a ravenous bird from the east—“I have spoken it, I will also bring it to pass.”",
 bg:["Bel is a form of Baal, meaning “lord,” here a title of Marduk, the chief god of Babylon. Nebo (Nabu) was the god of writing and wisdom. At the Babylonian new year, statues of the gods were carried in procession. Isaiah’s picture of the idols loaded as burdens on tired animals is a deliberate mockery.","The contrast is striking: the idols have to be carried, but the LORD carries his people “even to your old age” and “to hoar hairs” (verses 3–4). The “ravenous bird from the east” (verse 11) is generally understood as Cyrus, swooping in to do the LORD’s purpose."]},
"isa.47":{title:"Come down, and sit in the dust, O virgin daughter of Babylon",year:"About 700 BC (see note)",
 sum:"Babylon, the virgin daughter, is told to come down and sit in the dust—She must grind meal, uncover her locks and be unveiled—“I am, and none else beside me”—Widowhood and loss of children come in one moment—Her sorceries and astrologers cannot save her.",
 bg:["Babylon fell to Cyrus in 539 BC. Isaiah speaks of the city as a woman and calls her “virgin daughter” (a city that had never been conquered) and “lady of kingdoms.” Her fall is shown as a queen turned into a slave, forced to grind grain, a hard task given to servants, and to wade through rivers with her skirts lifted (verse 2).","Babylon’s “astrologers, the stargazers, the monthly prognosticators” (verse 13) were real: Babylonians kept careful records of the stars. Isaiah says they cannot save themselves from the fire. In later scripture Babylon came to stand for worldly pride and power, as in Revelation 17–18 and Doctrine and Covenants 1:16."]},
"isa.48":{title:"I have refined thee, but not with silver",year:"About 700 BC (see note)",
 sum:"The LORD speaks to Israel who swear by his name but not in truth—He has declared things from the beginning, lest Israel say an idol did it—“I have refined thee... I have chosen thee in the furnace of affliction”—“Go ye forth of Babylon”—“O that thou hadst hearkened to my commandments! then had thy peace been as a river”—“There is no peace, saith the LORD, unto the wicked.”",
 bg:["Nephi reads this chapter to his brothers as 1 Nephi 20, saying he wanted to “more fully persuade them to believe in the Lord their Redeemer” (1 Nephi 19:23). The Book of Mormon text adds the words “or out of the waters of baptism” in 1 Nephi 20:1, so that those who come “out of the waters of Judah” are also those who are baptized, and has several other small differences from the King James wording.","“Peace as a river, and thy righteousness as the waves of the sea” (verse 18) is a picture of steady, unfailing flow. Refining in the “furnace” (verse 10) is a metaphor from smelting, where heat burns away what is impure. The call to “go ye forth of Babylon” (verse 20) is a call to leave and to go home."]},
"isa.49":{title:"I will also give thee for a light to the Gentiles",year:"About 700 BC (see note)",
 sum:"The servant is called from the womb, with his mouth like a sharp sword—“Thou art my servant, O Israel, in whom I will be glorified”—He is given “for a light to the Gentiles” that salvation may reach the ends of the earth—Zion says she is forsaken, but the LORD cannot forget her—Kings shall be her nursing fathers—The prey of the mighty shall be delivered.",
 bg:["Nephi reads this chapter as 1 Nephi 21 and then explains it in 1 Nephi 22. Jacob also quotes verses 24–26 in 2 Nephi 6:16–18. The Book of Mormon version begins with an address to those “broken off” and driven out, a line not found in the King James text. Acts 13:47 quotes verse 6 for Paul’s mission to the Gentiles.","The LORD says, “Can a woman forget her sucking child?” and answers that even if she could, he will not forget Zion; “I have graven thee upon the palms of my hands” (verses 15–16). These are pictures of a mother’s love and of a name marked on the hand so that it cannot be lost. Zion is also surprised to find her lost children returning (verses 20–21)."]},
"isa.50":{title:"The Lord GOD hath given me the tongue of the learned",year:"About 700 BC (see note)",
 sum:"Israel was sold for iniquities, yet the LORD’s hand is not shortened—The servant’s “tongue of the learned” speaks a word to the weary—He gives his back to the smiters and hides not his face from shame and spitting—He sets his face “like a flint”—Those who walk in darkness should trust the LORD, not kindle their own sparks.",
 bg:["Jacob quotes this chapter in 2 Nephi 7 and the next in 2 Nephi 8, with small differences from the King James wording. The servant’s words about giving his back to the smiters and not hiding from “shame and spitting” (verse 6) are fulfilled for Latter-day Saints in the suffering of Jesus Christ, as when they spat on him and struck him (Matthew 26:67).","“Have I set my face like a flint” (verse 7) pictures a face as hard as the stone, a firm resolve not to turn back. In verse 11 the people who light their own fire and walk in its sparks are those who depend on their own small lights rather than on the LORD, and so lie down in sorrow. The “bill of divorcement” in verse 1 recalls Deuteronomy 24:1; the LORD says no bill was ever given, so Israel’s separation was her own doing."]},
"isa.51":{title:"Look unto the rock whence ye are hewn",year:"About 700 BC (see note)",
 sum:"“Look unto the rock whence ye are hewn”—Abraham and Sarah, and the LORD comforting Zion, making the wilderness like Eden—“My salvation shall be for ever”—Fear not the reproach of men—“Awake, awake, put on strength, O arm of the LORD”—The ransomed return to Zion with singing—The cup of trembling is taken from Zion.",
 bg:["This chapter is quoted in 2 Nephi 8, which carries on through Isaiah 52:1–2. Jacob read these words so the people could learn about the Lord’s covenants with Israel (see 2 Nephi 9:1–2). Verses 1–2 point Israel back to Abraham, “the rock,” and Sarah, “the hole of the pit,” as the source of the people, while verse 3 promises that the Lord will make Zion’s waste places “like Eden.”","When the prophet cries, “Art thou not it that hath cut Rahab, and wounded the dragon?” (verse 9), Rahab is not the woman of Jericho but a poetic name for a sea monster, used here as a picture of Egypt’s power defeated at the Red Sea. The people ask the arm of the LORD to awake and do the same again."]},
"isa.52":{title:"How beautiful upon the mountains are the feet of him that bringeth good tidings",year:"About 700 BC (see note)",
 sum:"“Awake, awake; put on thy strength, O Zion”—Israel was sold for nought and shall be redeemed without money—“How beautiful upon the mountains are the feet of him that bringeth good tidings”—Watchmen lift up the voice together—“Depart ye... be ye clean, that bear the vessels of the LORD”—My servant shall be “exalted and extolled,” though his visage was marred.",
 bg:["Verses 1–2 are quoted in 2 Nephi 8:24–25. Abinadi’s priests read verses 7–10 in Mosiah 12:21–24, and Abinadi explains them in Mosiah 15:14–18, saying those who publish peace are the prophets and all who teach the gospel. The risen Jesus also quotes this chapter to the Nephites, in 3 Nephi 16:18–20 and 20:32–45. Paul quotes verse 7 in Romans 10:15.","The “how beautiful upon the mountains are the feet” are those of a runner coming over the hills to bring news of victory to a city waiting in fear. The watchmen see him coming and sing. Verses 13–15 begin the song of the servant which goes on through chapter 53, and in 3 Nephi 20:43–45 Jesus quotes them as a promise about himself."]},
"isa.53":{title:"He is despised and rejected of men",year:"About 700 BC (see note)",
 sum:"The servant grows up as a tender plant and a root out of dry ground—“He is despised and rejected of men; a man of sorrows, and acquainted with grief”—“Surely he hath borne our griefs, and carried our sorrows”—“With his stripes we are healed”—Like a lamb dumb before her shearers—He makes his soul an offering for sin and bears the sins of many.",
 bg:["Abinadi quotes the whole chapter in Mosiah 14, with a few small differences, and then explains it in Mosiah 15. In Acts 8:32–35 Philip finds an Ethiopian official reading verses 7–8 and “preached unto him Jesus.” Matthew 8:17 and 1 Peter 2:24 also apply the chapter to Christ.","Several words are stronger in Hebrew than in English. The “griefs” and “sorrows” of verse 4 mean sicknesses and pains, “borne” and “carried” mean lifted up and taken away, and “stripes” are wounds or bruises. All of us are “like sheep” that have “gone astray,” and the shepherd takes our turn of suffering instead (verses 5–6)."]},
"isa.54":{title:"Enlarge the place of thy tent",year:"About 700 BC (see note)",
 sum:"“Sing, O barren”—“Enlarge the place of thy tent”—“Fear not; for thou shalt not be ashamed”—“Thy Maker is thine husband”—“For a small moment have I forsaken thee; but with great mercies will I gather thee”—The waters of Noah—Though mountains depart, his kindness shall not depart—“No weapon that is formed against thee shall prosper.”",
 bg:["The risen Jesus quotes this whole chapter in 3 Nephi 22 and then says, “Great are the words of Isaiah” (3 Nephi 23:1), commanding the people to search them. The Book of Mormon text follows the King James closely, with some differences. Moroni 10:31 echoes verse 2 when he speaks of strengthening the stakes and enlarging the borders of Zion.","The image is of a woman without children given so many that her tent must be made bigger, with the curtains stretched and the stakes driven deep (verse 2). Tent stakes support the whole, which is why the Church uses the word stake. Later the city has foundations of sapphires and gates of carbuncles (verses 11–12), pictures of beauty and strength rather than details of building."]},
"isa.55":{title:"Ho, every one that thirsteth, come ye to the waters",year:"About 700 BC (see note)",
 sum:"“Ho, every one that thirsteth, come ye to the waters”—Buy wine and milk without money and without price—An everlasting covenant, the sure mercies of David—“Seek ye the LORD while he may be found”—“My thoughts are not your thoughts”—Rain and snow water the earth, so the word shall not return void—The mountains sing and the trees clap their hands.",
 bg:["Jacob quotes verses 1–2 in 2 Nephi 9:50–51, with his own call to come and not spend on what has no worth. Revelation 22:17 and Alma 5:34 also echo the invitation. “The sure mercies of David” (verse 3) are quoted in Acts 13:34 as pointing to the resurrection of Jesus Christ. The invitation is to the poor: those with no money are invited to buy for free.","Verses 8–9 say, “For my thoughts are not your thoughts, neither are your ways my ways,” as the heavens are higher than the earth. The reason follows: his word is like rain and snow that come down and water the earth and bring bread, and do not return to heaven until they have done their work (verses 10–11)."]},
"isa.56":{title:"Mine house shall be called an house of prayer for all people",year:"About 700 BC (see note)",
 sum:"“Keep ye judgment, and do justice”—Blessed is the man who keeps the sabbath—Foreigners and eunuchs who hold fast the covenant shall not be shut out—“A name better than of sons and of daughters”—“Mine house shall be called an house of prayer for all people”—Blind watchmen and dumb dogs.",
 bg:["Deuteronomy 23:1–3 kept eunuchs and some foreigners out of the assembly, but Isaiah promises them “a place and a name” in the LORD’s house (verse 5). Jesus quoted verse 7 when he cleansed the temple (Mark 11:17). The Hebrew words for “a place and a name” (yad vashem) are the source of the name of Israel’s Holocaust memorial.","The rulers are called watchmen who are blind, and “dumb dogs” that cannot bark (verse 10): guard dogs that sleep while the flock is in danger. The shepherds care only for their own gain."]},
"isa.57":{title:"Peace, peace to him that is far off, and to him that is near",year:"About 700 BC (see note)",
 sum:"The righteous perish and no one lays it to heart, but they enter into peace—Children of the sorceress and the adulterer practice idolatry under the green trees—“I dwell in the high and holy place, with him also that is of a contrite and humble spirit”—“Peace, peace to him that is far off, and to him that is near”—“The wicked are like the troubled sea.”",
 bg:["Verses 3–13 describe idol worship in the groves and valleys, including offering children (verse 5), which the Old Testament elsewhere condemns (Jeremiah 7:31; 2 Kings 23:10). Verse 15 shows the contrast: the high and holy God who lives in eternity also lives with the humble, a thought echoed in the “broken heart and a contrite spirit” of 2 Nephi 2:7. Paul echoes verse 19 in Ephesians 2:17.","“The wicked are like the troubled sea, when it cannot rest, whose waters cast up mire and dirt” (verse 20). The sea in storm keeps churning up mud; it can never be calm. The verse is close to Isaiah 48:22, which says “There is no peace, saith my God, to the wicked.”"]},
"isa.58":{title:"Is not this the fast that I have chosen?",year:"About 700 BC (see note)",
 sum:"People fast and seek the LORD but quarrel and oppress their workers—“Is not this the fast that I have chosen?”—To loose the bands of wickedness, deal bread to the hungry and clothe the naked—“Then shall thy light break forth as the morning”—The repairer of the breach—“Call the sabbath a delight.”",
 bg:["The chapter asks what real fasting looks like. Members of the Church fast on the first Sunday of the month and give a fast offering to help people in need, in the spirit of verses 6–7. The promise that the LORD will say “Here I am” to those who call is given to those who feed the hungry and welcome the poor into their houses.","Light that breaks forth “as the morning” and a life like “like a watered garden, and like a spring of water, whose waters fail not” (verses 8, 11) are pictures of health and blessing. “Call the sabbath a delight” (verse 13) comes from the Hebrew word oneg, meaning delight or pleasure."]},
"isa.59":{title:"Your iniquities have separated between you and your God",year:"About 700 BC (see note)",
 sum:"The LORD’s hand is not shortened, but sins separate Israel from God—Hands are defiled with blood, feet run to evil, and judgment stands afar off—“He saw that there was no man”—He puts on righteousness as a breastplate and the helmet of salvation—The Redeemer shall come to Zion—The covenant of his Spirit and words.",
 bg:["The LORD is pictured as a warrior who puts on a breastplate of righteousness and a helmet of salvation (verse 17); Paul uses the same picture in Ephesians 6:14–17. Verse 20 is quoted in Romans 11:26–27.","Wicked people “hatch cockatrice’ eggs, and weave the spider’s web” (verse 5). “Cockatrice” is the King James word for a poisonous snake. The web cannot become clothing, which means their works are useless and their plans are deadly."]},
"isa.60":{title:"Arise, shine; for thy light is come",year:"About 700 BC (see note)",
 sum:"“Arise, shine; for thy light is come”—Gentiles and kings come to the light, and sons and daughters return from afar—Camels and ships of Tarshish bring gold and frankincense—Strangers build the walls and the gates are open continually—“The LORD shall be unto thee an everlasting light”—“I the LORD will hasten it in his time.”",
 bg:["The wealth that flows to Zion includes camels from Midian and Ephah and gold and incense from Sheba (verse 6); Matthew 2:11 mentions wise men bringing gold and frankincense, which many link to this verse. Tarshish was a distant trading place whose location is uncertain, perhaps in Spain. Revelation 21:23–25 echoes verses 11 and 19–20, with open gates and no need of the sun.","The prophet says the gates will be called Praise and the walls Salvation (verse 18), and that the sun and moon will no longer be needed because the LORD will be the light. “A little one shall become a thousand” (verse 22) promises growth beyond measure, and “I the LORD will hasten it in his time” has a parallel in Doctrine and Covenants 88:73."]},
"isa.61":{title:"The Spirit of the Lord GOD is upon me",year:"About 700 BC (see note)",
 sum:"“The Spirit of the Lord GOD is upon me”—Anointed to preach good tidings to the meek, bind up the brokenhearted and proclaim liberty to the captives—“The acceptable year of the LORD”—“Beauty for ashes, the oil of joy for mourning”—The ruins are rebuilt and strangers feed the flocks—“He hath clothed me with the garments of salvation.”",
 bg:["Jesus read verses 1–2 in the synagogue at Nazareth (Luke 4:18–19), stopped before “the day of vengeance of our God,” and said, “This day is this scripture fulfilled in your ears” (Luke 4:21). “Anointed” is the Hebrew mashiach, the root of the title Messiah. “The acceptable year of the LORD” recalls the jubilee, when slaves were freed and land returned (Leviticus 25:10).","“Beauty for ashes” is a wordplay in Hebrew: the word for a headdress or garland takes the place of the word for the ashes of mourning. In verse 3 the people are called “trees of righteousness” that the LORD planted, and verse 10 compares being clothed in salvation to a bridegroom and bride dressing for a wedding."]},
"isa.62":{title:"Thou shalt be called Hephzi-bah, and thy land Beulah",year:"About 700 BC (see note)",
 sum:"“For Zion’s sake will I not hold my peace”—Her righteousness goes forth as brightness—She will be a crown of glory and have a new name—“Thou shalt be called Hephzi-bah, and thy land Beulah”—Watchmen on the walls give the LORD no rest—“Go through the gates; prepare ye the way of the people”—“Behold, thy salvation cometh.”",
 bg:["The verse explains the names: Hephzibah means “my delight is in her,” and Beulah means “married” (verse 4). They replace the names Forsaken and Desolate. Hephzibah was also the name of King Hezekiah’s wife (2 Kings 21:1), the mother of Manasseh, though the chapter does not connect the two.","The relationship of the LORD and Zion is pictured as a marriage: “as the bridegroom rejoiceth over the bride, so shall thy God rejoice over thee” (verse 5). The call to “lift up a standard for the people” (verse 10) means to raise a banner, a signal to gather. Verse 11 says her salvation comes with a reward and a work before him."]},
"isa.63":{title:"Wherefore art thou red in thine apparel?",year:"About 700 BC (see note)",
 sum:"One comes from Edom, from Bozrah, glorious in red apparel—“I have trodden the winepress alone”—The LORD remembers his lovingkindnesses to Israel—“In all their affliction he was afflicted, and the angel of his presence saved them”—They rebelled and vexed his holy Spirit—The prayer, “Look down from heaven.”",
 bg:["Bozrah was a leading city of Edom, and its name sounds like Hebrew words for “sheepfold” or “enclosure,” and also for “grape harvest,” though scholars are not certain. Edom means “red” (see Genesis 25:30), which adds to the picture of the red garment. Scholars discuss whether Edom here stands for a literal enemy or for all of the Lord’s foes.","Doctrine and Covenants 133:46–51 uses verses 1–3 for the Savior’s coming in the last days, where he will be “red in thine apparel,” treading the winepress alone. In the winepress picture, people trampled grapes and the juice stained their clothing; here the stain is the cost of judgment. Verse 9, “in all their affliction he was afflicted,” has a parallel in Doctrine and Covenants 133:53."]},
"isa.64":{title:"Oh that thou wouldest rend the heavens, that thou wouldest come down",year:"About 700 BC (see note)",
 sum:"“Oh that thou wouldest rend the heavens, that thou wouldest come down”—Nobody has seen another God who works for those who wait for him—“All our righteousnesses are as filthy rags”—“We are the clay, and thou our potter”—Zion is a wilderness and the holy and beautiful house is burned with fire.",
 bg:["Verses 10–11 speak of cities as a wilderness and the temple burned, as Jerusalem was in 586 BC. Scholars who date these chapters to the exile read it as a prayer from that time, while others read Isaiah as speaking ahead from the viewpoint of the exiles. Hebrew Bibles number the verses one off from the English, so 64:1 is 63:19 there.","Paul quotes verse 4, “Eye hath not seen,” in 1 Corinthians 2:9. The “filthy rags” of verse 6 are a Hebrew phrase that means polluted or soiled garments, a picture of how even our good deeds fall short without God’s help. People who fade like leaves and are carried off by the wind of their sins (verse 6) pray to the potter who shaped them."]},
"isa.65":{title:"Behold, I create new heavens and a new earth",year:"About 700 BC (see note)",
 sum:"The LORD was found of those who did not ask; to a rebellious people he spread his hands all day—They sacrifice in gardens and eat swine’s flesh—His servants shall eat and drink while the rebels hunger—“Behold, I create new heavens and a new earth”—“The wolf and the lamb shall feed together”—“Before they call, I will answer.”",
 bg:["The new heavens and new earth (verse 17; also 66:22) are taken up in 2 Peter 3:13, Revelation 21:1 and Ether 13:9, which speaks of “a new heaven and a new earth.” The Articles of Faith say that “the earth will be renewed and receive its paradisiacal glory” (Articles of Faith 1:10). In verses 11–12 the worship of “that troop” and “that number” refers to Gad (fortune) and Meni (destiny), gods of luck whom the Lord rejects.","The city’s joy has no weeping; people build houses and live in them, plant vineyards and eat the fruit, and a child who dies at a hundred is considered young (verses 20–22), a picture of long, peaceful life. “The wolf and the lamb shall feed together” (verse 25) repeats the peaceful kingdom of 11:6–9."]},
"isa.66":{title:"The heaven is my throne, and the earth is my footstool",year:"About 700 BC (see note)",
 sum:"“The heaven is my throne, and the earth is my footstool”—The LORD looks to the poor and contrite who tremble at his word—Hypocritical worship is rebuked—Zion bears children in one day—“As one whom his mother comforteth, so will I comfort you”—The LORD comes with fire and gathers all nations—New heavens and new earth—“Their worm shall not die.”",
 bg:["Stephen quotes verses 1–2 as he speaks before his death (Acts 7:49–50). Jesus uses the closing words of verse 24, “their worm dieth not, and the fire is not quenched,” in Mark 9:48. The chapter also repeats the new heavens and new earth of 65:17 (see verse 22).","Zion is pictured as a woman who gives birth in one day, before her labor even begins (verses 7–9), and then as a mother who comforts her child and nurses her people at her breast (verses 11–13). Because verse 24 ends on a hard image, Jewish readers have the custom of repeating verse 23, about all flesh worshiping before the LORD, so the reading ends on a hopeful note."]},
"jer.1":{title:"Before I formed thee in the belly I knew thee",year:"About 627–609 BC",
 sum:"Jeremiah of Anathoth is called in Josiah’s thirteenth year—“Before I formed thee in the belly I knew thee… and I ordained thee a prophet unto the nations”—Jeremiah answers, “I cannot speak: for I am a child,” and the Lord touches his mouth—He sees an almond rod and a seething pot facing from the north—The Lord promises, “They shall fight against thee; but they shall not prevail against thee.”",
 bg:["Jeremiah’s name is often explained as “the LORD exalts” or “the LORD loosens,” though scholars are not certain which. His home, Anathoth, was a priestly town in Benjamin just north of Jerusalem, where the priest Abiathar was sent into exile (1 Kings 2:26). A chosen-before-birth calling is also taught in Abraham 3:23, which speaks of noble and great ones chosen before they were born.","The vision of the almond branch is a Hebrew wordplay: the almond is shaqed and the Lord says he will “hasten” or “watch” (shoqed) over his word (1:11–12). In the Book of Mormon, Lehi’s day is the same period: 1 Nephi 1:4 says many prophets were warning Jerusalem in Zedekiah’s first year, 1 Nephi 5:13 says Jeremiah’s prophecies were on the brass plates, and 1 Nephi 7:14 names Jeremiah as a prophet the people of Jerusalem had cast out."]},
"jer.2":{title:"They have forsaken me the fountain of living waters",year:"About 627–609 BC",
 sum:"The Lord remembers Israel’s youthful devotion in the wilderness—Fathers and priests went far from him and walked after vanity—Two evils: “they have forsaken me the fountain of living waters, and hewed them out cisterns, broken cisterns”—Israel is a degenerate vine who says, “I have loved strangers”—Judah trusts in Egypt and Assyria and will be ashamed.",
 bg:["A cistern was a pit cut into rock and plastered to hold rainwater. In a land with long dry summers they were common, but plaster cracked and the water leaked out, which makes the picture of “broken cisterns” easy to understand. A spring, by contrast, keeps flowing.","Jesus used the same image when he offered the woman at the well “living water” (John 4:10–14). Jeremiah often pictures Israel’s covenant as a marriage and a courtship in the wilderness (2:2), a theme also found in Hosea."]},
"jer.3":{title:"Return, thou backsliding Israel",year:"About 627–609 BC",
 sum:"Israel has played the harlot, yet the Lord says, “return again to me”—In Josiah’s day treacherous Judah is worse than backsliding Israel—“Return, thou backsliding Israel… I will not cause mine anger to fall upon you: for I am merciful”—The Lord promises shepherds after his own heart and a gathering to Zion—“Return, ye backsliding children, and I will heal your backslidings.”",
 bg:["The Hebrew verb shuv, “turn” or “return,” rings through this chapter and the next. The same word means both to turn back to God and to come home, so repentance in Jeremiah is a homecoming, not just regret. “Backsliding” here means turning away, and the northern kingdom of Israel had already been taken by Assyria about a century earlier.","Verses 16–17 say the ark of the covenant will no longer be remembered or missed, because Jerusalem itself will be called “the throne of the LORD.” Scholars read this as a promise that God’s presence will no longer depend on one sacred object; the text does not explain further."]},
"jer.4":{title:"Break up your fallow ground",year:"About 627–609 BC",
 sum:"If Israel returns, the nations will bless themselves in the Lord—“Break up your fallow ground, and sow not among thorns. Circumcise yourselves to the LORD”—A destroyer comes from the north, and the trumpet sounds through the land—Jeremiah mourns, “My bowels, my bowels! I am pained at my very heart”—He sees the earth “without form, and void,” yet the Lord says, “yet will I not make a full end.”",
 bg:["Fallow ground is hard, unplowed soil, and breaking it up is a picture of softening a stubborn heart. “Circumcise… the foreskins of your heart” (4:4) echoes Deuteronomy 10:16: the outward sign of the covenant was meant to be an inward change.","In 4:23 the words “without form, and void” are the same Hebrew words (tohu wabohu) as in Genesis 1:2. Jeremiah is saying that invasion would undo creation itself. The enemy is said to come “from the north” because armies from Babylon and Assyria usually reached Judah by marching around the desert and then south through Syria."]},
"jer.5":{title:"If ye can find a man",year:"About 627–609 BC",
 sum:"The Lord says to search Jerusalem for one person who does justice, “and I will pardon it”—The people swear falsely and have made their faces harder than a rock—Even the great have broken the yoke, so a lion, a wolf and a leopard will come—“A wonderful and horrible thing is committed in the land”—The prophets prophesy falsely and the priests rule by their means, “and my people love to have it so.”",
 bg:["The Lord’s offer to pardon a whole city for one honest person recalls Abraham’s bargaining over Sodom (Genesis 18:22–33). Here the search fails, which shows how deep the problem was.","Verse 31 says the prophets prophesy falsely, the priests rule by their own authority, and “my people love to have it so.” Jeremiah’s message was not popular, and he was in conflict with other prophets and priests throughout his ministry."]},
"jer.6":{title:"Peace, peace; when there is no peace",year:"About 627–609 BC",
 sum:"The children of Benjamin are told to flee as the trumpet sounds—The enemy builds siege mounds against a city “wholly oppression in the midst of her”—Prophets and priests “have healed also the hurt of the daughter of my people slightly, saying, Peace, peace”—“Ask for the old paths, where is the good way,” but they say, “We will not walk therein”—Judah is called refined silver that is rejected because the refining fails.",
 bg:["Tekoa and Beth-haccerem were towns south of Jerusalem where warning trumpets and fire signals could be passed along. Fire signals appear again in the Lachish Letters, ink-on-pottery messages written by a Judean officer just before Babylon’s final attack, who says he is watching for the signals of Lachish. Besiegers building an earth ramp against a wall (6:6) is also well documented; a large ramp still stands at Lachish from Assyria’s siege in 701 BC.","Verse 14 pictures a doctor who only bandages a wound and says all is well. The “old paths” (6:16) means the ancient ways of the covenant. Verse 16 goes on to promise “rest for your souls,” words Jesus echoes in Matthew 11:29."]},
"jer.7":{title:"The temple of the LORD, The temple of the LORD",year:"About 609–597 BC",
 sum:"Jeremiah stands in the temple gate: “Amend your ways and your doings”—“Trust ye not in lying words, saying, The temple of the LORD, The temple of the LORD”—Is this house “a den of robbers”? Remember what God did to Shiloh—Judah makes cakes for the queen of heaven and burns children in Tophet—The valley of Hinnom will become the valley of slaughter.",
 bg:["Jeremiah says the temple is not a lucky charm. People who steal, lie and worship Baal cannot be safe just because they stand in God’s house. Jesus quoted 7:11 (“a den of robbers”) when he cleansed the temple (Matthew 21:13). Shiloh was where the tabernacle stood in the days of Eli before the Philistines took the ark (1 Samuel 4). Jeremiah says that place was left in ruins, so Jerusalem is not exempt.","Verse 18 mentions cakes baked for “the queen of heaven,” a goddess whose exact identity scholars debate (often linked to Ishtar or similar Near Eastern figures). Tophet (7:31) was a place in the valley of Hinnom linked to child sacrifice, which God says never came into his mind. Chapter 26 describes the response to this same sermon."]},
"jer.8":{title:"Is there no balm in Gilead",year:"About 609–597 BC",
 sum:"The bones of the dead will be spread out under the sun and moon they loved—The people hold fast deceit, and even the stork knows her appointed times, “but my people know not the judgment of the LORD”—Prophets heal slightly, crying “Peace, peace”—“The harvest is past, the summer is ended, and we are not saved”—“Is there no balm in Gilead; is there no physician there?”",
 bg:["Gilead, east of the Jordan, was known for a fragrant resin used as medicine and perfume. Traders carrying balm from Gilead to Egypt are mentioned as far back as Genesis 37:25. Jeremiah’s question (8:22) asks why, with healing so close by, “is not the health of the daughter of my people recovered?”","Jeremiah is deeply pained by the message he must give. Verse 21 says, “For the hurt of the daughter of my people am I hurt; I am black; astonishment hath taken hold on me.” Many readers have heard the spiritual answer in the Savior, the Great Physician, though Jeremiah himself does not say so here."]},
"jer.9":{title:"Oh that my head were waters, and mine eyes a fountain of tears",year:"About 609–597 BC",
 sum:"Jeremiah wishes for a fountain of tears to weep for the slain of his people—They bend their tongues like a bow for lies, and every neighbor walks with slanders—Jerusalem will become heaps and the cities of Judah desolate—“Let not the wise man glory in his wisdom… but let him that glorieth glory in this, that he understandeth and knoweth me”—Israel, though circumcised in the flesh, is uncircumcised in heart.",
 bg:["Jeremiah is sometimes called “the weeping prophet,” and this chapter is the reason (9:1). The book of Lamentations is traditionally ascribed to him, though the book itself does not name its author.","The Lord says true boasting is in knowing him and in his “lovingkindness, judgment, and righteousness” (9:23–24). Paul draws on this when he writes, “He that glorieth, let him glory in the Lord” (1 Corinthians 1:31)."]},
"jer.10":{title:"Learn not the way of the heathen",year:"About 609–597 BC",
 sum:"Israel is told not to fear the signs of heaven as the nations do—An idol is carved from a tree and fastened with nails, “but speak not”—“The LORD is the true God, he is the living God, and an everlasting king”—“O LORD, I know that the way of man is not in himself”—Jeremiah asks to be corrected “but with judgment,” and calls for God’s anger on the nations.",
 bg:["Verse 11 is written in Aramaic, not Hebrew: “The gods that have not made the heavens and the earth, even they shall perish.” Scholars see it as a short saying people could use in reply to foreign neighbors who spoke Aramaic.","The chapter mocks idols as scarecrows that cannot walk, speak, or do good or harm (10:5). It contrasts them with the God who “hath made the earth by his power” (10:12). Tarshish, where the silver came from (10:9), was a distant trading port, probably in the western Mediterranean, though its exact location is debated."]},
"jer.11":{title:"Hear ye the words of this covenant",year:"About 609–597 BC",
 sum:"The Lord reminds Judah of the covenant given when he brought Israel “from the iron furnace”—Cursed is the man who does not obey it—Judah has broken it and turned to other gods, so the Lord will not hear their cry—Jeremiah is “like a lamb or an ox that is brought to the slaughter” as the men of Anathoth plot against him—The Lord promises to punish them.",
 bg:["“The iron furnace” (11:4) is a picture of Egypt as a place of fierce hardship (see also Deuteronomy 4:20). The covenant here is the one made at Sinai and renewed in Deuteronomy. It may refer to the book of the law found in the temple in Josiah’s reform (2 Kings 22–23), but the chapter does not say so directly.","The plot against Jeremiah came from his own hometown, Anathoth (11:21). It is the first of several places where Jeremiah speaks openly to the Lord about his pain, passages scholars often call his “confessions” (11:18–23; 12:1–6; 15:10–21; 17:14–18; 18:18–23; 20:7–18)."]},
"jer.12":{title:"Wherefore doth the way of the wicked prosper",year:"About 609–597 BC",
 sum:"Jeremiah asks, “Wherefore doth the way of the wicked prosper? wherefore are all they happy that deal very treacherously?”—The Lord answers that if running with footmen wearies him, he cannot contend with horses—Even his own family has dealt treacherously with him—The Lord’s heritage is laid waste, yet he promises to return and have compassion—Nations that turn to him will be built up, and those that will not obey will be plucked up.",
 bg:["This is one of the oldest questions in the Bible, also asked in Job 21, Psalm 73 and Habakkuk 1:2–4. God does not answer with an explanation but with a challenge: if the present trouble is hard, harder days are coming, and Jeremiah must be ready.","“The swelling of Jordan” (12:5) means the thick brush and lions along the Jordan’s banks, which flooded in spring. Verses 14–17 say that even Israel’s enemies can be restored, which shows God’s care goes beyond Israel. Jeremiah is among prophets who show the Lord’s patience and willingness to forgive."]},
"jer.13":{title:"The girdle was marred, it was profitable for nothing",year:"About 609–597 BC",
 sum:"Jeremiah buys a linen girdle, hides it by the Euphrates in a hole of the rock, and finds it ruined—So the Lord will mar the pride of Judah—Every bottle will be filled with wine, and the people will be dashed against one another—“Can the Ethiopian change his skin, or the leopard his spots?”—Jerusalem will be shamed for forgetting the Lord.",
 bg:["A linen girdle was a waistcloth worn next to the skin. The Lord says he meant Israel to cling to him in the same way, “that they might be unto me for a people, and for a name, and for a praise, and for a glory” (13:11). The Hebrew name Perath normally means the Euphrates, a very long trip from Jerusalem. Some scholars suggest it means Parah, a spring near Anathoth, and others think it is a vision. The text does not settle this.","Verse 18 speaks to “the king and to the queen,” usually understood as King Jehoiachin and his mother Nehushta, who were taken to Babylon in 597 BC (2 Kings 24:8–15). “Ethiopian” translates Cush, the land south of Egypt. The saying of the leopard’s spots (13:23) has become a proverb for the difficulty of change."]},
"jer.14":{title:"The prophets prophesy lies in my name",year:"About 609–597 BC",
 sum:"A great drought brings mourning to Judah, and the cry of Jerusalem goes up—Jeremiah confesses sin and pleads, “Leave us not”—The Lord tells him not to pray for this people—The prophets say there will be no sword or famine, but the Lord says “the prophets prophesy lies in my name”—Jeremiah pleads again, and asks, “Are there any among the vanities of the Gentiles that can cause rain?”",
 bg:["Drought was a serious test of the covenant. Deuteronomy 28:23–24 had said that disobedience would shut the sky like brass, and the farmers, nobles and animals in this chapter all suffer together. The land’s dryness was seen as a spiritual warning.","False prophets were a major problem in Jeremiah’s day. These men gave comfort, but the Lord says he did not send them (14:14–15). The chapter shows that the test of a prophet is not whether the message is welcome. It is whether the message is true."]},
"jer.15":{title:"Thy words were found, and I did eat them",year:"About 609–597 BC",
 sum:"Even if Moses and Samuel stood before him, the Lord’s mind could not be toward this people—Four kinds of destroyers are appointed, because of Manasseh’s sins—Jeremiah laments, “Woe is me, my mother, that thou hast borne me a man of strife”—“Thy words were found, and I did eat them; and thy word was unto me the joy and rejoicing of mine heart”—The Lord promises to make him “a fenced brasen wall” and to deliver him.",
 bg:["Moses and Samuel are named because both were known for pleading with God for the people (Exodus 32; 1 Samuel 7:9; 12:23). That God says even they could not save Judah shows how serious things were. Verse 4 points back to Manasseh, whose long and idolatrous reign (2 Kings 21) is blamed as the reason for the coming judgment.","Jeremiah’s picture of eating God’s words (15:16) means taking them in so fully that they become part of you. Ezekiel 3:1–3 and Revelation 10:9–10 describe similar experiences. The Lord’s reply to Jeremiah’s complaint is stern: “If thou return, then will I bring thee again, and thou shalt stand before me” (15:19)."]},
"jer.16":{title:"I will send for many fishers",year:"About 609–597 BC",
 sum:"Jeremiah is told not to take a wife or have children, and not to enter houses of mourning or feasting—The people are asked why the Lord has pronounced this evil, and he points to their fathers’ idolatry—Israel will be brought back from the land of the north, “and I will bring them again into their land”—“I will send for many fishers… and after will I send for many hunters”—The Gentiles shall come from the ends of the earth and say, “Surely our fathers have inherited lies.”",
 bg:["Jeremiah’s unmarried life was a living warning that ordinary family life in Judah was about to end. It was a heavy thing to ask in a society where family was central. His life was part of his message, as with other prophets who acted out God’s word.","Verses 14–15 say that someday people will speak less of the exodus from Egypt and more of the gathering from “the land of the north,” and the same promise appears again in 23:7–8. Latter-day Saints see this gathering as part of the latter-day gathering of Israel. The chapter does not itself explain who the fishers and hunters are, and readers differ on how to understand them."]},
"jer.17":{title:"The heart is deceitful above all things",year:"About 609–597 BC",
 sum:"Judah’s sin is written “with a pen of iron, and with the point of a diamond”—“Cursed be the man that trusteth in man”; “blessed is the man that trusteth in the LORD”—“The heart is deceitful above all things, and desperately wicked”—Jeremiah prays, “Heal me, O LORD, and I shall be healed”—Judah must keep the sabbath day holy or the gates of Jerusalem will burn.",
 bg:["The blessed man is “as a tree planted by the waters” whose leaf stays green in a year of drought (17:7–8), a picture much like Psalm 1. The Book of Mormon echoes the warning against trusting in people: Nephi says, “Cursed is he that putteth his trust in man or maketh flesh his arm” (2 Nephi 4:34).","A “pen of iron” with a diamond point means sin is cut into the heart as if into stone, so it cannot easily be erased. The sabbath was a sign of the covenant, and Jeremiah singles out carrying burdens through the city gates on that day (17:21–22). The chapter ends with a pledge that if Judah keeps it, Jerusalem will last forever."]},
"jer.18":{title:"As the clay is in the potter’s hand, so are ye in mine hand",year:"About 609–597 BC",
 sum:"At the potter’s house Jeremiah sees a marred vessel remade—The Lord can do with Israel as the potter with clay—If a nation turns from evil, the Lord will repent of the evil he planned; if it does evil, he will withhold the good—The people say, “There is no hope”—They plot against Jeremiah, and he prays against them.",
 bg:["The potter’s wheel was a pair of stones, with the lower one turned by foot. Jeremiah sees that when a pot is spoiled, the potter does not throw away the clay but makes something new. The Lord’s warnings in 18:7–10 show that prophecies of judgment or blessing depend on how people respond. A well-known example is Nineveh in Jonah 3:10. Paul also draws on the image in Romans 9:20–21.","Isaiah’s potter and clay image (Isaiah 29:16) appears in the Book of Mormon in 2 Nephi 27:27, where turning things upside down is treated as if the potter were thought no better than the clay. The chapter ends with a conspiracy against Jeremiah, one of the “confessions” (18:18–23)."]},
"jer.19":{title:"Then shalt thou break the bottle",year:"About 609–597 BC",
 sum:"Jeremiah takes a potter’s earthen bottle and elders of the people to the valley of Hinnom—He declares that the place will be filled with slaughter because Judah burned incense to other gods and offered their children to Baal—He breaks the bottle: “Even so will I break this people and this city”—Tophet will be defiled, and the city will be besieged—He returns and repeats the message in the court of the temple.",
 bg:["The valley of Hinnom lies south of Jerusalem. Its Hebrew name Ge-Hinnom became Gehenna in later Jewish and New Testament writings, a name for a place of final judgment. Tophet (19:6) was a site in the valley connected with child sacrifice, which the Lord says he never commanded (19:5).","In 1979 archaeologists found two small silver scrolls at Ketef Hinnom, in that valley, from about this period. They contain a version of the priestly blessing in Numbers 6:24–26 and are among the oldest known fragments of biblical text. Smashing the clay flask in front of witnesses was a strong visual warning, since a broken fired pot cannot be mended."]},
"jer.20":{title:"His word was in mine heart as a burning fire",year:"About 609–597 BC",
 sum:"The priest Pashur beats Jeremiah and puts him in the stocks—Jeremiah renames him Magor-missabib (meaning terror on every side) and says Babylon will take Judah—Jeremiah complains, “O LORD, thou hast deceived me… I am in derision daily”—“His word was in mine heart as a burning fire shut up in my bones”—He sings of the Lord as a mighty terrible one, then curses the day he was born.",
 bg:["Pashur’s name is probably of Egyptian origin, and its meaning is debated. Jeremiah gives him a new name, Magor-missabib, “terror on every side,” because he would see his own downfall. This Pashur son of Immer is a different man from Pashur son of Melchiah in chapter 21. The Hebrew phrase behind the name recurs in Jeremiah (for example 6:25 and 20:10).","Verses 7–18 are among the rawest prayers in scripture. Jeremiah says he cannot hold the word in: when he tries to stop speaking, it burns inside him (20:9). He ends in anguish, yet even here he stops to say, “the LORD is with me as a mighty terrible one” (20:11). The Hebrew verb behind “deceived” (20:7) can also mean “enticed” or “persuaded,” and scholars discuss which sense is meant."]},
"jer.21":{title:"I set before you the way of life, and the way of death",year:"About 597–586 BC",
 sum:"King Zedekiah sends Pashur and Zephaniah to ask Jeremiah about Nebuchadrezzar’s attack—Jeremiah answers that the Lord himself fights against the city—The people may choose “the way of life, and the way of death”: those who go out to the Chaldeans will live—The house of David is told to execute judgment in the morning and deliver the spoiled.",
 bg:["Zedekiah’s name is thought to mean “the LORD is my righteousness.” Babylon’s king gave it to him when he made him king in place of his nephew (2 Kings 24:17). He was the last king of Judah, and he wanted a miracle like the one in Hezekiah’s day, but Jeremiah gave a different answer. The Pashur here is the son of Melchiah, not the Pashur of chapter 20.","The two ways in 21:8 echo Deuteronomy 30:19, “I have set before you life and death… therefore choose life.” The Lachish Letters, written on pottery fragments during this same war, show Judean officers worried about falling morale and failing fire signals as the Babylonians closed in. Lachish Letter 4 says its writer can no longer see the signals of Azekah."]},
"jer.22":{title:"Execute ye judgment and righteousness",year:"About 609–597 BC",
 sum:"The king of Judah is told to do justice, deliver the spoiled, and not shed innocent blood—Shallum, who was taken away, will not return—Woe to Jehoiakim, who builds his house “by unrighteousness” and will be buried “with the burial of an ass”—Coniah is called a despised broken idol and cast out to a land he knew not—“Write ye this man childless.”",
 bg:["These kings were all sons or grandsons of Josiah. Shallum is another name for Jehoahaz (1 Chronicles 3:15), who was taken to Egypt after three months (2 Kings 23:31–34). Jehoiakim built a lavish palace with unpaid labor (22:13), while his father Josiah, as Jeremiah recalls, “do judgment and justice” and defended the poor (22:15–16). Coniah, also called Jeconiah or Jehoiachin, was exiled in 597 BC.","Babylonian ration tablets found in Babylon list oil and grain given to “Yaukin, king of the land of Yahud,” and his sons, from about 592 BC, which matches 2 Kings 25:27–30. Jeremiah’s harsh word about Coniah being “childless” (22:30) means none of his sons would rule on David’s throne; the Bible itself lists his sons elsewhere (1 Chronicles 3:17)."]},
"jer.23":{title:"I will raise unto David a righteous Branch",year:"About 627–586 BC",
 sum:"Woe to the shepherds who scatter the flock—The Lord will gather the remnant and raise up a righteous Branch of David, “THE LORD OUR RIGHTEOUSNESS”—Israel will be brought out of the north country—The Lord condemns prophets who speak “a vision of their own heart,” steal his words, and dream lies—“Is not my word like as a fire?”—The “burden of the LORD” becomes a burden of their own.",
 bg:["The King who will reign is named “THE LORD OUR RIGHTEOUSNESS” (Hebrew Yahweh Tsidkenu, 23:6), which is likely a deliberate play on the name of Zedekiah, “the LORD is my righteousness.” The last king of Judah bore the name but did not live up to it. A true righteous Branch of David is the hope; Latter-day Saints and other Christians see this fulfilled in Jesus Christ.","The Hebrew word massa (23:33–38) means both “oracle” and “burden.” The people asked Jeremiah, “What is the burden of the LORD?”, and the Lord answers, “Ye are the burden.” The chapter gives plain tests of false prophets: they speak peace without repentance, they do not stand in the Lord’s council (23:18, 22), and they live badly (23:14)."]},
"jer.24":{title:"Two baskets of figs",year:"About 597–586 BC",
 sum:"After Jeconiah is carried to Babylon, the Lord shows Jeremiah two baskets of figs before the temple—The good figs are the exiles: “I will set mine eyes upon them for good, and I will bring them again to this land”—He will give them “an heart to know me”—The bad figs are Zedekiah, his princes and those who stayed or went to Egypt—They will be a reproach among all nations.",
 bg:["This vision comes after the first deportation in 597 BC, when King Jehoiachin, officials and skilled craftsmen were taken to Babylon (2 Kings 24:10–16). People left in Jerusalem likely thought they were the lucky ones who had been spared. Jeremiah says the opposite: the future of God’s people lies with the exiles.","Figs that ripen first were treasured, and bad figs were so bitter or spoiled they “could not be eaten” (24:2). The Lord says the exiles will return “with their whole heart” (24:7), and Jeremiah’s later letter to them (chapter 29) urges them to seek the welfare of the city where they live. Ezekiel was among this group of exiles (Ezekiel 1:1–3)."]},
"jer.25":{title:"These nations shall serve the king of Babylon seventy years",year:"About 609–597 BC",
 sum:"In the fourth year of Jehoiakim, Jeremiah recalls twenty-three years of warning that were not heeded—The Lord will send Nebuchadrezzar as his servant, and the land will serve Babylon “seventy years”—Then Babylon itself will be punished—All the nations must drink the cup of the Lord’s fury, and Sheshach will drink after them—The Lord roars from on high, and the shepherds howl.",
 bg:["The fourth year of Jehoiakim (about 605 BC) was also the year Babylon defeated Egypt at Carchemish and Nebuchadrezzar became king. The seventy years (25:11–12; 29:10) are usually counted from about 605 BC to the fall of Babylon in 539 BC, or to the rebuilding of the temple in 516 BC. Daniel studied this prophecy (Daniel 9:2), and 2 Chronicles 36:21 mentions it. Scholars note that seventy may be a round number for a lifetime, and the text does not say which counting is meant.","“Sheshach” (25:26) is a cipher called Atbash, where the first letter of the Hebrew alphabet is swapped for the last, the second for the next to last, and so on. Run on the letters of Babel (Babylon) it gives Sheshach, so Jeremiah named Babylon without naming it. This is a well-documented Hebrew wordplay. In the same verse Babylon is said to drink last, after it had carried out its work."]},
"jer.26":{title:"Thou shalt surely die",year:"About 609–597 BC",
 sum:"Early in Jehoiakim’s reign the Lord tells Jeremiah to stand in the temple court and “diminish not a word”—He warns that the house will become like Shiloh—Priests and prophets seize him: “Thou shalt surely die”—Jeremiah says, “as for me, behold, I am in your hand”—The princes and elders defend him, citing Micah, and Ahikam keeps him from being put to death.",
 bg:["This chapter gives the setting for the temple sermon of chapter 7. The elders quote Micah of Moresheth, who had told Hezekiah’s Jerusalem that “Zion shall be plowed like a field” (26:18; compare Micah 3:12), and that king’s repentance spared the city. The prophet Uriah son of Shemaiah, who spoke the same words, was brought back from Egypt and killed by Jehoiakim (26:20–23), which shows how close Jeremiah came to the same fate.","Lehi’s experience in the Book of Mormon was similar: when he told the people of Jerusalem that the city would be destroyed, they mocked him and “sought his life” (1 Nephi 1:19–20). Ahikam son of Shaphan, a royal official who had served under Josiah, protected Jeremiah (26:24). The chapter does not say what happened to Jeremiah afterward in detail, only that he was not given into the hands of those who sought his death."]},
"jer.27":{title:"Bring your necks under the yoke of the king of Babylon",year:"About 597–586 BC",
 sum:"Jeremiah makes bonds and yokes and sends them to the kings of Edom, Moab, Ammon, Tyre and Zidon—The LORD has given these lands to “Nebuchadnezzar the king of Babylon, my servant”—Zedekiah and the people are told to “serve him and his people, and live”—Prophets who say Babylon will not prevail are lying—Even the vessels still in the temple will be carried to Babylon, until the LORD brings them back.",
 bg:["Verse 1 says “Jehoiakim,” but the message is addressed to Zedekiah (verses 3, 12, 20), and some Hebrew manuscripts and ancient versions read “Zedekiah” in verse 1. Most scholars think the name in verse 1 is an early copying slip. The yoke was an everyday farm object, so Jeremiah wore it as a living picture: submit to Babylon and survive.","Scholars have long noted that the Greek (Septuagint) text of Jeremiah is about one-eighth shorter than the Hebrew and arranges some sections in a different order. Fragments of Jeremiah among the Dead Sea Scrolls include a Hebrew form close to the shorter Greek text, so there were at least two early versions of the book. Our English Bible follows the longer Hebrew, so some verse details differ between versions. Lehi’s day began in this period (1 Nephi 1:4 places his call “in the commencement of the first year of the reign of Zedekiah”)."]},
"jer.28":{title:"Thou shalt make for them yokes of iron",year:"About 597–586 BC",
 sum:"Hananiah of Gibeon promises that within two years the LORD will break Babylon’s yoke and bring back the vessels and Jeconiah—Jeremiah answers, “Amen: the LORD do so,” but reminds him that true prophets have often foretold war—Hananiah breaks the wooden yoke from Jeremiah’s neck—“Thou hast broken the yokes of wood; but thou shalt make for them yokes of iron”—Hananiah dies that year, in the seventh month.",
 bg:["Hananiah (Hebrew “the LORD is gracious”) told people what they longed to hear. Jeremiah did not claim the right to settle the matter by argument. He first waited, and he pointed to the test in Deuteronomy 18:20–22, that a prophet’s word is proved when it comes true. Hananiah died, as Jeremiah said, in the seventh month of the same year.","This is a clear contrast between a comforting message and a true one. Jeremiah himself longed for peace (verse 6), yet he could not speak what the LORD had not said. The wooden yoke Hananiah broke was replaced by iron, a sign that resisting Babylon would only make the burden heavier."]},
"jer.29":{title:"Seek the peace of the city whither I have caused you to be carried away captives",year:"About 597–586 BC",
 sum:"Jeremiah writes to the exiles in Babylon—Build houses, plant gardens, marry, and “seek the peace of the city,” for in its peace you shall have peace—After seventy years the LORD will visit them: “I know the thoughts that I think toward you… thoughts of peace, and not of evil”—Ye shall seek me and find me when ye search with all your heart—False prophets Ahab and Zedekiah, and Shemaiah, are condemned.",
 bg:["The letter went to people taken in 597 BC along with King Jehoiachin, many of them hoping to go home soon. Jeremiah told them to settle down and pray for Babylon. The seventy years in verse 10 are also mentioned in Jeremiah 25:11–12; scholars read the number either as roughly literal or as a round figure for a long lifetime of exile. The promise in verse 11 was first spoken to people in exile, which helps explain its meaning: hope after hardship, not freedom from hardship.","In verse 11 “thoughts of peace” is the Hebrew shalom, meaning wholeness and well-being, and “an expected end” means a future and a hope. The word “peace” in verse 7 is also shalom, so the exiles are asked to seek the shalom of their captors’ city. The Hebrew of “seek me, and find me” echoes Deuteronomy 4:29."]},
"jer.30":{title:"Write thee all the words that I have spoken unto thee in a book",year:"About 597–586 BC",
 sum:"Jeremiah is told to write the LORD’s words in a book—A terrible day, “the time of Jacob’s trouble; but he shall be saved out of it”—The yoke will be broken from Israel’s neck, and they shall serve the LORD and David their king—The LORD will restore health and heal the wounds of an outcast people—“Ye shall be my people, and I will be your God.”",
 bg:["Chapters 30–33 are often called the Book of Consolation, because after so much warning they speak mostly of healing and return. They are among the most hopeful sections of Jeremiah, though they are still set in the hard years before and during Jerusalem’s fall. “Jacob” here means the whole people of God, and the name Jacob has long been connected with the Hebrew words for “heel” and “supplant,” a fitting name for a people who had struggled.","Verse 22 gives the covenant formula: “Ye shall be my people, and I will be your God.” It echoes Exodus 6:7 and Leviticus 26:12 and returns in 31:33 and 32:38, tying the promise of restoration to the covenant first made at Sinai."]},
"jer.31":{title:"I will make a new covenant with the house of Israel",year:"About 597–586 BC",
 sum:"The LORD will be the God of all the families of Israel—“Rahel weeping for her children refused to be comforted”—The LORD promises, “There is hope in thine end”—“I will make a new covenant”: “I will put my law in their inward parts, and write it in their hearts”—All shall know the LORD, and he will forgive and “remember their sin no more.”",
 bg:["Verse 15 pictures Rachel, the ancestress of Ephraim and Benjamin, weeping as the exiles pass by Ramah, where Nebuzaradan gathered captives (40:1). “Rachel” here is a poetic image of a mother grieving for her children, not a claim that she was alive. Matthew 2:18 quotes the verse in connection with the sorrow of Bethlehem, and the hope that follows in Jeremiah 31:16–17 is part of the thought.","Verses 31–34 are the only place in the Old Testament that uses the words “new covenant.” The newness is not a different God or a lost law but a law written “in their hearts” and forgiveness that reaches everyone. Hebrews 8:8–12 quotes it in full and Hebrews 10:16–17 quotes part, and at the Last Supper Jesus spoke of “the new testament in my blood” (Luke 22:20), since “testament” translates the same word as “covenant.”"]},
"jer.32":{title:"Houses and fields and vineyards shall be possessed again in this land",year:"About 597–586 BC",
 sum:"Jeremiah is shut up in the court of the prison while Babylon besieges the city—He buys his cousin Hanameel’s field at Anathoth for seventeen shekels of silver and seals the deed before witnesses—The deeds are put in an earthen vessel to last many days—Jeremiah prays, “Is there any thing too hard for me?”—The city will fall, but “fields shall be bought in this land,” and an everlasting covenant is promised.",
 bg:["It was the tenth year of Zedekiah, about 587 BC, when buying farmland made no sense because the Babylonians were already on the land. Anathoth was Jeremiah’s hometown, a few miles north of Jerusalem and probably already in enemy hands. Hanameel’s name means “God has been gracious.” Buying the field was a sign: ordinary life would one day return. The sealed deed in a clay jar was a normal way to preserve documents, and ancient jars of documents have been found in Judean deserts.","Jeremiah’s honest prayer in verses 16–25 and the LORD’s answer, “Behold, I am the LORD, the God of all flesh: is there any thing too hard for me?” (verse 27), show a man who obeyed even while he did not understand. Baruch, the scribe who held the deed, appears here for the first time (verses 12–16); his name means “blessed.”"]},
"jer.33":{title:"Call unto me, and I will answer thee",year:"About 597–586 BC",
 sum:"The LORD invites Jeremiah to “Call unto me, and I will answer thee, and shew thee great and mighty things”—Though the city is full of dead, he will heal it and bring back the captivity—Sounds of joy and gladness will return—“The Branch of righteousness” will grow up unto David—The covenant with David and the Levites will stand as sure as day and night.",
 bg:["Jeremiah was still in prison when these words came, so the promise of “great and mighty things” is a hopeful word for a man in a difficult place. The “Branch of righteousness” (verses 14–16) is a title for a future king from David’s line; Isaiah 11:1 and Zechariah 3:8 and 6:12 use similar language, and Christians see it fulfilled in Jesus Christ.","Verses 14–26 are missing in the shorter Greek (Septuagint) text of Jeremiah, which scholars take as one sign that the book was shaped and expanded over time. The English Bible follows the longer Hebrew text, so these verses are in it."]},
"jer.34":{title:"Proclaiming liberty, every one to his brother",year:"About 597–586 BC",
 sum:"While Babylon fights against Jerusalem, Lachish and Azekah, Zedekiah is told he will be taken to Babylon but will die in peace—Zedekiah and the people covenant to free their Hebrew servants—They take them back again—The LORD says they have not proclaimed liberty, so he will proclaim liberty for the sword, pestilence, and famine—Those who broke the covenant will be treated like the calf cut in two.",
 bg:["The law of Moses said Hebrew servants must go free in the seventh year (Exodus 21:2; Deuteronomy 15:12). Zedekiah’s court made a covenant to obey it, perhaps because slaves would be useful as defenders during the siege. When the Egyptian army briefly drew the Babylonians away (37:5), the owners took their servants back. Jeremiah calls this a betrayal of both the neighbor and the covenant.","The ceremony of cutting a calf in two and passing between the pieces (verses 18–19) is like the covenant rite in Genesis 15:9–18. It means, “May this happen to me if I break my word.” Lachish and Azekah (verse 7) were the last fortified towns holding out; ancient letters found at Lachish describe watching for signals from Azekah as the Babylonians advanced."]},
"jer.35":{title:"Unto this day they drink none, but obey their father’s commandment",year:"About 597–586 BC",
 sum:"In the days of Jehoiakim, Jeremiah brings the Rechabites into the temple and sets wine before them—They refuse, because their father Jonadab commanded them never to drink wine, build houses, or plant vineyards—They have kept his command for generations and live in tents—Judah has not hearkened to the LORD as the Rechabites obeyed Jonadab—The Rechabites are promised that Jonadab will “not want a man to stand before me for ever.”",
 bg:["The Rechabites were a family of nomadic herders who followed the rules of their ancestor Jonadab (Jehonadab), who had supported Jehu in 2 Kings 10:15–23. He was probably named for the idea “the LORD is generous.” They came into Jerusalem only because Nebuchadnezzar’s army had driven them in from the countryside (verse 11). Their stern way of life was a custom, not a commandment given to all Israel.","Jeremiah does not praise their rules but their faithfulness. If a family could keep a human father’s command for about two hundred and fifty years, why could Judah not keep the word of the LORD? This chapter is set earlier, in the days of Jehoiakim, and was placed here to contrast with the broken covenant in chapter 34."]},
"jer.36":{title:"Take thee a roll of a book",year:"About 605 BC",
 sum:"In Jehoiakim’s fourth year the LORD tells Jeremiah to write all his words in a roll—Baruch writes them down and reads them in the temple on a fast day—The princes hear it and warn Baruch and Jeremiah to hide—Jehudi reads the roll to the king, who cuts it with a penknife and burns it in the fire—Jeremiah dictates another roll with “many like words” added.",
 bg:["The fourth year of Jehoiakim is about 605 BC, the year Babylon won at Carchemish (46:2). Baruch (“blessed”) was a professional scribe, the son of Neriah, a name often explained as “the LORD is my lamp.” Clay seal impressions with names like “Gemaryahu son of Shaphan” (verse 10) have been found in Jerusalem’s ruins. One reading “Berekhyahu son of Neriyahu the scribe” is disputed because it did not come from a controlled dig.","The king burned the roll in the winter fire (verse 22), but the word of the LORD could not be destroyed; Jeremiah simply wrote it again with more added. Verse 29 notes the dismay of Jehoiakim; his reign ended with his death and Nebuchadnezzar’s first siege of Jerusalem. The scene shows how scripture, once spoken, can be preserved by faithful scribes."]},
"jer.37":{title:"Is there any word from the LORD?",year:"About 597–586 BC",
 sum:"Zedekiah reigns, but neither he nor the people hearken to the LORD’s words—He asks Jeremiah to pray for them—Pharaoh’s army draws the Chaldeans away, but they will return and burn the city—Jeremiah is arrested at the gate as a deserter and beaten and put in a dungeon—Zedekiah secretly asks, “Is there any word from the LORD?” and moves him to the court of the prison, with daily bread.",
 bg:["Zedekiah was a son of Josiah who was put on the throne by Nebuchadnezzar in place of his nephew Coniah (Jehoiachin). His birth name was Mattaniah; Nebuchadnezzar changed it to Zedekiah (2 Kings 24:17), a name meaning “righteousness of the LORD,” which makes the king’s weakness more striking. He respected Jeremiah in secret but feared his own officials.","The Book of Mormon agrees that Jeremiah suffered this way: Nephi records that the people of Jerusalem “have rejected the prophets, and Jeremiah have they cast into prison” (1 Nephi 7:14). Since Lehi and Jeremiah lived in the same years, this is one of the places the two records meet."]},
"jer.38":{title:"So Jeremiah sunk in the mire",year:"About 597–586 BC",
 sum:"Four princes tell Zedekiah that Jeremiah weakens the soldiers’ hands and should die—They lower him by cords into a muddy cistern and “Jeremiah sunk in the mire”—Ebed-melech the Ethiopian speaks to the king and pulls him out with old rags and cords—In secret Zedekiah again asks the LORD’s will; Jeremiah says, “Obey, I beseech thee,” and surrender—Zedekiah fears the Jews who have gone over to the Chaldeans and sends Jeremiah back to the court of the prison.",
 bg:["The “dungeon” was a cistern, a pit dug to collect rainwater, and in a dry season it held only mud. Ebed-melech (Hebrew “servant of the king”) was a Cushite, a man from the upper Nile region, and an official in the palace. A foreigner and servant spoke up when the leaders of Judah would not, and the LORD promises to protect him in 39:15–18.","Zedekiah’s weakness is plain. He gives in to his officials (verse 5), then secretly seeks Jeremiah, then makes him promise to tell no one. Jeremiah’s message never changed: surrender, and the city and the king’s life would be spared. Zedekiah feared that those who had already defected would mock him, and so he did not obey."]},
"jer.39":{title:"I will surely deliver thee, and thou shalt not fall by the sword",year:"About 597–586 BC",
 sum:"Babylon besieges Jerusalem and in Zedekiah’s eleventh year breaks through the wall—Zedekiah flees but is caught near Jericho; his sons are slain before him, his eyes put out, and he is bound in chains to Babylon—The city and temple are burned and the people carried away, though some of the poor are left with vineyards and fields—Nebuzar-adan treats Jeremiah kindly—The LORD promises Ebed-melech, “I will surely deliver thee.”",
 bg:["The dates in verses 1–2 match 2 Kings 25:1–4. Historians usually place the fall of Jerusalem in the summer of 586 BC, though 587 BC is also argued. The last thing Zedekiah saw was the death of his sons, a famous grim detail. Nebuzar-adan, the Babylonian captain of the guard, has an Akkadian name; its exact meaning is uncertain. Jeremiah was not freed because Babylon favored him but because he had told his people to submit.","The Book of Mormon tells of a son of Zedekiah named Mulek, whose people reached the Americas “at the time that Zedekiah, king of Judah, was carried away captive into Babylon” (Omni 1:15). The Bible here says only that the king’s sons were killed (verse 6), and the scriptures do not explain more than this."]},
"jer.40":{title:"Behold, I loose thee this day from the chains which were upon thine hand",year:"About 586–582 BC",
 sum:"Nebuzar-adan frees Jeremiah at Ramah and gives him a choice to go to Babylon or stay—Jeremiah goes to Gedaliah at Mizpah, whom Babylon has made governor—Scattered Jews gather and are told to dwell in the land and serve Babylon—Johanan warns Gedaliah that Ishmael is sent by the king of Ammon to kill him—Gedaliah will not believe it.",
 bg:["Gedaliah (Hebrew “the LORD is great”) was the grandson of Shaphan the scribe, and his father Ahikam had protected Jeremiah from death earlier (26:24). Babylon chose him as governor of a small, poor province centered at Mizpah, probably modern Tell en-Nasbeh, north of Jerusalem, because Jerusalem lay in ruins. He was a loyal man and a hopeful one, but also trusting enough to ignore a warning.","Johanan, son of Kareah (his name means “the LORD is gracious”), is the officer who warns Gedaliah and later leads the people (41:11; 42:1). Ishmael (“God hears”) is of the royal family and is helped by the king of Ammon. The Babylonian captain’s words in verses 2–3, giving credit to the LORD for the disaster, are striking coming from a foreigner."]},
"jer.41":{title:"Smote Gedaliah the son of Ahikam the son of Shaphan with the sword",year:"About 586–582 BC",
 sum:"In the seventh month Ishmael and ten men eat with Gedaliah at Mizpah and then kill him and the Chaldean soldiers with him—He murders seventy men from Shechem, Shiloh and Samaria who came with offerings, sparing ten who have hidden stores—Ishmael takes the people captive toward Ammon—Johanan rescues them near Gibeon, but Ishmael escapes—The people head toward Egypt, afraid of Babylon’s revenge.",
 bg:["Ishmael (“God hears”), son of Nethaniah (“gift of the LORD”), was of the royal line and may have hoped to regain power for David’s house. The chapter does not say when the killing took place, and scholars differ; some connect it to the further deportation of 582 BC in Jeremiah 52:30. Eating together was a sign of friendship and hospitality, which makes the murder at the table even worse.","The seventy pilgrims from the north came to mourn at the ruined temple site with offerings, showing that sacred worship still went on after the fall. Ishmael killed most of them, and the people feared Babylon’s revenge, so they stopped at Chimham near Bethlehem on the road to Egypt (verse 17). With Gedaliah dead, the last bit of local rule was gone."]},
"jer.42":{title:"Pray for us unto the LORD thy God",year:"About 586–582 BC",
 sum:"The captains and Johanan ask Jeremiah to pray for guidance and promise to obey—After ten days the answer comes: “If ye will still abide in this land, then will I build you”—Do not fear the king of Babylon, for the LORD will save you from him—But if you go to Egypt, the sword and famine you fear will follow you there—Jeremiah says they “dissembled” in their hearts.",
 bg:["The people asked Jeremiah for the LORD’s word and said they would obey whether it was “good or evil” (verse 6). Jeremiah waited ten days and then spoke, and verse 20 says they had already decided to go to Egypt before asking. Jeremiah 42:10 says “I repent me of the evil that I have done unto you,” which describes God’s compassion in human language, not regret in the way people feel it.","Egypt had once been Israel’s place of slavery, so a return there was a sign of lost faith, and Deuteronomy 17:16 warns against going back to Egypt. Jeremiah’s message was plain: the same Babylon they feared would no longer be their threat if they trusted the LORD, but fleeing would bring the troubles they hoped to escape."]},
"jer.43":{title:"Thou speakest falsely: the LORD our God hath not sent thee",year:"About 586–582 BC",
 sum:"Azariah, Johanan, and the proud men call Jeremiah a liar and refuse to stay in Judah—They take Jeremiah, Baruch, and the whole remnant to Egypt, and come to Tahpanhes—Jeremiah hides great stones in the brickwork at Pharaoh’s house—He says Nebuchadnezzar, “my servant,” will set his throne there and spread his royal pavilion over those stones—Babylon will break the images of Egypt’s gods.",
 bg:["Tahpanhes (Greek Daphnae, modern Tell Defenneh) was a border fortress in the eastern Nile Delta, and Judean refugees likely hoped for military protection there. Jeremiah’s symbolic act of hiding stones in the brickwork before “Pharaoh’s house” shows that the Babylonian king would be seated where they had expected safety. A fragmentary Babylonian text suggests Nebuchadnezzar did campaign against Egypt around 568 BC, though it does not give details.","Beth-shemesh (verse 13) means “house of the sun” and refers to the Egyptian city of Heliopolis, famous for its sun temple and obelisks. “Images” here are probably those pillars. Baruch’s name appears in verse 6; though he was once warned not to seek great things (chapter 45), he stayed with Jeremiah to the end."]},
"jer.44":{title:"We will certainly do whatsoever thing goeth forth out of our own mouth",year:"About 586–582 BC",
 sum:"Jeremiah speaks to all the Jews in Egypt, reminding them the LORD destroyed Jerusalem for idolatry—He asks them to stop their abominations—They answer that they will keep burning incense to the queen of heaven because things went better when they did—Jeremiah says their idolatry brought the ruin—A sign is given: Pharaoh-hophra will be given into the hand of his enemies, as Zedekiah was.",
 bg:["The Jews in Egypt lived in Migdol and Tahpanhes in the north, Noph (Memphis) in the center, and Pathros (Upper Egypt) in the south. The “queen of heaven” was a goddess honored with cakes and drink offerings; scholars debate who she was and often point to Ishtar or a similar goddess. The women’s argument is a common human mistake: they remembered good times under idol worship and forgot the reason Jerusalem fell.","Pharaoh-hophra (Apries) was killed in a revolt against him about 570 BC, which matches verse 30. Later documents from Elephantine, an island in southern Egypt, show a Jewish community there with its own temple in the fifth century BC, proof that Jews did continue in Egypt as Jeremiah describes."]},
"jer.45":{title:"Seekest thou great things for thyself? seek them not",year:"About 586 BC or later",
 sum:"In Jehoiakim’s fourth year Jeremiah speaks to Baruch, who was writing his words in a book—Baruch has said, “Woe is me now! for the LORD hath added grief to my sorrow”—The LORD will break down what he built—“Seekest thou great things for thyself? seek them not”—But Baruch’s life will be given to him “for a prey in all places whither thou goest.”",
 bg:["This short message is dated to 605 BC, the same year as the scroll in chapter 36, and was placed here as a closing word about Baruch (“blessed”). Baruch’s sorrow shows the cost of writing the words of a prophet whom the powerful hate. To “give his life for a prey” is a soldier’s phrase, meaning to escape with life as the only plunder.","The chapter is a simple pastoral word. Baruch was a trained scribe who could have sought position at court, but he was told to give up such hopes in a time of judgment. He stayed with Jeremiah and went with him to Egypt (43:6). Some early traditions credit him with later writings, but the Bible does not say this."]},
"jer.46":{title:"In vain shalt thou use many medicines",year:"About 586 BC or later",
 sum:"The word of the LORD against Egypt’s army, which Nebuchadnezzar defeated at Carchemish by the river Euphrates—Egypt’s mighty men flee and stumble; “in vain shalt thou use many medicines”—Egypt will be given to Nebuchadnezzar, and Amon of No will be punished—“Pharaoh king of Egypt is but a noise”—“Fear thou not, O Jacob my servant,” for the LORD will save him and correct him in measure.",
 bg:["The battle of Carchemish in 605 BC changed the balance of power in the ancient Near East, and Babylon replaced Egypt in Syria and Palestine. Jeremiah says even Egypt’s healing remedies from Gilead (verse 11; compare the “balm in Gilead” in 8:22) cannot help. “No” is the Egyptian city of Thebes, where the god Amon was worshiped.","In the Greek (Septuagint) text of Jeremiah the oracles against the nations are placed in a different order and in a different spot, in the middle of what is chapter 25 in our Bible. Scholars note this difference. Verses 27–28 are nearly the same as 30:10–11 and end the Egyptian oracle with comfort for Israel."]},
"jer.47":{title:"Waters rise up out of the north",year:"About 586 BC or later",
 sum:"The word of the LORD against the Philistines before Pharaoh smote Gaza—Waters rise out of the north like an overflowing flood; fathers will not look back to their children—The LORD will spoil the Philistines, the remnant of the country of Caphtor—Baldness comes upon Gaza, and Ashkelon is cut off—“O thou sword of the LORD, how long will it be ere thou be quiet?”",
 bg:["The Philistines lived on the coastal plain in cities such as Gaza and Ashkelon and were long-time enemies of Israel. They came, according to verse 4 and Amos 9:7, from Caphtor, usually identified with Crete or the Aegean region. The “waters” from the north are an army, which the Bible often describes as a flood (compare Isaiah 8:7–8). Babylonian records say Nebuchadnezzar destroyed Ashkelon in 604 BC.","Shaving the head (“baldness”) was a sign of mourning. The chapter does not say which attack on Gaza is meant, and scholars disagree on whether it was by Pharaoh Necho or another. Jeremiah’s own cry at the end, asking the “sword of the LORD” to rest, shows how heavy the work of a prophet of war was."]},
"jer.48":{title:"Moab hath been at ease from his youth",year:"About 586 BC or later",
 sum:"Woe to Nebo, Kiriathaim, and Heshbon, for Moab is spoiled—“Cursed be he that doeth the work of the LORD deceitfully”—Moab has trusted in his works and treasures and has been “at ease from his youth,” like wine left on its lees—Chemosh will go into captivity with his priests—The LORD weeps for Moab, yet at last promises to “bring again the captivity of Moab.”",
 bg:["Moab lay east of the Dead Sea, and its people were relatives of Israel through Lot (Genesis 19:37). Its national god was Chemosh, who is named on the Mesha Stele, a ninth-century BC Moabite inscription that also mentions Nebo. Verses 29–38 are close to Isaiah 15–16, and scholars discuss whether Jeremiah borrowed from Isaiah or both used an older source.","The picture in verse 11 is of wine left sitting undisturbed on its lees, or sediment. Wine poured from jar to jar was cleared and improved, but wine left alone kept its old taste. Moab had never been shaken, so it never changed. Even in judgment the LORD says, “I will wail for Moab” (verse 31), and the chapter ends with a promise of restoration."]},
"jer.49":{title:"Leave thy fatherless children, I will preserve them alive",year:"About 586 BC or later",
 sum:"Against Ammon, whose god Malcham has taken Gad—Against Edom: Teman’s wisdom is gone, and Esau is made bare—“Leave thy fatherless children, I will preserve them alive; and let thy widows trust in me”—Edom dwells in the clefts of the rock, but the LORD will bring her down—Judgments on Damascus, Kedar and Hazor, and Elam, with a promise to bring back Elam’s captivity.",
 bg:["Several nations in this chapter are Israel’s relatives or neighbors: Ammon (descendants of Lot), Edom (descendants of Esau), and the nomads of Kedar. Edom’s rocky mountain strongholds, such as those near Bozrah and Teman, seemed safe, which explains verse 16, “O thou that dwellest in the clefts of the rock.” Verses 9–16 are very close to Obadiah 1:1–9, and scholars note that one prophet likely used the other or both drew on a common source.","Verse 11 is a gentle line in a chapter of judgment, a promise that orphans and widows can trust in the LORD. Elam lay east of Babylon in what is now southwestern Iran. The prophecy against Elam is dated to Zedekiah’s reign (verse 34), a reminder that Jeremiah’s warnings reached far beyond Judah."]},
"jer.50":{title:"Their Redeemer is strong; the LORD of hosts is his name",year:"About 586 BC or later",
 sum:"Babylon is taken; Bel is confounded and Merodach is broken—Out of the north a nation comes against her—Israel and Judah weep and seek Zion, saying, “Let us join ourselves to the LORD in a perpetual covenant that shall not be forgotten”—“Remove out of the midst of Babylon”—Babylon’s pride is brought low: “Their Redeemer is strong.”",
 bg:["Bel (“lord”) and Merodach (Marduk) were the chief gods of Babylon, so their downfall means the downfall of Babylon’s power. The “nation out of the north” (verse 3) was a Persian-led alliance that took Babylon in 539 BC under Cyrus, though the chapter speaks in broad poetic terms. “Redeemer” in verse 34 is the Hebrew go’el, the family protector who buys back a relative in need.","The call to leave Babylon (verse 8; also 51:6, 45) became a picture of leaving sin and worldliness. A modern revelation says, “Go ye out from among the nations, even from Babylon, from the midst of wickedness, which is spiritual Babylon” (Doctrine and Covenants 133:14). The covenant in verse 5 is called “perpetual,” and this echoes the “everlasting covenant” of 32:40."]},
"jer.51":{title:"Babylon hath been a golden cup in the LORD’s hand",year:"About 586 BC or later",
 sum:"The LORD stirs up a destroying wind against Babylon—“Babylon hath been a golden cup in the LORD’s hand, that made all the earth drunken”—Israel is not forsaken; “my people, go ye out of the midst of her”—The LORD is the maker of all things, unlike idols—“How is Sheshach taken!”—Seraiah reads the book in Babylon, binds a stone to it, and casts it into the Euphrates: “Thus shall Babylon sink.”",
 bg:["“Sheshach” (verse 41) is a cipher called Atbash, in which the first letter of the Hebrew alphabet is swapped with the last, the second with the second-to-last, and so on. Applied to the Hebrew letters of Babel, it gives Sheshach. “Leb-kamai” in 51:1 is the same cipher for Chaldea (Kasdim). Scholars suggest the code may be a literary play, or a way of speaking carefully about a powerful enemy.","The action in verses 59–64 was carried out in Zedekiah’s fourth year by Seraiah, brother of Baruch and called a “quiet prince” (the Hebrew phrase is uncertain; some translate it as the official in charge of travel arrangements). Seraiah’s name is often explained as “the LORD has prevailed,” though scholars differ. “Thus far are the words of Jeremiah” closes the book’s prophecies. Revelation 18:4 (“Come out of her, my people”) and 18:21 (a stone cast into the sea) echo this chapter."]},
"jer.52":{title:"And spake kindly unto him",year:"About 586 BC (parallels 2 Kings 24–25)",
 sum:"Zedekiah reigns eleven years and rebels against Babylon—Jerusalem is besieged, the wall is broken, and Zedekiah is captured, blinded, and taken to Babylon—The temple is burned and its bronze pillars and vessels carried away, and the leaders are executed at Riblah—Three groups of exiles are counted—In the thirty-seventh year, Evil-merodach lifts up Jehoiachin out of prison and sets his seat above the other kings.",
 bg:["This chapter closely matches 2 Kings 24:18–25:30 and was likely added after “Thus far are the words of Jeremiah” (51:64) to show that his warnings came true. Verses 28–30 give exile counts (3,023, 832, and 745 people) that are not found in 2 Kings; scholars have not agreed on how they relate to the larger deportations. Lehi’s own prophecy of Jerusalem’s fall is in 1 Nephi 1:13.","Evil-merodach is the Hebrew form of the Babylonian name Amel-Marduk, meaning “man of Marduk.” He became king in 562 BC and released Jehoiachin in the thirty-seventh year of his exile (about 561 BC). Babylonian ration tablets list oil and grain for “Yaukin, king of Yahud,” confirming that Jehoiachin was fed at Babylon. The book ends on a quiet note of hope for David’s line."]},
"ezek.1":{title:"The heavens were opened, and I saw visions of God",year:"About 593 BC",
 sum:"Ezekiel, a priest among the captives by the river Chebar, sees a great cloud and fire from the north—Four living creatures, each with four faces and four wings, appear with wheels full of eyes—Above them is a firmament and a throne—“This was the appearance of the likeness of the glory of the LORD”—Ezekiel falls upon his face.",
 bg:["Ezekiel (Hebrew Yechezqel, “God strengthens”) was a priest, the son of Buzi, taken to Babylon with King Jehoiachin in 597 BC. The river Chebar was a canal of the Euphrates near the city of Nippur. The “thirtieth year” (verse 1) is not explained in the text; scholars suggest it was his age or a reckoning of years, and verse 2 says it was the fifth year of Jehoiachin’s captivity.","The four faces (man, lion, ox, eagle) and the eyes and wings are symbols. Doctrine and Covenants 77:2–4 explains that the beasts John saw in Revelation 4 represent heavenly beings, and that their eyes stand for light and knowledge and their wings for power to move and act. Ezekiel’s vision of a throne, living creatures and a rainbow of glory is a close parallel to Revelation 4. Lehi, a prophet of the same era (1 Nephi 1:4), also saw God on his throne surrounded by heavenly beings (1 Nephi 1:8)."]},
"ezek.2":{title:"Son of man, stand upon thy feet, and I will speak unto thee",year:"About 593 BC",
 sum:"The Spirit sets Ezekiel on his feet to hear the voice of the Lord—He is sent to “a rebellious nation” that has transgressed to this very day—Whether they hear or refuse, “yet shall know that there hath been a prophet among them”—He is told not to fear them or their words—A hand holds out a scroll written within and without with “lamentations, and mourning, and woe.”",
 bg:["“Son of man” (Hebrew ben adam) is a Hebrew idiom meaning simply “human being,” and God uses it about ninety times to address Ezekiel. It reminds the prophet that he is a mortal who speaks for God, not a god himself. In the New Testament Jesus often uses the same title of himself, and Latter-day Saint scripture also teaches that “Son of Man” is a name of the Savior (Moses 6:57).","Prophets in the ancient world often delivered unwelcome messages. Ezekiel is told that his task is to speak faithfully, whether or not the people listen, which echoes how other prophets were called, such as Isaiah (Isaiah 6:9–10) and Jeremiah (Jeremiah 1:7–8)."]},
"ezek.3":{title:"I have made thee a watchman unto the house of Israel",year:"About 593 BC",
 sum:"Ezekiel eats the scroll and it is “as honey for sweetness”—He is sent to Israel, whose heart is hard, but the Lord makes his forehead “as an adamant”—The Spirit lifts him up, and he sits astonished among the exiles at Tel-abib seven days—He is made a watchman who must warn the wicked and the righteous—He is told to be silent until the Lord opens his mouth.",
 bg:["Eating the scroll means taking God’s word fully inside oneself before speaking it. The same image appears in Revelation 10:9–10, where John’s scroll is sweet in the mouth, and in 1 Nephi 1:11–12, where Lehi reads from a book he is given in a vision. A watchman stood on a city wall and sounded a trumpet when danger came (compare 33:1–7), and he was not blamed if the people ignored the warning.","The Lord says the watchman is responsible for warning others (verses 17–21). In our dispensation the Lord likewise says, “it becometh every man who hath been warned to warn his neighbor” (Doctrine and Covenants 88:81). Tel-abib (“mound of the flood” or “mound of grain”) was an exile settlement near the Chebar canal."]},
"ezek.4":{title:"Take thee a tile, and lay it before thee, and pourtray upon it the city, even Jerusalem",year:"About 593 BC",
 sum:"Ezekiel draws Jerusalem on a clay tile and acts out a siege against it—He lies on his left side 390 days and on his right side 40 days, each day for a year of Israel’s and Judah’s iniquity—He eats scarce, measured bread baked over dung and drinks water by measure—The sign shows the famine to come upon Jerusalem.",
 bg:["Ezekiel’s “sign-acts” turned his own body and daily life into a message, because many exiles did not believe Jerusalem would fall. The tile was a sun-dried clay brick of the kind used in Babylon, where such bricks were also used to draw plans. How the 390 and 40 days relate to history is not explained clearly in the text, and scholars understand them in different ways.","The siege of Jerusalem would indeed bring terrible hunger (compare Lamentations 4:4–10). When Ezekiel protests that cooking over human dung would make him unclean, the Lord allows cow’s dung instead (verses 12–15), a small mercy from a God who is firm but not careless of his prophet."]},
"ezek.5":{title:"I, even I, am against thee, and will execute judgments in the midst of thee",year:"About 593 BC",
 sum:"Ezekiel shaves his head and beard and divides the hair into three parts—One part is burned, one is struck with a sword, and one is scattered to the wind, with a few hairs saved in his skirts—Jerusalem has been set in the midst of the nations but has rebelled more than they—The Lord announces famine, sword and pestilence—Yet he will “accomplish my fury” and then be comforted.",
 bg:["Shaving was a sign of mourning or shame, and for a priest like Ezekiel it was a striking act, since priests were to be careful with their hair (Leviticus 21:5). The three portions picture those who would die in the city by plague and famine, those killed by the sword outside, and those scattered among the nations.","Verse 5 calls Jerusalem the center of the nations. Because the city had been given the law, its failure to keep it brought a greater accountability. Verse 3 notes that a small remnant would be kept safe, a hint of mercy that grows in later chapters."]},
"ezek.6":{title:"Then shall ye know that I am the LORD",year:"About 593 BC",
 sum:"Ezekiel is told to prophesy against the mountains of Israel—Their high places and idol altars will be destroyed, and the slain will lie among the idols—A remnant will survive in other nations and remember the Lord—They will loathe themselves for their evils—The people are told to strike their hands and stamp their feet because of the abominations of Israel.",
 bg:["The “high places” were hilltop shrines where Israelites, like their Canaanite neighbors, offered sacrifices under green trees. Idols here are called gillulim, a Hebrew word that sounds like a word for dung, a deliberate insult. The phrase “ye shall know that I am the LORD” appears about sixty times in the book and is one of its main themes.","The promise that “yet will I leave a remnant” (verse 8) is the first of many in Ezekiel. Even when the message is harsh, the purpose is for scattered people to remember the Lord and turn back to him, an idea echoed in Book of Mormon prophecies about the scattering and gathering of Israel (1 Nephi 22:3–5)."]},
"ezek.7":{title:"An end, the end is come upon the four corners of the land",year:"About 593 BC",
 sum:"The Lord declares that “an end” has come upon the land of Israel—Judgment and abominations will be repaid, and no pity will be shown—Silver and gold will not deliver them in the day of wrath—The sanctuary will be profaned, and rumor will follow rumor—The leaders have no vision, priests no law, and elders no counsel.",
 bg:["This chapter is a short, fast poem with repeated words like “end,” “come,” and “now,” which gives it a drumming sound of approaching disaster. The “four corners of the land” (verse 2) means the whole country, not just the capital.","The people trusted in riches, but verse 19 says their silver and gold would be cast away like something unclean. A theme throughout the book of Ezekiel is that when people trust in wealth or power instead of in God, those things cannot save them (compare Zephaniah 1:18)."]},
"ezek.8":{title:"Seest thou what they do? even the great abominations",year:"About 592 BC",
 sum:"In the sixth year, a figure like fire lifts Ezekiel by a lock of his hair in vision to Jerusalem—He sees the image of jealousy at the north gate—Elders worship creeping things and idols in secret chambers, women weep for Tammuz, and men bow to the sun with their backs to the temple—“The LORD seeth us not.”",
 bg:["The date is about September 592 BC, fourteen months after Ezekiel’s first vision (1:1–2), while the elders of Judah sat before him. Tammuz (Hebrew Tammuz, from the Mesopotamian Dumuzi) was a god of vegetation who was thought to die each summer, and women held ceremonial mourning for him. Even the month of Tammuz in the Hebrew calendar carries his name, a reminder of how much Babylonian culture had influenced Judah.","In the vision, idol worship fills the temple that was supposed to be the dwelling of the Lord. The leaders’ words, “The LORD seeth us not” (verse 12), show their mistaken belief that God had left or could not see. In the next chapters, he does leave."]},
"ezek.9":{title:"Set a mark upon the foreheads of the men that sigh and that cry",year:"About 592 BC",
 sum:"Six men with slaughter weapons come with a man in linen who has a writer’s inkhorn—The man in linen is told to mark those who sigh and cry for the abominations in Jerusalem—The others are to be slain, beginning at the sanctuary—Ezekiel pleads for Israel—The Lord says the iniquity is exceedingly great, and the man reports, “I have done as thou hast commanded me.”",
 bg:["The mark on the forehead (verse 4) is the Hebrew letter taw, which in the old script was shaped like a cross or an X. It was a sign that those who grieved over wickedness would be spared, much like the blood on the doorposts in Exodus 12:7, 13 or the seal on the servants of God in Revelation 7:3.","The idea that the righteous grieve over sin is important in scripture. Those marked are not people who have never failed, but those who “sigh and cry” because of what is happening. Notice also that the judgment starts “at my sanctuary” (verse 6), with the elders in front of the house, a sign that those with the most responsibility are held most accountable."]},
"ezek.10":{title:"The glory of the LORD departed from off the threshold of the house",year:"About 592 BC",
 sum:"The man in linen is told to take coals of fire from between the cherubim and scatter them over the city—The glory of the Lord rises from the cherub to the threshold and fills the court with brightness—The wheels and living creatures of chapter 1 are seen again and identified as cherubim—The glory departs from the threshold and stands over the cherubim, which lift up their wings and go.",
 bg:["Verse 20 says, “This is the living creature that I saw under the God of Israel by the river of Chebar; and I knew that they were the cherubims.” In the temple, golden cherubim stood over the ark of the covenant (Exodus 25:18–22), so the Lord’s throne-chariot in Babylon and the throne in Jerusalem are linked. God is not limited to one place, which is a comfort to the exiles.","The movement of the glory out of the temple, in stages (9:3; 10:4, 18–19; 11:22–23), is one of the most solemn scenes in the Bible. It shows that the temple could not protect a people who had left the covenant. The glory returns in Ezekiel 43:1–5."]},
"ezek.11":{title:"I will put a new spirit within you",year:"About 592 BC",
 sum:"Ezekiel sees twenty-five men at the east gate who give wicked counsel, and Pelatiah dies as he prophesies—The Lord promises those scattered abroad that he will be “a little sanctuary” to them and bring them back—“I will put a new spirit within you … and will give them an heart of flesh”—The glory of the Lord leaves the city and stands on the mountain to the east—The Spirit carries Ezekiel back to the exiles.",
 bg:["The promise of “one heart,” a “new spirit,” and a “heart of flesh” instead of a “stony heart” (verse 19) is about inner change, not just outward obedience. It prepares for 36:26, and echoes Jeremiah 31:33. In the Book of Mormon, King Benjamin’s people describe the same blessing as “a mighty change in us, or in our hearts” (Mosiah 5:2).","The leaders in Jerusalem said, “This city is the caldron, and we be the flesh” (verse 3), meaning they felt safe inside the walls. The Lord turns the saying around: the city will be the pot, but they will be taken out of it. The “mountain which is on the east side of the city” (verse 23) is the Mount of Olives, where the glory pauses before leaving."]},
"ezek.12":{title:"Prepare thee stuff for removing, and remove by day in their sight",year:"About 592 BC",
 sum:"Ezekiel packs an exile’s baggage, and by day and by night digs through the wall and goes out as a sign to “a rebellious house”—The prince in Jerusalem will be taken and will not see the land he is brought to—Ezekiel eats and drinks with trembling as a picture of the land’s fear—The people say the vision is for times far off, but the Lord says no word shall be prolonged.",
 bg:["Ezekiel’s sign-act foretells the fate of King Zedekiah, who in 586 BC tried to escape Jerusalem by night through a gap in the wall, was captured near Jericho, and was blinded and taken to Babylon (2 Kings 25:4–7). The statement that he would not “see” Babylon (verse 13) is explained by his blinding, though Ezekiel gave it years earlier.","The people dismissed prophetic warnings with sayings like “The days are prolonged, and every vision faileth” (verse 22). The Lord answers that the time “is at hand.” This is a familiar pattern: people put off repentance by assuming that the warnings apply to another time (compare 2 Peter 3:3–4)."]},
"ezek.13":{title:"They have seduced my people, saying, Peace; and there was no peace",year:"About 592 BC",
 sum:"The Lord denounces prophets who speak from their own hearts and have “seen nothing”—They are like foxes in the desert and have not gone up into the gaps to build the hedge for Israel—They say “Peace” and daub a wall with untempered mortar—Women who sew pillows for the wrists and make kerchiefs to hunt souls are also rebuked.",
 bg:["“Untempered morter” (verse 10) is a poor wash or whitewash painted over a weak wall to make it look sound. A storm shows the wall is not strong. The image says that false prophets covered the real problem, which was Israel’s sin, with comforting words.","The false prophets (compare Jeremiah 6:14, “They have healed also the hurt … saying, Peace, peace; when there is no peace”) are known by their words, not their titles. True prophets, by contrast, stood “in the gap” (compare 22:30) and warned. The women’s magic charms in verses 17–23 show that some people sold fortunes and cures, which the Lord condemns."]},
"ezek.14":{title:"These men have set up their idols in their heart",year:"About 592 BC",
 sum:"Elders come to Ezekiel to inquire of the Lord, but they have idols in their hearts—The Lord calls them to repent and turn from their idols—Even if Noah, Daniel, and Job were in a land under judgment, “they should deliver but their own souls”—Four severe judgments—famine, wild beasts, sword, and pestilence—are described, yet a remnant will be left to comfort Ezekiel.",
 bg:["Noah, Daniel, and Job (verse 14) are named as righteous men of old. Noah and Job are known from Genesis 6–9 and the book of Job. This “Daniel” is probably not the Daniel of Babylon, who was still young, and many scholars think it refers to a famous righteous figure named Dan’el known from ancient Canaanite stories, though this is not certain. The point is that someone else’s righteousness cannot rescue a person.","The chapter introduces what chapter 18 teaches directly: each person is accountable to God. Idols “in the heart” means that worship can be corrupted even without a statue, which is why the Lord says, “Repent, and turn yourselves from your idols” (verse 6)."]},
"ezek.15":{title:"What is the vine tree more than any tree?",year:"About 592 BC",
 sum:"The Lord asks what makes the wood of a vine better than other wood—It cannot be used to make anything or even to hang a vessel on—When fire has burned both ends and the middle, it is good for nothing—So the inhabitants of Jerusalem will be given to the fire—Their land will be desolate because they have trespassed.",
 bg:["Israel is often pictured as a vine or vineyard (Psalm 80:8–16; Isaiah 5:1–7; Hosea 10:1). Ezekiel adds a twist: a grapevine is valuable only for its fruit, and its wood is thin and nearly useless. A vine that bears no fruit has no other purpose, so its fate is fire.","This short chapter does not say that Israel is worthless, only that a fruitless vine has failed its purpose. Compare Jesus’s teaching in John 15:1–6 about branches that do not abide in the true vine. The same Lord who warns here also promises fruit again later (Ezekiel 17:22–24)."]},
"ezek.16":{title:"I will remember my covenant with thee in the days of thy youth",year:"About 592 BC",
 sum:"Jerusalem is pictured as an abandoned infant that the Lord found, cared for, and made his own in covenant—She grew beautiful but trusted her beauty and turned to others, even sacrificing her children—Her unfaithfulness exceeded that of Samaria and Sodom—Yet the Lord will remember his covenant and “establish unto thee an everlasting covenant.”",
 bg:["This chapter is an extended parable that pictures God’s covenant with Jerusalem as a marriage and Israel’s idolatry and foreign alliances as unfaithfulness. The language is deliberately shocking and should be read as a symbol of broken loyalty, not as a literal story. The prophets used marriage imagery in a similar way (Hosea 1–3; Jeremiah 2–3). Its “Amorite” and “Hittite” origin (verse 3) refers to the city’s Canaanite roots before David conquered it.","The chapter ends with grace. Verses 60–63 promise that the Lord will remember his covenant and make an “everlasting covenant,” so that she will know he has forgiven her. The pattern of judgment followed by restoration is central to Ezekiel’s message and reflects the Lord’s steadfast love."]},
"ezek.17":{title:"A great eagle with great wings, longwinged, full of feathers",year:"About 591 BC",
 sum:"A riddle tells of a great eagle that takes the top of a cedar to a land of traffic and plants a seed of the land as a vine—A second eagle draws the vine’s roots toward it—The riddle is explained: Babylon took Judah’s king, but Zedekiah broke his oath and sought help from Egypt—The Lord will plant “a tender one” on a high mountain, where it will become a goodly cedar.",
 bg:["The first eagle is Nebuchadnezzar of Babylon, who took King Jehoiachin to Babylon in 597 BC and set Zedekiah on the throne under a sworn treaty. The second eagle is Egypt. Zedekiah turned to Egypt for help against Babylon, which Ezekiel treats as breaking an oath made in God’s name (verses 15–19; compare 2 Kings 24:20).","The chapter ends with a promise (verses 22–24): God himself will take a tender twig from the top of the cedar and plant it on a high mountain, where birds will nest in its shade. Many Christians and Latter-day Saints see this as a hope fulfilled in the Messiah and the kingdom of God, though the passage itself does not name him."]},
"ezek.18":{title:"The soul that sinneth, it shall die",year:"About 591 BC",
 sum:"The Lord rejects the proverb, “The fathers have eaten sour grapes, and the children’s teeth are set on edge”—“All souls are mine”—The righteous man lives, his violent son dies, and the grandson who sees and avoids his father’s sins lives—“The son shall not bear the iniquity of the father”—A wicked man who repents shall live—“Make you a new heart and a new spirit.”",
 bg:["The people had a saying that they suffered because of their ancestors’ sins (verse 2; compare Jeremiah 31:29–30), and they used it to excuse themselves. The Lord answers that each person is accountable for their own choices. This is echoed in the Second Article of Faith: “We believe that men will be punished for their own sins, and not for Adam’s transgression.”","Verses 21–23 and 30–32 show that God’s purpose is mercy: “Have I any pleasure at all that the wicked should die? … and not that he should return from his ways, and live?” A person is never stuck in the past, and a righteous person must keep choosing right. The call to “make you a new heart and a new spirit” (verse 31) pairs with God’s promise to give one in 36:26."]},
"ezek.19":{title:"Take thou up a lamentation for the princes of Israel",year:"About 591 BC",
 sum:"Ezekiel sings a lament for the princes of Israel—A lioness rears two young lions, and one is taken in a pit and brought to Egypt—Another is trained, then trapped by the nations and carried to the king of Babylon—The mother is then pictured as a vine, once fruitful by the waters but now plucked up and planted in the wilderness, with no strong rod left to be a scepter.",
 bg:["A “lamentation” (Hebrew qinah) was a funeral song with a limping rhythm, often sung in mourning. The two young lions probably stand for Kings Jehoahaz, taken to Egypt in 609 BC (2 Kings 23:31–34), and Jehoiachin, taken to Babylon in 597 BC (2 Kings 24:8–15), though scholars debate whether the second lion might be Zedekiah.","The “mother” is the royal house of Judah, shown first as a lioness and then as a vine. Here Ezekiel shows that Judah’s kings, who should have been strong, were caught and removed. The last line, that it is “a lamentation, and shall be for a lamentation,” shows how deeply Ezekiel mourned for his people."]},
"ezek.20":{title:"I gave them my sabbaths, to be a sign between me and them",year:"About 591 BC",
 sum:"Elders come to inquire of the Lord, and he reviews Israel’s history of rebellion from Egypt through the wilderness and the promised land—Though he spared them for his name’s sake, they profaned his sabbaths and followed idols—The Lord will gather them out from the nations, purge the rebels, and bring them back—A prophecy against the south field is spoken.",
 bg:["The date is about August 591 BC (verse 1), the seventh year of Jehoiachin’s captivity. This chapter retells Israel’s story, beginning with Egypt, to show that rebellion was not new. Yet the Lord repeatedly says that he held back his anger “for my name’s sake” (verses 9, 14, 22), meaning his reputation for faithfulness.","The sabbath is called “a sign between me and them, that they might know that I am the LORD that sanctify them” (verse 12; compare Exodus 31:13). For Israelites in exile, far from the temple, keeping the sabbath became a key way of staying identified with the covenant. The Lord’s promise to “bring you out from the people” and gather them (verses 34, 41) is a recurring Ezekiel theme."]},
"ezek.21":{title:"I will overturn, overturn, overturn, it",year:"About 589 BC",
 sum:"Ezekiel is told to set his face toward Jerusalem and prophesy that the Lord’s sword will be drawn from its sheath—He is to sigh with a broken heart, as the sword is sharpened and furbished—The king of Babylon stands at the parting of the way and uses divination to choose between Jerusalem and Rabbah of the Ammonites—The prince of Israel is told his day has come—Judgment will last “until he come whose right it is.”",
 bg:["Nebuchadnezzar’s army came to a crossroads, and the king used divination (verse 21): shaking arrows, consulting household idols, and inspecting a liver. Ancient kings often did this before battle. The Lord says that even this will lead the king to Jerusalem.","The words “until he come whose right it is” (verse 27) point to a rightful king. Many Jewish and Christian readers have seen here the hope of a coming Davidic king. The wording is also close to Genesis 49:10, where the scepter does not depart from Judah “until Shiloh come.” Both passages teach that even when the throne is overturned, God’s promise remains."]},
"ezek.22":{title:"I sought for a man among them, that should make up the hedge, and stand in the gap",year:"About 589 BC",
 sum:"The Lord calls Jerusalem “the bloody city” because of her violence, idolatry, and corrupt dealing—Parents are dishonored, strangers are oppressed, and the sabbaths are profaned—Israel is like dross in a furnace—The priests, princes, and prophets are all corrupt—The Lord looks for someone to stand in the gap, “but I found none.”",
 bg:["Verses 1–16 list the city’s sins in many kinds: shedding blood, dishonoring parents, oppressing the stranger, the fatherless, and the widow (compare Exodus 22:21–22), and unjust gain. Israel’s silver has become “dross” (verse 18), which is the useless waste left after refining metal. The furnace is where the Lord intends to melt them.","The image in verse 30 is of a broken wall: someone needed to repair the breach and stand in it to defend the city. Jesus’s role as mediator and intercessor stands against this background, because he is the one who always stands in the gap for us (compare Isaiah 59:16; Hebrews 7:25). The verse also reminds readers that one faithful person can matter."]},
"ezek.23":{title:"Aholah the elder, and Aholibah her sister",year:"About 588 BC",
 sum:"Two sisters, Oholah (Samaria) and Oholibah (Jerusalem), are pictured as belonging to the Lord but turning to Assyria, Egypt, and Babylon—Oholah is handed over to her lovers, the Assyrians—Oholibah is worse and is judged by Babylon—The Lord says they also defiled his sanctuary and sabbaths, and the judgment will teach them to know the Lord GOD.",
 bg:["This chapter is a painful allegory, like chapter 16, that uses the language of unfaithful marriage to describe the northern kingdom of Israel (Samaria) and Judah (Jerusalem) seeking alliances and gods from Assyria, Egypt, and Babylon. It is meant to shock readers into seeing political and spiritual unfaithfulness as a betrayal of covenant love, and it should be read as symbol, not as a description of real women.","The names are a wordplay. Oholah means “her tent,” and Oholibah means “my tent is in her” (verse 4). A tent can mean a sanctuary, so the sisters’ names point to the Lord dwelling in Jerusalem’s temple but not in Samaria’s rival shrines. The names also stress that both belonged to him (“they were mine”) yet were unfaithful."]},
"ezek.24":{title:"Set on a pot, set it on, and also pour water into it",year:"About 588 BC",
 sum:"On the very day the king of Babylon besieges Jerusalem, the Lord gives a parable of a cooking pot full of choice pieces, and then of rusty scum that will not come out—Jerusalem is “the bloody city”—Ezekiel’s wife, “the desire of thine eyes,” dies, and he is told not to mourn publicly—The people will see that he is a sign.",
 bg:["The date in verse 1 is the tenth day of the tenth month of the ninth year, about January 588 BC, when Nebuchadnezzar’s army began its siege of Jerusalem (compare 2 Kings 25:1; Jeremiah 52:4). Ezekiel, far away in Babylon, was told to write down the date, and the news later reached the exiles to confirm that he had spoken truly (33:21).","Ezekiel’s wife is called “the desire of thine eyes” (verse 16), a tender phrase that is not found often in the Bible. He is told not to hold the usual public mourning so that the people will understand that the loss of the temple, “the desire of your eyes” (verse 21), will be too deep for ordinary mourning. Ezekiel’s obedience at personal cost is described in verses 18 and 24."]},
"ezek.25":{title:"Thou saidst, Aha, against my sanctuary",year:"About 586 BC",
 sum:"Ammon gloats over Jerusalem’s fall—Moab and Seir say Judah is “like unto all the heathen”—Edom takes vengeance on Judah—The Philistines act with “a despiteful heart”—God judges each, “and they shall know that I am the LORD.”",
 bg:["This chapter opens a block of messages against foreign nations (chapters 25–32), spoken to people who had just watched Jerusalem fall. Each neighbor is judged for cheering Judah’s ruin or taking advantage of it. The phrase “they shall know that I am the LORD” runs through the whole book of Ezekiel.","Ammon and Moab were Israel’s relatives, traced back to Lot’s two daughters (Genesis 19:36–38), and Edom to Esau. The Hebrew name Ammon is linked there to “son of my people.” The Philistines’ “Cherethims” (verse 16) are usually thought to be Cretans or people from the Aegean, though scholars are not certain."]},
"ezek.26":{title:"I will make thee like the top of a rock",year:"About 586 BC",
 sum:"Tyre rejoices that Jerusalem is broken, since trade will now come to her—The Lord will bring “many nations” against Tyre—Nebuchadnezzar, “a king of kings,” will break her walls—Her dust is scraped away and she becomes a place to spread nets—The isles shake at her fall.",
 bg:["Tyre’s name in Hebrew (Tsor) means “rock,” which fits a city built partly on an island fortress off the Phoenician coast, so the words “like the top of a rock” are a grim play on its name. Babylon’s siege of Tyre is traditionally said to have lasted about thirteen years, though the sources are late and the exact dates are debated.","Verses 4–5 and 12 speak of scraping Tyre’s dust into the sea. In 332 BC Alexander the Great built a causeway out to the island city using rubble from the old mainland town. Some readers see that as a later fulfillment; the chapter itself names Nebuchadnezzar and does not say how every detail would come about."]},
"ezek.27":{title:"O Tyrus, thou hast said, I am of perfect beauty",year:"About 586 BC",
 sum:"Ezekiel is told to lament for Tyre—Tyre is pictured as a beautiful ship built of cedar, fir and oak—Nations from Tarshish to Arabia supply her sailors, goods and wealth—The ship is wrecked “in the midst of the seas”—Merchants and kings mourn her sudden end.",
 bg:["The chapter is a funeral song (a lament) that pictures the trading city as a great ship, and then lists the goods and peoples that filled her markets. Judah and Israel appear in verse 17 selling wheat, honey, oil and balm. Tarshish (verse 12) is often thought to be in southern Spain, but its location is not certain.","Centuries later John’s vision of Babylon’s fall in Revelation 18 borrows this scene: kings, merchants and sailors stand far off and weep because their trade has ended. Ezekiel’s point is that pride in beauty and wealth cannot protect a city."]},
"ezek.28":{title:"Thou hast said, I am a God",year:"About 586 BC",
 sum:"The prince of Tyre says, “I am a God,” yet is “a man, and not God”—He will die “the deaths of them that are slain”—A lament for the king of Tyre, once in Eden and cast out for his iniquity—Judgment on Sidon—Israel will one day dwell safely in her land.",
 bg:["Verses 11–19 call the king of Tyre a perfect “seal,” a “covering cherub,” in “Eden the garden of God,” later cast from “the mountain of God.” Readers differ: many scholars see a poetic taunt of a proud human king using Eden imagery, while many Jewish and Christian writers, and many Latter-day Saints, also see an echo of the fall of Satan, as in Isaiah 14:12–15 (2 Nephi 24:12–15). The text itself does not say which, and it also differs from Genesis 3.","The chapter ends with good news. Verses 24–26 say Israel will no longer have “a pricking brier” among her neighbors and will live safely in the land given to “Jacob my servant.” Sidon, Tyre’s sister city on the coast, is judged in verses 20–23."]},
"ezek.29":{title:"A staff of reed to the house of Israel",year:"About 586–571 BC",
 sum:"The Lord is against Pharaoh, “the great dragon that lieth in the midst of his rivers”—Egypt has been “a staff of reed” that pierces the hand that leans on it—The land will be desolate forty years—Nebuchadnezzar is given Egypt as wages for his labor at Tyre—Israel’s horn will bud.",
 bg:["Verses 1–16 are dated to the tenth year of exile (about 587 BC), when Egypt’s promised help was failing Jerusalem, as the reed image shows. A reed breaks when you lean on it and cuts your hand. Verses 17–21 are the latest dated message in the book, given in the twenty-seventh year (about 571 BC), after Nebuchadnezzar’s long siege of Tyre ended with little loot.","The “forty years” in verses 11–13 are a round number, and scholars do not agree on how they match Egypt’s history. Verse 14 says Egypt will return but become “a base kingdom,” which fits its later place under foreign rulers. The “dragon” (a crocodile-like river monster) was a fitting picture of Pharaoh and the Nile."]},
"ezek.30":{title:"Howl ye, Woe worth the day",year:"About 586 BC",
 sum:"The “day of the LORD” is near for Egypt and her allies—Cities from Migdol to Syene fall by the sword—Pharaoh’s arm is broken and Babylon’s arm strengthened—Noph, Zoan, No, Sin, Pathros and Tahpanhes are judged—Egypt’s idols fail.",
 bg:["“Woe worth the day” is old English for “Woe worth the day!” The “day of the LORD” was often pictured as a day of rescue for Israel, but here it is a day of judgment on a nation. The names are real Egyptian cities: Noph is Memphis, No is Thebes, and Tahpanhes was a border city in the Nile Delta.","Verse 21 is dated to the eleventh year (about 586 BC) and says Pharaoh’s arm has been broken. Jeremiah 37:5–11 describes an Egyptian army that came to help Jerusalem and then withdrew. KJV “Ethiopia,” “Put” and “Lud” in verses 5 and 9 refer to lands south and west of Egypt; “Chub” is not certainly identified."]},
"ezek.31":{title:"The cedars in the garden of God could not hide him",year:"About 586 BC",
 sum:"Pharaoh is asked, “Whom art thou like in thy greatness?”—Assyria was a cedar of Lebanon so fair that the trees of Eden envied it—Pride led to its being cut down and left on the mountains—Pharaoh will go down to the pit with the trees of Eden.",
 bg:["Ezekiel tells Egypt’s pride a story about a cedar. Assyria, once the greatest empire, towered like a tree whose branches sheltered birds and beasts, and then it was felled. If mighty Assyria fell, Pharaoh should not think he is safe. The date in verse 1 is the eleventh year, about 586 BC, only weeks before Jerusalem fell.","Eden is mentioned here (verses 8–9, 16–18) as the garden where the most beautiful trees grew. Notice that it is only a picture: no one in the chapter claims a real tree was in Eden, only that no tree matched the beauty of this one."]},
"ezek.32":{title:"Son of man, wail for the multitude of Egypt",year:"About 585 BC",
 sum:"Another lament for Pharaoh, “like a young lion of the nations”—Babylon’s sword brings him down and darkens the heavens—Egypt’s multitude goes “down into the pit”—In the underworld Assyria, Elam, Meshech, Tubal, Edom and others already lie slain.",
 bg:["The chapter is dated to the twelfth year (about 585 BC), a year after Jerusalem’s fall. The picture of the dead in “the nether parts of the earth” uses the Hebrew idea of Sheol, a shadowy place where the dead wait. Great nations that once terrorized others are shown lying there, so Egypt will not escape the same end.","“The uncircumcised” in verses 19–32 means those outside Israel’s covenant, and fighting men buried without honor was a deep shame in the ancient world. Verses 31–32 close the section of messages against the nations, which began in chapter 25."]},
"ezek.33":{title:"I have set thee a watchman unto the house of Israel",year:"About 585 BC",
 sum:"Ezekiel is again set as a watchman who must sound the alarm—“Turn ye, turn ye from your evil ways; for why will ye die, O house of Israel?”—A righteous man who sins and a wicked man who repents are each judged by their change—A survivor brings the news, “The city is smitten”—The people hear Ezekiel as “a very lovely song” but do not do his words.",
 bg:["Ezekiel’s first call as a watchman (3:16–21) is repeated here, and now the warnings have come true. Verse 21 gives the date, the twelfth year in the tenth month (about 585 BC), when a refugee arrived to say Jerusalem had fallen, and Ezekiel’s speechlessness, which began in 24:27, ended. After that his messages turn from judgment to hope. Verses 14–16 teach that repentance is real: “none of his sins that he hath committed shall be mentioned.” Compare D&C 58:42, “he who has repented of his sins, the same is forgiven, and I, the Lord, remember them no more.”","The watchman’s duty is also taught in D&C 88:81: “it becometh every man who hath been warned to warn his neighbor.” In verses 30–32 the people enjoy Ezekiel the way they would enjoy a singer, but hearing is not the same as doing."]},
"ezek.34":{title:"Behold, I, even I, will both search my sheep, and seek them out",year:"About 586–571 BC",
 sum:"“Woe be to the shepherds of Israel that do feed themselves”—They ate the fat and left the flock scattered—The Lord Himself will seek out His sheep, feed them and bring them home—He will judge between fat and lean sheep—David “my servant” will be their one shepherd, with a covenant of peace and “showers of blessing.”",
 bg:["In the ancient Near East, kings were called shepherds of their people, so this chapter charges Israel’s kings and leaders with failing in their duty. The Lord promises to be the shepherd Himself (verses 11–16) and to set up “my servant David” (verses 23–24), a promise Christians read as fulfilled in Jesus. In John 10:11–16 Jesus says, “I am the good shepherd,” and speaks of “other sheep” not of this fold. Compare Luke 15:4–7.","Latter-day Saints connect those “other sheep” to Jesus’s words to the Nephites in 3 Nephi 15:21. Alma 5:38–39 also calls Christ “the good shepherd” who calls His sheep by name. The “covenant of peace” in verse 25 is repeated in 37:26."]},
"ezek.35":{title:"Behold, O mount Seir, I am against thee",year:"About 586–571 BC",
 sum:"Ezekiel is told to prophesy against Mount Seir—Edom had “a perpetual hatred” and shed Israel’s blood in the day of their calamity—Edom said the two lands would be hers—Because she rejoiced at Israel’s ruin, she will be desolate—“They shall know that I am the LORD.”",
 bg:["Mount Seir is the hill country of Edom, south of the Dead Sea. Edom means “red” and is tied to Esau in Genesis 25:30. Seir’s meaning is uncertain, though it is often taken to mean “hairy” or “rough,” in word-play with Esau’s hairiness (Genesis 25:25). The long feud between Jacob’s and Esau’s families is the background for the charge of “perpetual hatred.”","Edom is judged elsewhere for gloating over Jerusalem’s fall (Psalm 137:7; Obadiah 10–14). Chapters 35 and 36 belong together: while Edom’s mountains fall silent, the mountains of Israel will bloom."]},
"ezek.36":{title:"A new heart also will I give you, and a new spirit will I put within you",year:"About 586–571 BC",
 sum:"Ezekiel speaks to the mountains of Israel, which will again be fruitful—Israel profaned God’s name among the nations, so He will act “for mine holy name’s sake”—He will sprinkle clean water, give “a new heart,” and put His Spirit within them—The ruined land will become “like the garden of Eden.”",
 bg:["Verse 22 says the Lord restores Israel “not for your sakes” but for the sake of His name, which was disgraced among the nations when His people were scattered. The “clean water,” the “new heart” and the “new spirit” (verses 25–27) describe an inner change, not just a return to the land. Jesus’s talk with Nicodemus about being born “of water and of the Spirit” (John 3:5) is often compared.","Latter-day Saints often connect the “new heart” with Mosiah 5:2, where King Benjamin’s people say that the Spirit has wrought “a mighty change in us, or in our hearts.” The “stony heart” and “heart of flesh” are pictures of a heart that refuses to be moved and one that responds."]},
"ezek.37":{title:"Son of man, can these bones live?",year:"About 586–571 BC",
 sum:"In a valley of very dry bones Ezekiel prophesies, and bone, sinew and flesh come together, then breath—“These bones are the whole house of Israel,” who say, “our hope is lost”—The Lord will open their graves and bring them home—The sticks of Judah and Joseph become one in His hand—David is their prince and God’s sanctuary is among them forever.",
 bg:["The Hebrew word ruach in verses 5–14 can mean “breath,” “wind” or “spirit,” and Ezekiel plays on all three. The vision is explained in verse 11: it is a picture of Israel, buried in exile and without hope, brought back to life. Many readers also see in it a promise of resurrection, and the vision was later used that way by Jewish and Christian readers.","In verses 15–20 Ezekiel joins two sticks, “for Judah” and “for Joseph.” Many readers see this as the reuniting of the divided kingdoms under one king. Latter-day Saints also read the sticks as the Bible (Judah) and the Book of Mormon (Joseph, through Ephraim), pointing to 2 Nephi 3:12, 2 Nephi 29:8 and D&C 27:5, where the Lord speaks of “the stick of Ephraim.” That is a Latter-day Saint reading, and the chapter itself speaks of the nation’s unity."]},
"ezek.38":{title:"Set thy face against Gog, the land of Magog",year:"About 586–571 BC",
 sum:"Ezekiel prophesies against Gog of the land of Magog, “chief prince of Meshech and Tubal”—God puts hooks in his jaws and brings him and his allies against a land of unwalled villages—“Thou shalt ascend and come like a storm”—God judges Gog with earthquake, sword, pestilence, fire and brimstone.",
 bg:["Gog, Magog and the places named here are not clearly identified. Meshech and Tubal were peoples in Asia Minor, and Gomer and Togarmah were also in the north; the meaning of “Gog” and “Magog” is uncertain. Some scholars link Gog to a king of Lydia named Gyges, but others disagree. Verse 2 may also read “prince of Rosh” in the Hebrew, and translations differ.","The picture of a final attack on God’s people from the far north was used later by John in Revelation 20:7–9, where “Gog and Magog” stand for the nations Satan gathers at the end. Latter-day Saints also compare D&C 29:21–22. Readers differ on whether Ezekiel means a real future war, a symbol of the last battle between good and evil, or both."]},
"ezek.39":{title:"They shall call it The valley of Hamon-gog",year:"About 586–571 BC",
 sum:"God is against Gog and breaks him on the mountains of Israel—The weapons are burned for seven years—Gog’s multitude is buried in “the valley of Hamon-gog” for seven months—Birds and beasts are called to the great feast—Israel knows the Lord; He will “Neither will I hide my face any more from them” and pours out His spirit.",
 bg:["Hamon-gog means “the multitude of Gog.” A nearby city is named Hamonah, “multitude,” in verse 16. The burying of the dead for seven months, and the burning of weapons for seven years, use the number seven to show a thorough work of cleansing the land.","The feast for the birds and wild animals (verses 17–20) is paralleled in Revelation 19:17–18. Verses 21–29 end with a note of hope: God’s people know why they were exiled (they were unfaithful), and He promises to gather them and pour out His Spirit on the house of Israel, a promise Latter-day Saints also link to the latter-day gathering."]},
"ezek.40":{title:"A man, whose appearance was like the appearance of brass",year:"About 573 BC",
 sum:"In “the five and twentieth year of our captivity,” Ezekiel is taken in visions to a very high mountain in Israel—A man like brass, with a line of flax and a measuring reed, stands at the gate—He measures the east gate, the outer court and the other gates—He measures the inner court and the tables for the offerings—Ezekiel is told to declare all he sees.",
 bg:["This begins the long vision of a new temple (chapters 40–48), dated about fourteen years after Jerusalem fell. The measuring reed is six “long cubits,” a cubit plus a handbreadth (verse 5), roughly 20–21 inches, so the reed is about ten feet. Ezekiel was a priest (1:3), and the care taken with gates, courts and measurements fits that.","Readers differ on how to take this vision. Some see a plan for a literal temple to be built; others see an ideal or symbolic picture of God dwelling with a restored people; and some link it to the temple of the last days. Ezekiel’s temple is not the same as Solomon’s, nor the later temple Zerubbabel built, and scholars note that its plan was never built as described. Latter-day Saints value temples that are built by revelation, as when the Lord gave the Kirtland Temple’s dimensions in D&C 95:13–17."]},
"ezek.41":{title:"This is the table that is before the LORD",year:"About 573 BC",
 sum:"The man measures the temple’s main hall, its doorposts and its side chambers—He enters the inner room, the most holy place, twenty cubits square—Cherubim and palm trees are carved on the walls—A wooden altar stands before the holy place—The doors and doorposts are carved with cherubim and palm trees.",
 bg:["The most holy place is a perfect square, twenty cubits on each side (verse 4), as in Solomon’s temple (1 Kings 6:20). Carved cherubim and palm trees (verses 18–20) also decorated Solomon’s temple, and each cherub had two faces, a man’s and a lion’s. Palms and the figures of living creatures remind readers of Eden and of God’s presence.","The “altar of wood” in verse 22 is called “the table that is before the LORD,” a picture of the table of showbread in the earlier tabernacle and temple. Only the man guiding Ezekiel enters the most holy place; Ezekiel himself stays outside it."]},
"ezek.42":{title:"A wall, to make a separation between the sanctuary and the profane place",year:"About 573 BC",
 sum:"The man brings Ezekiel to the priests’ chambers on the north and south sides—These are holy chambers where priests eat the most holy offerings and leave their garments—The man measures the outer wall on all four sides—The wall separates “the sanctuary and the profane place.”",
 bg:["The priests’ chambers are where those who minister “shall eat the most holy things” and put on other clothes before going out to the people (verse 14), so that holiness is not carried carelessly outside. The Hebrew word for “profane” here means common, ordinary. The wall separates what is set apart for God from everyday space.","The wall is “five hundred reeds” on each side (verses 16–20). With the long reed that would be roughly a mile, much larger than Solomon’s temple area, and some ancient versions read “cubits” instead of reeds, which is why readers are not agreed on the size or on whether to read the numbers literally."]},
"ezek.43":{title:"The glory of the LORD came into the house",year:"About 573 BC",
 sum:"The glory of the God of Israel comes from the east, “like a noise of many waters”—It fills the house, and the Lord says, “this is the place of my throne”—Israel is told to put away whoredom and the carcasses of their kings, and to show the house to the people—The altar is measured and consecrated over seven days.",
 bg:["This is the answer to Ezekiel 10–11, where the glory of the Lord left the old temple by the east gate. Now the same glory enters by the east gate, and the voice is “like a noise of many waters” (verse 2), a phrase echoed in Revelation 1:15. Latter-day Saints also read of the Lord’s voice “as the sound of the rushing of great waters” in D&C 110:3, and His words in D&C 110:7, “my name shall be here,” at the Kirtland Temple’s dedication.","Verse 10 says the plan is shown so the people “may be ashamed of their iniquities,” and verse 12 gives the law of the house: the whole area on the mountaintop “shall be most holy.” The altar is measured in verses 13–17, and its horns and steps are given. Its consecration (verses 18–27) takes seven days, with the Lord’s acceptance promised on the eighth."]},
"ezek.44":{title:"The priests the Levites, the sons of Zadok",year:"About 573 BC",
 sum:"The outer east gate stays shut, for the Lord has entered by it—Only the prince may sit there to eat bread—Foreigners “uncircumcised in heart” are kept from the sanctuary, and Levites who strayed are given lower tasks—The sons of Zadok serve at the altar in linen—Priests must teach the people the difference between holy and common.",
 bg:["Zadok means “righteous.” He served as priest under David and Solomon (1 Kings 2:35), and his family is honored here because they “kept the charge of my sanctuary” when others went astray (verse 15). Priests wear linen, not wool, in the inner court, so that they do not sweat (verses 17–18), and they may not drink wine when they enter (verse 21). Many scholars see in this the later rise of the Zadokite priesthood, though readers also differ on how literally to read it.","The priests’ teaching task is in verse 23: “they shall teach my people the difference between the holy and profane, and cause them to discern between the unclean and the clean.” Verse 9 insists that no foreigner “uncircumcised in heart” may enter, but Ezekiel 47:22–23 later welcomes foreigners living among Israel."]},
"ezek.45":{title:"Ye shall have just balances, and a just ephah, and a just bath",year:"About 573 BC",
 sum:"A holy portion of the land is set apart for the sanctuary, the Levites, the city and the prince—“Let it suffice you, O princes of Israel: remove violence and spoil, and execute judgment and justice”—Weights and measures must be honest—The prince provides the offerings—Passover and the feast of tabernacles are kept.",
 bg:["The ephah (for dry goods) and the bath (for liquids) were the standard measures, about the same size as each other; estimates put them at around five or six gallons, though the exact size is debated. Telling honest weights from false ones was a constant concern in the law and the prophets (Leviticus 19:35–36; Amos 8:5; Micah 6:11). The commands to the princes (verses 8–9) show that Ezekiel’s plan puts justice at the center of the temple’s life.","The set-aside portion of land (verses 1–6) is a square of 25,000 cubits, with the sanctuary in the middle, as though the whole land were arranged around God’s house. Some of the feast days listed differ from those in the law of Moses, a difference Jewish and Christian scholars have discussed for many centuries without full agreement."]},
"ezek.46":{title:"That my people be not scattered every man from his possession",year:"About 573 BC",
 sum:"The east gate is opened on sabbaths and new moons for the prince to worship—The prince’s daily and sabbath offerings are set out—The prince may give gifts of land to his sons but may not take people’s inheritance—Kitchens are set where the priests and people’s offerings are boiled and baked.",
 bg:["Verse 18 protects ordinary families: the prince must not take land from the people by force. This reflects the real problem that earlier kings had done that, as in the story of Naboth’s vineyard (1 Kings 21). The gift of land to a servant lasts only until “the year of liberty” (verse 17), which most scholars link to the jubilee year in Leviticus 25.","The chapter’s last scene (verses 19–24) is about kitchens, where the offerings are cooked so the priests do not carry holy food into the outer court. It is a reminder that in the ancient world worship included shared meals, as well as sacrifice."]},
"ezek.47":{title:"Every thing shall live whither the river cometh",year:"About 573 BC",
 sum:"Waters flow out from under the temple threshold and grow from ankle-deep to a river too deep to cross—They flow down to the Arabah and heal the salt sea—Fish multiply and fishers stand from En-gedi to En-eglaim—On both banks grow trees with fruit every month and healing leaves—The borders of the land are set, and foreigners share the inheritance.",
 bg:["The river runs east and south toward the Dead Sea, which is about 1,400 feet below sea level and too salty for fish. En-gedi is a real oasis on its western shore; En-eglaim’s location is uncertain. Verse 11 says the marshes will stay salt, which supplied salt for the people. The same picture appears in Joel 3:18 and Zechariah 14:8, and John saw a river of the water of life with the tree of life “for the healing of the nations” in Revelation 22:1–2, which echoes this chapter’s trees and leaves.","Readers differ on whether the river is literal, symbolic of the Spirit’s life flowing from God’s presence, or both. Latter-day Saints sometimes compare it to Nephi’s vision of the “fountain of living waters” and the tree of life in 1 Nephi 11:25, which is a similar picture, though a different vision."]},
"ezek.48":{title:"The name of the city from that day shall be, The LORD is there",year:"About 573 BC",
 sum:"The land is divided among the twelve tribes in parallel strips, from Dan in the north to Gad in the south—A holy portion holds the sanctuary, the priests of Zadok, the Levites and the prince’s land—The city has twelve gates named for the tribes—“The name of the city from that day shall be, The LORD is there.”",
 bg:["The last words of the book give the city’s name in Hebrew, Yahweh Shammah (“the LORD is there”), the answer to the vision in chapters 8–11 where the glory left the city. The tribes are laid out in equal east-west strips, with each of the twelve gates named after a tribe (verses 30–34), as in the twelve gates of the new Jerusalem in Revelation 21:12–13.","Readers disagree about whether the plan describes a future temple and city, a symbolic picture of God dwelling with His people, or both. Latter-day Saints, who hold that both a New Jerusalem in the Americas and the old Jerusalem will be built up in the last days, point to Ether 13:5–11 and D&C 45:66–67, but Ezekiel’s chapter does not itself say so."]},
});
